       ----mmaaccooss--ttiittllee--bbaarr--ccoolloorr==<<ccoolloorr>>
              Sets the color of the title bar (default: completely
              transparent). Is influenced by ----mmaaccooss--ttiittllee--bbaarr--aappppeeaarraannccee and
              ----mmaaccooss--ttiittllee--bbaarr--mmaatteerriiaall.  See ----ssuubb--ccoolloorr for color syntax.

       ----mmaaccooss--ffss--aanniimmaattiioonn--dduurraattiioonn==<<ddeeffaauulltt||00--11000000>>
              Sets the fullscreen resize animation duration in ms (default:
              default).  The default value is slightly less than the system's
              animation duration (500ms) to prevent some problems when the end
              of an async animation happens at the same time as the end of the
              system wide fullscreen animation. Setting anything higher than
              500ms will only prematurely cancel the resize animation after
              the system wide animation ended. The upper limit is still set at
              1000ms since it's possible that Apple or the user changes the
              system defaults. Anything higher than 1000ms though seems too
              long and shouldn't be set anyway.  (macOS)

       ----mmaaccooss--aapppp--aaccttiivvaattiioonn--ppoolliiccyy==<<rreegguullaarr||aacccceessssoorryy||pprroohhiibbiitteedd>>
              Changes the App activation policy. With accessory the mpv icon
              in the Dock can be hidden. (default: regular)

              macOS only.

       ----mmaaccooss--ggeeoommeettrryy--ccaallccuullaattiioonn==<<vviissiibbllee||wwhhoollee>>
              This changes the rectangle which is used to calculate the screen
              position and size of the window (default: visible). vviissiibbllee
              takes the the menu bar and Dock into account and the window is
              only positioned/sized within the visible screen frame rectangle,
              wwhhoollee takes the whole screen frame rectangle and ignores the
              menu bar and Dock. Other previous restrictions still apply, like
              the window can't be placed on top of the menu bar etc.

              macOS only.

       ----mmaaccooss--rreennddeerr--ttiimmeerr==<<ttiimmeerr>>
              Sets the mode (default: callback) for syncing the rendering of
              frames to the display's vertical refresh rate.  macOS and Vulkan
              (macvk) only.

              <<ttiimmeerr>> can be one of the following:

              ccaallllbbaacckk
                     Syncs to the CVDisplayLink callback

              pprreecciissee
                     Syncs to the time of the next vertical display refresh
                     reported by the CVDisplayLink callback provided
                     information

              ssyysstteemm No manual syncing, depend on the layer mechanic and the
                     next drawable

              ffeeeeddbbaacckk
                     Same as precise but uses the presentation feedback core
                     mechanism

       ----mmaaccooss--mmeennuu--sshhoorrttccuuttss==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
              Enables the default menu bar shortcuts (default: yes). The menu
              bar shortcuts always take precedence over any other shortcuts,
              they are not propagated to the mpv core and they can't be used
              in config files like iinnppuutt..ccoonnff or script bindings.

       ----mmaaccooss--bbuunnddllee--ppaatthh==ppaatthh11,,ppaatthh22,,......
              App Bundles operate in their own shell environment that is
              different from the one in the terminal. The default PATH
              variable for all Bundles is //uussrr//bbiinn:://bbiinn:://uussrr//ssbbiinn:://ssbbiinn.
              Because of that mpv can not find binaries installed by package
              manager that might be used in scripts for example. This option
              prepends all given paths to the default Bundle PATH.

              Default value in following order:

              //uussrr//llooccaall//bbiinn
                     homebrew (Intel) install path

              //uussrr//llooccaall//ssbbiinn
                     homebrew (Intel) install path

              //oopptt//llooccaall//bbiinn
                     MacPorts install path

              //oopptt//llooccaall//ssbbiinn
                     MacPorts install path

              //oopptt//hhoommeebbrreeww//bbiinn
                     homebrew (ARM) install path

              //oopptt//hhoommeebbrreeww//ssbbiinn
                     homebrew (ARM) install path

       ----aannddrrooiidd--ssuurrffaaccee--ssiizzee==<<WWxxHH>>
              Set dimensions of the rendering surface used by the Android gpu
              context.  Needs to be set by the embedding application if the
              dimensions change during runtime (i.e. if the device is
              rotated), via the surfaceChanged callback.

              Android with ----ggppuu--ccoonntteexxtt==aannddrrooiidd only.

       ----ggppuu--ssww
              Continue even if a software renderer is detected. This only
              works with OpenGL/Vulkan backends. For d3d11, see ----dd33dd1111--wwaarrpp.

       ----ggppuu--ccoonntteexxtt==<<ccoonntteexxtt11,,ccoonntteexxtt22,,......[[,,]]>>
              Specify a priority list of the GPU contexts to be used.  The
              value aauuttoo (the default) selects the GPU context with the
              default autoprobe order. You can also pass hheellpp to get a
              complete list of compiled in backends (sorted by the default
              autoprobe order).

              Note that the default GPU context is subject to change, and must
              not be relied upon.  If a certain GPU context needs to be used,
              it must be explicitly specified.

              aauuttoo   auto-select (default). Note that this context must be
                     used alone and does not participate in the priority list.

              wwiinn    Win32/WGL

              wwiinnvvkk  VK_KHR_win32_surface

              aannggllee  Direct3D11 through the OpenGL ES translation layer ANGLE.
                     This supports almost everything the wwiinn backend does (if
                     the ANGLE build is new enough).

              ddxxiinntteerroopp ((eexxppeerriimmeennttaall))
                     Win32, using WGL for rendering and Direct3D 9Ex for
                     presentation. Works on Nvidia and AMD. Newer Intel chips
                     with the latest drivers may also work.

              dd33dd1111  Win32, with native Direct3D 11 rendering.

              xx1111    X11/GLX (deprecated/legacy, EGL is preferred these days)

              xx1111vvkk  VK_KHR_xlib_surface

              wwaayyllaanndd
                     Wayland/EGL

              wwaayyllaannddvvkk
                     VK_KHR_wayland_surface

              ddrrmm    DRM/EGL

              ddiissppllaayyvvkk
                     VK_KHR_display. This backend is roughly the Vulkan
                     equivalent of DRM/EGL, allowing for direct rendering via
                     Vulkan without a display manager.

              xx1111eeggll X11/EGL

              aannddrrooiidd
                     Android/EGL. Requires ----wwiidd be set to an
                     aannddrrooiidd..vviieeww..SSuurrffaaccee.

              mmaaccvvkk  Vulkan on macOS with a metal surface through a
                     translation layer (experimental)

              This is an object settings list option. See List Options for
              details.

       ----ggppuu--aappii==<<ttyyppee11,,ttyyppee22,,......[[,,]]>>
              Specify a priority list of accepted graphics APIs.

              aauuttoo   Use any available API (default). Note that the default
                     GPU API used for this value is subject to change, and
                     must not be relied upon. If a certain GPU API needs to be
                     used, it must be explicitly specified.

              ooppeennggll Allow only OpenGL (requires OpenGL 2.1+ or GLES 2.0+)

              vvuullkkaann Allow only Vulkan (requires a valid/working
                     ----ssppiirrvv--ccoommppiilleerr)

              dd33dd1111  Allow only ----ggppuu--ccoonntteexxtt==dd33dd1111

              This is an object settings list option. See List Options for
              details.

       ----ooppeennggll--eess==<<mmooddee>>
              Controls which type of OpenGL context will be accepted:

              aauuttoo   Allow all types of OpenGL (default)

              yyeess    Only allow GLES

              nnoo     Only allow desktop/core GL

       ----ffbboo--ffoorrmmaatt==<<ffmmtt>>
              Selects the internal format of textures used for FBOs. The
              format can influence performance and quality of the video
              output. ffmmtt can be one of: rgb8, rgb10, rgb10_a2, rgb16, rgb16f,
              rgb32f, rgba12, rgba16, rgba16f, rgba16hf, rgba32f.

              Default: aauuttoo, which first attempts to utilize 16bit float
              (rgba16f, rgba16hf), and falls back to rgba16 if those are not
              available.  Finally, attempts to utilize rgb10_a2 or rgba8 if
              all of the previous formats are not available.

       ----ggaammmmaa--ffaaccttoorr==<<00..11....22..00>>
              Set an additional raw gamma factor (default: 1.0). If gamma is
              adjusted in other ways (like with the ----ggaammmmaa option or key
              bindings and the ggaammmmaa property), the value is multiplied with
              the other gamma value.

       ----ggaammmmaa--aauuttoo
              Automatically corrects the gamma value depending on ambient
              lighting conditions (adding a gamma boost for bright rooms).

              This option is deprecated and may be removed in the future.

              NOTE: Only implemented on macOS and ----vvoo==ggppuu.

       ----iimmaaggee--lluutt==<<ffiillee>>
              Specifies a custom LUT file (in Adobe .cube format) to apply to
              the colors during image decoding. The exact interpretation of
              the LUT depends on the value of ----iimmaaggee--lluutt--ttyyppee. (Only for
              ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt)

       ----iimmaaggee--lluutt--ttyyppee==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Controls the interpretation of color values fed to and from the
              LUT specified as ----iimmaaggee--lluutt. Valid values are:

              aauuttoo   Chooses the interpretation of the LUT automatically from
                     tagged metadata, and otherwise falls back to nnaattiivvee.
                     (Default)

              nnaattiivvee Applied to the raw image contents in its native
                     colorspace, before decoding to RGB. For example, for a
                     HDR10 image, this would be fed PQ-encoded YCbCr values in
                     the range 0.0 - 1.0.

              nnoorrmmaalliizzeedd
                     Applied to the normalized RGB image contents, after
                     decoding from its native color encoding, but before
                     linearization.

              ccoonnvveerrssiioonn
                     Fully replaces the color decoding. A LUT of this type
                     should ingest the image's native colorspace and output
                     normalized non-linear RGB.

       ----ttaarrggeett--ccoolloorrssppaaccee--hhiinntt==<<aauuttoo||yyeess||nnoo>>
              Automatically configure the output colorspace of the display to
              pass through the input values of the stream (e.g. for HDR
              passthrough), if possible. In aauuttoo mode, the target colorspace
              is only set, if the display signals support for HDR colorspace.
              Requires a supporting driver and ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt. (Default: nnoo)

       ----ttaarrggeett--pprriimm==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Specifies the primaries of the display. Video colors will be
              adapted to this colorspace when ICC color management is not
              being used. Valid values are:

              aauuttoo   Disable any adaptation, except for atypical color spaces.
                     Specifically, wide/unusual gamuts get automatically
                     adapted to BT.709, while standard gamut (i.e. BT.601 and
                     BT.709) content is not touched. (default)

              bbtt..447700mm
                     ITU-R BT.470 M

              bbtt..660011--552255
                     ITU-R BT.601 (525-line SD systems, eg. NTSC), SMPTE
                     170M/240M

              bbtt..660011--662255
                     ITU-R BT.601 (625-line SD systems, eg. PAL/SECAM), ITU-R
                     BT.470 B/G

              bbtt..770099 ITU-R BT.709 (HD), IEC 61966-2-4 (sRGB), SMPTE RP177
                     Annex B

              bbtt..22002200
                     ITU-R BT.2020 (UHD)

              aappppllee  Apple RGB

              aaddoobbee  Adobe RGB (1998)

              pprroopphhoottoo
                     ProPhoto RGB (ROMM)

              cciiee11993311
                     CIE 1931 RGB (not to be confused with CIE XYZ)

              ddccii--pp33 DCI-P3 (Digital Cinema Colorspace), SMPTE RP431-2

              vv--ggaammuutt
                     Panasonic V-Gamut (VARICAM) primaries

              ss--ggaammuutt
                     Sony S-Gamut (S-Log) primaries

       ----ttaarrggeett--ttrrcc==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Specifies the transfer characteristics (gamma) of the display.
              Video colors will be adjusted to this curve when ICC color
              management is not being used.  Valid values are:

              aauuttoo   Disable any adaptation, except for atypical transfers.
                     Specifically, HDR or linear light source material gets
                     automatically converted to gamma 2.2, while SDR content
                     is not touched. (default)

              bbtt..11888866
                     ITU-R BT.1886 curve (assuming infinite contrast)

              ssrrggbb   IEC 61966-2-4 (sRGB)

              lliinneeaarr Linear light output

              ggaammmmaa11..88
                     Pure power curve (gamma 1.8), also used for Apple RGB

              ggaammmmaa22..00
                     Pure power curve (gamma 2.0)

              ggaammmmaa22..22
                     Pure power curve (gamma 2.2)

              ggaammmmaa22..44
                     Pure power curve (gamma 2.4)

              ggaammmmaa22..66
                     Pure power curve (gamma 2.6)

              ggaammmmaa22..88
                     Pure power curve (gamma 2.8), also used for BT.470-BG

              pprroopphhoottoo
                     ProPhoto RGB (ROMM)

              ppqq     ITU-R BT.2100 PQ (Perceptual quantizer) curve, aka SMPTE
                     ST2084

              hhllgg    ITU-R BT.2100 HLG (Hybrid Log-gamma) curve, aka ARIB
                     STD-B67

              vv--lloogg  Panasonic V-Log (VARICAM) curve

              ss--lloogg11 Sony S-Log1 curve

              ss--lloogg22 Sony S-Log2 curve

              NNOOTTEE::
                 When using HDR output formats, mpv will encode to the
                 specified curve but it will not set any HDMI flags or other
                 signalling that might be required for the target device to
                 correctly display the HDR signal.  The user should
                 independently guarantee this before using these signal
                 formats for display.

       ----ttaarrggeett--ppeeaakk==<<aauuttoo||nniittss>>
              Specifies the measured peak brightness of the output display, in
              cd/m^2 (AKA nits). The interpretation of this brightness depends
              on the configured ----ttaarrggeett--ttrrcc. In all cases, it imposes a limit
              on the signal values that will be sent to the display. If the
              source exceeds this brightness level, a tone mapping filter will
              be inserted. For HLG, it has the additional effect of
              parametrizing the inverse OOTF, in order to get colorimetrically
              consistent results with the mastering display. For SDR, or when
              using an ICC (profile (----iicccc--pprrooffiillee), setting this to a value
              above 203 essentially causes the display to be treated as if it
              were an HDR display in disguise. (See the note below)

              In aauuttoo mode (the default), the chosen peak is an appropriate
              value based on the TRC in use. For SDR curves, it uses 203. For
              HDR curves, it uses 203 * the transfer function's nominal peak.
              If available, it will use the target display's peak brightness
              as reported by the display.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 When using an SDR transfer function, this is normally not
                 needed, and setting it may lead to very unexpected results.
                 The one time it _i_s useful is if you want to calibrate a HDR
                 display using traditional transfer functions and calibration
                 equipment. In such cases, you can set your HDR display to a
                 high brightness such as 800 cd/m^2, and then calibrate it to
                 a standard curve like gamma2.8. Setting this value to 800
                 would then instruct mpv to essentially treat it as an HDR
                 display with the given peak. This may be a good alternative
                 in environments where PQ or HLG input to the display is not
                 possible, and makes it possible to use HDR displays with mpv
                 regardless of operating system support for HDMI HDR metadata.

                 In such a configuration, we highly recommend setting
                 ----ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg to mmoobbiiuuss or even cclliipp.

       ----ttaarrggeett--ccoonnttrraasstt==<<aauuttoo||1100--11000000000000||iinnff>>
              Specifies the measured contrast of the output display.
              ----ttaarrggeett--ccoonnttrraasstt in conjunction with ----ttaarrggeett--ppeeaakk value is
              used to calculate display black point. Used in black point
              compensation during HDR tone-mapping.  aauuttoo is the default and
              assumes 1000:1 contrast as a typical SDR display would have or
              an infinite contrast when HDR ----ttaarrggeett--ttrrcc is used.  If
              supported by the API, display contrast will be used as reported.
              iinnff contrast specifies display with perfect black level, in
              practice OLED.  (Only for ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt)

       ----ttaarrggeett--ggaammuutt==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Constrains the gamut of the display. You can use this option to
              output e.g.  DCIP3-in-BT.2020. Set ----ttaarrggeett--pprriimm to the
              primaries of the containing colorspace (into which values will
              be encoded), and ----ttaarrggeett--ggaammuutt to the gamut you want to limit
              colors to. Takes the same values as ----ttaarrggeett--pprriimm. (Only for
              ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt)

       ----ttaarrggeett--lluutt==<<ffiillee>>
              Specifies a custom LUT file (in Adobe .cube format) to apply to
              the colors before display on-screen. This LUT is fed values in
              normalized RGB, after encoding into the target colorspace, so
              after the application of ----ttaarrggeett--ttrrcc. (Only for ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt)

       ----ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Specifies the algorithm used for tone-mapping images onto the
              target display. This is relevant for both HDR->SDR conversion as
              well as gamut reduction (e.g. playing back BT.2020 content on a
              standard gamut display).  Valid values are:

              aauuttoo   Maps to bbtt..22339900 when using ----vvoo==ggppuu, and to sspplliinnee with
                     ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt. (Default)

              cclliipp   Hard-clip any out-of-range values. Use this when you care
                     about perfect color accuracy for in-range values at the
                     cost of completely distorting out-of-range values. Not
                     generally recommended.

              mmoobbiiuuss Generalization of Reinhard to a Möbius transform with
                     linear section.  Smoothly maps out-of-range values while
                     retaining contrast and colors for in-range material as
                     much as possible. Use this when you care about color
                     accuracy more than detail preservation. This is somewhere
                     in between cclliipp and rreeiinnhhaarrdd, depending on the value of
                     ----ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg--ppaarraamm.

              rreeiinnhhaarrdd
                     Reinhard tone mapping algorithm. Very simple continuous
                     curve.  Preserves overall image brightness but uses
                     nonlinear contrast, which results in flattening of
                     details and degradation in color accuracy.

              hhaabbllee  Similar to rreeiinnhhaarrdd but preserves both dark and bright
                     details better (slightly sigmoidal), at the cost of
                     slightly darkening / desaturating everything. Developed
                     by John Hable for use in video games. Use this when you
                     care about detail preservation more than color/brightness
                     accuracy. This is roughly equivalent to
                     ----ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg==rreeiinnhhaarrdd ----ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg--ppaarraamm==00..2244. If
                     possible, you should also enable ----hhddrr--ccoommppuuttee--ppeeaakk for
                     the best results.

              bbtt..22339900
                     Perceptual tone mapping curve (EETF) specified in ITU-R
                     Report BT.2390.

              ggaammmmaa  Fits a logarithmic transfer between the tone curves.

              lliinneeaarr Linearly stretches the entire reference gamut to (a
                     linear multiple of) the display.

              sspplliinnee Perceptually linear single-pivot polynomial.
                     (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

              bbtt..22444466aa
                     HDR<->SDR mapping specified in ITU-R Report BT.2446,
                     method A. This is the recommended curve for well-mastered
                     content. (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

              sstt22009944--4400
                     Dynamic HDR10+ tone-mapping method specified in SMPTE
                     ST2094-40 Annex B. In the absence of metadata, falls back
                     to a fixed spline matched to the input/output average
                     brightness characteristics. (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

              sstt22009944--1100
                     Dynamic tone-mapping method specified in SMPTE ST2094-10
                     Annex B.2.  Conceptually simpler than ST2094-40, and
                     generally produces worse results.

       ----ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg--ppaarraamm==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Set tone mapping parameters. By default, this is set to the
              special string ddeeffaauulltt, which maps to an algorithm-specific
              default value. Ignored if the tone mapping algorithm is not
              tunable. This affects the following tone mapping algorithms:

              cclliipp   Specifies an extra linear coefficient to multiply into
                     the signal before clipping. Defaults to 1.0.

              mmoobbiiuuss Specifies the transition point from linear to mobius
                     transform. Every value below this point is guaranteed to
                     be mapped 1:1. The higher the value, the more accurate
                     the result will be, at the cost of losing bright details.
                     Defaults to 0.3, which due to the steep initial slope
                     still preserves in-range colors fairly accurately.

              rreeiinnhhaarrdd
                     Specifies the local contrast coefficient at the display
                     peak. Defaults to 0.5, which means that in-gamut values
                     will be about half as bright as when clipping.

              bbtt..22339900
                     Specifies the offset for the knee point. Defaults to 1.0,
                     which is higher than the value from the original ITU-R
                     specification (0.5).  (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

              ggaammmmaa  Specifies the exponent of the function. Defaults to 1.8.

              lliinneeaarr Specifies the scale factor to use while stretching.
                     Defaults to 1.0.

              sspplliinnee Specifies the knee point (in PQ space). Defaults to 0.30.

              sstt22009944--1100
                     Specifies the contrast (slope) at the knee point.
                     Defaults to 1.0.

       ----iinnvveerrssee--ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg
              If set, allows inverse tone mapping (expanding SDR to HDR). Not
              supported by all tone mapping curves. Use with caution.
              (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

       ----ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg--mmaaxx--bboooosstt==<<11..00....1100..00>>
              Upper limit for how much the tone mapping algorithm is allowed
              to boost the average brightness by over-exposing the image. The
              default value of 1.0 allows no additional brightness boost. A
              value of 2.0 would allow over-exposing by a factor of 2, and so
              on. Raising this setting can help reveal details that would
              otherwise be hidden in dark scenes, but raising it too high will
              make dark scenes appear unnaturally bright. (----vvoo==ggppuu only)

       ----ttoonnee--mmaappppiinngg--vviissuuaalliizzee
              Display a (PQ-PQ) graph of the active tone-mapping LUT. Intended
              only for debugging purposes. The X axis shows PQ input values,
              the Y axis shows PQ output values. The tone-mapping curve is
              shown in green/yellow. Yellow means the brightness has been
              boosted from the source, dark blue regions show where the
              brightness has been reduced. The extra colored regions and lines
              indicate various monitor limits, as well a reference diagonal
              (neutral tone-mapping) and source scene average brightness
              information (if available). (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

       ----ggaammuutt--mmaappppiinngg--mmooddee
              Specifies the algorithm used for reducing the gamut of images
              for the target display, after any tone mapping is done.

              aauuttoo   Choose the best mode automatically. (Default)

              cclliipp   Hard-clip to the gamut (per-channel). Very low quality,
                     but free.

              ppeerrcceeppttuuaall
                     Performs a perceptually balanced gamut mapping using a
                     soft knee function to roll-off clipped regions, and a hue
                     shifting function to preserve saturation. (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt
                     only)

              rreellaattiivvee
                     Performs relative colorimetric clipping, while
                     maintaining an exponential relationship between
                     brightness and chromaticity.  (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

              ssaattuurraattiioonn
                     Performs simple RGB->RGB saturation mapping. The input
                     R/G/B channels are mapped directly onto the output R/G/B
                     channels. Will never clip, but will distort all hues
                     and/or result in a faded look.  (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

              aabbssoolluuttee
                     Performs absolute colorimetric clipping. Like rreellaattiivvee,
                     but does not adapt the white point. (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

              ddeessaattuurraattee
                     Performs constant-luminance colorimetric clipping,
                     desaturing colors towards white until they're in-range.

              ddaarrkkeenn Uniformly darkens the input slightly to prevent clipping
                     on blown-out highlights, then clamps colorimetrically to
                     the input gamut boundary, biased slightly to preserve
                     chromaticity over luminance.  (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

              wwaarrnn   Performs no gamut mapping, but simply highlights
                     out-of-gamut pixels.

              lliinneeaarr Linearly/uniformly desaturates the image in order to
                     bring the entire image into the target gamut.
                     (----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only)

       ----hhddrr--ccoommppuuttee--ppeeaakk==<<aauuttoo||yyeess||nnoo>>
              Compute the HDR peak and frame average brightness per-frame
              instead of relying on tagged metadata. These values are averaged
              over local regions as well as over several frames to prevent the
              value from jittering around too much. This option basically
              gives you dynamic, per-scene tone mapping.  Requires compute
              shaders, which is a fairly recent OpenGL feature, and will
              probably also perform horribly on some drivers, so enable at
              your own risk.  The special value aauuttoo (default) will enable HDR
              peak computation automatically if compute shaders and SSBOs are
              supported.

       ----aallllooww--ddeellaayyeedd--ppeeaakk--ddeetteecctt
              When using ----hhddrr--ccoommppuuttee--ppeeaakk, allow delaying the detected peak
              by a frame when beneficial for performance. In particular, this
              is required to avoid an unnecessary FBO indirection when no
              advanced rendering is required otherwise. Has no effect if there
              already is an indirect pass, such as when advanced scaling is
              enabled. Defaults to no. (Only affects ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt, note that
              ----vvoo==ggppuu always delays the peak.)

       ----hhddrr--ppeeaakk--ppeerrcceennttiillee==<<00..00....110000..00>>
              Which percentile of the input image brightness histogram to
              consider as the true peak of the scene. If this is set to 100
              (default), the brightest pixel is measured. Otherwise, the top
              of the frequency distribution is progressively cut off. Setting
              this too low will cause clipping of very bright details, but can
              improve the dynamic brightness range of scenes with very bright
              isolated highlights. Values other than 100 come with a small
              performance penalty. (Only for ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt)

       ----hhddrr--ppeeaakk--ddeeccaayy--rraattee==<<00..00....11000000..00>>
              The decay rate used for the HDR peak detection algorithm
              (default: 20.0).  This is only relevant when ----hhddrr--ccoommppuuttee--ppeeaakk
              is enabled. Higher values make the peak decay more slowly,
              leading to more stable values at the cost of more "eye
              adaptation"-like effects (although this is mitigated somewhat by
              ----hhddrr--sscceennee--tthhrreesshhoolldd). A value of 0.0 (the lowest possible)
              disables all averaging, meaning each frame's value is used
              directly as measured, but doing this is not recommended for
              "noisy" sources since it may lead to excessive flicker. (In
              signal theory terms, this controls the time constant "tau" of an
              IIR low pass filter)

       ----hhddrr--sscceennee--tthhrreesshhoolldd--llooww==<<00..00....110000..00>>,,
       ----hhddrr--sscceennee--tthhrreesshhoolldd--hhiigghh==<<00..00....110000..00>>
              The lower and upper thresholds (in dB) for a brightness
              difference to be considered a scene change (default: 1.0 low,
              3.0 high). This is only relevant when ----hhddrr--ccoommppuuttee--ppeeaakk is
              enabled. Normally, small fluctuations in the frame brightness
              are compensated for by the peak averaging mechanism, but for
              large jumps in the brightness this can result in the frame
              remaining too bright or too dark for up to several seconds,
              depending on the value of ----hhddrr--ppeeaakk--ddeeccaayy--rraattee. To counteract
              this, when the brightness between the running average and the
              current frame exceeds the low threshold, mpv will make the
              averaging filter more aggressive, up to the limit of the high
              threshold (at which point the filter becomes instant).

       ----hhddrr--ccoonnttrraasstt--rreeccoovveerryy==<<00..00....22..00>>,,
       ----hhddrr--ccoonnttrraasstt--ssmmooootthhnneessss==<<11..00....110000..00>>
              Enables the HDR contrast recovery algorithm, which is to
              designed to enhance contrast of HDR video after tone mapping.
              The strength (default: 0.0) indicates the degree of contrast
              recovery, with 0.0 being completely disabled and 1.0 being 100%
              strength. Values higher than 1.0 are allowed, but may result in
              excessive sharpening. The smoothness (default: 3.5) indicates
              the degree to which the HDR source is low-passed in order to
              obtain contrast information - a value of 2.0 corresponds to 2x
              downscaling.  Users on low DPI displays (<= 100) may want to
              lower this value, while users on very high DPI displays
              ("retina") may want to increase it. (Only for vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt)

       ----uussee--eemmbbeeddddeedd--iicccc--pprrooffiillee
              Load the embedded ICC profile contained in media files such as
              PNG images.  (Default: yes). Note that this option only works
              when also using a display ICC profile (----iicccc--pprrooffiillee or
              ----iicccc--pprrooffiillee--aauuttoo), and also requires LittleCMS 2 support.

       ----iicccc--pprrooffiillee==<<ffiillee>>
              Load an ICC profile and use it to transform video RGB to screen
              output.  Needs LittleCMS 2 support compiled in. This option
              overrides the ----ttaarrggeett--pprriimm, ----ttaarrggeett--ttrrcc and ----iicccc--pprrooffiillee--aauuttoo
              options.

       ----iicccc--pprrooffiillee--aauuttoo
              Automatically select the ICC display profile currently specified
              by the display settings of the operating system.

              NOTE: On Windows, the default profile must be an ICC profile.
              WCS profiles are not supported.

              Applications using libmpv with the render API need to provide
              the ICC profile via MMPPVV__RREENNDDEERR__PPAARRAAMM__IICCCC__PPRROOFFIILLEE.

       ----iicccc--ccaacchhee
              Store and load 3DLUTs created from the ICC profile on disk in
              the cache directory (Default: yyeess). This can be used to speed up
              loading, since LittleCMS 2 can take a while to create a 3D LUT.
              Note that these files contain uncompressed LUTs. Their size
              depends on the ----iicccc--33ddlluutt--ssiizzee, and can be very big.

              On _-_-_v_o_=_g_p_u_-_n_e_x_t, files that have not been accessed in the last
              24 hours may be cleared if the cache limit (1.5 GiB) is
              exceeded.

              On ----vvoo==ggppuu, this is not cleaned automatically, so old, unused
              cache files may stick around indefinitely.

       ----iicccc--ccaacchhee--ddiirr
              The directory where icc cache is stored. Cache is stored in the
              system's cache directory (usually ~~//..ccaacchhee//mmppvv) if this is
              unset.

       ----iicccc--iinntteenntt==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Specifies the ICC intent used for the color transformation (when
              using ----iicccc--pprrooffiillee).

              00      perceptual

              11      relative colorimetric (default)

              22      saturation

              33      absolute colorimetric

       ----iicccc--33ddlluutt--ssiizzee==<<aauuttoo||RRxxGGxxBB>>
              Size of the 3D LUT generated from the ICC profile in each
              dimension. The default of aauuttoo means to pick the size
              automatically based on the profile characteristics. Sizes may
              range from 2 to 512.

              NOTE: Setting this option to anything other than aauuttoo is
              ssttrroonnggllyy discouraged, except for testing.

       ----iicccc--ffoorrccee--ccoonnttrraasstt==<<nnoo||00--11000000000000||iinnff>>
              Override the target device's detected contrast ratio by a
              specific value.  This is detected automatically from the profile
              if possible, but for some profiles it might be missing, causing
              the contrast to be assumed as infinite. As a result, video may
              appear darker than intended. If this is the case, setting this
              option might help. This only affects BT.1886 content. The
              default of nnoo means to use the profile values. The special value
              iinnff causes the BT.1886 curve to be treated as a pure power gamma
              2.4 function.

       ----iicccc--uussee--lluummaa
              Use ICC profile luminance value. (Only for ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt)

       ----lluutt==<<ffiillee>>
              Specifies a custom LUT (in Adobe .cube format) to apply to the
              colors as part of color conversion. The exact interpretation
              depends on the value of ----lluutt--ttyyppee. (Only for ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt)

       ----lluutt--ttyyppee==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Controls the interpretation of color values fed to and from the
              LUT specified as ----lluutt. Valid values are:

              aauuttoo   Chooses the interpretation of the LUT automatically from
                     tagged metadata, and otherwise falls back to nnaattiivvee.
                     (Default)

              nnaattiivvee Applied to raw image contents in its native RGB
                     colorspace (non-linear light), before conversion to the
                     output color space.

              nnoorrmmaalliizzeedd
                     Applied to the normalized RGB image contents, in linear
                     light, before conversion to the output color space.

              ccoonnvveerrssiioonn
                     Fully replaces the conversion from the image color space
                     to the output color space. If such a LUT is present, it
                     has the highest priority, and overrides any ICC profiles,
                     as well as options related to tone mapping and output
                     colorimetry (----ttaarrggeett--pprriimm, ----ttaarrggeett--ttrrcc etc.).

       ----bblleenndd--ssuubbttiittlleess==<<yyeess||vviiddeeoo||nnoo>>
              Blend subtitles directly onto upscaled video frames, before
              interpolation and/or color management (default: no). Enabling
              this causes subtitles to be affected by ----iicccc--pprrooffiillee,
              ----ttaarrggeett--pprriimm, ----ttaarrggeett--ttrrcc, ----iinntteerrppoollaattiioonn, ----ggaammmmaa--ffaaccttoorr and
              ----ggllssll--sshhaaddeerrss. It also increases subtitle performance when
              using ----iinntteerrppoollaattiioonn.

              The downside of enabling this is that it restricts subtitles to
              the visible portion of the video, so you can't have subtitles
              exist in the black margins below a video (for example).

              If vviiddeeoo is selected, the behavior is similar to yyeess, but subs
              are drawn at the video's native resolution, and scaled along
              with the video.

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 This changes the way subtitle colors are handled. Normally,
                 subtitle colors are assumed to be in sRGB and color managed
                 as such. Enabling this makes them treated as being in the
                 video's color space instead. This is good if you want things
                 like softsubbed ASS signs to match the video colors, but may
                 cause SRT subtitles or similar to look slightly off.

       ----bbaacckkggrroouunndd==<<nnoonnee||ccoolloorr||ttiilleess>>
              If the frame has an alpha component, decide what kind of
              background, if any, to blend it with. This does nothing if there
              is no alpha component.

              ccoolloorr  Blend the frame against the background color
                     (----bbaacckkggrroouunndd--ccoolloorr, normally black).

              ttiilleess  Blend the frame against a 16x16 gray/white tiles
                     background (default).

              nnoonnee   Do not blend the frame and leave the alpha as is.

              Background transparency on d3d11 requires ----dd33dd1111--fflliipp==nnoo.

              Before mpv 0.38.0, this option used to accept a color value
              specifying the background color. This is now done by the
              ----bbaacckkggrroouunndd--ccoolloorr option.  Use that instead.

       ----bbaacckkggrroouunndd--ccoolloorr==<<ccoolloorr>>
              Color used to draw parts of the mpv window not covered by video.
              See the ----ssuubb--ccoolloorr option for how colors are defined.

       ----bboorrddeerr--bbaacckkggrroouunndd==<<nnoonnee||ccoolloorr||ttiilleess>>
              Same as ----bbaacckkggrroouunndd but only applies to the black bar/border
              area of the window. vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt only. Defaults to ccoolloorr.

       ----ooppeennggll--rreeccttaannggllee--tteexxttuurreess
              Force use of rectangle textures (default: no). Normally this
              shouldn't have any advantages over normal textures. Note that
              hardware decoding overrides this flag. Could be removed any
              time.

       ----ggppuu--tteexx--ppaadd--xx,, ----ggppuu--tteexx--ppaadd--yy
              Enlarge the video source textures by this many pixels. For
              debugging only (normally textures are sized exactly, but due to
              hardware decoding interop we may have to deal with additional
              padding, which can be tested with these options). Could be
              removed any time.

       ----ooppeennggll--eeaarrllyy--fflluusshh==<<yyeess||nnoo||aauuttoo>>
              Call ggllFFlluusshh(()) after rendering a frame and before attempting to
              display it (default: auto). Can fix stuttering in some cases, in
              other cases probably causes it. The aauuttoo mode will call
              ggllFFlluusshh(()) only if the renderer is going to wait for a while
              after rendering, instead of flipping GL front and backbuffers
              immediately (i.e. it doesn't call it in display-sync mode).

              On macOS this is always deactivated because it only causes
              performance problems and other regressions.

       ----ggppuu--dduummbb--mmooddee==<<yyeess||nnoo||aauuttoo>>
              This mode is extremely restricted, and will disable most
              extended features. That includes high quality scalers and custom
              shaders!

              It is intended for hardware that does not support FBOs
              (including GLES, which supports it insufficiently), or to get
              some more performance out of bad or old hardware.

              This mode is forced automatically if needed, and this option is
              mostly useful for debugging. The default of aauuttoo will enable it
              automatically if nothing uses features which require FBOs.

              This option might be silently removed in the future.

       ----ggppuu--sshhaaddeerr--ccaacchhee
              Store and load compiled GLSL shaders in the cache directory
              (Default: yyeess). Normally, shader compilation is very fast, so
              this is not usually needed. It mostly matters for anything
              involving GLSL to SPIR-V conversion, that is: D3D11, ANGLE or
              Vulkan, as well as on some other proprietary drivers. Enabling
              this can improve startup performance on these platforms.

              On _-_-_v_o_=_g_p_u_-_n_e_x_t, files that have not been accessed in the last
              24 hours may be cleared if the cache limit (128 MiB) is
              exceeded.

              On ----vvoo==ggppuu, this is not cleaned automatically, so old, unused
              cache files may stick around indefinitely.

       ----ggppuu--sshhaaddeerr--ccaacchhee--ddiirr
              The directory where gpu shader cache is stored. Cache is stored
              in the system's cache directory (usually ~~//..ccaacchhee//mmppvv) if this
              is unset.

       ----lliibbppllaacceebboo--ooppttss==<<kkeeyy>>==<<vvaalluuee>>[[,,<<kkeeyy>>==<<vvaalluuee>>[[,,......]]]]
              Passes extra raw option to the libplacebo rendering backend
              (used by ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt). May override the effects of any other
              options set using the normal options system. Requires libplacebo
              v6.309 or higher. Included for debugging purposes only. For more
              information, see:

               <https://libplacebo.org/options/>

   VViiddeeoo SSyynncc

       ----mmcc==<<sseeccoonnddss//ffrraammee>>
              Maximum A-V sync correction per frame (in seconds)

       ----aauuttoossyynncc==<<ffaaccttoorr>>
              Gradually adjusts the A/V sync based on audio delay
              measurements.  Specifying ----aauuttoossyynncc==00, the default, will cause
              frame timing to be based entirely on audio delay measurements.
              Specifying ----aauuttoossyynncc==11 will do the same, but will subtly change
              the A/V correction algorithm. An uneven video framerate in a
              video which plays fine with ----aauuddiioo==nnoo can often be helped by
              setting this to an integer value greater than 1. The higher the
              value, the closer the timing will be to ----aauuddiioo==nnoo. Try
              ----aauuttoossyynncc==3300 to smooth out problems with sound drivers which do
              not implement a perfect audio delay measurement. With this
              value, if large A/V sync offsets occur, they will only take
              about 1 or 2 seconds to settle out. This delay in reaction time
              to sudden A/V offsets should be the only side effect of turning
              this option on, for all sound drivers.

       ----vviiddeeoo--ttiimmiinngg--ooffffsseett==<<sseeccoonnddss>>
              Control how long before video display target time the frame
              should be rendered (default: 0.050). If a video frame should be
              displayed at a certain time, the VO will start rendering the
              frame earlier, and then will perform a blocking wait until the
              display time, and only then "swap" the frame to display. The
              rendering cannot start before the previous frame is displayed,
              so this value is implicitly limited by the video framerate. With
              normal video frame rates, the default value will ensure that
              rendering is always immediately started after the previous frame
              was displayed. On the other hand, setting a too high value can
              reduce responsiveness with low FPS value.

              This option is interesting for client API users using the render
              API because you can stop it from limiting your FPS (see
              mmppvv__rreennddeerr__ccoonntteexxtt__rreennddeerr(()) documentation).

              This applies only to audio timing modes (e.g.
              ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==aauuddiioo). In other modes (----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==ddiissppllaayy--......),
              video timing relies on vsync blocking, and this option is not
              used.

       ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==<<aauuddiioo||......>>
              How the player synchronizes audio and video.

              If you use this option, you usually want to set it to
              ddiissppllaayy--rreessaammppllee to enable a timing mode that tries to not skip
              or repeat frames when for example playing 24fps video on a 24Hz
              screen.

              The modes starting with ddiissppllaayy-- try to output video frames
              completely synchronously to the display, using the detected
              display vertical refresh rate as a hint how fast frames will be
              displayed on average. These modes change video speed slightly to
              match the display. See ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc--...... options for fine tuning.
              The robustness of this mode is further reduced by making a some
              idealized assumptions, which may not always apply in reality.
              Behavior can depend on the VO and the system's video and audio
              drivers.  Media files must use constant framerate. Section-wise
              VFR might work as well with some container formats (but not e.g.
              mkv).

              Under some circumstances, the player automatically reverts to
              aauuddiioo mode for some time or permanently. This can happen on very
              low framerate video, or if the framerate cannot be detected.

              Also in display-sync modes it can happen that interruptions to
              video playback (such as toggling fullscreen mode, or simply
              resizing the window) will skip the video frames that should have
              been displayed, while aauuddiioo mode will display them after the
              renderer has resumed (typically resulting in a short A/V desync
              and the video "catching up").

              Before mpv 0.30.0, there was a fallback to aauuddiioo mode on severe
              A/V desync. This was changed for the sake of not sporadically
              stopping. Now, ddiissppllaayy--ddeessyynncc does what it promises and may
              desync with audio by an arbitrary amount, until it is manually
              fixed with a seek.

              These modes also require a vsync blocked presentation mode. For
              OpenGL, this translates to ----ooppeennggll--sswwaappiinntteerrvvaall==11. For Vulkan,
              it translates to ----vvuullkkaann--sswwaapp--mmooddee==ffiiffoo (or ffiiffoo--rreellaaxxeedd).

              The modes with ddeessyynncc in their names do not attempt to keep
              audio/video in sync. They will slowly (or quickly) desync, until
              e.g. the next seek happens. These modes are meant for testing,
              not serious use.

              aauuddiioo  Time video frames to audio. This is the most robust mode,
                     because the player doesn't have to assume anything about
                     how the display behaves. The disadvantage is that it can
                     lead to occasional frame drops or repeats. If audio is
                     disabled, this uses the system clock. This is the default
                     mode.

              ddiissppllaayy--rreessaammppllee
                     Resample audio to match the video. This mode will also
                     try to adjust audio speed to compensate for other drift.
                     (This means it will play the audio at a different speed
                     every once in a while to reduce the A/V difference.)

              ddiissppllaayy--rreessaammppllee--vvddrroopp
                     Resample audio to match the video. Drop video frames to
                     compensate for drift.

              ddiissppllaayy--rreessaammppllee--ddeessyynncc
                     Like the previous mode, but no A/V compensation.

              ddiissppllaayy--tteemmppoo
                     Same as ddiissppllaayy--rreessaammppllee, but apply audio speed changes
                     to audio filters instead of resampling to avoid the
                     change in pitch. Beware that some audio filters don't do
                     well with a speed close to 1. It is recommend to use a
                     conditional profile to automatically switch to
                     ddiissppllaayy--rreessaammppllee when speed gets too close to 1 for your
                     filter setup. Use (speed * video_speed_correction) to get
                     the actual playback speed in the condition.  See
                     Conditional auto profiles for details.

              ddiissppllaayy--vvddrroopp
                     Drop or repeat video frames to compensate desyncing
                     video. (Although it should have the same effects as
                     aauuddiioo, the implementation is very different.)

              ddiissppllaayy--aaddrroopp
                     Drop or repeat audio data to compensate desyncing video.
                     This mode will cause severe audio artifacts if the real
                     monitor refresh rate is too different from the reported
                     or forced rate. Since mpv 0.33.0, this acts on entire
                     audio frames, instead of single samples.

              ddiissppllaayy--ddeessyynncc
                     Sync video to display, and let audio play on its own.

              ddeessyynncc Sync video according to system clock, and let audio play
                     on its own.

       ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc--mmaaxx--ffaaccttoorr==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Maximum multiple for which to try to fit the video's FPS to the
              display's FPS (default: 5).

              For example, if this is set to 1, the video FPS is forced to an
              integer multiple of the display FPS, as long as the speed change
              does not exceed the value set by ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc--mmaaxx--vviiddeeoo--cchhaannggee.

              See ----iinntteerrppoollaattiioonn--tthhrreesshhoolldd for how this option affects
              interpolation.

       ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc--mmaaxx--vviiddeeoo--cchhaannggee==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Maximum speed difference in percent that is applied to video
              with ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==ddiissppllaayy--...... (default: 1). Display sync mode
              will be disabled if the monitor and video refresh way do not
              match within the given range. It tries multiples as well:
              playing 30 fps video on a 60 Hz screen will duplicate every
              second frame. Playing 24 fps video on a 60 Hz screen will play
              video in a 2-3-2-3-... pattern.

              The default settings are not loose enough to speed up 23.976 fps
              video to 25 fps. We consider the pitch change too extreme to
              allow this behavior by default. Set this option to a value of 55
              to enable it.

              Note that ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==ddiissppllaayy--tteemmppoo avoids this pitch change.

              Also note that in the ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==ddiissppllaayy--rreessaammppllee or
              ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==ddiissppllaayy--tteemmppoo mode, audio speed will additionally
              be changed by a small amount if necessary for A/V sync. See
              ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc--mmaaxx--aauuddiioo--cchhaannggee.

       ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc--mmaaxx--aauuddiioo--cchhaannggee==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Maximum _a_d_d_i_t_i_o_n_a_l speed difference in percent that is applied
              to audio with ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==ddiissppllaayy--...... (default: 0.125).
              Normally, the player plays the audio at the speed of the video.
              But if the difference between audio and video position is too
              high, e.g. due to drift or other timing errors, it will attempt
              to speed up or slow down audio by this additional factor. Too
              low values could lead to video frame dropping or repeating if
              the A/V desync cannot be compensated, too high values could lead
              to chaotic frame dropping due to the audio "overshooting" and
              skipping multiple video frames before the sync logic can react.

   MMiisscceellllaanneeoouuss

       ----ddiissppllaayy--ttaaggss==ttaagg11,,ttaaggss22,,......
              Set the list of tags that should be displayed on the terminal
              and stats.  Tags that are in the list, but are not present in
              the played file, will not be shown. If a value ends with **, all
              tags are matched by prefix (though there is no general
              globbing). Just passing ** essentially filtering.

              The default includes a common list of tags, call mpv with
              ----lliisstt--ooppttiioonnss to see it.

              This is a string list option. See List Options for details.

       ----mmff--ffppss==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Framerate used when decoding from multiple PNG or JPEG files
              with mmff:://// (default: 1).

       ----mmff--ttyyppee==<<vvaalluuee>>
              Input file type for mmff:://// (available: jpeg, png, tga, sgi). By
              default, this is guessed from the file extension.

       ----ssttrreeaamm--dduummpp==<<ddeessttiinnaattiioonn--ffiilleennaammee>>
              Instead of playing a file, read its byte stream and write it to
              the given destination file. The destination is overwritten. Can
              be useful to test network-related behavior.

       ----ssttrreeaamm--llaavvff--oo==oopptt11==vvaalluuee11,,oopptt22==vvaalluuee22,,......
              Set AVOptions on streams opened with libavformat. Unknown or
              misspelled options are silently ignored. (They are mentioned in
              the terminal output in verbose mode, i.e. ----vv. In general we
              can't print errors, because other options such as e.g. user
              agent are not available with all protocols, and printing errors
              for unknown options would end up being too noisy.)

              This is a key/value list option. See List Options for details.

       ----bbaacckkddrroopp--ttyyppee==<<aauuttoo||nnoonnee||mmiiccaa||aaccrryylliicc||mmiiccaa--aalltt>>
              (Windows only) Controls the backdrop/border style.

              aauuttoo   Default Windows behavior

              nnoonnee   The backdrop will be black or white depending on the
                     system's theme settings.

              mmiiccaa   Enables the Mica style, which is the default on Windows
                     11.

              aaccrryylliicc
                     Enables the Acrylic style (frosted glass look).

              mmiiccaa--aalltt
                     Same as Mica, except reversed.

       ----wwiinnddooww--aaffffiinniittyy==<<ddeeffaauulltt||eexxcclluuddeeffrroommccmmccaappttuurree||mmoonniittoorr>>
              (Windows only) Controls the window affinity behavior of mpv.

              ddeeffaauulltt
                     Default Windows behavior

              eexxcclluuddeeffrroommccaappttuurree
                     mpv's window will be completely excluded from capture by
                     external applications or screen recording software.

              mmoonniittoorr
                     Blacks out the mpv window

       ----vvoo--mmmmccssss--pprrooffiillee==<<nnaammee>>
              (Windows only) Set the MMCSS profile for the video renderer
              thread (default: PPllaayybbaacckk).

       ----pprriioorriittyy==<<pprriioo>>
              (Windows only) Set process priority for mpv according to the
              predefined priorities available under Windows.

              Possible values of <<pprriioo>>:
              idle|belownormal|normal|abovenormal|high|realtime

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 Using realtime priority can cause system lockup.

       ----mmeeddiiaa--ccoonnttrroollss==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
              (Windows only) Enable integration of media control interface
              SystemMediaTransportControls.  Default: yes (except for libmpv)

       ----ffoorrccee--mmeeddiiaa--ttiittllee==<<ssttrriinngg>>
              Force the contents of the mmeeddiiaa--ttiittllee property to this value.
              Useful for scripts which want to set a title, without overriding
              the user's setting in ----ttiittllee.

       ----eexxtteerrnnaall--ffiilleess==<<ffiillee--lliisstt>>
              Load a file and add all of its tracks. This is useful to play
              different files together (for example audio from one file, video
              from another), or for advanced ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx used (like
              playing two video files at the same time).

              Unlike ----ssuubb--ffiilleess and ----aauuddiioo--ffiilleess, this includes all tracks,
              and does not cause default stream selection over the "proper"
              file. This makes it slightly less intrusive. (In mpv 0.28.0 and
              before, this was not quite strictly enforced.)

              This is a path list option. See List Options for details.

       ----eexxtteerrnnaall--ffiillee==<<ffiillee>>
              CLI/config file only alias for ----eexxtteerrnnaall--ffiilleess--aappppeenndd. Each use
              of this option will add a new external file.

       ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--ffiilleess==<<ffiillee--lliisstt>>
              Use an external file as cover art while playing audio. This
              makes it appear on the track list and subject to automatic track
              selection. Options like ----aauuddiioo--ddiissppllaayy control whether such
              tracks are supposed to be selected.

              (The difference to loading a file with ----eexxtteerrnnaall--ffiilleess is that
              video tracks will be marked as being pictures, which affects the
              auto-selection method. If the passed file is a video, only the
              first frame will be decoded and displayed. Enabling the cover
              art track during playback may show a random frame if the source
              file is a video. Normally you're not supposed to pass videos to
              this option, so this paragraph describes the behavior
              coincidentally resulting from implementation details.)

              This is a path list option. See List Options for details.

       ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--ffiillee==<<ffiillee>>
              CLI/config file only alias for ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--ffiilleess--aappppeenndd. Each
              use of this option will add a new external file.

       ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--aauuttoo==<<nnoo||eexxaacctt||ffuuzzzzyy||aallll>>
              Whether to load _external_ cover art automatically. Similar to
              ----ssuubb--aauuttoo and ----aauuddiioo--ffiillee--aauuttoo. If a video already has tracks
              (which are not marked as cover art), external cover art will not
              be loaded.

              nnoo     Don't automatically load cover art.

              eexxaacctt  Load the media filename with an image file extension
                     (default).

              ffuuzzzzyy  Load all cover art containing the media filename.

              aallll    Load all images in the current directory.

              See ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--ffiilleess for details about what constitutes cover
              art.

              See ----aauuddiioo--ddiissppllaayy how to control display of cover art (this
              can be used to disable cover art that is part of the file).

       ----iimmaaggee--eexxttss==eexxtt11,,eexxtt22,,......
              Image file extensions to try to match when using
              ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--aauuttoo, ----aauuttooccrreeaattee--ppllaayylliisstt or
              ----ddiirreeccttoorryy--ffiilltteerr--ttyyppeess.

              This is a string list option. See List Options for details.  Use
              ----hheellpp==iimmaaggee--eexxttss to see default extensions.

       ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--wwhhiitteelliisstt==ffiilleennaammee11,,ffiilleennaammee22,,......
              Filenames to load as cover art, sorted by descending priority.
              They are combined with the extensions in ----iimmaaggee--eexxttss. This has
              no effect if ccoovveerr--aarrtt--aauuttoo is nnoo.

              Default:
              AAllbbuummAArrtt,,AAllbbuumm,,ccoovveerr,,ffrroonntt,,AAllbbuummAArrttSSmmaallll,,FFoollddeerr,,..ffoollddeerr,,tthhuummbb

              This is a string list option. See List Options for details.

       ----vviiddeeoo--eexxttss==eexxtt11,,eexxtt22,,......
              Video file extensions to try to match when using
              ----aauuttooccrreeaattee--ppllaayylliisstt or ----ddiirreeccttoorryy--ffiilltteerr--ttyyppeess.

              This is a string list option. See List Options for details.  Use
              ----hheellpp==vviiddeeoo--eexxttss to see default extensions.

       ----aarrcchhiivvee--eexxttss==eexxtt11,,eexxtt22,,......
              Archive file extensions to try to match when using
              ----aauuttooccrreeaattee--ppllaayylliisstt or ----ddiirreeccttoorryy--ffiilltteerr--ttyyppeess.

              This is a string list option. See List Options for details. Use
              ----hheellpp==aarrcchhiivvee--eexxttss to see the default extensions.

       ----ppllaayylliisstt--eexxttss==eexxtt11,,eexxtt22,,......
              Playlist file extensions to try to match when using
              ----aauuttooccrreeaattee--ppllaayylliisstt or ----ddiirreeccttoorryy--ffiilltteerr--ttyyppeess.

              This is a string list option. See List Options for details. Use
              ----hheellpp==ppllaayylliisstt--eexxttss to see the default extensions.

       ----aauuttoollooaadd--ffiilleess==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
              Automatically load/select external files (default: yes).

              If set to nnoo, then do not automatically load external files as
              specified by ----ssuubb--aauuttoo, ----aauuddiioo--ffiillee--aauuttoo and ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--aauuttoo.
              If external files are forcibly added (like with ----ssuubb--ffiilleess),
              they will not be auto-selected.

              This does not affect playlist expansion, redirection, or other
              loading of referenced files like with ordered chapters.

       ----ssttrreeaamm--rreeccoorrdd==<<ffiillee>>
              Write received/read data from the demuxer to the given output
              file. The output file will always be overwritten without asking.
              The output format is determined by the extension of the output
              file.

              Switching streams or seeking during recording might result in
              recording being stopped and/or broken files. Use with care.

              Seeking outside of the demuxer cache will result in "skips" in
              the output file, but seeking within  the demuxer cache should
              not affect recording. One exception is when you seek back far
              enough to exceed the forward buffering size, in which case the
              cache stops actively reading. This will return in dropped data
              if it's a live stream.

              If this is set at runtime, the old file is closed, and the new
              file is opened. Note that this will write only data that is
              appended at the end of the cache, and the already cached data
              cannot be written. You can try the dduummpp--ccaacchhee command as an
              alternative.

              External files (----aauuddiioo--ffiillee etc.) are ignored by this, it works
              on the "main" file only. Using this with files using ordered
              chapters or EDL files will also not work correctly in general.

              There are some glitches with this because it uses FFmpeg's
              libavformat for writing the output file. For example, it's
              typical that it will only work if the output format is the same
              as the input format. This is the case even if it works with the
              ffffmmppeegg tool. One reason for this is that ffffmmppeegg and its
              libraries contain certain hacks and workarounds for these
              issues, that are unavailable to outside users.

       ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx==<<ssttrriinngg>>
              Set a "complex" libavfilter filter, which means a single filter
              graph can take input from multiple source audio and video
              tracks. The graph can result in a single audio or video output
              (or both).

              Currently, the filter graph labels are used to select the
              participating input tracks and audio/video output. The following
              rules apply:

              • A label of the form aaiiddNN selects audio track N as input (e.g.
                aaiidd11).

              • A label of the form vviiddNN selects video track N as input.

              • A label named aaoo will be connected to the audio output.

              • A label named vvoo will be connected to the video output.

              Each label can be used only once. If you want to use e.g. an
              audio stream for multiple filters, you need to use the aasspplliitt
              filter. Multiple video or audio outputs are not possible, but
              you can use filters to merge them into one.

              It's not possible to change the tracks connected to the filter
              at runtime, unless you explicitly change the llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx
              property and set new track assignments. When the graph is
              changed, the track selection is changed according to the used
              labels as well.

              Other tracks, as long as they're not connected to the filter,
              and the corresponding output is not connected to the filter, can
              still be freely changed with the normal methods.

              Note that the normal filter chains (----aaff, ----vvff) are applied
              between the complex graphs (e.g. aaoo label) and the actual
              output.

                 Examples

                 • ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx==''[[aaiidd11]] [[aaiidd22]] aammiixx [[aaoo]]'' Play audio track
                   1 and 2 at the same time.

                 • ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx==''[[vviidd11]] [[vviidd22]] vvssttaacckk [[vvoo]]'' Stack video
                   track 1 and 2 and play them at the same time. Note that
                   both tracks need to have the same width, or filter
                   initialization will fail (you can add ssccaallee filters before
                   the vvssttaacckk filter to fix the size).  To load a video track
                   from another file, you can use ----eexxtteerrnnaall--ffiillee==ootthheerr..mmkkvv.

                 • ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx==''[[vviidd11]] [[vviidd22]] [[vviidd33]] hhssttaacckk==iinnppuuttss==33 [[vvoo]]''
                   Use the inputs option to stack more than 2 tracks.

                 • ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx==''[[aaiidd11]] aasspplliitt [[tt11]] [[aaoo]] ;; [[tt11]] sshhoowwvvoolluummee
                   [[tt22]] ;; [[vviidd11]] [[tt22]] oovveerrllaayy [[vvoo]]'' Play audio track 1, and
                   overlay the measured volume for each speaker over video
                   track 1.

              See the FFmpeg libavfilter documentation for details on the
              available filters.

       ----mmeettaaddaattaa--ccooddeeppaaggee==<<ccooddeeppaaggee>>
              Codepage for various input metadata (default: aauuttoo). This
              affects how file tags, chapter titles, etc. are interpreted. In
              most cases, this merely evaluates to UTF-8 as non-UTF-8
              codepages are obscure.

              See ----ssuubb--ccooddeeppaaggee option on how codepages are specified and
              further details regarding autodetection and codepage conversion.
              (The underlying code is the same.)

              Conversion is not applied to metadata that is updated at
              runtime.

       ----cclliippbbooaarrdd--bbaacckkeennddss==<<bbaacckkeenndd11,,bbaacckkeenndd22,,......[[,,]]>>
              Specify a priority list of the clipboard backends to be used.
              You can also pass hheellpp to get a complete list of compiled in
              backends.

              If the list is not empty, it enables native clipboard support
              for the specified backends. This allows reading and writing to
              the cclliippbbooaarrdd property to get and set clipboard contents.

              Native clipboard support is enabled by default. To disable this,
              remove all backends in this list with ----cclliippbbooaarrdd--bbaacckkeennddss--ccllrr.

              Note that the default clipboard backends are subject to change,
              and must not be relied upon.

              The following clipboard backends are implemented:

              wwiinn3322  Windows backend.

              mmaacc    macOS backend.

              wwaayyllaanndd
                     Wayland backend. This backend is only available if the
                     compositor supports the eexxtt--ddaattaa--ccoonnttrrooll--vv11 protocol.

              vvoo     VO backend. Requires an active VO window, and support
                     differs across platforms. Currently, this is used as a
                     fallback for Wayland compositors without support for the
                     eexxtt--ddaattaa--ccoonnttrrooll--vv11 protocol, or if the wwaayyllaanndd backend
                     is disabled.

              This is an object settings list option. See List Options for
              details.

       ----cclliippbbooaarrdd--mmoonniittoorr==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
              (Windows, Wayland and macOS only)

              Enable clipboard monitoring so that the cclliippbbooaarrdd property can
              be observed for content changes (default: no). This only affects
              clipboard implementations which use polling to monitor clipboard
              updates.  Other platforms currently ignore this option and
              always/never notify changes.

              On Wayland, this option only has effect on the wwaayyllaanndd backend,
              and not for the vvoo backend. See ccuurrrreenntt--cclliippbbooaarrdd--bbaacckkeenndd
              property for more details.

AAUUDDIIOO OOUUTTPPUUTT DDRRIIVVEERRSS
       Audio output drivers are interfaces to different audio output
       facilities. The syntax is:

       ----aaoo==<<ddrriivveerr11,,ddrriivveerr22,,......[[,,]]>>
              Specify a priority list of audio output drivers to be used.

       If the list has a trailing ',', mpv will fall back on drivers not
       contained in the list.

       This is an object settings list option. See List Options for details.

       NNOOTTEE::
          See ----aaoo==hheellpp for a list of compiled-in audio output drivers sorted
          by autoprobe order.

          Note that the default audio output driver is subject to change, and
          must not be relied upon. If a certain AO needs to be used, it must
          be explicitly specified.

       Available audio output drivers are:

       aallssaa   ALSA audio output driver.

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----aallssaa--rreessaammppllee==yyeess
                     Enable ALSA resampling plugin. (This is disabled by
                     default, because some drivers report incorrect audio
                     delay in some cases.)

              ----aallssaa--mmiixxeerr--ddeevviiccee==<<ddeevviiccee>>
                     Set the mixer device used with aaoo--vvoolluummee (default:
                     ddeeffaauulltt).

              ----aallssaa--mmiixxeerr--nnaammee==<<nnaammee>>
                     Set the name of the mixer element (default: MMaasstteerr). This
                     is for example PPCCMM or MMaasstteerr.

              ----aallssaa--mmiixxeerr--iinnddeexx==<<nnuummbbeerr>>
                     Set the index of the mixer channel (default: 0). Consider
                     the output of "aammiixxeerr ssccoonnttrroollss", then the index is the
                     number that follows the name of the element.

              ----aallssaa--nnoonn--iinntteerrlleeaavveedd
                     Allow output of non-interleaved formats (if the audio
                     decoder uses this format). Currently disabled by default,
                     because some popular ALSA plugins are utterly broken with
                     non-interleaved formats.

              ----aallssaa--iiggnnoorree--cchhmmaapp
                     Don't read or set the channel map of the ALSA device -
                     only request the required number of channels, and then
                     pass the audio as-is to it. This option most likely
                     should not be used. It can be useful for debugging, or
                     for static setups with a specially engineered ALSA
                     configuration (in this case you should always force the
                     same layout with ----aauuddiioo--cchhaannnneellss, or it will work only
                     for files which use the layout implicit to your ALSA
                     device).

              ----aallssaa--bbuuffffeerr--ttiimmee==<<mmiiccrroosseeccoonnddss>>
                     Set the requested buffer time in microseconds. A value of
                     0 skips requesting anything from the ALSA API. This and
                     the ----aallssaa--ppeerriiooddss option uses the ALSA nneeaarr functions to
                     set the requested parameters. If doing so results in an
                     empty configuration set, setting these parameters is
                     skipped.

                     Both options control the buffer size. A low buffer size
                     can lead to higher CPU usage and audio dropouts, while a
                     high buffer size can lead to higher latency in volume
                     changes and other filtering.

              ----aallssaa--ppeerriiooddss==<<nnuummbbeerr>>
                     Number of periods requested from the ALSA API. See
                     ----aallssaa--bbuuffffeerr--ttiimmee for further remarks.

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 To get multichannel/surround audio, use
                 ----aauuddiioo--cchhaannnneellss==aauuttoo. The default for this option is
                 aauuttoo--ssaaffee, which makes this audio output explicitly reject
                 multichannel output, as there is no way to detect whether a
                 certain channel layout is actually supported.

                 You can also try using the upmix plugin
                 <https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/wiki/ALSA-Surround-Sound-
                 and-Upmixing> .  This setup enables multichannel audio on the
                 ddeeffaauulltt device with automatic upmixing with shared access, so
                 playing stereo and multichannel audio at the same time will
                 work as expected.

       oossss    OSS audio output driver

       jjaacckk   JACK (Jack Audio Connection Kit) audio output driver.

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----jjaacckk--ppoorrtt==<<nnaammee>>
                     Connects to the ports with the given name (default:
                     physical ports).

              ----jjaacckk--nnaammee==<<cclliieenntt>>
                     Client name that is passed to JACK (default: mmppvv). Useful
                     if you want to have certain connections established
                     automatically.

              ----jjaacckk--aauuttoossttaarrtt==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Automatically start jackd if necessary (default:
                     disabled). Note that this tends to be unreliable and will
                     flood stdout with server messages.

              ----jjaacckk--ccoonnnneecctt==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Automatically create connections to output ports
                     (default: enabled).  When enabled, the maximum number of
                     output channels will be limited to the number of
                     available output ports.

              ----jjaacckk--ssttdd--cchhaannnneell--llaayyoouutt==<<wwaavveeeexxtt||aannyy>>
                     Select the standard channel layout (default: waveext).
                     JACK itself has no notion of channel layouts (i.e.
                     assigning which speaker a given channel is supposed to
                     map to) - it just takes whatever the application outputs,
                     and reroutes it to whatever the user defines. This means
                     the user and the application are in charge of dealing
                     with the channel layout. wwaavveeeexxtt uses
                     WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE order, which, even though it was
                     defined by Microsoft, is the standard on many systems.
                     The value aannyy makes JACK accept whatever comes from the
                     audio filter chain, regardless of channel layout and
                     without reordering. This mode is probably not very
                     useful, other than for debugging or when used with fixed
                     setups.

       ccoorreeaauuddiioo ((mmaaccOOSS oonnllyy))
              Native macOS audio output driver using AudioUnits and the
              CoreAudio sound server.

              Automatically redirects to ccoorreeaauuddiioo__eexxcclluussiivvee when playing
              compressed formats.

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----ccoorreeaauuddiioo--cchhaannggee--pphhyyssiiccaall--ffoorrmmaatt==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Change the physical format to one similar to the
                     requested audio format (default: no). This has the
                     advantage that multichannel audio output will actually
                     work. The disadvantage is that it will change the
                     system-wide audio settings. This is equivalent to
                     changing the FFoorrmmaatt setting in the AAuuddiioo DDeevviicceess dialog
                     in the AAuuddiioo MMIIDDII SSeettuupp utility. Note that this does not
                     affect the selected speaker setup.

              ----ccoorreeaauuddiioo--ssppddiiff--hhaacckk==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Try to pass through AC3/DTS data as PCM. This is useful
                     for drivers which do not report AC3 support. It converts
                     the AC3 data to float, and assumes the driver will do the
                     inverse conversion, which means a typical A/V receiver
                     will pick it up as compressed IEC framed AC3 stream,
                     ignoring that it's marked as PCM. This disables normal
                     AC3 passthrough (even if the device reports it as
                     supported). Use with extreme care.

       ccoorreeaauuddiioo__eexxcclluussiivvee ((mmaaccOOSS oonnllyy))
              Native macOS audio output driver using direct device access and
              exclusive mode (bypasses the sound server).

       aavvffoouunnddaattiioonn ((mmaaccOOSS oonnllyy))
              Native macOS audio output driver using
              AAVVSSaammpplleeBBuuffffeerrAAuuddiiooRReennddeerreerr in AVFoundation, which supports
              spatial audio <https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT211775> .

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 Turning on spatial audio may hang the playback if mpv is not
                 started out of the bundle, though playback with spatial audio
                 off always works.

       aauuddiioouunniitt ((iiOOSS oonnllyy))
              Native iOS audio output driver using AAuuddiiooUUnniittss and
              AudioToolbox.

       ooppeennaall OpenAL audio output driver.

              ----ooppeennaall--nnuumm--bbuuffffeerrss==<<22--112288>>
                     Specify the number of audio buffers to use. Lower values
                     are better for lower CPU usage. Default: 4.

              ----ooppeennaall--nnuumm--ssaammpplleess==<<225566--3322776688>>
                     Specify the number of complete samples to use for each
                     buffer. Higher values are better for lower CPU usage.
                     Default: 8192.

              ----ooppeennaall--ddiirreecctt--cchhaannnneellss==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Enable OpenAL Soft's direct channel extension when
                     available to avoid tinting the sound with ambisonics or
                     HRTF. Default: yes.

       ppuullssee  PulseAudio audio output driver

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----ppuullssee--hhoosstt==<<hhoosstt>>
                     Specify the host to use. An empty <host> string uses a
                     local connection, "localhost" uses network transfer (most
                     likely not what you want).

              ----ppuullssee--bbuuffffeerr==<<11--22000000||nnaattiivvee>>
                     Set the audio buffer size in milliseconds. A higher value
                     buffers more data, and has a lower probability of buffer
                     underruns. A smaller value makes the audio stream react
                     faster, e.g. to playback speed changes. "native" lets the
                     sound server determine buffers.

              ----ppuullssee--llaatteennccyy--hhaacckkss==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Enable hacks to workaround PulseAudio timing bugs
                     (default: yes). If enabled, mpv will do elaborate latency
                     calculations on its own. If disabled, it will use
                     PulseAudio automatically updated timing information.
                     Disabling this might help with e.g. networked audio or
                     some plugins, while enabling it might help in some
                     unknown situations (it is currently enabled due to known
                     bugs with PulseAudio 16.0).

              ----ppuullssee--aallllooww--ssuussppeennddeedd==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Allow mpv to use PulseAudio even if the sink is suspended
                     (default: no).  Can be useful if PulseAudio is running as
                     a bridge to jack and mpv has its sink-input set to the
                     one jack is using.

       ppiippeewwiirree
              PipeWire audio output driver

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----ppiippeewwiirree--bbuuffffeerr==<<11--22000000||nnaattiivvee>>
                     Set the audio buffer size in milliseconds. A higher value
                     buffers more data, and has a lower probability of buffer
                     underruns. A smaller value makes the audio stream react
                     faster, e.g. to playback speed changes. "native" lets the
                     sound server determine buffers.

              ----ppiippeewwiirree--rreemmoottee==<<rreemmoottee>>
                     Specify the PipeWire remote daemon name to connect to via
                     local UNIX sockets.  An empty <remote> string uses the
                     default remote named ppiippeewwiirree--00.

              ----ppiippeewwiirree--vvoolluummee--mmooddee==<<cchhaannnneell||gglloobbaall>>
                     Specify if the aaoo--vvoolluummee property should apply to the
                     channel volumes or the global volume.  By default the
                     channel volumes are used.

       ssddll    SDL 2.0+ audio output driver. Should work on any platform
              supported by SDL 2.0, but may require the SSDDLL__AAUUDDIIOODDRRIIVVEERR
              environment variable to be set appropriately for your system.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This driver is for compatibility with extremely foreign
                 environments, such as systems where none of the other drivers
                 are available.

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----ssddll--bbuufflleenn==<<lleennggtthh>>
                     Sets the audio buffer length in seconds. Is used only as
                     a hint by the sound system. Playing a file with --vv will
                     show the requested and obtained exact buffer size. A
                     value of 0 selects the sound system default.

       nnuullll   Produces no audio output but maintains video playback speed. You
              can use ----aaoo==nnuullll ----aaoo--nnuullll--uunnttiimmeedd for benchmarking.

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--uunnttiimmeedd
                     Do not simulate timing of a perfect audio device. This
                     means audio decoding will go as fast as possible, instead
                     of timing it to the system clock.

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--bbuuffffeerr
                     Simulated buffer length in seconds.

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--oouuttbbuurrsstt
                     Simulated chunk size in samples.

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--ssppeeeedd
                     Simulated audio playback speed as a multiplier. Usually,
                     a real audio device will not go exactly as fast as the
                     system clock. It will deviate just a little, and this
                     option helps to simulate this.

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--llaatteennccyy
                     Simulated device latency. This is additional to EOF.

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--bbrrookkeenn--eeooff
                     Simulate broken audio drivers, which always add the fixed
                     device latency to the reported audio playback position.

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--bbrrookkeenn--ddeellaayy
                     Simulate broken audio drivers, which don't report latency
                     correctly.

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--cchhaannnneell--llaayyoouuttss
                     If not empty, this is a ,, separated list of channel
                     layouts the AO allows. This can be used to test channel
                     layout selection.

              ----aaoo--nnuullll--ffoorrmmaatt
                     Force the audio output format the AO will accept. If
                     unset accepts any.

       ppccmm    Raw PCM/WAVE file writer audio output

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----aaoo--ppccmm--wwaavveehheeaaddeerr==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Include or do not include the WAVE header (default:
                     included). When not included, raw PCM will be generated.

              ----aaoo--ppccmm--ffiillee==<<ffiilleennaammee>>
                     Write the sound to <<ffiilleennaammee>> instead of the default
                     aauuddiioodduummpp..wwaavv. If nnoo--wwaavveehheeaaddeerr is specified, the default
                     is aauuddiioodduummpp..ppccmm.

              ----aaoo--ppccmm--aappppeenndd==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Append to the file, instead of overwriting it. Always use
                     this with the nnoo--wwaavveehheeaaddeerr option - with wwaavveehheeaaddeerr it's
                     broken, because it will write a WAVE header every time
                     the file is opened.

       ssnnddiioo  Audio output to the OpenBSD sndio sound system

              (Note: only supports mono, stereo, 4.0, 5.1 and 7.1 channel
              layouts.)

       wwaassaappii Audio output to the Windows Audio Session API.

              The following global options are supported by this audio output:

              ----wwaassaappii--eexxcclluussiivvee--bbuuffffeerr==<<ddeeffaauulltt||mmiinn||11--22000000000000>>
                     Set buffer duration in exclusive mode (i.e., with
                     ----aauuddiioo--eexxcclluussiivvee==yyeess). ddeeffaauulltt and mmiinn use the default
                     and minimum device period reported by WASAPI,
                     respectively. You can also directly specify the buffer
                     duration in microseconds, in which case a duration
                     shorter than the minimum device period will be rounded up
                     to the minimum period.

                     The default buffer duration should provide robust
                     playback in most cases, but reportedly on some devices
                     there are glitches following stream resets under the
                     default setting. In such cases, specifying a shorter
                     duration might help.

VVIIDDEEOO OOUUTTPPUUTT DDRRIIVVEERRSS
       Video output drivers are interfaces to different video output
       facilities. The syntax is:

       ----vvoo==<<ddrriivveerr11,,ddrriivveerr22,,......[[,,]]>>
              Specify a priority list of video output drivers to be used.

       If the list has a trailing ,,, mpv will fall back on drivers not
       contained in the list.

       This is an object settings list option. See List Options for details.

       NNOOTTEE::
          See ----vvoo==hheellpp for a list of compiled-in video output drivers.

          The recommended output driver is ----vvoo==ggppuu, which is the default. All
          other drivers are for compatibility or special purposes. If the
          default does not work, it will fallback to other drivers (in the
          same order as listed by ----vvoo==hheellpp).

          Note that the default video output driver is subject to change, and
          must not be relied upon. If a certain VO needs to be used (e.g. for
          lliibbmmppvv rendering API), it must be explicitly specified.

       Available video output drivers are:

       ggppuu    General purpose, customizable, GPU-accelerated video output
              driver. It supports extended scaling methods, dithering, color
              management, custom shaders, HDR, and more.

              See GPU renderer options for options specific to this VO.

              By default, mpv utilizes settings that balance quality and
              performance.  Additionally, two predefined profiles are
              available: ffaasstt for maximum performance and hhiigghh--qquuaalliittyy for
              superior rendering quality. You can apply a specific profile
              using the ----pprrooffiillee==<<nnaammee>> option and inspect its contents using
              ----sshhooww--pprrooffiillee==<<nnaammee>>.

              This VO abstracts over several possible graphics APIs and
              windowing contexts, which can be influenced using the ----ggppuu--aappii
              and ----ggppuu--ccoonntteexxtt options.

              Hardware decoding over OpenGL-interop is supported to some
              degree. Note that in this mode, some corner case might not be
              gracefully handled, and color space conversion and chroma
              upsampling is generally in the hand of the hardware decoder
              APIs.

              ggppuu makes use of FBOs by default. Sometimes you can achieve
              better quality or performance by changing the ----ffbboo--ffoorrmmaatt
              option to rrggbb1166ff, rrggbb3322ff or rrggbb. Known problems include
              Mesa/Intel not accepting rrggbb1166, Mesa sometimes not being
              compiled with float texture support, and some macOS setups being
              very slow with rrggbb1166 but fast with rrggbb3322ff. If you have problems,
              you can also try enabling the ----ggppuu--dduummbb--mmooddee==yyeess option.

       ggppuu--nneexxtt
              Experimental video renderer based on lliibbppllaacceebboo. This supports
              almost the same set of features as ----vvoo==ggppuu. See GPU renderer
              options for a list.

              Should generally be faster and higher quality, but some features
              may still be missing or misbehave. Expect (and report!) bugs.
              See here for a list of known differences and bugs:

               <https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/wiki/GPU-Next-vs-GPU>

       xxvv ((XX1111 oonnllyy))
              Uses the XVideo extension to enable hardware-accelerated
              display. This is the most compatible VO on X, but may be
              low-quality, and has issues with OSD and subtitle display.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This driver is for compatibility with old systems.

              The following global options are supported by this video output:

              ----xxvv--aaddaappttoorr==<<nnuummbbeerr>>
                     Select a specific XVideo adapter (check xvinfo results).

              ----xxvv--ppoorrtt==<<nnuummbbeerr>>
                     Select a specific XVideo port.

              ----xxvv--cckk==<<ccuurr||uussee||sseett>>
                     Select the source from which the color key is taken
                     (default: cur).

                     ccuurr    The default takes the color key currently set in
                            Xv.

                     uussee    Use but do not set the color key from mpv (use the
                            ----ccoolloorrkkeeyy option to change it).

                     sseett    Same as use but also sets the supplied color key.

              ----xxvv--cckk--mmeetthhoodd==<<nnoonnee||mmaann||bbgg||aauuttoo>>
                     Sets the color key drawing method (default: man).

                     nnoonnee   Disables color-keying.

                     mmaann    Draw the color key manually (reduces flicker in
                            some cases).

                     bbgg     Set the color key as window background.

                     aauuttoo   Let Xv draw the color key.

              ----xxvv--ccoolloorrkkeeyy==<<nnuummbbeerr>>
                     Changes the color key to an RGB value of your choice.
                     00xx000000000000 is black and 00xxffffffffffff is white.

              ----xxvv--bbuuffffeerrss==<<nnuummbbeerr>>
                     Number of image buffers to use for the internal
                     ringbuffer (default: 2).  Increasing this will use more
                     memory, but might help with the X server not responding
                     quickly enough if video FPS is close to or higher than
                     the display refresh rate.

       xx1111 ((XX1111 oonnllyy))
              Shared memory video output driver without hardware acceleration
              that works whenever X11 is present.

              Since mpv 0.30.0, you may need to use ----pprrooffiillee==ssww--ffaasstt to get
              decent performance.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This is a fallback only, and should not be normally used.

       vvddppaauu ((XX1111 oonnllyy))
              Uses the VDPAU interface to display and optionally also decode
              video.  Hardware decoding is used with ----hhwwddeecc==vvddppaauu. Note that
              there is absolutely no reason to use this, other than
              compatibility. We strongly recommend that you use ----vvoo==ggppuu with
              ----hhwwddeecc==nnvvddeecc instead.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 Earlier versions of mpv (and MPlayer, mplayer2) provided
                 sub-options to tune vdpau post-processing, like ddeeiinntt,
                 sshhaarrppeenn, ddeennooiissee, cchhrroommaa--ddeeiinntt, ppuulllluupp, hhqqssccaalliinngg. These
                 sub-options are deprecated, and you should use the vvddppaauupppp
                 video filter instead.

              The following global options are supported by this video output:

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--sshhaarrppeenn==<<--11--11>>
                     (Deprecated. See note about vvddppaauupppp.)

                     For positive values, apply a sharpening algorithm to the
                     video, for negative values a blurring algorithm (default:
                     0).

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--ddeennooiissee==<<00--11>>
                     (Deprecated. See note about vvddppaauupppp.)

                     Apply a noise reduction algorithm to the video (default:
                     0; no noise reduction).

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--cchhrroommaa--ddeeiinntt
                     (Deprecated. See note about vvddppaauupppp.)

                     Makes temporal deinterlacers operate both on luma and
                     chroma (default).  Use no-chroma-deint to solely use luma
                     and speed up advanced deinterlacing. Useful with slow
                     video memory.

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--ppuulllluupp
                     (Deprecated. See note about vvddppaauupppp.)

                     Try to apply inverse telecine, needs motion adaptive
                     temporal deinterlacing.

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--hhqqssccaalliinngg==<<00--99>>
                     (Deprecated. See note about vvddppaauupppp.)

                     00      Use default VDPAU scaling (default).

                     11--99    Apply high quality VDPAU scaling (needs capable
                            hardware).

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--ffppss==<<nnuummbbeerr>>
                     Override autodetected display refresh rate value (the
                     value is needed for framedrop to allow video playback
                     rates higher than display refresh rate, and for
                     vsync-aware frame timing adjustments). Default 0 means
                     use autodetected value. A positive value is interpreted
                     as a refresh rate in Hz and overrides the autodetected
                     value. A negative value disables all timing adjustment
                     and framedrop logic.

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--ccoommppoossiittee--ddeetteecctt
                     NVIDIA's current VDPAU implementation behaves somewhat
                     differently under a compositing window manager and does
                     not give accurate frame timing information. With this
                     option enabled, the player tries to detect whether a
                     compositing window manager is active. If one is detected,
                     the player disables timing adjustments as if the user had
                     specified ffppss==--11 (as they would be based on incorrect
                     input). This means timing is somewhat less accurate than
                     without compositing, but with the composited mode
                     behavior of the NVIDIA driver, there is no hard playback
                     speed limit even without the disabled logic. Enabled by
                     default, use ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--ccoommppoossiittee--ddeetteecctt==nnoo to disable.

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--qquueeuueettiimmee--wwiinnddoowweedd==<<nnuummbbeerr>> aanndd qquueeuueettiimmee--ffss==<<nnuummbbeerr>>
                     Use VDPAU's presentation queue functionality to queue
                     future video frame changes at most this many milliseconds
                     in advance (default: 50).  See below for additional
                     information.

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--oouuttppuutt--ssuurrffaacceess==<<22--1155>>
                     Allocate this many output surfaces to display video
                     frames (default: 3). See below for additional
                     information.

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--ccoolloorrkkeeyy==<<##RRRRGGGGBBBB||##AAAARRRRGGGGBBBB>>
                     Set the VDPAU presentation queue background color, which
                     in practice is the colorkey used if VDPAU operates in
                     overlay mode (default: ##002200550077, some shade of black). If
                     the alpha component of this value is 0, the default VDPAU
                     colorkey will be used instead (which is usually green).

              ----vvoo--vvddppaauu--ffoorrccee--yyuuvv
                     Never accept RGBA input. This means mpv will insert a
                     filter to convert to a YUV format before the VO.
                     Sometimes useful to force availability of certain
                     YUV-only features, like video equalizer or deinterlacing.

              Using the VDPAU frame queuing functionality controlled by the
              queuetime options makes mpv's frame flip timing less sensitive
              to system CPU load and allows mpv to start decoding the next
              frame(s) slightly earlier, which can reduce jitter caused by
              individual slow-to-decode frames. However, the NVIDIA graphics
              drivers can make other window behavior such as window moves
              choppy if VDPAU is using the blit queue (mainly happens if you
              have the composite extension enabled) and this feature is
              active. If this happens on your system and it bothers you then
              you can set the queuetime value to 0 to disable this feature.
              The settings to use in windowed and fullscreen mode are separate
              because there should be no reason to disable this for fullscreen
              mode (as the driver issue should not affect the video itself).

              You can queue more frames ahead by increasing the queuetime
              values and the oouuttppuutt__ssuurrffaacceess count (to ensure enough surfaces
              to buffer video for a certain time ahead you need at least as
              many surfaces as the video has frames during that time, plus
              two). This could help make video smoother in some cases. The
              main downsides are increased video RAM requirements for the
              surfaces and laggier display response to user commands (display
              changes only become visible some time after they're queued). The
              graphics driver implementation may also have limits on the
              length of maximum queuing time or number of queued surfaces that
              work well or at all.

       ddiirreecctt33dd ((WWiinnddoowwss oonnllyy))
              Video output driver that uses the Direct3D interface.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This driver is for compatibility with systems that don't
                 provide proper OpenGL drivers, and where ANGLE does not
                 perform well.

              The following global options are supported by this video output:

              ----vvoo--ddiirreecctt33dd--ddiissaabbllee--tteexxttuurree--aalliiggnn
                     Normally texture sizes are always aligned to 16. With
                     this option enabled, the video texture will always have
                     exactly the same size as the video itself.

              Debug options. These might be incorrect, might be removed in the
              future, might crash, might cause slow downs, etc. Contact the
              developers if you actually need any of these for performance or
              proper operation.

              ----vvoo--ddiirreecctt33dd--ffoorrccee--ppoowweerr--ooff--22
                     Always force textures to power of 2, even if the device
                     reports non-power-of-2 texture sizes as supported.

              ----vvoo--ddiirreecctt33dd--tteexxttuurree--mmeemmoorryy==<<mmooddee>>
                     Only affects operation with shaders/texturing enabled,
                     and (E)OSD.  Possible values:

                     ddeeffaauulltt ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                            Use DD33DDPPOOOOLL__DDEEFFAAUULLTT, with a DD33DDPPOOOOLL__SSYYSSTTEEMMMMEEMM
                            texture for locking. If the driver supports
                            DD33DDDDEEVVCCAAPPSS__TTEEXXTTUURREESSYYSSTTEEMMMMEEMMOORRYY, DD33DDPPOOOOLL__SSYYSSTTEEMMMMEEMM
                            is used directly.

                     ddeeffaauulltt--ppooooll
                            Use DD33DDPPOOOOLL__DDEEFFAAUULLTT. (Like ddeeffaauulltt, but never use
                            a shadow-texture.)

                     ddeeffaauulltt--ppooooll--sshhaaddooww
                            Use DD33DDPPOOOOLL__DDEEFFAAUULLTT, with a DD33DDPPOOOOLL__SSYYSSTTEEMMMMEEMM
                            texture for locking. (Like ddeeffaauulltt, but always
                            force the shadow-texture.)

                     mmaannaaggeedd
                            Use DD33DDPPOOOOLL__MMAANNAAGGEEDD.

                     ssccrraattcchh
                            Use DD33DDPPOOOOLL__SSCCRRAATTCCHH, with a DD33DDPPOOOOLL__SSYYSSTTEEMMMMEEMM
                            texture for locking.

              ----vvoo--ddiirreecctt33dd--sswwaapp--ddiissccaarrdd
                     Use DD33DDSSWWAAPPEEFFFFEECCTT__DDIISSCCAARRDD, which might be faster.  Might
                     be slower too, as it must(?) clear every frame.

              ----vvoo--ddiirreecctt33dd--eexxaacctt--bbaacckkbbuuffffeerr
                     Always resize the backbuffer to window size.

       ssddll    SDL 2.0+ Render video output driver, depending on system with or
              without hardware acceleration. Should work on all platforms
              supported by SDL 2.0.  For tuning, refer to your copy of the
              file SSDDLL__hhiinnttss..hh.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This driver is for compatibility with systems that don't
                 provide proper graphics drivers.

              The following global options are supported by this video output:

              ----ssddll--ssww
                     Continue even if a software renderer is detected.

              ----ssddll--sswwiittcchh--mmooddee
                     Instruct SDL to switch the monitor video mode when going
                     fullscreen.

       ddmmaabbuuff--wwaayyllaanndd
              Experimental Wayland output driver designed for use with either
              drm stateless or VA API hardware decoding. The driver is
              designed to avoid any GPU to CPU copies, and to perform scaling
              and color space conversion using fixed-function hardware, if
              available, rather than GPU shaders. This frees up GPU resources
              for other tasks.  It is highly recommended to use this VO with
              the appropriate ----hhwwddeecc option such as aauuttoo--ssaaffee. It can still
              work in some circumstances without ----hhwwddeecc due to mpv's internal
              conversion filters, but this is not recommended as it's a
              needless extra step. Correct output depends on support from your
              GPU, drivers, and compositor.  This requires the compositor and
              mpv to support ccoolloorr--mmaannaaggeemmeenntt--vv11 to accurately display
              colorspaces that are different from the compositor default
              (bt.601 in most cases).

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 This driver is not required for mpv to work on Wayland.
                 vvoo==ggppuu and vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt will switch to the appropriate Wayland
                 context automatically. This driver is experimental and
                 generally lower quality than ggppuu/ggppuu--nneexxtt.

       vvaaaappii  Intel VA API video output driver with support for hardware
              decoding. Note that there is absolutely no reason to use this,
              other than compatibility.  This is low quality, and has issues
              with OSD. We strongly recommend that you use ----vvoo==ggppuu with
              ----hhwwddeecc==vvaaaappii instead.

              The following global options are supported by this video output:

              ----vvoo--vvaaaappii--ssccaalliinngg==<<aallggoorriitthhmm>>

                     ddeeffaauulltt
                            Driver default (mpv default as well).

                     ffaasstt   Fast, but low quality.

                     hhqq     Unspecified driver dependent high-quality scaling,
                            slow.

                     nnllaa    nnoonn--lliinneeaarr aannaammoorrpphhiicc ssccaalliinngg

              ----vvoo--vvaaaappii--ssccaalleedd--oossdd==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     If enabled, then the OSD is rendered at video resolution
                     and scaled to display resolution. By default, this is
                     disabled, and the OSD is rendered at display resolution
                     if the driver supports it.

       nnuullll   Produces no video output. Useful for benchmarking.

              Usually, it's better to disable video with ----vviiddeeoo==nnoo instead.

              The following global options are supported by this video output:

              ----vvoo--nnuullll--ffppss==<<vvaalluuee>>
                     Simulate display FPS. This artificially limits how many
                     frames the VO accepts per second.

       ccaaccaa   Color ASCII art video output driver that works on a text
              console.

              This driver reserves some keys for runtime configuration. These
              keys are hardcoded and cannot be bound:

              dd aanndd DD
                     Toggle dithering algorithm.

              aa aanndd AA
                     Toggle antialiasing method.

              hh aanndd HH
                     Toggle charset method.

              cc aanndd CC
                     Toggle color method.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This driver is a joke.

       ttcctt    Color Unicode art video output driver that works on a text
              console.  By default depends on support of true color by modern
              terminals to display the images at full color range, but
              256-colors output is also supported (see below). On Windows it
              requires an ansi terminal such as mintty.

              Since mpv 0.30.0, you may need to use ----pprrooffiillee==ssww--ffaasstt to get
              decent performance.

              Note: the TCT image output is not synchronized with other
              terminal output from mpv, which can lead to broken images. The
              options ----tteerrmmiinnaall==nnoo or ----rreeaallllyy--qquuiieett can help with that.

              ----vvoo--ttcctt--aallggoo==<<aallggoo>>
                     Select how to write the pixels to the terminal.

                     hhaallff--bblloocckkss
                            Uses Unicode LOWER HALF BLOCK character to achieve
                            higher vertical resolution. (Default.)

                     ppllaaiinn  Uses spaces. Causes vertical resolution to drop
                            twofolds, but in theory works in more places.

              ----vvoo--ttcctt--bbuuffffeerriinngg==<<ppiixxeell||lliinnee||ffrraammee>>
                     Specifies the size of data batches buffered before being
                     sent to the terminal.

                     TCT image output is not synchronized with other terminal
                     output from mpv, which can lead to broken images. Sending
                     data to the terminal in small batches may improve
                     parallelism between terminal processing and mpv
                     processing but incurs a static overhead of generating
                     tens of thousands of small writes. Also, depending on the
                     terminal used, sending frames in one chunk might help
                     with tearing of the output, especially if not used with
                     ----rreeaallllyy--qquuiieett and other logs interrupt the data stream.

                     ppiixxeell  Send data to terminal for each pixel.

                     lliinnee   Send data to terminal for each line. (Default)

                     ffrraammee  Send data to terminal for each frame.

              ----vvoo--ttcctt--wwiiddtthh==<<wwiiddtthh>> ----vvoo--ttcctt--hheeiigghhtt==<<hheeiigghhtt>>
                     Assume the terminal has the specified character width
                     and/or height.  These default to 80x25 if the terminal
                     size cannot be determined.

              ----vvoo--ttcctt--225566==<<yyeess||nnoo>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: nnoo))
                     Use 256 colors - for terminals which don't support true
                     color.

       kkiittttyy  Graphical output for the terminal, using the kitty graphics
              protocol.  Tested with kitty and Konsole.

              You may need to use ----pprrooffiillee==ssww--ffaasstt to get decent performance.

              Kitty size and alignment options:

              ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--ccoollss==<<ccoolluummnnss>>,, ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--rroowwss==<<rroowwss>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: 00))
                     Specify the terminal size in character cells, otherwise
                     (0) read it from the terminal, or fall back to 80x25.

              ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--wwiiddtthh==<<wwiiddtthh>>,, ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--hheeiigghhtt==<<hheeiigghhtt>> ((ddeeffaauulltt::
              00))     Specify the available size in pixels, otherwise (0) read
                     it from the terminal, or fall back to 320x240.

              ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--lleefftt==<<ccooll>>,, ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--ttoopp==<<rrooww>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: 00))
                     Specify the position in character cells where the image
                     starts (1 is the first column or row). If 0 (default)
                     then try to automatically determine it according to the
                     other values and the image aspect ratio and zoom.

              ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--ccoonnffiigg--cclleeaarr==<<yyeess||nnoo>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: yyeess))
                     Whether or not to clear the terminal whenever the output
                     is reconfigured (e.g. when video size changes).

              ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--aalltt--ssccrreeeenn==<<yyeess||nnoo>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: yyeess))
                     Whether or not to use the alternate screen buffer and
                     return the terminal to its previous state on exit. When
                     set to no, the last kitty image stays on screen after
                     quit, with the cursor following it.

              ----vvoo--kkiittttyy--uussee--sshhmm==<<yyeess||nnoo>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: nnoo))
                     Use shared memory objects to transfer image data to the
                     terminal.  This is much faster than sending the data as
                     escape codes, but is not supported by as many terminals.
                     It also only works on the local machine and not via e.g.
                     SSH connections.

                     This option is not implemented on Windows.

       ssiixxeell  Graphical output for the terminal, using sixels. Tested with
              mmlltteerrmm and xxtteerrmm.

              Note: the Sixel image output is not synchronized with other
              terminal output from mpv, which can lead to broken images.  The
              option ----rreeaallllyy--qquuiieett can help with that, and is recommended.
              On some platforms, using the ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--bbuuffffeerreedd option may work
              as well.

              You may need to use ----pprrooffiillee==ssww--ffaasstt to get decent performance.

              Note: at the time of writing, xxtteerrmm does not enable sixel by
              default - launching it as xxtteerrmm --ttii 334400 is one way to enable it.
              Also, xxtteerrmm does not display images bigger than 1000x1000 pixels
              by default.

              To render and align sixel images correctly, mpv needs to know
              the terminal size both in cells and in pixels. By default it
              tries to use values which the terminal reports, however, due to
              differences between terminals this is an error-prone process
              which cannot be automated with certainty - some terminals report
              the size in pixels including the padding - e.g. xxtteerrmm, while
              others report the actual usable number of pixels - like mmlltteerrmm.
              Additionally, they may behave differently when maximized or in
              fullscreen, and mpv cannot detect this state using standard
              methods.

              Sixel size and alignment options:

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--ccoollss==<<ccoolluummnnss>>,, ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--rroowwss==<<rroowwss>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: 00))
                     Specify the terminal size in character cells, otherwise
                     (0) read it from the terminal, or fall back to 80x25.
                     Note that mpv doesn't use the the last row with sixel
                     because this seems to result in scrolling.

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--wwiiddtthh==<<wwiiddtthh>>,, ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--hheeiigghhtt==<<hheeiigghhtt>> ((ddeeffaauulltt::
              00))     Specify the available size in pixels, otherwise (0) read
                     it from the terminal, or fall back to 320x240. Other than
                     excluding the last line, the height is also further
                     rounded down to a multiple of 6 (sixel unit height) to
                     avoid overflowing below the designated size.

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--lleefftt==<<ccooll>>,, ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--ttoopp==<<rrooww>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: 00))
                     Specify the position in character cells where the image
                     starts (1 is the first column or row). If 0 (default)
                     then try to automatically determine it according to the
                     other values and the image aspect ratio and zoom.

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--ppaadd--xx==<<ppaadd__xx>>,, ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--ppaadd--yy==<<ppaadd__yy>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: --11))
                     Used only when mpv reads the size in pixels from the
                     terminal.  Specify the number of padding pixels (on one
                     side) which are included at the size which the terminal
                     reports. If -1 (default) then the number of pixels is
                     rounded down to a multiple of number of cells (per axis),
                     to take into account padding at the report - this only
                     works correctly when the overall padding per axis is
                     smaller than the number of cells.

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--ccoonnffiigg--cclleeaarr==<<yyeess||nnoo>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: yyeess))
                     Whether or not to clear the terminal whenever the output
                     is reconfigured (e.g. when video size changes).

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--aalltt--ssccrreeeenn==<<yyeess||nnoo>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: yyeess))
                     Whether or not to use the alternate screen buffer and
                     return the terminal to its previous state on exit. When
                     set to no, the last sixel image stays on screen after
                     quit, with the cursor following it.

                     ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--eexxiitt--cclleeaarr is a deprecated alias for this
                     option and may be removed in the future.

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--bbuuffffeerreedd==<<yyeess||nnoo>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: nnoo))
                     Buffers the full output sequence before writing it to the
                     terminal.  On POSIX platforms, this can help prevent
                     interruption (including from other applications) and thus
                     broken images, but may come at a performance cost with
                     some terminals and is subject to implementation details.

              Sixel image quality options:

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--ddiitthheerr==<<aallggoo>>
                     Selects the dither algorithm which libsixel should apply.
                     Can be one of the below list as per libsixel's
                     documentation.

                     aauuttoo ((DDeeffaauulltt))
                            Let libsixel choose the dithering method.

                     nnoonnee   Don't diffuse

                     aattkkiinnssoonn
                            Diffuse with Bill Atkinson's method.

                     ffss     Diffuse with Floyd-Steinberg method

                     jjaajjuunnii Diffuse with Jarvis, Judice & Ninke method

                     ssttuucckkii Diffuse with Stucki's method

                     bbuurrkkeess Diffuse with Burkes' method

                     aarriitthhmmeettiicc
                            Positionally stable arithmetic dither

                     xxoorr    Positionally stable arithmetic xor based dither

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--ffiixxeeddppaalleettttee==<<yyeess||nnoo>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: yyeess))
                     Use libsixel's built-in static palette using the XTERM256
                     profile for dither. Fixed palette uses 256 colors for
                     dithering. Note that using nnoo (at the time of writing)
                     will slow down xxtteerrmm.

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--rreeqqccoolloorrss==<<ccoolloorrss>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: 225566))
                     Has no effect with fixed palette. Set up libsixel to use
                     required number of colors for dynamic palette. This value
                     depends on the terminal emulator as well. Xterm supports
                     256 colors. Can set this to a lower value for faster
                     performance.

              ----vvoo--ssiixxeell--tthhrreesshhoolldd==<<tthhrreesshhoolldd>> ((ddeeffaauulltt:: --11))
                     Has no effect with fixed palette. Defines the threshold
                     to change the palette - as percentage of the number of
                     colors, e.g. 20 will change the palette when the number
                     of colors changed by 20%. It's a simple measure to reduce
                     the number of palette changes, because it can be slow in
                     some terminals (xxtteerrmm). The default (-1) will choose a
                     palette on every frame and will have better quality.

       iimmaaggee  Output each frame into an image file in the current directory.
              Each file takes the frame number padded with leading zeros as
              name.

              The following global options are supported by this video output:

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--ffoorrmmaatt==<<ffoorrmmaatt>>
                     Select the image file format.

                     jjppgg    JPEG files, extension .jpg. (Default.)

                     jjppeegg   JPEG files, extension .jpeg.

                     ppnngg    PNG files.

                     wweebbpp   WebP files.

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--ppnngg--ccoommpprreessssiioonn==<<00--99>>
                     PNG compression factor (speed vs. file size tradeoff)
                     (default: 7)

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--ppnngg--ffiilltteerr==<<00--55>>
                     Filter applied prior to PNG compression (0 = none; 1 =
                     sub; 2 = up; 3 = average; 4 = Paeth; 5 = mixed) (default:
                     5)

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--jjppeegg--qquuaalliittyy==<<00--110000>>
                     JPEG quality factor (default: 90)

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--jjppeegg--ooppttiimmiizzee==<<00--110000>>
                     JPEG optimization factor (default: 100)

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--wweebbpp--lloosssslleessss==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Enable writing lossless WebP files (default: no)

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--wweebbpp--qquuaalliittyy==<<00--110000>>
                     WebP quality (default: 75)

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--wweebbpp--ccoommpprreessssiioonn==<<00--66>>
                     WebP compression factor (default: 4)

              ----vvoo--iimmaaggee--oouuttddiirr==<<ddiirrnnaammee>>
                     Specify the directory to save the image files to
                     (default: ..//).

       lliibbmmppvv For use with libmpv direct embedding. As a special case, on
              macOS it is used like a normal VO within mpv (cocoa-cb).
              Otherwise useless in any other contexts.  (See <<mmppvv//rreennddeerr..hh>>.)

              This also supports many of the options the ggppuu VO has, depending
              on the backend.

       ddrrmm ((DDiirreecctt RReennddeerriinngg MMaannaaggeerr))
              Video output driver using Kernel Mode Setting / Direct Rendering
              Manager.  Should be used when one doesn't want to install
              full-blown graphical environment (e.g. no X). Does not support
              hardware acceleration (if you need this, check the ddrrmm backend
              for ggppuu VO).

              Since mpv 0.30.0, you may need to use ----pprrooffiillee==ssww--ffaasstt to get
              decent performance.

              The following global options are supported by this video output:

              ----ddrrmm--ccoonnnneeccttoorr==<<nnaammee>>
                     Select the connector to use (usually this is a monitor.)
                     If <<nnaammee>> is empty or aauuttoo, mpv renders the output on the
                     first available connector. Use ----ddrrmm--ccoonnnneeccttoorr==hheellpp to
                     get a list of available connectors. (default: empty)

              ----ddrrmm--ddeevviiccee==<<ppaatthh>>
                     Select the DRM device file to use. If specified this
                     overrides automatic card selection. (default: empty)

              ----ddrrmm--mmooddee==<<pprreeffeerrrreedd||hhiigghheesstt||NN||WWxxHH[[@@RR]]>>
                     Mode to use (resolution and frame rate).  Possible
                     values:

                     pprreeffeerrrreedd
                            Use the preferred mode for the screen on the
                            selected connector. (default)

                     hhiigghheesstt
                            Use the mode with the highest resolution available
                            on the selected connector.

                     NN      Select mode by index.

                     WWxxHH[[@@RR]]
                            Specify mode by width, height, and optionally
                            refresh rate.  In case several modes match,
                            selects the mode that comes first in the EDID list
                            of modes.

                     Use ----ddrrmm--mmooddee==hheellpp to get a list of available modes for
                     all active connectors.

              ----ddrrmm--ddrraaww--ppllaannee==<<pprriimmaarryy||oovveerrllaayy||NN>>
                     Select the DRM plane to which video and OSD is drawn to,
                     under normal circumstances. The plane can be specified as
                     pprriimmaarryy, which will pick the first applicable primary
                     plane; oovveerrllaayy, which will pick the first applicable
                     overlay plane; or by index. The index is zero based, and
                     related to the CRTC.  (default: primary)

                     When using this option with the drmprime-overlay hwdec
                     interop, only the OSD is rendered to this plane.

              ----ddrrmm--ddrrmmpprriimmee--vviiddeeoo--ppllaannee==<<pprriimmaarryy||oovveerrllaayy||NN>>
                     Select the DRM plane to use for video with the
                     drmprime-overlay hwdec interop (used by e.g. the rkmpp
                     hwdec on RockChip SoCs, and v4l2 hwdec:s on various other
                     SoC:s). The plane is unused otherwise. This option
                     accepts the same values as ----ddrrmm--ddrraaww--ppllaannee. (default:
                     overlay)

                     To be able to successfully play 4K video on various SoCs
                     you might need to set ----ddrrmm--ddrraaww--ppllaannee==oovveerrllaayy
                     ----ddrrmm--ddrrmmpprriimmee--vviiddeeoo--ppllaannee==pprriimmaarryy and setting
                     ----ddrrmm--ddrraaww--ssuurrffaaccee--ssiizzee==11992200xx11008800, to render the OSD at a
                     lower resolution (the video when handled by the hwdec
                     will be on the drmprime-video plane and at full 4K
                     resolution)

              ----ddrrmm--ffoorrmmaatt==<<xxrrggbb88888888||xxbbggrr88888888||xxrrggbb22110011001100||xxbbggrr22110011001100||yyuuyyvv>>
                     Select the DRM format to use (default: xrgb8888). This
                     allows you to choose the bit depth and color type of the
                     DRM mode.

                     xrgb8888 is your usual 24bpp packed RGB format with 8
                     bits of padding.  xrgb2101010 is a 30bpp packed RGB
                     format with 2 bits of padding.  yuyv is a 32bpp packed
                     YUV 4:2:2 format. No planar formats are currently
                     supported.

                     There are cases when xrgb2101010 will work with the ddrrmm
                     VO, but not with the ddrrmm backend for the ggppuu VO. This is
                     because with the ggppuu VO, in addition to requiring support
                     in your DRM driver, requires support for xrgb2101010 in
                     your EGL driver.  yuyv only ever works with the ddrrmm VO.

              ----ddrrmm--ddrraaww--ssuurrffaaccee--ssiizzee==<<[[WWxxHH]]>>
                     Sets the size of the surface used on the draw plane. The
                     surface will then be upscaled to the current screen
                     resolution. This option can be useful when used together
                     with the drmprime-overlay hwdec interop at high
                     resolutions, as it allows scaling the draw plane (which
                     in this case only handles the OSD) down to a size the GPU
                     can handle.

                     When used without the drmprime-overlay hwdec interop this
                     option will just cause the video to get rendered at a
                     different resolution and then scaled to screen size.

                     (default: display resolution)

              ----ddrrmm--vvrrrr--eennaabblleedd==<<nnoo||yyeess||aauuttoo>>
                     Toggle use of Variable Refresh Rate (VRR), aka Freesync
                     or Adaptive Sync on compatible systems. VRR allows for
                     the display to be refreshed at any rate within a range
                     (usually ~40Hz-60Hz for 60Hz displays). This can help
                     with playback of 24/25/50fps content. Support depends on
                     the use of a compatible monitor, GPU, and a sufficiently
                     new kernel with drivers that support the feature.

                     nnoo     Do not attempt to enable VRR. (default)

                     yyeess    Attempt to enable VRR, whether the capability is
                            reported or not.

                     aauuttoo   Attempt to enable VRR if support is reported.

       mmeeddiiaaccooddeecc__eemmbbeedd ((AAnnddrrooiidd))
              Renders IIMMGGFFMMTT__MMEEDDIIAACCOODDEECC frames directly to an
              aannddrrooiidd..vviieeww..SSuurrffaaccee.  Requires ----hhwwddeecc==mmeeddiiaaccooddeecc for hardware
              decoding, along with ----vvoo==mmeeddiiaaccooddeecc__eemmbbeedd and
              ----wwiidd==((iinnttppttrr__tt))((**aannddrrooiidd..vviieeww..SSuurrffaaccee)).

              Since this video output driver uses native decoding and
              rendering routines, many of mpv's features (subtitle rendering,
              OSD/OSC, video filters, etc) are not available with this driver.

              To use hardware decoding with ----vvoo==ggppuu instead, use
              ----hhwwddeecc==mmeeddiiaaccooddeecc or mmeeddiiaaccooddeecc--ccooppyy along with
              ----ggppuu--ccoonntteexxtt==aannddrrooiidd.

       wwllsshhmm ((WWaayyllaanndd oonnllyy))
              Shared memory video output driver without hardware acceleration
              that works whenever Wayland is present.

              Since mpv 0.30.0, you may need to use ----pprrooffiillee==ssww--ffaasstt to get
              decent performance.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This is a fallback only, and should not be normally used.

AAUUDDIIOO FFIILLTTEERRSS
       Audio filters allow you to modify the audio stream and its properties.
       The syntax is:

       ----aaff==......
              Setup a chain of audio filters. See ----vvff (VIDEO FILTERS) for the
              full syntax.

              This is an object settings list option. See List Options for
              details.

       NNOOTTEE::
          To get a full list of available audio filters, see ----aaff==hheellpp.

          Also, keep in mind that most actual filters are available via the
          llaavvffii wrapper, which gives you access to most of libavfilter's
          filters. This includes all filters that have been ported from
          MPlayer to libavfilter.

          The ----vvff description describes how libavfilter can be used and how
          to workaround deprecated mpv filters.

       See ----vvff group of options for info on how ----aaff--aadddd, ----aaff--pprree, ----aaff--ccllrr,
       and possibly others work.

       Available filters are:

       llaavvccaacc33eenncc[[==ooppttiioonnss]]
              Encode multi-channel audio to AC-3 at runtime using libavcodec.
              Supports 16-bit native-endian input format, maximum 6 channels.
              The output is big-endian when outputting a raw AC-3 stream,
              native-endian when outputting to S/PDIF. If the input sample
              rate is not 48 kHz, 44.1 kHz or 32 kHz, it will be resampled to
              48 kHz.

              ttoossppddiiff==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Output raw AC-3 stream if nnoo, output to S/PDIF for
                     pass-through if yyeess (default).

              bbiittrraattee==<<rraattee>>
                     The bitrate use for the AC-3 stream. Set it to 384 to get
                     384 kbps.

                     The default is 640. Some receivers might not be able to
                     handle this.

                     Valid values: 32, 40, 48, 56, 64, 80, 96, 112, 128, 160,
                     192, 224, 256, 320, 384, 448, 512, 576, 640.

                     The special value aauuttoo selects a default bitrate based on
                     the input channel number:

                     11cchh    96

                     22cchh    192

                     33cchh    224

                     44cchh    384

                     55cchh    448

                     66cchh    448

              mmiinncchh==<<nn>>
                     If the input channel number is less than <<mmiinncchh>>, the
                     filter will detach itself (default: 3).

              eennccooddeerr==<<nnaammee>>
                     Select the libavcodec encoder used. Currently, this
                     should be an AC-3 encoder, and using another codec will
                     fail horribly.

       ffoorrmmaatt==ffoorrmmaatt::ssrraattee::cchhaannnneellss::oouutt--ssrraattee::oouutt--cchhaannnneellss
              Does not do any format conversion itself. Rather, it may cause
              the filter system to insert necessary conversion filters before
              or after this filter if needed. It is primarily useful for
              controlling the audio format going into other filters. To
              specify the format for audio output, see ----aauuddiioo--ffoorrmmaatt,
              ----aauuddiioo--ssaammpplleerraattee, and ----aauuddiioo--cchhaannnneellss. This filter is able to
              force a particular format, whereas ----aauuddiioo--** may be overridden
              by the ao based on output compatibility.

              All parameters are optional. The first 3 parameters restrict
              what the filter accepts as input. They will therefore cause
              conversion filters to be inserted before this one.  The oouutt--
              parameters tell the filters or audio outputs following this
              filter how to interpret the data without actually doing a
              conversion. Setting these will probably just break things unless
              you really know you want this for some reason, such as testing
              or dealing with broken media.

              <<ffoorrmmaatt>>
                     Force conversion to this format. Use
                     ----aaff==ffoorrmmaatt==ffoorrmmaatt==hheellpp to get a list of valid formats.

              <<ssrraattee>>
                     Force conversion to a specific sample rate. The rate is
                     an integer, 48000 for example.

              <<cchhaannnneellss>>
                     Force mixing to a specific channel layout. See
                     ----aauuddiioo--cchhaannnneellss option for possible values.

              <<oouutt--ssrraattee>>

              <<oouutt--cchhaannnneellss>>

              _N_O_T_E: this filter used to be named ffoorrccee. The old ffoorrmmaatt filter
              used to do conversion itself, unlike this one which lets the
              filter system handle the conversion.

       ssccaalleetteemmppoo[[==ooppttiioonn11::ooppttiioonn22::......]]
              Scales audio tempo without altering pitch, optionally synced to
              playback speed.

              This works by playing 'stride' ms of audio at normal speed then
              consuming 'stride*scale' ms of input audio. It pieces the
              strides together by blending 'overlap'% of stride with audio
              following the previous stride. It optionally performs a short
              statistical analysis on the next 'search' ms of audio to
              determine the best overlap position.

              ssccaallee==<<aammoouunntt>>
                     Nominal amount to scale tempo. Scales this amount in
                     addition to speed. (default: 1.0)

              ssttrriiddee==<<aammoouunntt>>
                     Length in milliseconds to output each stride. Too high of
                     a value will cause noticeable skips at high scale amounts
                     and an echo at low scale amounts. Very low values will
                     alter pitch. Increasing improves performance. (default:
                     60)

              oovveerrllaapp==<<ffaaccttoorr>>
                     Factor of stride to overlap. Decreasing improves
                     performance.  (default: .20)

              sseeaarrcchh==<<aammoouunntt>>
                     Length in milliseconds to search for best overlap
                     position. Decreasing improves performance greatly. On
                     slow systems, you will probably want to set this very
                     low. (default: 14)

              ssppeeeedd==<<tteemmppoo||ppiittcchh||bbootthh||nnoonnee>>
                     Set response to speed change.

                     tteemmppoo  Scale tempo in sync with speed (default).

                     ppiittcchh  Reverses effect of filter. Scales pitch without
                            altering tempo.  Add this to your iinnppuutt..ccoonnff to
                            step by musical semi-tones:

                               [ multiply speed 0.9438743126816935
                               ] multiply speed 1.059463094352953

                            WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                               Loses sync with video.

                     bbootthh   Scale both tempo and pitch.

                     nnoonnee   Ignore speed changes.

                 Examples

                 mmppvv ----aaff==ssccaalleetteemmppoo ----ssppeeeedd==11..22 mmeeddiiaa..oogggg
                        Would play media at 1.2x normal speed, with audio at
                        normal pitch. Changing playback speed would change
                        audio tempo to match.

                 mmppvv ----aaff==ssccaalleetteemmppoo==ssccaallee==11..22::ssppeeeedd==nnoonnee ----ssppeeeedd==11..22
                 mmeeddiiaa..oogggg
                        Would play media at 1.2x normal speed, with audio at
                        normal pitch, but changing playback speed would have
                        no effect on audio tempo.

                 mmppvv ----aaff==ssccaalleetteemmppoo==ssttrriiddee==3300::oovveerrllaapp==..5500::sseeaarrcchh==1100 mmeeddiiaa..oogggg
                        Would tweak the quality and performance parameters.

                 mmppvv ----aaff==ssccaalleetteemmppoo==ssccaallee==11..22::ssppeeeedd==ppiittcchh aauuddiioo..oogggg
                        Would play media at 1.2x normal speed, with audio at
                        normal pitch.  Changing playback speed would change
                        pitch, leaving audio tempo at 1.2x.

       ssccaalleetteemmppoo22[[==ooppttiioonn11::ooppttiioonn22::......]]
              Scales audio tempo without altering pitch.  The algorithm is
              ported from chromium and uses the Waveform Similarity
              Overlap-and-add (WSOLA) method.  It seems to achieves higher
              audio quality than scaletempo, and rubberband R2 engine, or
              eennggiinnee==ffaasstteerr. This filter is inserted automatically if
              aauuddiioo--ppiittcchh--ccoorrrreeccttiioonn option is used (on by default) when the
              playback speed is changed.

              By default, the sseeaarrcchh--iinntteerrvvaall and wwiinnddooww--ssiizzee parameters have
              the same values as in chromium.

              mmiinn--ssppeeeedd==<<ssppeeeedd>>
                     Mute audio if the playback speed is below <<ssppeeeedd>>.
                     (default: 0.25)

              mmaaxx--ssppeeeedd==<<ssppeeeedd>>
                     Mute audio if the playback speed is above <<ssppeeeedd>> and
                     <<ssppeeeedd>> !!== 00. (default: 8.0)

              sseeaarrcchh--iinntteerrvvaall==<<aammoouunntt>>
                     Length in milliseconds to search for best overlap
                     position. (default: 40)

              wwiinnddooww--ssiizzee==<<aammoouunntt>>
                     Length in milliseconds of the overlap-and-add window.
                     (default: 12)

       rruubbbbeerrbbaanndd
              High quality pitch correction with librubberband. This can be
              used in place of ssccaalleetteemmppoo and ssccaalleetteemmppoo22, and will be used to
              adjust audio pitch when playing at speed different from normal.
              It can also be used to adjust audio pitch without changing
              playback speed.

              ppiittcchh--ssccaallee==<<aammoouunntt>>
                     Sets the pitch scaling factor. Frequencies are multiplied
                     by this value.  (default: 1.0)

              eennggiinnee==<<ffaasstteerr||ffiinneerr>>
                     Select the core Rubberband engine to be used. There are
                     two available:

                     FFaasstteerr This is the Rubberband R2 engine. It uses
                            significantly less CPU than the Finer (R3) engine.

                     FFiinneerr  This is the Rubberband R3 engine. This engine is
                            only available with librubberband version 3 or
                            newer. This produces significantly higher quality
                            output, at the cost of higher CPU usage. (Default
                            if available)

              This filter has a number of additional sub-options. You can list
              them with mmppvv ----aaff==rruubbbbeerrbbaanndd==hheellpp. This will also show the
              default values for each option. The options are not documented
              here, because they are merely passed to librubberband. Look at
              the librubberband documentation to learn what each option does:
               <https://breakfastquay.com/rubberband/code-doc/classRubberBand_1_1RubberBandStretcher.html>
              Do note that certain options are only applicable to one of R2
              (faster) and R3 (finer) engines.  (The mapping of the mpv
              rubberband filter sub-option names and values to those of
              librubberband follows a simple pattern: ""OOppttiioonn"" ++ NNaammee ++
              VVaalluuee.)

              This filter supports the following aaff--ccoommmmaanndd commands:

              sseett--ppiittcchh
                     Set the <<ppiittcchh--ssccaallee>> argument dynamically. This can be
                     used to change the playback pitch at runtime. Note that
                     speed is controlled using the standard ssppeeeedd property,
                     not aaff--ccoommmmaanndd.

              mmuullttiippllyy--ppiittcchh <<ffaaccttoorr>>
                     Multiply the current value of <<ppiittcchh--ssccaallee>> dynamically.

       llaavvffii==ggrraapphh
              Filter audio using FFmpeg's libavfilter.

              <<ggrraapphh>>
                     Libavfilter graph. See llaavvffii video filter for details -
                     the graph syntax is the same.

                     WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                        Don't forget to quote libavfilter graphs as described
                        in the lavfi video filter section.

              oo==<<ssttrriinngg>>
                     AVOptions.

              ffiixx--ppttss==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Determine PTS based on sample count (default: no). If
                     this is enabled, the player won't rely on libavfilter
                     passing through PTS accurately.  Instead, it pass a
                     sample count as PTS to libavfilter, and compute the PTS
                     used by mpv based on that and the input PTS. This helps
                     with filters which output a recomputed PTS instead of the
                     original PTS (including filters which require the PTS to
                     start at 0). mpv normally expects filters to not touch
                     the PTS (or only to the extent of changing frame
                     boundaries), so this is not the default, but it will be
                     needed to use broken filters. In practice, these broken
                     filters will either cause slow A/V desync over time (with
                     some files), or break playback completely if you seek or
                     start playback from the middle of a file.

       ddrroopp   This filter drops or repeats audio frames to adapt to playback
              speed. It always operates on full audio frames, because it was
              made to handle SPDIF (compressed audio passthrough). This is
              used automatically if the ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==ddiissppllaayy--aaddrroopp option is
              used. Do not use this filter (or the given option); they are
              extremely low quality.

VVIIDDEEOO FFIILLTTEERRSS
       Video filters allow you to modify the video stream and its properties.
       All of the information described in this section applies to audio
       filters as well (generally using the prefix ----aaff instead of ----vvff).

       The exact syntax is:

       ----vvff==<<ffiilltteerr11[[==ppaarraammeetteerr11::ppaarraammeetteerr22::......]],,ffiilltteerr22,,......>>
              Setup a chain of video filters. This consists on the filter
              name, and an option list of parameters after ==. The parameters
              are separated by :: (not ,,, as that starts a new filter entry).

              Before the filter name, a label can be specified with @@nnaammee::,
              where name is an arbitrary user-given name, which identifies the
              filter. This is only needed if you want to toggle the filter at
              runtime.

              A !! before the filter name means the filter is disabled by
              default. It will be skipped on filter creation. This is also
              useful for runtime filter toggling.

              See the vvff command (and ttooggggllee sub-command) for further
              explanations and examples.

              This is an object settings list option. See List Options for
              details.

              The general filter entry syntax is:
                 [[""@@""<<llaabbeell--nnaammee>>""::""]] [[""!!""]] <<ffiilltteerr--nnaammee>> [[ ""==""
                 <<ffiilltteerr--ppaarraammeetteerr--lliisstt>> ]]

              or for the special "toggle" syntax (see vvff command):
                 ""@@""<<llaabbeell--nnaammee>>

              and the ffiilltteerr--ppaarraammeetteerr--lliisstt:
                 <<ffiilltteerr--ppaarraammeetteerr>> || <<ffiilltteerr--ppaarraammeetteerr>> "",,""
                 <<ffiilltteerr--ppaarraammeetteerr--lliisstt>>

              and ffiilltteerr--ppaarraammeetteerr:
                 (( <<ppaarraamm--nnaammee>> ""=="" <<ppaarraamm--vvaalluuee>> )) || <<ppaarraamm--vvaalluuee>>

              ppaarraamm--vvaalluuee can further be quoted in [[ / ]] in case the value
              contains characters like ,, or ==. This is used in particular with
              the llaavvffii filter, which uses a very similar syntax as mpv
              (MPlayer historically) to specify filters and their parameters.

       NNOOTTEE::
          ----vvff can only take a single track as input, even if the filter
          supports dynamic input. Filters that require multiple inputs can't
          be used.  Use ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx for such a use case. This also applies
          for ----aaff.

       Filters can be manipulated at run time. You can use @@ labels as
       described above in combination with the vvff command (see COMMAND
       INTERFACE) to get more control over this. Initially disabled filters
       with !! are useful for this as well.

       NNOOTTEE::
          To get a full list of available video filters, see ----vvff==hheellpp and
           <https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-filters.html>  .

          Also, keep in mind that most actual filters are available via the
          llaavvffii wrapper, which gives you access to most of libavfilter's
          filters. This includes all filters that have been ported from
          MPlayer to libavfilter.

          Most builtin filters are deprecated in some ways, unless they're
          only available in mpv (such as filters which deal with mpv
          specifics, or which are implemented in mpv only).

          If a filter is not builtin, the llaavvffii--bbrriiddggee will be automatically
          tried. This bridge does not support help output, and does not verify
          parameters before the filter is actually used. Although the mpv
          syntax is rather similar to libavfilter's, it's not the same. (Which
          means not everything accepted by vf_lavfi's ggrraapphh option will be
          accepted by ----vvff.)

          You can also prefix the filter name with llaavvffii-- to force the
          wrapper.  This is helpful if the filter name collides with a
          deprecated mpv builtin filter. For example ----vvff==llaavvffii--ssccaallee==aarrggss
          would use libavfilter's ssccaallee filter over mpv's deprecated builtin
          one.

       Video filters are managed in lists. There are a few commands to manage
       the filter list.

       ----vvff--aappppeenndd==ffiilltteerr
              Appends the filter given as arguments to the filter list.

       ----vvff--aadddd==ffiilltteerr
              Appends the filter given as arguments to the filter list.
              (Passing multiple filters is currently still possible, but
              deprecated.)

       ----vvff--pprree==ffiilltteerr
              Prepends the filters given as arguments to the filter list.
              (Passing multiple filters is currently still possible, but
              deprecated.)

       ----vvff--rreemmoovvee==ffiilltteerr
              Deletes the filter from the list. The filter can be either given
              the way it was added (filter name and its full argument list),
              or by label (prefixed with @@). Matching of filters works as
              follows: if either of the compared filters has a label set, only
              the labels are compared. If none of the filters have a label,
              the filter name, arguments, and argument order are compared.
              (Passing multiple filters is currently still possible, but
              deprecated.)

       ----vvff--ttooggggllee==ffiilltteerr
              Add the given filter to the list if it was not present yet, or
              remove it from the list if it was present. Matching of filters
              works as described in ----vvff--rreemmoovvee.

       ----vvff--ccllrr
              Completely empties the filter list.

       With filters that support it, you can access parameters by their name.

       ----vvff==<<ffiilltteerr>>==hheellpp
              Prints the parameter names and parameter value ranges for a
              particular filter.

       Available mpv-only filters are:

       ffoorrmmaatt==ffmmtt==<<vvaalluuee>>::ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx==<<vvaalluuee>>::......
              Applies video parameter overrides, with optional conversion. By
              default, this overrides the video's parameters without
              conversion (except for the ffmmtt parameter), but can be made to
              perform an appropriate conversion with ccoonnvveerrtt==yyeess for
              parameters for which conversion is supported.

              <<ffmmtt>>  Image format name, e.g. rgb15, bgr24, 420p, etc.
                     (default: don't change).

                     This filter always performs conversion to the given
                     format.

                     NNOOTTEE::
                        For a list of available formats, use
                        ----vvff==ffoorrmmaatt==ffmmtt==hheellpp.

                     NNOOTTEE::
                        Conversion between hardware formats is supported in
                        some cases.  eg: ccuuddaa to vvuullkkaann, or vvaaaappii to vvuullkkaann.

              <<ccoonnvveerrtt==yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Force conversion of color parameters (default: no).

                     If this is disabled (the default), the only conversion
                     that is possibly performed is format conversion if <<ffmmtt>>
                     is set. All other parameters (like <<ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx>>) are
                     forced without conversion. This mode is typically useful
                     when files have been incorrectly tagged.

                     If this is enabled, libswscale or zimg is used if any of
                     the parameters mismatch. zimg is used of the input/output
                     image formats are supported by mpv's zimg wrapper, and if
                     ----sswwss--aallllooww--zziimmgg==yyeess is used. Both libraries may not
                     support all kinds of conversions. This typically results
                     in silent incorrect conversion. zimg has in many cases a
                     better chance of performing the conversion correctly.

                     In both cases, the color parameters are set on the output
                     stage of the image format conversion (if ffmmtt was set).
                     The difference is that with ccoonnvveerrtt==nnoo, the color
                     parameters are not passed on to the converter.

                     If input and output video parameters are the same,
                     conversion is always skipped.

                     When converting between hardware formats, this parameter
                     has no effect, and the only conversion that is done is
                     the format conversion.

                        Examples

                        mmppvv tteesstt..mmkkvv ----vvff==ffoorrmmaatt::ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx==yyccggccoo
                               Results in incorrect colors (if test.mkv was
                               tagged correctly).

                        mmppvv tteesstt..mmkkvv ----vvff==ffoorrmmaatt::ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx==yyccggccoo::ccoonnvveerrtt==yyeess
                        ----sswwss--aallllooww--zziimmgg
                               Results in true conversion to yyccggccoo, assuming
                               the renderer supports it (----vvoo==ggppuu normally
                               does). You can add ----vvoo==xxvv to force a VO which
                               definitely does not support it, which should
                               show incorrect colors as confirmation.

                               Using ----sswwss--aallllooww--zziimmgg==nnoo (or disabling zimg at
                               build time) will use libswscale, which cannot
                               perform this conversion as of this writing.

              <<ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx>>
                     Controls the YUV to RGB color space conversion when
                     playing video. There are various standards. Normally,
                     BT.601 should be used for SD video, and BT.709 for HD
                     video. (This is done by default.) Using incorrect color
                     space results in slightly under or over saturated and
                     shifted colors.

                     These options are not always supported. Different video
                     outputs provide varying degrees of support. The ggppuu and
                     vvddppaauu video output drivers usually offer full support.
                     The xxvv output can set the color space if the system video
                     driver supports it, but not input and output levels. The
                     ssccaallee video filter can configure color space and input
                     levels, but only if the output format is RGB (if the
                     video output driver supports RGB output, you can force
                     this with ----vvff==ssccaallee,,ffoorrmmaatt==rrggbbaa).

                     If this option is set to aauuttoo (which is the default), the
                     video's color space flag will be used. If that flag is
                     unset, the color space will be selected automatically.
                     This is done using a simple heuristic that attempts to
                     distinguish SD and HD video. If the video is larger than
                     1279x576 pixels, BT.709 (HD) will be used; otherwise
                     BT.601 (SD) is selected.

                     Available color spaces are:

                     aauuttoo   automatic selection (default)

                     bbtt..660011 ITU-R Rec. BT.601 (SD)

                     bbtt..770099 ITU-R Rec. BT.709 (HD)

                     bbtt..22002200--nnccll
                            ITU-R Rec. BT.2020 (non-constant luminance)

                     bbtt..22002200--ccll
                            ITU-R Rec. BT.2020 (constant luminance)

                     bbtt..22110000--ppqq
                            ITU-R Rec. BT.2100 ICtCp PQ variant

                     bbtt..22110000--hhllgg
                            ITU-R Rec. BT.2100 ICtCp HLG variant

                     ddoollbbyyvviissiioonn
                            Dolby Vision

                     ssmmppttee--224400mm
                            SMPTE-240M

              <<ccoolloorrlleevveellss>>
                     YUV color levels used with YUV to RGB conversion. This
                     option is only necessary when playing broken files which
                     do not follow standard color levels or which are flagged
                     wrong. If the video does not specify its color range, it
                     is assumed to be limited range.

                     The same limitations as with <<ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx>> apply.

                     Available color ranges are:

                     aauuttoo   automatic selection (normally limited range)
                            (default)

                     lliimmiitteedd
                            limited range (16-235 for luma, 16-240 for chroma)

                     ffuullll   full range (0-255 for both luma and chroma)

              <<pprriimmaarriieess>>
                     RGB primaries the source file was encoded with. Normally
                     this should be set in the file header, but when playing
                     broken or mistagged files this can be used to override
                     the setting.

                     This option only affects video output drivers that
                     perform color management, for example ggppuu with the
                     ttaarrggeett--pprriimm or iicccc--pprrooffiillee suboptions set.

                     If this option is set to aauuttoo (which is the default), the
                     video's primaries flag will be used. If that flag is
                     unset, the color space will be selected automatically,
                     using the following heuristics: If the <<ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx>> is
                     set or determined as BT.2020 or BT.709, the corresponding
                     primaries are used. Otherwise, if the video height is
                     exactly 576 (PAL), BT.601-625 is used. If it's exactly
                     480 or 486 (NTSC), BT.601-525 is used. If the video
                     resolution is anything else, BT.709 is used.

                     Available primaries are:

                     aauuttoo   automatic selection (default)

                     bbtt..660011--552255
                            ITU-R BT.601 (SD) 525-line systems (NTSC, SMPTE-C)

                     bbtt..660011--662255
                            ITU-R BT.601 (SD) 625-line systems (PAL, SECAM)

                     bbtt..770099 ITU-R BT.709 (HD) (same primaries as sRGB)

                     bbtt..22002200
                            ITU-R BT.2020 (UHD)

                     aappppllee  Apple RGB

                     aaddoobbee  Adobe RGB (1998)

                     pprroopphhoottoo
                            ProPhoto RGB (ROMM)

                     cciiee11993311
                            CIE 1931 RGB

                     ddccii--pp33 DCI-P3 (Digital Cinema)

                     vv--ggaammuutt
                            Panasonic V-Gamut primaries

              <<ggaammmmaa>>
                     Gamma function the source file was encoded with. Normally
                     this should be set in the file header, but when playing
                     broken or mistagged files this can be used to override
                     the setting.

                     This option only affects video output drivers that
                     perform color management.

                     If this option is set to aauuttoo (which is the default), the
                     gamma will be set to BT.1886 for YCbCr content, sRGB for
                     RGB content and Linear for XYZ content.

                     Available gamma functions are:

                     aauuttoo   automatic selection (default)

                     bbtt..11888866
                            ITU-R BT.1886 (EOTF corresponding to
                            BT.601/BT.709/BT.2020)

                     ssrrggbb   IEC 61966-2-4 (sRGB)

                     lliinneeaarr Linear light

                     ggaammmmaa11..88
                            Pure power curve (gamma 1.8)

                     ggaammmmaa22..00
                            Pure power curve (gamma 2.0)

                     ggaammmmaa22..22
                            Pure power curve (gamma 2.2)

                     ggaammmmaa22..44
                            Pure power curve (gamma 2.4)

                     ggaammmmaa22..66
                            Pure power curve (gamma 2.6)

                     ggaammmmaa22..88
                            Pure power curve (gamma 2.8)

                     pprroopphhoottoo
                            ProPhoto RGB (ROMM) curve

                     ppqq     ITU-R BT.2100 PQ (Perceptual quantizer) curve

                     hhllgg    ITU-R BT.2100 HLG (Hybrid Log-gamma) curve

                     vv--lloogg  Panasonic V-Log transfer curve

                     ss--lloogg11 Sony S-Log1 transfer curve

                     ss--lloogg22 Sony S-Log2 transfer curve

              <<ssiigg--ppeeaakk>>
                     Reference peak illumination for the video file, relative
                     to the signal's reference white level. This is mostly
                     interesting for HDR, but it can also be used tone map SDR
                     content to simulate a different exposure. Normally
                     inferred from tags such as MaxCLL or mastering metadata.

                     The default of 0.0 will default to the source's nominal
                     peak luminance.

              <<lliigghhtt>>
                        Light type of the scene. This is mostly correctly
                        inferred based on the gamma function, but it can be
                        useful to override this when viewing raw camera
                        footage (e.g. V-Log), which is normally scene-referred
                        instead of display-referred.

                        Available light types are:

                     aauuttoo   Automatic selection (default)

                     ddiissppllaayy
                            Display-referred light (most content)

                     hhllgg    Scene-referred using the HLG OOTF (e.g. HLG
                            content)

                     770099--11888866
                            Scene-referred using the BT709+BT1886 interaction

                     ggaammmmaa11..22
                            Scene-referred using a pure power OOTF (gamma=1.2)

              <<ddoollbbyyvviissiioonn==yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Whether or not to include Dolby Vision metadata (default:
                     yes). If disabled, any Dolby Vision metadata will be
                     stripped from frames.

              <<hhddrr1100pplluuss==yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Whether or not to include HDR10+ metadata (default: yes).
                     If disabled, any HDR10+ metadata will be stripped from
                     frames.

              <<ffiillmm--ggrraaiinn==yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Whether or not to include film grain metadata (default:
                     yes). If disabled, any film grain metadata will be
                     stripped from frames.

              <<cchhrroommaa--llooccaattiioonn>>
                     Set the chroma loc of the video. Use
                     ----vvff==ffoorrmmaatt::cchhrroommaa--llooccaattiioonn==hheellpp to list all available
                     modes.

              <<sstteerreeoo--iinn>>
                     Set the stereo mode the video is assumed to be encoded
                     in. Use ----vvff==ffoorrmmaatt::sstteerreeoo--iinn==hheellpp to list all available
                     modes. Check with the sstteerreeoo33dd filter documentation to
                     see what the names mean.

              <<rroottaattee>>
                     Set the rotation the video is assumed to be encoded with
                     in degrees.  The special value --11 uses the input format.

              <<ww>>,, <<hh>>
                     If not 0, perform conversion to the given size. Ignored
                     if ccoonnvveerrtt==yyeess is not set.

              <<ddww>>,, <<ddhh>>
                     Set the display size. Note that setting the display size
                     such that the video is scaled in both directions instead
                     of just changing the aspect ratio is an implementation
                     detail, and might change later.

              <<ddaarr>>  Set the display aspect ratio of the video frame. This is
                     a float, but values such as [[1166::99]] can be passed too
                     ([[......]] for quoting to prevent the option parser from
                     interpreting the :: character).

              <<ffoorrccee--ssccaalleerr==aauuttoo||zziimmgg||sswwss>>
                     Force a specific scaler backend, if applicable. This is a
                     debug option and could go away any time.

              <<aallpphhaa==aauuttoo||ssttrraaiigghhtt||pprreemmuull>>
                     Set the kind of alpha the video uses. Undefined effect if
                     the image format has no alpha channel (could be ignored
                     or cause an error, depending on how mpv internals
                     evolve). Setting this may or may not cause downstream
                     image processing to treat alpha differently, depending on
                     support. With ccoonnvveerrtt and zimg used, this will convert
                     the alpha.  libswscale and other FFmpeg components
                     completely ignore this.

       llaavvffii==ggrraapphh[[::sswwss--ffllaaggss[[::oo==ooppttss]]]]
              Filter video using FFmpeg's libavfilter.

              <<ggrraapphh>>
                     The libavfilter graph string. The filter must have a
                     single video input pad and a single video output pad.

                     See  <https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-filters.html>  for syntax
                     and available filters.

                     WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                        If you want to use the full filter syntax with this
                        option, you have to quote the filter graph in order to
                        prevent mpv's syntax and the filter graph syntax from
                        clashing. To prevent a quoting and escaping mess,
                        consider using ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx if you know which video
                        track you want to use from the input file. (There is
                        only one video track for nearly all video files
                        anyway.)

                        Examples

                        ----vvff==llaavvffii==[[ggrraaddffuunn==2200::3300,,vvfflliipp]]
                               ggrraaddffuunn filter with nonsense parameters,
                               followed by a vvfflliipp filter. (This demonstrates
                               how libavfilter takes a graph and not just a
                               single filter.) The filter graph string is
                               quoted with [[ and ]]. This requires no
                               additional quoting or escaping with some shells
                               (like bash), while others (like zsh) require
                               additional "" quotes around the option string.

                        ''----vvff==llaavvffii==""ggrraaddffuunn==2200::3300,,vvfflliipp""''
                               Same as before, but uses quoting that should be
                               safe with all shells. The outer '' quotes make
                               sure that the shell does not remove the ""
                               quotes needed by mpv.

                        ''----vvff==llaavvffii==ggrraapphh==""ggrraaddffuunn==rraaddiiuuss==3300::ssttrreennggtthh==2200,,vvfflliipp""''
                               Same as before, but uses named parameters for
                               everything.

              <<sswwss--ffllaaggss>>
                     If libavfilter inserts filters for pixel format
                     conversion, this option gives the flags which should be
                     passed to libswscale. This option is numeric and takes a
                     bit-wise combination of SSWWSS__ flags.

                     See
                     hhttttppss::////ggiitt..vviiddeeoollaann..oorrgg//??pp==ffffmmppeegg..ggiitt;;aa==bblloobb;;ff==lliibbsswwssccaallee//sswwssccaallee..hh.

              <<oo>>    Set AVFilterGraph options. These should be documented by
                     FFmpeg.

                        Example

                        ''----vvff==llaavvffii==yyaaddiiff::oo==""tthhrreeaaddss==22,,tthhrreeaadd__ttyyppee==sslliiccee""''
                               forces a specific threading configuration.

       ssuubb==[[==bboottttoomm--mmaarrggiinn::ttoopp--mmaarrggiinn]]
              Moves subtitle rendering to an arbitrary point in the filter
              chain, or force subtitle rendering in the video filter as
              opposed to using video output OSD support.

              <<bboottttoomm--mmaarrggiinn>>
                     Adds a black band at the bottom of the frame. The SSA/ASS
                     renderer can place subtitles there (with
                     ----ssuubb--uussee--mmaarrggiinnss).

              <<ttoopp--mmaarrggiinn>>
                     Black band on the top for toptitles  (with
                     ----ssuubb--uussee--mmaarrggiinnss).

                 Examples

                 ----vvff==ssuubb,,eeqq
                        Moves sub rendering before the eq filter. This will
                        put both subtitle colors and video under the influence
                        of the video equalizer settings.

       vvaappoouurrssyynntthh==ffiillee::bbuuffffeerreedd--ffrraammeess::ccoonnccuurrrreenntt--ffrraammeess::uusseerr--ddaattaa
              Loads a VapourSynth filter script. This is intended for streamed
              processing: mpv actually provides a source filter, instead of
              using a native VapourSynth video source. The mpv source will
              answer frame requests only within a small window of frames (the
              size of this window is controlled with the bbuuffffeerreedd--ffrraammeess
              parameter), and requests outside of that will return errors. As
              such, you can't use the full power of VapourSynth, but you can
              use certain filters.

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 Do not use this filter, unless you have expert knowledge in
                 VapourSynth, and know how to fix bugs in the mpv VapourSynth
                 wrapper code.

              If you just want to play video generated by VapourSynth (i.e.
              using a native VapourSynth video source), it's better to use
              vvssppiippee and a pipe or FIFO to feed the video to mpv. The same
              applies if the filter script requires random frame access (see
              bbuuffffeerreedd--ffrraammeess parameter).

              ffiillee   Filename of the script source. Currently, this is always
                     a python script (..vvppyy in VapourSynth convention).

                     The variable vviiddeeoo__iinn is set to the mpv video source, and
                     it is expected that the script reads video from it.
                     (Otherwise, mpv will decode no video, and the video
                     packet queue will overflow, eventually leading to only
                     audio playing, or worse.)

                     The filter graph created by the script is also expected
                     to pass through timestamps using the __DDuurraattiioonnNNuumm and
                     __DDuurraattiioonnDDeenn frame properties.

                     See the end of the option list for a full list of script
                     variables defined by mpv.

                        Example:

                           import vapoursynth as vs
                           from vapoursynth import core
                           core.std.AddBorders(video_in, 10, 10, 20, 20).set_output()

                     WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                        The script will be reloaded on every seek. This is
                        done to reset the filter properly on discontinuities.

              bbuuffffeerreedd--ffrraammeess
                     Maximum number of decoded video frames that should be
                     buffered before the filter (default: 4). This specifies
                     the maximum number of frames the script can request in
                     backward direction.

                     E.g. if bbuuffffeerreedd--ffrraammeess==55, and the script just requested
                     frame 15, it can still request frame 10, but frame 9 is
                     not available anymore.  If it requests frame 30, mpv will
                     decode 15 more frames, and keep only frames 25-30.

                     The only reason why this buffer exists is to serve the
                     random access requests the VapourSynth filter can make.

                     The VapourSynth API has a ggeettFFrraammeeAAssyynncc function, which
                     takes an absolute frame number. Source filters must
                     respond to all requests. For example, a source filter can
                     request frame 2432, and then frame 3.  Source filters
                     typically implement this by pre-indexing the entire file.

                     mpv on the other hand is stream oriented, and does not
                     allow filters to seek. (And it would not make sense to
                     allow it, because it would ruin performance.) Filters get
                     frames sequentially in playback direction, and cannot
                     request them out of order.

                     To compensate for this mismatch, mpv allows the filter to
                     access frames within a certain window. bbuuffffeerreedd--ffrraammeess
                     controls the size of this window. Most VapourSynth
                     filters happen to work with this, because mpv requests
                     frames sequentially increasing from it, and most filters
                     only require frames "close" to the requested frame.

                     If the filter requests a frame that has a higher frame
                     number than the highest buffered frame, new frames will
                     be decoded until the requested frame number is reached.
                     Excessive frames will be flushed out in a FIFO manner
                     (there are only at most bbuuffffeerreedd--ffrraammeess in this buffer).

                     If the filter requests a frame that has a lower frame
                     number than the lowest buffered frame, the request cannot
                     be satisfied, and an error is returned to the filter.
                     This kind of error is not supposed to happen in a
                     "proper" VapourSynth environment. What exactly happens
                     depends on the filters involved.

                     Increasing this buffer will not improve performance.
                     Rather, it will waste memory, and slow down seeks (when
                     enough frames to fill the buffer need to be decoded at
                     once). It is only needed to prevent the error described
                     in the previous paragraph.

                     How many frames a filter requires depends on filter
                     implementation details, and mpv has no way of knowing. A
                     scale filter might need only 1 frame, an interpolation
                     filter may require a small number of frames, and the
                     RReevveerrssee filter will require an infinite number of frames.

                     If you want reliable operation to the full extend
                     VapourSynth is capable, use vvssppiippee.

                     The actual number of buffered frames also depends on the
                     value of the ccoonnccuurrrreenntt--ffrraammeess option. Currently, both
                     option values are multiplied to get the final buffer
                     size.

              ccoonnccuurrrreenntt--ffrraammeess
                     Number of frames that should be requested in parallel.
                     The level of concurrency depends on the filter and how
                     quickly mpv can decode video to feed the filter. This
                     value should probably be proportional to the number of
                     cores on your machine. Most time, making it higher than
                     the number of cores can actually make it slower.

                     Technically, mpv will call the VapourSynth ggeettFFrraammeeAAssyynncc
                     function in a loop, until there are ccoonnccuurrrreenntt--ffrraammeess
                     frames that have not been returned by the filter yet.
                     This also assumes that the rest of the mpv filter chain
                     reads the output of the vvaappoouurrssyynntthh filter quickly
                     enough. (For example, if you pause the player, filtering
                     will stop very soon, because the filtered frames are
                     waiting in a queue.)

                     Actual concurrency depends on many other factors.

                     By default, this uses the special value aauuttoo, which sets
                     the option to the number of detected logical CPU cores.

              uusseerr--ddaattaa
                     Optional arbitrary string that is passed to the script.
                     Default to empty string if not set.

              The following ..vvppyy script variables are defined by mpv:

              vviiddeeoo__iinn
                     The mpv video source as vapoursynth clip. Note that this
                     has an incorrect (very high) length set, which confuses
                     many filters. This is necessary, because the true number
                     of frames is unknown. You can use the TTrriimm filter on the
                     clip to reduce the length.

              vviiddeeoo__iinn__ddww,, vviiddeeoo__iinn__ddhh
                     Display size of the video. Can be different from video
                     size if the video does not use square pixels (e.g. DVD).

              ccoonnttaaiinneerr__ffppss
                     FPS value as reported by file headers. This value can be
                     wrong or completely broken (e.g. 0 or NaN). Even if the
                     value is correct, if another filter changes the real FPS
                     (by dropping or inserting frames), the value of this
                     variable will not be useful. Note that the
                     ----ccoonnttaaiinneerr--ffppss--oovveerrrriiddee command line option overrides
                     this value.

                     Useful for some filters which insist on having a FPS.

              ddiissppllaayy__ffppss
                     Refresh rate of the current display. Note that this value
                     can be 0.

              ddiissppllaayy__rreess
                     Resolution of the current display. This is an integer
                     array with the first entry corresponding to the width and
                     the second entry corresponding to the height. These
                     values can be 0. Note that this will not respond to
                     monitor changes and may not work on all platforms.

              uusseerr__ddaattaa
                     User data passed from the filter. This variable always
                     exists, and defaults to empty string.

       vvaavvpppp  VA-API video post processing. Requires the system to support
              VA-API, i.e. Linux/BSD only. Works with ----vvoo==vvaaaappii and ----vvoo==ggppuu
              only.  Currently deinterlaces. This filter is automatically
              inserted if deinterlacing is requested (either using the dd key,
              by default mapped to the command ccyyccllee ddeeiinntteerrllaaccee, or the
              ----ddeeiinntteerrllaaccee option).

              ddeeiinntt==<<mmeetthhoodd>>
                     Select the deinterlacing algorithm.

                     nnoo     Don't perform deinterlacing.

                     aauuttoo   Select the best quality deinterlacing algorithm
                            (default). This goes by the order of the options
                            as documented, with mmoottiioonn--ccoommppeennssaatteedd being
                            considered best quality.

                     ffiirrsstt--ffiieelldd
                            Show only first field.

                     bboobb    bob deinterlacing.

                     wweeaavvee,, mmoottiioonn--aaddaappttiivvee,, mmoottiioonn--ccoommppeennssaatteedd
                            Advanced deinterlacing algorithms. Whether these
                            actually work depends on the GPU hardware, the GPU
                            drivers, driver bugs, and mpv bugs.

              <<iinntteerrllaacceedd--oonnllyy>>

                     nnoo     Deinterlace all frames (default).

                     yyeess    Only deinterlace frames marked as interlaced.

              rreevveerrssaall--bbuugg==<<yyeess||nnoo>>

                     nnoo     Use the API as it was interpreted by older Mesa
                            drivers. While this interpretation was more
                            obvious and intuitive, it was apparently wrong,
                            and not shared by Intel driver developers.

                     yyeess    Use Intel interpretation of surface forward and
                            backwards references (default). This is what Intel
                            drivers and newer Mesa drivers expect. Matters
                            only for the advanced deinterlacing algorithms.

       vvddppaauupppp
              VDPAU video post processing. Works with ----vvoo==vvddppaauu and ----vvoo==ggppuu
              only. This filter is automatically inserted if deinterlacing is
              requested (either using the dd key, by default mapped to the
              command ccyyccllee ddeeiinntteerrllaaccee, or the ----ddeeiinntteerrllaaccee option). When
              enabling deinterlacing, it is always preferred over software
              deinterlacer filters if the vvddppaauu VO is used, and also if ggppuu is
              used and hardware decoding was activated at least once (i.e.
              vdpau was loaded).

              sshhaarrppeenn==<<--11--11>>
                     For positive values, apply a sharpening algorithm to the
                     video, for negative values a blurring algorithm (default:
                     0).

              ddeennooiissee==<<00--11>>
                     Apply a noise reduction algorithm to the video (default:
                     0; no noise reduction).

              ddeeiinntt==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Whether deinterlacing is enabled (default: no). If
                     enabled, it will use the mode selected with ddeeiinntt--mmooddee.

              ddeeiinntt--mmooddee==<<ffiirrsstt--ffiieelldd||bboobb||tteemmppoorraall||tteemmppoorraall--ssppaattiiaall>>
                     Select deinterlacing mode (default: temporal).

                     Note that there's currently a mechanism that allows the
                     vvddppaauu VO to change the ddeeiinntt--mmooddee of auto-inserted
                     vvddppaauupppp filters. To avoid confusion, it's recommended not
                     to use the ----vvoo==vvddppaauu suboptions related to filtering.

                     ffiirrsstt--ffiieelldd
                            Show only first field.

                     bboobb    Bob deinterlacing.

                     tteemmppoorraall
                            Motion-adaptive temporal deinterlacing. May lead
                            to A/V desync with slow video hardware and/or high
                            resolution.

                     tteemmppoorraall--ssppaattiiaall
                            Motion-adaptive temporal deinterlacing with
                            edge-guided spatial interpolation. Needs fast
                            video hardware.

              cchhrroommaa--ddeeiinntt
                     Makes temporal deinterlacers operate both on luma and
                     chroma (default).  Use no-chroma-deint to solely use luma
                     and speed up advanced deinterlacing. Useful with slow
                     video memory.

              ppuulllluupp Try to apply inverse telecine, needs motion adaptive
                     temporal deinterlacing.

              iinntteerrllaacceedd--oonnllyy==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     If yyeess, only deinterlace frames marked as interlaced
                     (default: no).

              hhqqssccaalliinngg==<<00--99>>

                     00      Use default VDPAU scaling (default).

                     11--99    Apply high quality VDPAU scaling (needs capable
                            hardware).

       dd33dd1111vvpppp
              Direct3D 11 video post-processing. Requires a D3D11 context and
              works best with hardware decoding. Software frames are
              automatically uploaded to hardware for processing.

              ffoorrmmaatt Convert to the selected image format, e.g., nv12, p010,
                     etc. (default: don't change).  Format names can be
                     queried with ----vvff==dd33dd1111vvpppp==ffoorrmmaatt==hheellpp.  Note that only a
                     limited subset is supported, and actual support depends
                     on your hardware. Normally, this shouldn't be changed
                     unless some processing only works with a specific format,
                     in which case it can be selected here.

              ddeeiinntt==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     Whether deinterlacing is enabled (default: no).

              ssccaallee  Scaling factor for the video frames (default: 1.0).

              ssccaalliinngg--mmooddee==<<ssttaannddaarrdd,,iinntteell,,nnvviiddiiaa>>
                     Select the scaling mode to be used. Note that this only
                     enables the appropriate processing extensions; whether it
                     actually works or not depends on your hardware and the
                     settings in your GPU driver's control panel (default:
                     standard).

                     ssttaannddaarrdd
                            Default scaling mode as decided by d3d11vpp
                            implementation.

                     iinntteell  Intel Video Super Resolution.

                     nnvviiddiiaa NVIDIA RTX Super Resolution.

              iinntteerrllaacceedd--oonnllyy==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
                     If yyeess, only deinterlace frames marked as interlaced
                     (default: no).

              mmooddee==<<bblleenndd||bboobb||aaddaappttiivvee||mmooccoommpp||iivvccttcc||nnoonnee>>
                     Tries to select a video processor with the given
                     processing capability.  If a video processor supports
                     multiple capabilities, it is not clear which algorithm is
                     actually selected. nnoonnee always falls back. On most if not
                     all hardware, this option will probably do nothing,
                     because a video processor usually supports all modes or
                     none.

              nnvviiddiiaa--ttrruuee--hhddrr
                     Enable NVIDIA RTX Video HDR processing.

       ffiinnggeerrpprriinntt==......
              Compute video frame fingerprints and provide them as metadata.
              Actually, it currently barely deserved to be called ffiinnggeerrpprriinntt,
              because it does not compute "proper" fingerprints, only tiny
              downscaled images (but which can be used to compute image hashes
              or for similarity matching).

              The main purpose of this filter is to support the sskkiipp--llooggoo..lluuaa
              script.  If this script is dropped, or mpv ever gains a way to
              load user-defined filters (other than VapourSynth), this filter
              will be removed. Due to the "special" nature of this filter, it
              will be removed without warning.

              The intended way to read from the filter is using vvff--mmeettaaddaattaa
              (also see cclleeaarr--oonn--qquueerryy filter parameter). The property will
              return a list of key/value pairs as follows:

                 fp0.pts = 1.2345
                 fp0.hex = 1234abcdef...bcde
                 fp1.pts = 1.4567
                 fp1.hex = abcdef1234...6789
                 ...
                 fpN.pts = ...
                 fpN.hex = ...
                 type = gray-hex-16x16

              Each ffpp<<NN>> entry is for a frame. The ppttss entry specifies the
              timestamp of the frame (within the filter chain; in simple cases
              this is the same as the display timestamp). The hheexx field is the
              hex encoded fingerprint, whose size and meaning depend on the
              ttyyppee filter option.  The ttyyppee field has the same value as the
              option the filter was created with.

              This returns the frames that were filtered since the last query
              of the property. If cclleeaarr--oonn--qquueerryy==nnoo was set, a query doesn't
              reset the list of frames. In both cases, a maximum of 10 frames
              is returned. If there are more frames, the oldest frames are
              discarded. Frames are returned in filter order.

              (This doesn't return a structured list for the per-frame details
              because the internals of the vvff--mmeettaaddaattaa mechanism suck. The
              returned format may change in the future.)

              This filter uses zimg for speed and profit. However, it will
              fallback to libswscale in a number of situations: lesser pixel
              formats, unaligned data pointers or strides, or if zimg fails to
              initialize for unknown reasons. In these cases, the filter will
              use more CPU. Also, it will output different fingerprints,
              because libswscale cannot perform the full range expansion we
              normally request from zimg. As a consequence, the filter may be
              slower and not work correctly in random situations.

              ttyyppee==......
                     What fingerprint to compute. Available types are:

                     ggrraayy--hheexx--88xx88
                            grayscale, 8 bit, 8x8 size

                     ggrraayy--hheexx--1166xx1166
                            grayscale, 8 bit, 16x16 size (default)

                     Both types simply remove all colors, downscale the image,
                     concatenate all pixel values to a byte array, and convert
                     the array to a hex string.

              cclleeaarr--oonn--qquueerryy==yyeess||nnoo
                     Clear the list of frame fingerprints if the vvff--mmeettaaddaattaa
                     property for this filter is queried (default: yes). This
                     requires some care by the user. Some types of accesses
                     might query the filter multiple times, which leads to
                     lost frames.

              pprriinntt==yyeess||nnoo
                     Print computed fingerprints to the terminal (default:
                     no). This is mostly for testing and such. Scripts should
                     use vvff--mmeettaaddaattaa to read information from this filter
                     instead.

       ggppuu==......
              Convert video to RGB using the Vulkan or OpenGL renderer
              normally used with ----vvoo==ggppuu. In case of OpenGL, this requires
              that the EGL implementation supports off-screen rendering on the
              default display. (This is the case with Mesa.)

              Sub-options:

              aappii==<<ttyyppee>>
                     The value ttyyppee selects the rendering API. You can also
                     pass hheellpp to get a complete list of compiled in backends.

                     eeggll    EGL (default if available)

                     vvuullkkaann Vulkan

              ww==<<ppiixxeellss>>,, hh==<<ppiixxeellss>>
                     Size of the output in pixels (default: 0). If not
                     positive, this will use the size of the first filtered
                     input frame.

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 This is highly experimental. Performance is bad, and it will
                 not work everywhere in the first place. Some features are not
                 supported.

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 This does not do OSD rendering. If you see OSD, then it has
                 been rendered by the VO backend. (Subtitles are rendered by
                 the ggppuu filter, if possible.)

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 If you use this with encoding mode, keep in mind that
                 encoding mode will convert the RGB filter's output back to
                 yuv420p in software, using the configured software scaler.
                 Using zziimmgg might improve this, but in any case it might go
                 against your goals when using this filter.

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 Do not use this with ----vvoo==ggppuu. It will apply filtering twice,
                 since most ----vvoo==ggppuu options are unconditionally applied to
                 the ggppuu filter. There is no mechanism in mpv to prevent this.

EENNCCOODDIINNGG
       You can encode files from one format/codec to another using this
       facility.

       ----oo==<<ffiilleennaammee>>
              Enables encoding mode and specifies the output file name.

       ----ooff==<<ffoorrmmaatt>>
              Specifies the output format (overrides autodetection by the file
              name extension of the file specified by ----oo). See ----ooff==hheellpp for
              a full list of supported formats.

       ----ooffooppttss==<<ooppttiioonnss>>
              Specifies the output format options for libavformat.  See
              ----ooffooppttss==hheellpp for a full list of supported options.

              This is a key/value list option. See List Options for details.

              ----ooffooppttss--aadddd==<<ooppttiioonn>>
                     Appends the option given as an argument to the options
                     list. (Passing multiple options is currently still
                     possible, but deprecated.)

              ----ooffooppttss==""""
                     Completely empties the options list.

       ----ooaacc==<<ccooddeecc>>
              Specifies the output audio codec. See ----ooaacc==hheellpp for a full list
              of supported codecs.

       ----ooaaccooppttss==<<ooppttiioonnss>>
              Specifies the output audio codec options for libavcodec.  See
              ----ooaaccooppttss==hheellpp for a full list of supported options.

                 Example

                 ""----ooaacc==lliibbmmpp33llaammee ----ooaaccooppttss==bb==112288000000""
                        selects 128 kbps MP3 encoding.

              This is a key/value list option. See List Options for details.

              ----ooaaccooppttss--aadddd==<<ooppttiioonn>>
                     Appends the option given as an argument to the options
                     list. (Passing multiple options is currently still
                     possible, but deprecated.)

              ----ooaaccooppttss==""""
                     Completely empties the options list.

       ----oovvcc==<<ccooddeecc>>
              Specifies the output video codec. See ----oovvcc==hheellpp for a full list
              of supported codecs.

       ----oovvccooppttss==<<ooppttiioonnss>>
              Specifies the output video codec options for libavcodec.  See
              --ovcopts=help for a full list of supported options.

                 Examples

                 ""----oovvcc==mmppeegg44 ----oovvccooppttss==qqssccaallee==55""
                        selects constant quantizer scale 5 for MPEG-4
                        encoding.

                 ""----oovvcc==lliibbxx226644 ----oovvccooppttss==ccrrff==2233""
                        selects VBR quality factor 23 for H.264 encoding.

              This is a key/value list option. See List Options for details.

              ----oovvccooppttss--aadddd==<<ooppttiioonn>>
                     Appends the option given as an argument to the options
                     list. (Passing multiple options is currently still
                     possible, but deprecated.)

              ----oovvccooppttss==""""
                     Completely empties the options list.

       ----oorraawwttss
              Copies input pts to the output video (not supported by some
              output container formats, e.g. AVI). In this mode,
              discontinuities are not fixed and all pts are passed through
              as-is. Never seek backwards or use multiple input files in this
              mode!

       ----ooccooppyy--mmeettaaddaattaa==<<yyeess||nnoo>>
              Copy metadata from input files to output files when encoding
              (default: yes).

       ----oosseett--mmeettaaddaattaa==<<mmeettaaddaattaa--ttaagg[[,,mmeettaaddaattaa--ttaagg,,......]]>>
              Specifies metadata to include in the output file.  Supported
              keys vary between output formats. For example, Matroska (MKV)
              and FLAC allow almost arbitrary keys, while support in MP4 and
              MP3 is more limited.

              This is a key/value list option. See List Options for details.

                 Example

                 ""----oosseett--mmeettaaddaattaa==ttiittllee==""OOuuttppuutt ttiittllee"",,ccoommmmeenntt==""AAnnootthheerr ttaagg""""
                        adds a title and a comment to the output file.

       ----oorreemmoovvee--mmeettaaddaattaa==<<mmeettaaddaattaa--ttaagg[[,,mmeettaaddaattaa--ttaagg,,......]]>>
              Specifies metadata to exclude from the output file when copying
              from the input file.

              This is a string list option. See List Options for details.

                 Example

                 ""----oorreemmoovvee--mmeettaaddaattaa==ccoommmmeenntt,,ggeennrree""
                        excludes copying of the the comment and genre tags to
                        the output file.

CCOOMMMMAANNDD IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE
       The mpv core can be controlled with commands and properties. A number
       of ways to interact with the player use them: key bindings
       (iinnppuutt..ccoonnff), OSD (showing information with properties), JSON IPC, the
       client API (lliibbmmppvv), and the classic slave mode.

   iinnppuutt..ccoonnff
       The input.conf file consists of a list of key bindings, for example:

          s screenshot      # take a screenshot with the s key
          LEFT seek 15      # map the left-arrow key to seeking forward by 15 seconds

       Each line maps a key to an input command. Keys are specified with their
       literal value (upper case if combined with SShhiifftt), or a name for
       special keys. For example, aa maps to the aa key without shift, and AA
       maps to aa with shift.

       The file is located in the mpv configuration directory (normally at
       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//iinnppuutt..ccoonnff depending on platform). The default bindings
       are defined here:

          https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/blob/master/etc/input.conf

       A list of special keys can be obtained with
          mmppvv ----iinnppuutt--kkeeyylliisstt

       In general, keys can be combined with SShhiifftt, CCttrrll and AAlltt:

          ctrl+q quit

       mmppvv can be started in input test mode, which displays key bindings and
       the commands they're bound to on the OSD, instead of executing the
       commands:

          mpv --input-test --force-window --idle

       (Only closing the window will make mmppvv exit, pressing normal keys will
       merely display the binding, even if mapped to quit.)

       Also see Key names.

   iinnppuutt..ccoonnff ssyynnttaaxx
       [[SShhiifftt++]][[CCttrrll++]][[AAlltt++]][[MMeettaa++]]<<kkeeyy>> [[{{<<sseeccttiioonn>>}}]] <<ccoommmmaanndd>> (( ;; <<ccoommmmaanndd>>
       ))**

       Note that by default, the right Alt key can be used to create special
       characters, and thus does not register as a modifier. This can be
       changed with ----iinnppuutt--rriigghhtt--aalltt--ggrr option.

       Newlines always start a new binding. ## starts a comment (outside of
       quoted string arguments). To bind commands to the ## key, SSHHAARRPP can be
       used.

       <<kkeeyy>> is either the literal character the key produces (ASCII or
       Unicode character), or a symbolic name (as printed by ----iinnppuutt--kkeeyylliisstt).

       <<sseeccttiioonn>> (braced with {{ and }}) is the input section for this command.

       <<ccoommmmaanndd>> is the command itself. It consists of the command name and
       multiple (or none) arguments, all separated by whitespace. String
       arguments should be quoted, typically with "". See FFllaatt ccoommmmaanndd ssyynnttaaxx.

       You can bind multiple commands to one key. For example:
       a show-text "command 1" ; show-text "command 2"


       It's also possible to bind a command to a sequence of keys:
       a-b-c show-text "command run after a, b, c have been pressed"


       (This is not shown in the general command syntax.)

       If aa or aa--bb or bb are already bound, this will run the first command
       that matches, and the multi-key command will never be called.
       Intermediate keys can be remapped to iiggnnoorree in order to avoid this
       issue. The maximum number of (non-modifier) keys for combinations is
       currently 4.

   KKeeyy nnaammeess
       All mouse and keyboard input is to converted to mpv-specific key names.
       Key names are either special symbolic identifiers representing a
       physical key, or text key names, which are Unicode code points encoded
       as UTF-8. These are what keyboard input would normally produce, for
       example aa for the A key.  These are influenced by keyboard modifiers
       which affect produced text, such as shift and caps lock. As a
       consequence, mpv uses input translated by the current OS keyboard
       layout, rather than physical scan codes.

       Currently there is the hardcoded assumption that every text key can be
       represented as a single Unicode code point (in NFKC form).

       All key names can be combined with the modifiers SShhiifftt, CCttrrll, AAlltt,
       MMeettaa. They must be prefixed to the actual key name, where each modifier
       is followed by a ++ (for example ccttrrll++qq).

       NNOOTTEE::
          The SShhiifftt modifier requires some attention. In general, when the
          SShhiifftt modifier is combined with a key which produces text, the
          actual produced text key name when shift is pressed should be used.

          For instance, on the US keyboard layout, SShhiifftt++22 should usually be
          specified as key-name @@ at iinnppuutt..ccoonnff, and similarly the combination
          AAlltt++SShhiifftt++22 is usually AAlltt++@@, etc.

          In general, the SShhiifftt modifier, when specified with text key names,
          is ignored: for instance, mpv interprets SShhiifftt++22 as 22.  The only
          exceptions are ASCII letters, which are normalized by mpv.  For
          example, SShhiifftt++aa is interpreted as AA.

          Special key names like SShhiifftt++LLEEFFTT work as expected.  If in doubt -
          use ----iinnppuutt--tteesstt to check how a key/combination is seen by mpv.

       Symbolic key names and modifier names are case-insensitive. Unicode key
       names are case-sensitive just like how keyboard text input would
       produce.

       Another type of key names are hexadecimal key names, which start with
       00xx, followed by the hexadecimal value of the key. The hexadecimal value
       can be either a Unicode code point value, or can serve as fallback for
       special keys that do not have a special mpv defined name. They will
       break as soon as mpv adds proper names for them, but can enable you to
       use a key at all if that does not happen.

       All symbolic names are listed by ----iinnppuutt--kkeeyylliisstt. ----iinnppuutt--tteesstt is a
       special mode that prints all input on the OSD.

       Comments on some symbolic names:

       KKPP**    Keypad names. Behavior varies by backend (whether they implement
              this, and on how they treat numlock), but typically, mpv tries
              to map keys on the keypad to separate names, even if they
              produce the same text as normal keys.

       MMOOUUSSEE__BBTTNN**,, MMBBTTNN**
              Various mouse buttons.

              Depending on backend, the mouse wheel might also be represented
              as a button.  In addition, MMOOUUSSEE__BBTTNN33 to MMOOUUSSEE__BBTTNN66 are
              deprecated aliases for WWHHEEEELL__UUPP, WWHHEEEELL__DDOOWWNN, WWHHEEEELL__LLEEFFTT,
              WWHHEEEELL__RRIIGGHHTT.

              MMBBTTNN** are aliases for MMOOUUSSEE__BBTTNN**.

       WWHHEEEELL__**
              Mouse wheels and touch pads (typically).

              These key are scalable when used with scalable commands if the
              underlying device supports high-resolution scrolling (e.g. touch
              pads).

       AAXXIISS__** Deprecated aliases for WWHHEEEELL__**.

       **__DDBBLL  Mouse button double clicks.

       MMOOUUSSEE__MMOOVVEE,, MMOOUUSSEE__EENNTTEERR,, MMOOUUSSEE__LLEEAAVVEE
              Emitted by mouse move events. Enter/leave happens when the mouse
              enters or leave the mpv window (or the current mouse region,
              using the deprecated mouse region input section mechanism).

       CCLLOOSSEE__WWIINN
              Pseudo key emitted when closing the mpv window using the OS
              window manager (for example, by clicking the close button in the
              window title bar).

       GGAAMMEEPPAADD__**
              Keys emitted by the SDL gamepad backend.

       UUNNMMAAPPPPEEDD
              Pseudo-key that matches any unmapped key. (You should probably
              avoid this if possible, because it might change behavior or get
              removed in the future.)

       AANNYY__UUNNIICCOODDEE
              Pseudo-key that matches any key that produces text. (You should
              probably avoid this if possible, because it might change
              behavior or get removed in the future.)

   FFllaatt ccoommmmaanndd ssyynnttaaxx
       This is the syntax used in input.conf, and referred to "input.conf
       syntax" in a number of other places.

       <<ccoommmmaanndd>>  ::::== [[<<pprreeffiixxeess>>]] <<ccoommmmaanndd__nnaammee>> ((<<aarrgguummeenntt>>))**
       <<aarrgguummeenntt>> ::::== ((<<uunnqquuootteedd>> || "" <<ddoouubbllee__qquuootteedd>> "" || '' <<ssiinnggllee__qquuootteedd>> '' || ``XX <<ccuussttoomm__qquuootteedd>> XX``))


       ccoommmmaanndd__nnaammee is an unquoted string with the command name itself. See
       List of Input Commands for a list.

       Arguments are separated by whitespaces even if the command expects only
       one argument. Arguments with whitespaces or other special characters
       must be quoted, or the command cannot be parsed correctly.

       Double quotes interpret JSON/C-style escaping, like \\tt or \\"" or \\\\.
       JSON escapes according to RFC 8259, minus surrogate pair escapes. This
       is the only form which allows newlines at the value - as \\nn.

       Single quotes take the content literally, and cannot include the
       single-quote character at the value.

       Custom quotes also take the content literally, but are more flexible
       than single quotes. They start with `` (back-quote) followed by any
       ASCII character, and end at the first occurrence of the same pair in
       reverse order, e.g.  ``--ffoooo--`` or ````bbaarr````. The final pair sequence is not
       allowed at the value - in these examples --`` and ```` respectively. In the
       second example the last character of the value also can't be a
       back-quote.

       Mixed quoting at the same argument, like ''ffoooo''""bbaarr"", is not supported.

       Note that argument parsing and property expansion happen at different
       stages.  First, arguments are determined as described above, and then,
       where applicable, properties are expanded - regardless of argument
       quoting. However, expansion can still be prevented with the rraaww prefix
       or $$>>. See Input Command Prefixes and Property Expansion.

   CCoommmmaannddss ssppeecciiffiieedd aass aarrrraayyss
       This applies to certain APIs, such as mmpp..ccoommmmaannddvv(()) or
       mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee(()) (with array parameters) in Lua scripting, or
       mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd(()) or mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd__nnooddee(()) (with MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY) in the
       C libmpv client API.

       The command as well as all arguments are passed as a single array.
       Similar to the Flat command syntax, you can first pass prefixes as
       strings (each as separate array item), then the command name as string,
       and then each argument as string or a native value.

       Since these APIs pass arguments as separate strings or native values,
       they do not expect quotes, and do support escaping. Technically, there
       is the input.conf parser, which first splits the command string into
       arguments, and then invokes argument parsers for each argument. The
       input.conf parser normally handles quotes and escaping. The array
       command APIs mentioned above pass strings directly to the argument
       parsers, or can sidestep them by the ability to pass non-string values.

       Property expansion is disabled by default for these APIs. This can be
       changed with the eexxppaanndd--pprrooppeerrttiieess prefix. See Input Command Prefixes.

       Sometimes commands have string arguments, that in turn are actually
       parsed by other components (e.g. filter strings with vvff aadddd) - in these
       cases, you you would have to double-escape in input.conf, but not with
       the array APIs.

       For complex commands, consider using Named arguments instead, which
       should give slightly more compatibility. Some commands do not support
       named arguments and inherently take an array, though.

   NNaammeedd aarrgguummeennttss
       This applies to certain APIs, such as mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee(()) (with tables
       that have string keys) in Lua scripting, or mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd__nnooddee(()) (with
       MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP) in the C libmpv client API.

       The name of the command is provided with a nnaammee string field. The name
       of each command is defined in each command description in the List of
       Input Commands. ----iinnppuutt--ccmmddlliisstt also lists them. See the ssuubbpprroocceessss
       command for an example.

       Some commands do not support named arguments (e.g. rruunn command). You
       need to use APIs that pass arguments as arrays.

       Named arguments are not supported in the "flat" input.conf syntax,
       which means you cannot use them for key bindings in input.conf at all.

       Property expansion is disabled by default for these APIs. This can be
       changed with the eexxppaanndd--pprrooppeerrttiieess prefix. See Input Command Prefixes.

   LLiisstt ooff IInnppuutt CCoommmmaannddss
       Commands with parameters have the parameter name enclosed in << / >>.
       Don't add those to the actual command. Optional arguments are enclosed
       in [[ / ]]. If you don't pass them, they will be set to a default value.

       Remember to quote string arguments in input.conf (see Flat command
       syntax).

   PPllaayybbaacckk CCoonnttrrooll

       sseeeekk <<ttaarrggeett>> [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Change the playback position. By default, seeks by a relative
              amount of seconds.

              The second argument consists of flags controlling the seek mode:

              rreellaattiivvee ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     Seek relative to current position (a negative value seeks
                     backwards).

              aabbssoolluuttee
                     Seek to a given time (a negative value starts from the
                     end of the file).

              aabbssoolluuttee--ppeerrcceenntt
                     Seek to a given percent position.

              rreellaattiivvee--ppeerrcceenntt
                     Seek relative to current position in percent.

              kkeeyyffrraammeess
                     Always restart playback at keyframe boundaries (fast).

              eexxaacctt  Always do exact/hr/precise seeks (slow).

              Multiple flags can be combined, e.g.: aabbssoolluuttee++kkeeyyffrraammeess.

              By default, kkeeyyffrraammeess is used for rreellaattiivvee, rreellaattiivvee--ppeerrcceenntt,
              and aabbssoolluuttee--ppeerrcceenntt seeks, while eexxaacctt is used for aabbssoolluuttee
              seeks.

              Before mpv 0.9, the kkeeyyffrraammeess and eexxaacctt flags had to be passed
              as 3rd parameter (essentially using a space instead of ++). The
              3rd parameter is still parsed, but is considered deprecated.

              This is a scalable command. See the documentation of nnoonnssccaallaabbllee
              input command prefix in Input Command Prefixes for details.

       rreevveerrtt--sseeeekk [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Undoes the sseeeekk command, and some other commands that seek (but
              not necessarily all of them). Calling this command once will
              jump to the playback position before the seek. Calling it a
              second time undoes the rreevveerrtt--sseeeekk command itself. This only
              works within a single file.

              The first argument is optional, and can change the behavior:

              mmaarrkk   Mark the current time position. The next normal
                     rreevveerrtt--sseeeekk command will seek back to this point, no
                     matter how many seeks happened since last time.

              mmaarrkk--ppeerrmmaanneenntt
                     If set, mark the current position, and do not change the
                     mark position before the next rreevveerrtt--sseeeekk command that
                     has mmaarrkk or mmaarrkk--ppeerrmmaanneenntt set (or playback of the
                     current file ends). Until this happens, rreevveerrtt--sseeeekk will
                     always seek to the marked point. This flag cannot be
                     combined with mmaarrkk.

              Using it without any arguments gives you the default behavior.

       ssuubb--sseeeekk <<sskkiipp>> [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Change video and audio position such that the subtitle event
              after <<sskkiipp>> subtitle events is displayed. For example, ssuubb--sseeeekk
              11 skips to the next subtitle, ssuubb--sseeeekk --11 skips to the previous
              subtitles, and ssuubb--sseeeekk 00 seeks to the beginning of the current
              subtitle.

              This is similar to ssuubb--sstteepp, except that it seeks video and
              audio instead of adjusting the subtitle delay.

              Secondary argument:

              pprriimmaarryy ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     Seeks through the primary subtitles.

              sseeccoonnddaarryy
                     Seeks through the secondary subtitles.

              For embedded subtitles (like with Matroska), this works only
              with subtitle events that have already been displayed, or are
              within a short prefetch range. See Cache for details on how to
              control the available prefetch range.

       ffrraammee--sstteepp [[<<ffrraammeess>>]] [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Go forward or backwards by a given amount of frames. If <<ffrraammeess>>
              is omitted, the value is assumed to be 11.

              The second argument consists of flags controlling the frameskip
              mode:

              ppllaayy ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     Play the video forward by the desired amount of frames
                     and then pause.  This only works with a positive value
                     (i.e. frame stepping forwards).

              sseeeekk   Perform a very exact seek that attempts to seek by the
                     desired amount of frames. If <<ffrraammeess>> is --11, this will go
                     exactly to the previous frame.

              mmuuttee   The same as ppllaayy but mutes the audio stream if there is
                     any during the duration of the frame step.

              Note that the default frameskip mode, play, is more accurate but
              can be slow depending on how many frames you are skipping (i.e.
              skipping forward 100 frames will play 100 frames of video before
              stopping). This mode only works when going forwards. Frame
              stepping back always performs a seek.

              When using seek mode, this can still be very slow (it tries to
              be precise, not fast), and sometimes fails to behave as
              expected. How well this works depends on whether precise seeking
              works correctly (e.g. see the ----hhrr--sseeeekk--ddeemmuuxxeerr--ooffffsseett option).
              Video filters or other video post-processing that modifies
              timing of frames (e.g. deinterlacing) should usually work, but
              might make framestepping silently behave incorrectly in corner
              cases. Using ----hhrr--sseeeekk--ffrraammeeddrroopp==nnoo should help, although it
              might make precise seeking slower. Also if the video is VFR,
              framestepping using seeks will probably not work correctly
              except for the --11 case.

              This does not work with audio-only playback.

       ffrraammee--bbaacckk--sstteepp
              Calls ffrraammee--sstteepp with a value of --11 and the sseeeekk flag.

              This does not work with audio-only playback.

       ssttoopp [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Stop playback and clear playlist. With default settings, this is
              essentially like qquuiitt. Useful for the client API: playback can
              be stopped without terminating the player.

              The first argument is optional, and supports the following
              flags:

              kkeeeepp--ppllaayylliisstt
                     Do not clear the playlist.

   PPrrooppeerrttyy MMaanniippuullaattiioonn

       sseett <<nnaammee>> <<vvaalluuee>>
              Set the given property or option to the given value.

       ddeell <<nnaammee>>
              Delete the given property. Most properties cannot be deleted.

       aadddd <<nnaammee>> [[<<vvaalluuee>>]]
              Add the given value to the property or option. On overflow or
              underflow, clamp the property to the maximum. If <<vvaalluuee>> is
              omitted, assume 11.

              Whether or not key-repeat is enabled by default depends on the
              property.  Currently properties with continuous values are
              repeatable by default (like vvoolluummee), while discrete values are
              not (like oossdd--lleevveell).

              This is a scalable command. See the documentation of nnoonnssccaallaabbllee
              input command prefix in Input Command Prefixes for details.

       mmuullttiippllyy <<nnaammee>> <<vvaalluuee>>
              Similar to aadddd, but multiplies the property or option with the
              numeric value.

       ccyyccllee <<nnaammee>> [[<<vvaalluuee>>]]
              Cycle the given property or option. The second argument can be
              uupp or ddoowwnn to set the cycle direction. On overflow, set the
              property back to the minimum, on underflow set it to the
              maximum. If uupp or ddoowwnn is omitted, assume uupp.

              Whether or not key-repeat is enabled by default depends on the
              property.  Currently properties with continuous values are
              repeatable by default (like vvoolluummee), while discrete values are
              not (like oossdd--lleevveell).

              This is a scalable command. See the documentation of nnoonnssccaallaabbllee
              input command prefix in Input Command Prefixes for details.

       ccyyccllee--vvaalluueess [[<<""!!rreevveerrssee"">>]] <<pprrooppeerrttyy>> <<vvaalluuee11>> [[<<vvaalluuee22>> [[......]]]]
              Cycle through a list of values. Each invocation of the command
              will set the given property to the next value in the list. The
              command will use the current value of the property/option, and
              use it to determine the current position in the list of values.
              Once it has found it, it will set the next value in the list
              (wrapping around to the first item if needed).

              This command has a variable number of arguments, and cannot be
              used with named arguments.

              The special argument !!rreevveerrssee can be used to cycle the value
              list in reverse. The only advantage is that you don't need to
              reverse the value list yourself when adding a second key binding
              for cycling backwards.

       cchhaannggee--lliisstt <<nnaammee>> <<ooppeerraattiioonn>> <<vvaalluuee>>
              This command changes list options as described in List Options.
              The <<nnaammee>> parameter is the normal option name, while
              <<ooppeerraattiioonn>> is the suffix or action used on the option.

              Some operations take no value, but the command still requires
              the value parameter. In these cases, the value must be an empty
              string.

                 Example

                        cchhaannggee--lliisstt ggllssll--sshhaaddeerrss aappppeenndd ffiillee..ggllssll

                        Add a filename to the ggllssll--sshhaaddeerrss list. The command
                        line equivalent is ----ggllssll--sshhaaddeerrss--aappppeenndd==ffiillee..ggllssll or
                        alternatively ----ggllssll--sshhaaddeerr==ffiillee..ggllssll.

   PPllaayylliisstt MMaanniippuullaattiioonn

       ppllaayylliisstt--nneexxtt [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Go to the next entry on the playlist.

              First argument:

              wweeaakk ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     If the last file on the playlist is currently played, do
                     nothing.

              ffoorrccee  Terminate playback if there are no more files on the
                     playlist.

       ppllaayylliisstt--pprreevv [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Go to the previous entry on the playlist.

              First argument:

              wweeaakk ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     If the first file on the playlist is currently played, do
                     nothing.

              ffoorrccee  Terminate playback if the first file is being played.

       ppllaayylliisstt--nneexxtt--ppllaayylliisstt
              Go to the next entry on the playlist with a different
              ppllaayylliisstt--ppaatthh.

       ppllaayylliisstt--pprreevv--ppllaayylliisstt
              Go to the first of the previous entries on the playlist with a
              different ppllaayylliisstt--ppaatthh.

       ppllaayylliisstt--ppllaayy--iinnddeexx <<iinntteeggeerr||ccuurrrreenntt||nnoonnee>>
              Start (or restart) playback of the given playlist index. In
              addition to the 0-based playlist entry index, it supports the
              following values:

              <<ccuurrrreenntt>>
                     The current playlist entry (as in ppllaayylliisstt--ccuurrrreenntt--ppooss)
                     will be played again (unload and reload). If none is set,
                     playback is stopped.  (In corner cases,
                     ppllaayylliisstt--ccuurrrreenntt--ppooss can point to a playlist entry even
                     if playback is currently inactive,

              <<nnoonnee>> Playback is stopped. If idle mode (----iiddllee) is enabled,
                     the player will enter idle mode, otherwise it will exit.

              This command is similar to llooaaddffiillee in that it only manipulates
              the state of what to play next, without waiting until the
              current file is unloaded, and the next one is loaded.

              Setting ppllaayylliisstt--ppooss or similar properties can have a similar
              effect to this command. However, it's more explicit, and
              guarantees that playback is restarted if for example the new
              playlist entry is the same as the previous one.

       llooaaddffiillee <<uurrll>> [[<<ffllaaggss>> [[<<iinnddeexx>> [[<<ooppttiioonnss>>]]]]]]
              Load the given file or URL and play it. Technically, this is
              just a playlist manipulation command (which either replaces the
              playlist or adds an entry to it). Actual file loading happens
              independently. For example, a llooaaddffiillee command that replaces the
              current file with a new one returns before the current file is
              stopped, and the new file even begins loading.

              Second argument:

              <<rreeppllaaccee>> ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     Stop playback of the current file, and play the new file
                     immediately.

              <<aappppeenndd>>
                     Append the file to the playlist.

              <<aappppeenndd--ppllaayy>>
                     Append the file, and if nothing is currently playing,
                     start playback.  (Always starts with the added file, even
                     if the playlist was not empty before running this
                     command.)

              <<iinnsseerrtt--nneexxtt>>
                     Insert the file into the playlist, directly after the
                     current entry.

              <<iinnsseerrtt--nneexxtt--ppllaayy>>
                     Insert the file next, and if nothing is currently
                     playing, start playback.  (Always starts with the added
                     file, even if the playlist was not empty before running
                     this command.)

              <<iinnsseerrtt--aatt>>
                     Insert the file into the playlist, at the index given in
                     the third argument.

              <<iinnsseerrtt--aatt--ppllaayy>>
                     Insert the file at the index given in the third argument,
                     and if nothing is currently playing, start playback.
                     (Always starts with the added file, even if the playlist
                     was not empty before running this command.)

              The third argument is an insertion index, used only by the
              iinnsseerrtt--aatt and iinnsseerrtt--aatt--ppllaayy actions. When used with those
              actions, the new item will be inserted at the index position in
              the playlist, or appended to the end if index is less than 0 or
              greater than the size of the playlist. This argument will be
              ignored for all other actions. This argument is added in mpv
              0.38.0.

              The fourth argument is a list of options and values which should
              be set while the file is playing. It is of the form
              oopptt11==vvaalluuee11,,oopptt22==vvaalluuee22,,.....  When using the client API, this can
              be a MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE__MMAAPP (or a Lua table), however the values
              themselves must be strings currently. These options are set
              during playback, and restored to the previous value at end of
              playback (see Per-File Options).

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 Since mpv 0.38.0, an insertion index argument is added as the
                 third argument.  This breaks all existing uses of this
                 command which make use of the argument to include the list of
                 options to be set while the file is playing. To address this
                 problem, the third argument now needs to be set to -1 if the
                 fourth argument needs to be used.

       llooaaddlliisstt <<uurrll>> [[<<ffllaaggss>> [[<<iinnddeexx>>]]]]
              Load the given playlist file or URL (like ----ppllaayylliisstt).

              Second argument:

              <<rreeppllaaccee>> ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     Stop playback and replace the internal playlist with the
                     new one.

              <<aappppeenndd>>
                     Append the new playlist at the end of the current
                     internal playlist.

              <<aappppeenndd--ppllaayy>>
                     Append the new playlist, and if nothing is currently
                     playing, start playback. (Always starts with the new
                     playlist, even if the internal playlist was not empty
                     before running this command.)

              <<iinnsseerrtt--nneexxtt>>
                     Insert the new playlist into the current internal
                     playlist, directly after the current entry.

              <<iinnsseerrtt--nneexxtt--ppllaayy>>
                     Insert the new playlist, and if nothing is currently
                     playing, start playback. (Always starts with the new
                     playlist, even if the internal playlist was not empty
                     before running this command.)

              <<iinnsseerrtt--aatt>>
                     Insert the new playlist at the index given in the third
                     argument.

              <<iinnsseerrtt--aatt--ppllaayy>>
                     Insert the new playlist at the index given in the third
                     argument, and if nothing is currently playing, start
                     playback. (Always starts with the new playlist, even if
                     the internal playlist was not empty before running this
                     command.)

              The third argument is an insertion index, used only by the
              iinnsseerrtt--aatt and iinnsseerrtt--aatt--ppllaayy actions. When used with those
              actions, the new playlist will be inserted at the index position
              in the internal playlist, or appended to the end if index is
              less than 0 or greater than the size of the internal playlist.
              This argument will be ignored for all other actions.

       ppllaayylliisstt--cclleeaarr
              Clear the playlist, except the currently played file.

       ppllaayylliisstt--rreemmoovvee <<iinnddeexx>>
              Remove the playlist entry at the given index. Index values start
              counting with 0. The special value ccuurrrreenntt removes the current
              entry. Note that removing the current entry also stops playback
              and starts playing the next entry.

       ppllaayylliisstt--mmoovvee <<iinnddeexx11>> <<iinnddeexx22>>
              Move the playlist entry at index1, so that it takes the place of
              the entry index2. (Paradoxically, the moved playlist entry will
              not have the index value index2 after moving if index1 was lower
              than index2, because index2 refers to the target entry, not the
              index the entry will have after moving.)

       ppllaayylliisstt--sshhuuffffllee
              Shuffle the playlist. This is similar to what is done on start
              if the ----sshhuuffffllee option is used.

       ppllaayylliisstt--uunnsshhuuffffllee
              Attempt to revert the previous ppllaayylliisstt--sshhuuffffllee command. This
              works only once (multiple successive ppllaayylliisstt--uunnsshhuuffffllee commands
              do nothing).  May not work correctly if new recursive playlists
              have been opened since a ppllaayylliisstt--sshhuuffffllee command.

   TTrraacckk MMaanniippuullaattiioonn

       ssuubb--aadddd <<uurrll>> [[<<ffllaaggss>> [[<<ttiittllee>> [[<<llaanngg>>]]]]]]
              Load the given subtitle file or stream. By default, it is
              selected as current subtitle  after loading.

              The ffllaaggss argument is one of the following values:

              <select>
                 Select the subtitle immediately (default).

              <auto>
                 Don't select the subtitle. (Or in some special situations,
                 let the default stream selection mechanism decide.)

              <cached>
                 Select the subtitle. If a subtitle with the same filename was
                 already added, that one is selected, instead of loading a
                 duplicate entry.  (In this case, title/language are ignored,
                 and if the was changed since it was loaded, these changes
                 won't be reflected.)

              Additionally the following flags can be added with a ++:

              <hearing-impaired>
                 Marks the track as suitable for the hearing impaired.

              <visual-impaired>
                 Marks the track as suitable for the visually impaired.

              <attached-picture> (only for vviiddeeoo--aadddd)
                 Marks the track as an attached picture, same as aallbbuummaarrtt
                 argument for ``vviiddeeoo--aadddd.

              The ttiittllee argument sets the track title in the UI.

              The llaanngg argument sets the track language, and can also
              influence stream selection with ffllaaggss set to aauuttoo.

       ssuubb--rreemmoovvee [[<<iidd>>]]
              Remove the given subtitle track. If the iidd argument is missing,
              remove the current track. (Works on external subtitle files
              only.)

       ssuubb--rreellooaadd [[<<iidd>>]]
              Reload the given subtitle tracks. If the iidd argument is missing,
              reload the current track. (Works on external subtitle files
              only.)

              This works by unloading and re-adding the subtitle track.

       ssuubb--sstteepp <<sskkiipp>> [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Change subtitle timing such, that the subtitle event after the
              next <<sskkiipp>> subtitle events is displayed. <<sskkiipp>> can be negative
              to step backwards.

              Secondary argument:

              pprriimmaarryy ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     Steps through the primary subtitles.

              sseeccoonnddaarryy
                     Steps through the secondary subtitles.

       aauuddiioo--aadddd <<uurrll>> [[<<ffllaaggss>> [[<<ttiittllee>> [[<<llaanngg>>]]]]]]
              Load the given audio file. See ssuubb--aadddd command.

       aauuddiioo--rreemmoovvee [[<<iidd>>]]
              Remove the given audio track. See ssuubb--rreemmoovvee command.

       aauuddiioo--rreellooaadd [[<<iidd>>]]
              Reload the given audio tracks. See ssuubb--rreellooaadd command.

       vviiddeeoo--aadddd <<uurrll>> [[<<ffllaaggss>> [[<<ttiittllee>> [[<<llaanngg>> [[<<aallbbuummaarrtt>>]]]]]]]]
              Load the given video file. See ssuubb--aadddd command for common
              options.

              aallbbuummaarrtt ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__FFLLAAGG))
                     If enabled, mpv will load the given video as album art.

       vviiddeeoo--rreemmoovvee [[<<iidd>>]]
              Remove the given video track. See ssuubb--rreemmoovvee command.

       vviiddeeoo--rreellooaadd [[<<iidd>>]]
              Reload the given video tracks. See ssuubb--rreellooaadd command.

       rreessccaann--eexxtteerrnnaall--ffiilleess [[<<mmooddee>>]]
              Rescan external files according to the current ----ssuubb--aauuttoo,
              ----aauuddiioo--ffiillee--aauuttoo and ----ccoovveerr--aarrtt--aauuttoo settings. This can be
              used to auto-load external files _a_f_t_e_r the file was loaded.

              The mmooddee argument is one of the following:

              <<rreesseelleecctt>> ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     Select the default audio and subtitle streams, which
                     typically selects external files with the highest
                     preference. (The implementation is not perfect, and could
                     be improved on request.)

              <<kkeeeepp--sseelleeccttiioonn>>
                     Do not change current track selections.

   TTeexxtt MMaanniippuullaattiioonn

       pprriinntt--tteexxtt <<tteexxtt>>
              Print text to stdout. The string can contain properties (see
              Property Expansion). Take care to put the argument in quotes.

       eexxppaanndd--tteexxtt <<tteexxtt>>
              Property-expand the argument and return the expanded string.
              This can be used only through the client API or from a script
              using mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee. (see Property Expansion).

       eexxppaanndd--ppaatthh <<tteexxtt>>
              Expand a path's double-tilde placeholders into a
              platform-specific path.  As eexxppaanndd--tteexxtt, this can only be used
              through the client API or from a script using mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee.

                 Example

                        mmpp..oossdd__mmeessssaaggee((mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee(({{""eexxppaanndd--ppaatthh"",,
                        ""~~~~hhoommee//""}}))))

                        This line of Lua would show the location of the user's
                        mpv configuration directory on the OSD.

       nnoorrmmaalliizzee--ppaatthh <<ffiilleennaammee>>
              Return a canonical representation of the path ffiilleennaammee by
              converting it to an absolute path, removing consecutive slashes,
              removing .. components, resolving .... components, and converting
              slashes to backslashes on Windows. Symlinks are not resolved
              unless the platform is Unix-like and one of the path components
              is ..... If ffiilleennaammee is a URL, it is returned unchanged. This can
              only be used through the client API or from a script using
              mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee.

                 Example

                        mmpp..oossdd__mmeessssaaggee((mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee(({{""nnoorrmmaalliizzee--ppaatthh"",,
                        ""//ffoooo////..//bbaarr""}}))))

                        This line of Lua prints "/foo/bar" on the OSD.

       eessccaappee--aassss <<tteexxtt>>
              Modify tteexxtt so that commands and functions that interpret ASS
              tags, such as oossdd--oovveerrllaayy and mmpp..ccrreeaattee__oossdd__oovveerrllaayy, will
              display it verbatim, and return it. This can only be used
              through the client API or from a script using mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee.

                 Example

                        mmpp..oossdd__mmeessssaaggee((mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee(({{""eessccaappee--aassss"",, ""ffoooo
                        {{bbaarr}}""}}))))

                        This line of Lua prints "foo \{bar}" on the OSD.

   CCoonnffiigguurraattiioonn CCoommmmaannddss

       aappppllyy--pprrooffiillee <<nnaammee>> [[<<mmooddee>>]]
              Apply the contents of a named profile. This is like using
              pprrooffiillee==nnaammee in a config file, except you can map it to a key
              binding to change it at runtime.

              The mode argument:

              aappppllyy  Apply the profile. Default if the argument is omitted.

              rreessttoorree
                     Restore options set by a previous aappppllyy--pprrooffiillee command
                     for this profile. Only works if the profile has
                     pprrooffiillee--rreessttoorree set to a relevant mode. Prints a warning
                     if nothing could be done. See Runtime profiles for
                     details.

       llooaadd--ccoonnffiigg--ffiillee <<ffiilleennaammee>>
              Load a configuration file, similar to the ----iinncclluuddee option. If
              the file was already included, its previous options are not
              reset before it is reparsed.

       wwrriittee--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ccoonnffiigg
              Write the resume config file that the qquuiitt--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr command
              writes, but continue playback normally.

       ddeelleettee--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ccoonnffiigg [[<<ffiilleennaammee>>]]
              Delete any existing resume config file that was written by
              qquuiitt--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr or wwrriittee--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ccoonnffiigg. If a filename is
              specified, then the deleted config is for that file; otherwise,
              it is the same one as would be written by qquuiitt--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr or
              wwrriittee--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ccoonnffiigg in the current circumstance.

   OOSSDD CCoommmmaannddss

       sshhooww--tteexxtt <<tteexxtt>> [[<<dduurraattiioonn>>||--11 [[<<lleevveell>>]]]]
              Show text on the OSD. The string can contain properties, which
              are expanded as described in Property Expansion. This can be
              used to show playback time, filename, and so on. nnoo--oossdd has no
              effect on this command.

              <<dduurraattiioonn>>
                     The time in ms to show the message for. By default, it
                     uses the same value as ----oossdd--dduurraattiioonn.

              <<lleevveell>>
                     The minimum OSD level to show the text at (see
                     ----oossdd--lleevveell).

       sshhooww--pprrooggrreessss
              Show the progress bar, the elapsed time and the total duration
              of the file on the OSD. nnoo--oossdd has no effect on this command.

       oovveerrllaayy--aadddd <<iidd>> <<xx>> <<yy>> <<ffiillee>> <<ooffffsseett>> <<ffmmtt>> <<ww>> <<hh>> <<ssttrriiddee>> <<ddww>>
       <<ddhh>>   Add an OSD overlay sourced from raw data. This might be useful
              for scripts and applications controlling mpv, and which want to
              display things on top of the video window.

              Overlays are usually displayed in screen resolution, but with
              some VOs, the resolution is reduced to that of the video's. You
              can read the oossdd--wwiiddtthh and oossdd--hheeiigghhtt properties. At least with
              ----vvoo--xxvv and anamorphic video (such as DVD), oossdd--ppaarr should be
              read as well, and the overlay should be aspect-compensated.

              This has the following named arguments. The order of them is not
              guaranteed, so you should always call them with named arguments,
              see Named arguments.

              iidd is an integer between 0 and 63 identifying the overlay
              element. The ID can be used to add multiple overlay parts,
              update a part by using this command with an already existing ID,
              or to remove a part with oovveerrllaayy--rreemmoovvee. Using a previously
              unused ID will add a new overlay, while reusing an ID will
              update it.

              xx and yy specify the position where the OSD should be displayed.

              ffiillee specifies the file the raw image data is read from. It can
              be either a numeric UNIX file descriptor prefixed with @@ (e.g.
              @@44), or a filename. The file will be mapped into memory with
              mmmmaapp(()), copied, and unmapped before the command returns (changed
              in mpv 0.18.1).

              It is also possible to pass a raw memory address for use as
              bitmap memory by passing a memory address as integer prefixed
              with an && character.  Passing the wrong thing here will crash
              the player. This mode might be useful for use with libmpv. The
              ooffffsseett parameter is simply added to the memory address (since
              mpv 0.8.0, ignored before).

              ooffffsseett is the byte offset of the first pixel in the source file.
              (The current implementation always mmap's the whole file from
              position 0 to the end of the image, so large offsets should be
              avoided. Before mpv 0.8.0, the offset was actually passed
              directly to mmmmaapp, but it was changed to make using it easier.)

              ffmmtt is a string identifying the image format. Currently, only
              bbggrraa is defined. This format has 4 bytes per pixels, with 8 bits
              per component.  The least significant 8 bits are blue, and the
              most significant 8 bits are alpha (in little endian, the
              components are B-G-R-A, with B as first byte). This uses
              premultiplied alpha: every color component is already multiplied
              with the alpha component. This means the numeric value of each
              component is equal to or smaller than the alpha component.
              (Violating this rule will lead to different results with
              different VOs: numeric overflows resulting from blending broken
              alpha values is considered something that shouldn't happen, and
              consequently implementations don't ensure that you get
              predictable behavior in this case.)

              ww, hh, and ssttrriiddee specify the size of the overlay. ww is the
              visible width of the overlay, while ssttrriiddee gives the width in
              bytes in memory. In the simple case, and with the bbggrraa format,
              ssttrriiddee====44**ww.  In general, the total amount of memory accessed is
              ssttrriiddee ** hh.  (Technically, the minimum size would be ssttrriiddee ** ((hh
              -- 11)) ++ ww ** 44, but for simplicity, the player will access all
              ssttrriiddee ** hh bytes.)

              ddww and ddhh specify the (optional) display size of the overlay.
              The overlay visible portion of the overlay (ww and hh) is scaled
              to in display to ddww and ddhh.  If parameters are not present, the
              values for ww and hh are used.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 Before mpv 0.18.1, you had to do manual "double buffering"
                 when updating an overlay by replacing it with a different
                 memory buffer. Since mpv 0.18.1, the memory is simply copied
                 and doesn't reference any of the memory indicated by the
                 command's arguments after the command returns.  If you want
                 to use this command before mpv 0.18.1, reads the old docs to
                 see how to handle this correctly.

       oovveerrllaayy--rreemmoovvee <<iidd>>
              Remove an overlay added with oovveerrllaayy--aadddd and the same ID. Does
              nothing if no overlay with this ID exists.

       oossdd--oovveerrllaayy
              Add/update/remove an OSD overlay.

              (Although this sounds similar to oovveerrllaayy--aadddd, oossdd--oovveerrllaayy is for
              text overlays, while oovveerrllaayy--aadddd is for bitmaps. Maybe
              oovveerrllaayy--aadddd will be merged into oossdd--oovveerrllaayy to remove this
              oddity.)

              You can use this to add text overlays in ASS format. ASS has
              advanced positioning and rendering tags, which can be used to
              render almost any kind of vector graphics.

              This command accepts the following parameters:

              iidd     Arbitrary integer that identifies the overlay. Multiple
                     overlays can be added by calling this command with
                     different iidd parameters. Calling this command with the
                     same iidd replaces the previously set overlay.

                     There is a separate namespace for each libmpv client
                     (i.e. IPC connection, script), so IDs can be made up and
                     assigned by the API user without conflicting with other
                     API users.

                     If the libmpv client is destroyed, all overlays
                     associated with it are also deleted. In particular,
                     connecting via ----iinnppuutt--iippcc--sseerrvveerr, adding an overlay, and
                     disconnecting will remove the overlay immediately again.

              ffoorrmmaatt String that gives the type of the overlay. Accepts the
                     following values (HTML rendering of this is broken, view
                     the generated manpage instead, or the raw RST source):

                     aassss--eevveennttss
                            The ddaattaa parameter is a string. The string is
                            split on the newline character. Every line is
                            turned into the TTeexxtt part of a DDiiaalloogguuee ASS event.
                            Timing is unused (but behavior of timing dependent
                            ASS tags may change in future mpv versions).

                            Note that it's better to put multiple lines into
                            ddaattaa, instead of adding multiple OSD overlays.

                            This provides 2 ASS SSttyylleess. OOSSDD contains the text
                            style as defined by the current ----oossdd--...... options.
                            DDeeffaauulltt is similar, and contains style that OOSSDD
                            would have if all options were set to the default.

                            In addition, the rreess__xx and rreess__yy options specify
                            the value of the ASS PPllaayyRReessXX and PPllaayyRReessYY header
                            fields. If rreess__yy is set to 0, PPllaayyRReessYY is
                            initialized to an arbitrary default value (but
                            note that the default for this command is 720, not
                            0).  If rreess__xx is set to 0, PPllaayyRReessXX is set based
                            on rreess__yy such that a virtual ASS pixel has a
                            square pixel aspect ratio.

                     nnoonnee   Special value that causes the overlay to be
                            removed. Most parameters other than iidd and ffoorrmmaatt
                            are mostly ignored.

              ddaattaa   String defining the overlay contents according to the
                     ffoorrmmaatt parameter.

              rreess__xx,, rreess__yy
                     Used if ffoorrmmaatt is set to aassss--eevveennttss (see description
                     there).  Optional, defaults to 0/720.

              zz      The Z order of the overlay. Optional, defaults to 0.

                     Note that Z order between different overlays of different
                     formats is static, and cannot be changed (currently, this
                     means that bitmap overlays added by oovveerrllaayy--aadddd are
                     always on top of the ASS overlays added by oossdd--oovveerrllaayy).
                     In addition, the builtin OSD components are always below
                     any of the custom OSD. (This includes subtitles of any
                     kind as well as text rendered by sshhooww--tteexxtt.)

                     It's possible that future mpv versions will randomly
                     change how Z order between different OSD formats and
                     builtin OSD is handled.

              hhiiddddeenn If set to true, do not display this (default: false).

              ccoommppuuttee__bboouunnddss
                     If set to true, attempt to determine bounds and write
                     them to the command's result value as xx00, xx11, yy00, yy11
                     rectangle (default: false). If the rectangle is empty,
                     not known, or somehow degenerate, it is not set. xx11/yy11 is
                     the coordinate of the bottom exclusive corner of the
                     rectangle.

                     The result value may depend on the VO window size, and is
                     based on the last known window size at the time of the
                     call. This means the results may be different from what
                     is actually rendered.

                     For aassss--eevveennttss, the result rectangle is recomputed to
                     PPllaayyRReess coordinates (rreess__xx/rreess__yy). If window size is not
                     known, a fallback is chosen.

                     You should be aware that this mechanism is very
                     inefficient, as it renders the full result, and then uses
                     the bounding box of the rendered bitmap list (even if
                     hhiiddddeenn is set). It will flush various caches.  Its
                     results also depend on the used libass version.

                     This feature is experimental, and may change in some way
                     again.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 Always use named arguments (mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd__nnooddee(())). Lua scripts
                 should use the mmpp..ccrreeaattee__oossdd__oovveerrllaayy(()) helper instead of
                 invoking this command directly.

   IInnppuutt aanndd KKeeyybbiinndd CCoommmmaannddss

       mmoouussee <<xx>> <<yy>> [[<<bbuuttttoonn>> [[<<mmooddee>>]]]]
              Send a mouse event with given coordinate (<<xx>>, <<yy>>).

              Second argument:

              <<bbuuttttoonn>>
                     The button number of clicked mouse button. This should be
                     one of 0-19.  If <<bbuuttttoonn>> is omitted, only the position
                     will be updated.

              Third argument:

              <<ssiinnggllee>> ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     The mouse event represents regular single click.

              <<ddoouubbllee>>
                     The mouse event represents double-click.

       kkeeyypprreessss <<nnaammee>> [[<<ssccaallee>>]]
              Send a key event through mpv's input handler, triggering
              whatever behavior is configured to that key. nnaammee uses the
              iinnppuutt..ccoonnff naming scheme for keys and modifiers. ssccaallee is used
              to scale numerical change effected by the bound command (same
              mechanism as precise scrolling).  Useful for the client API: key
              events can be sent to libmpv to handle internally.

       kkeeyyddoowwnn <<nnaammee>>
              Similar to kkeeyypprreessss, but sets the KKEEYYDDOOWWNN flag so that if the
              key is bound to a repeatable command, it will be run repeatedly
              with mpv's key repeat timing until the kkeeyyuupp command is called.

       kkeeyyuupp [[<<nnaammee>>]]
              Set the KKEEYYUUPP flag, stopping any repeated behavior that had been
              triggered. nnaammee is optional. If nnaammee is not given or is an empty
              string, KKEEYYUUPP will be set on all keys. Otherwise, KKEEYYUUPP will
              only be set on the key specified by nnaammee.

       kkeeyybbiinndd <<nnaammee>> <<ccmmdd>> [[<<ccoommmmeenntt>>]]
              Binds a key to an input command. ccmmdd must be a complete command
              containing all the desired arguments and flags. Both nnaammee and
              ccmmdd use the iinnppuutt..ccoonnff naming scheme. ccoommmmeenntt is an optional
              string which can be read as the ccoommmmeenntt entry of iinnppuutt--bbiinnddiinnggss.
              This is primarily useful for the client API.

       eennaabbllee--sseeccttiioonn <<nnaammee>> [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              This command is deprecated, except for mpv-internal uses.

              Enable all key bindings in the named input section.

              The enabled input sections form a stack. Bindings in sections on
              the top of the stack are preferred to lower sections. This
              command puts the section on top of the stack. If the section was
              already on the stack, it is implicitly removed beforehand. (A
              section cannot be on the stack more than once.)

              The ffllaaggss parameter can be a combination (separated by ++) of the
              following flags:

              <<eexxcclluussiivvee>>
                     All sections enabled before the newly enabled section are
                     disabled.  They will be re-enabled as soon as all
                     exclusive sections above them are removed. In other
                     words, the new section shadows all previous sections.

              <<aallllooww--hhiiddee--ccuurrssoorr>>
                     This feature can't be used through the public API.

              <<aallllooww--vvoo--ddrraaggggiinngg>>
                     Same.

       ddiissaabbllee--sseeccttiioonn <<nnaammee>>
              This command is deprecated, except for mpv-internal uses.

              Disable the named input section. Undoes eennaabbllee--sseeccttiioonn.

       ddeeffiinnee--sseeccttiioonn <<nnaammee>> <<ccoonntteennttss>> [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              This command is deprecated, except for mpv-internal uses.

              Create a named input section, or replace the contents of an
              already existing input section. The ccoonntteennttss parameter uses the
              same syntax as the iinnppuutt..ccoonnff file (except that using the
              section syntax in it is not allowed), including the need to
              separate bindings with a newline character.

              If the ccoonntteennttss parameter is an empty string, the section is
              removed.

              The section with the name ddeeffaauulltt is the normal input section.

              In general, input sections have to be enabled with the
              eennaabbllee--sseeccttiioonn command, or they are ignored.

              The last parameter has the following meaning:

              <<ddeeffaauulltt>> ((aallssoo uusseedd iiff ppaarraammeetteerr oommiitttteedd))
                     Use a key binding defined by this section only if the
                     user hasn't already bound this key to a command.

              <<ffoorrccee>>
                     Always bind a key. (The input section that was made
                     active most recently wins if there are ambiguities.)

              This command can be used to dispatch arbitrary keys to a script
              or a client API user. If the input section defines
              ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg commands, it is also possible to get separate
              events on key up/down, and relatively detailed information about
              the key state. The special key name uunnmmaappppeedd can be used to
              match any unmapped key.

       llooaadd--iinnppuutt--ccoonnff <<ffiilleennaammee>>
              Load an input configuration file, similar to the ----iinnppuutt--ccoonnff
              option. If the file was already included, its previous bindings
              are not reset before it is reparsed.

   EExxeeccuuttiioonn CCoommmmaannddss

       rruunn <<ccoommmmaanndd>> [[<<aarrgg11>> [[<<aarrgg22>> [[......]]]]]]
              Run the given command. Unlike in MPlayer/mplayer2 and earlier
              versions of mpv (0.2.x and older), this doesn't call the shell.
              Instead, the command is run directly, with each argument passed
              separately. Each argument is expanded like in Property
              Expansion.

              This command has a variable number of arguments, and cannot be
              used with named arguments.

              The program is run in a detached way. mpv doesn't wait until the
              command is completed, but continues playback right after
              spawning it.

              To get the old behavior, use //bbiinn//sshh and --cc as the first two
              arguments.

                 Example

                        rruunn ""//bbiinn//sshh"" ""--cc"" ""eecchhoo $${{ttiittllee}} >> //ttmmpp//ppllaayyiinngg""

                        This is not a particularly good example, because it
                        doesn't handle escaping, and a specially prepared file
                        might allow an attacker to execute arbitrary shell
                        commands. It is recommended to write a small shell
                        script, and call that with rruunn.

       ssuubbpprroocceessss
              Similar to rruunn, but gives more control about process execution
              to the caller, and does not detach the process.

              You can avoid blocking until the process terminates by running
              this command asynchronously. (For example
              mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee__aassyynncc(()) in Lua scripting.)

              This has the following named arguments. The order of them is not
              guaranteed, so you should always call them with named arguments,
              see Named arguments.

              aarrggss ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE__AARRRRAAYY[[MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__SSTTRRIINNGG]]))
                     Array of strings with the command as first argument, and
                     subsequent command line arguments following. This is just
                     like the rruunn command argument list.

                     The first array entry is either an absolute path to the
                     executable, or a filename with no path components, in
                     which case the executable is searched in the directories
                     in the PPAATTHH environment variable. On Unix, this is
                     equivalent to ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp and eexxeeccvvpp behavior.

              ppllaayybbaacckk__oonnllyy ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__FFLLAAGG))
                     Boolean indicating whether the process should be killed
                     when playback of the current playlist entry terminates
                     (optional, default: true). If enabled, stopping playback
                     will automatically kill the process, and you can't start
                     it outside of playback.

              ccaappttuurree__ssiizzee ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__IINNTT6644))
                     Integer setting the maximum number of stdout plus stderr
                     bytes that can be captured (optional, default: 64MB). If
                     the number of bytes exceeds this, capturing is stopped.
                     The limit is per captured stream.

              ccaappttuurree__ssttddoouutt ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__FFLLAAGG))
                     Capture all data the process outputs to stdout and return
                     it once the process ends (optional, default: no).

              ccaappttuurree__ssttddeerrrr ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__FFLLAAGG))
                     Same as ccaappttuurree__ssttddoouutt, but for stderr.

              ddeettaacchh ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__FFLLAAGG))
                     Whether to run the process in detached mode (optional,
                     default: no). In this mode, the process is run in a new
                     process session, and the command does not wait for the
                     process to terminate. If neither ccaappttuurree__ssttddoouutt nor
                     ccaappttuurree__ssttddeerrrr have been set to true, the command returns
                     immediately after the new process has been started,
                     otherwise the command will read as long as the pipes are
                     open.

              eennvv ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE__AARRRRAAYY[[MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__SSTTRRIINNGG]]))
                     Set a list of environment variables for the new process
                     (default: empty).  If an empty list is passed, the
                     environment of the mpv process is used instead. (Unlike
                     the underlying OS mechanisms, the mpv command cannot
                     start a process with empty environment. Fortunately, that
                     is completely useless.) The format of the list is as in
                     the eexxeeccllee(()) syscall. Each string item defines an
                     environment variable as in NNAAMMEE==VVAALLUUEE.

                     On Lua, you may use uuttiillss..ggeett__eennvv__lliisstt(()) to retrieve the
                     current environment if you e.g. simply want to add a new
                     variable.

              ssttddiinn__ddaattaa ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__SSTTRRIINNGG))
                     Feed the given string to the new process' stdin. Since
                     this is a string, you cannot pass arbitrary binary data.
                     If the process terminates or closes the pipe before all
                     data is written, the remaining data is silently
                     discarded. Probably does not work on win32.

              ppaasssstthhrroouugghh__ssttddiinn ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__FFLLAAGG))
                     If enabled, wire the new process' stdin to mpv's stdin
                     (default: no).  Before mpv 0.33.0, this argument did not
                     exist, but the behavior was as if this was set to true.

              The command returns the following result (as
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE__MMAAPP):

              ssttaattuuss ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__IINNTT6644))
                     Typically this is the process exit code (0 or positive)
                     if the process terminates normally, or negative for other
                     errors (failed to start, terminated by mpv, and others).
                     The meaning of negative values is undefined, other than
                     meaning error (and does not correspond to OS low level
                     exit status values).

                     On Windows, it can happen that a negative return value is
                     returned even if the process terminates normally, because
                     the win32 UUIINNTT exit code is assigned to an iinntt variable
                     before being set as iinntt6644__tt field in the result map. This
                     might be fixed later.

              ssttddoouutt ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__BBYYTTEE__AARRRRAAYY))
                     Captured stdout stream, limited to ccaappttuurree__ssiizzee.

              ssttddeerrrr ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__BBYYTTEE__AARRRRAAYY))
                     Same as ssttddoouutt, but for stderr.

              eerrrroorr__ssttrriinngg ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__SSTTRRIINNGG))
                     Empty string if the process terminated normally. The
                     string kkiilllleedd if the process was terminated in an unusual
                     way. The string iinniitt if the process could not be started.

                     On Windows, kkiilllleedd is only returned when the process has
                     been killed by mpv as a result of ppllaayybbaacckk__oonnllyy being set
                     to true.

              kkiilllleedd__bbyy__uuss ((MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__FFLLAAGG))
                     Whether the process has been killed by mpv, for example
                     as a result of ppllaayybbaacckk__oonnllyy being set to true, aborting
                     the command (e.g. by mmpp..aabboorrtt__aassyynncc__ccoommmmaanndd(())), or if the
                     player is about to exit.

              Note that the command itself will always return success as long
              as the parameters are correct. Whether the process could be
              spawned or whether it was somehow killed or returned an error
              status has to be queried from the result value.

              This command can be asynchronously aborted via API. Also see
              Asynchronous command details. Only the rruunn command can start
              processes in a truly detached way.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 The subprocess will always be terminated on player exit if it
                 wasn't started in detached mode, even if ppllaayybbaacckk__oonnllyy is
                 false.

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 Don't forget to set the ppllaayybbaacckk__oonnllyy field to false if you
                 want the command to run while the player is in idle mode, or
                 if you don't want the end of playback to kill the command.

                 Example

                    local r = mp.command_native({
                        name = "subprocess",
                        playback_only = false,
                        capture_stdout = true,
                        args = {"cat", "/proc/cpuinfo"},
                    })
                    if r.status == 0 then
                        print("result: " .. r.stdout)
                    end

                 This is a fairly useless Lua example, which demonstrates how
                 to run a process in a blocking manner, and retrieving its
                 stdout output.

       qquuiitt [[<<ccooddee>>]]
              Exit the player. If an argument is given, it's used as process
              exit code.

       qquuiitt--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr [[<<ccooddee>>]]
              Exit player, and store current playback position. Playing that
              file later will seek to the previous position on start. The
              (optional) argument is exactly as in the qquuiitt command. See
              RESUMING PLAYBACK.

   SSccrriippttiinngg CCoommmmaannddss

       ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee [[<<aarrgg11>> [[<<aarrgg22>> [[......]]]]]]
              Send a message to all clients, and pass it the following list of
              arguments.  What this message means, how many arguments it
              takes, and what the arguments mean is fully up to the receiver
              and the sender. Every client receives the message, so be careful
              about name clashes (or use ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo).

              This command has a variable number of arguments, and cannot be
              used with named arguments.

       ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo <<ttaarrggeett>> [[<<aarrgg11>> [[<<aarrgg22>> [[......]]]]]]
              Same as ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee, but send it only to the client named
              <<ttaarrggeett>>. Each client (scripts etc.) has a unique name. For
              example, Lua scripts can get their name via
              mmpp..ggeett__ssccrriipptt__nnaammee(()). Note that client names only consist of
              alphanumeric characters and __.

              This command has a variable number of arguments, and cannot be
              used with named arguments.

       ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg <<nnaammee>> [[<<aarrgg>>]]
              Invoke a script-provided key binding. This can be used to remap
              key bindings provided by external Lua scripts.

              <<nnaammee>> is the name of the binding. <<aarrgg>> is a user-provided
              arbitrary string which can be used to provide extra information.

              It can optionally be prefixed with the name of the script, using
              // as separator, e.g. ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg ssccrriippttnnaammee//bbiinnddiinnggnnaammee. Note
              that script names only consist of alphanumeric characters and __.

              For completeness, here is how this command works internally. The
              details could change any time. On any matching key event,
              ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo or ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee is called (depending on
              whether the script name is included), with the following
              arguments in string format:

              1. The string kkeeyy--bbiinnddiinngg.

              2. The name of the binding (as established above).

              3. The key state as string (see below).

              4. The key name (since mpv 0.15.0).

              5. The text the key would produce, or empty string if not
                 applicable.

              6. The scale of the key, such as the ones produced by WWHHEEEELL__**
                 keys.  The scale is 1 if the key is nonscalable.

              7. The user-provided string <<aarrgg>>, or empty string if the
                 argument is not used.

              The 5th argument is only set if no modifiers are present (using
              the shift key with a letter is normally not emitted as having a
              modifier, and results in upper case text instead, but some
              backends may mess up).

              The key state consists of 3 characters:

              1. One of dd (key was pressed down), uu (was released), rr (key is
                 still down, and was repeated; only if key repeat is enabled
                 for this binding), pp (key was pressed; happens if up/down
                 can't be tracked).

              2. Whether the event originates from the mouse, either mm (mouse
                 button) or -- (something else).

              3. Whether the event results from a cancellation (e.g. the key
                 is logically released but not physically released), either cc
                 (canceled) or -- (something else). Not all types of
                 cancellations set this flag.

              Future versions can add more arguments and more key state
              characters to support more input peculiarities.

              This is a scalable command. See the documentation of nnoonnssccaallaabbllee
              input command prefix in Input Command Prefixes for details.

       llooaadd--ssccrriipptt <<ffiilleennaammee>>
              Load a script, similar to the ----ssccrriipptt option. Whether this
              waits for the script to finish initialization or not changed
              multiple times, and the future behavior is left undefined.

              On success, returns a mmppvv__nnooddee with a cclliieenntt__iidd field set to the
              return value of the mmppvv__cclliieenntt__iidd(()) API call of the newly
              created script handle.

   SSccrreeeennsshhoott CCoommmmaannddss

       ssccrreeeennsshhoott [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Take a screenshot.

              Multiple flags are available (some can be combined with ++):

              <<ssuubbttiittlleess>> ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     Save the video image, in its original resolution, and
                     with subtitles.  Some video outputs may still include the
                     OSD in the output under certain circumstances.

              <<vviiddeeoo>>
                     Like ssuubbttiittlleess, but typically without OSD or subtitles.
                     The exact behavior depends on the selected video output.

              <<wwiinnddooww>>
                     Save the contents of the mpv window. Typically scaled,
                     with OSD and subtitles. The exact behavior depends on the
                     selected video output.

              <<eeaacchh--ffrraammee>>
                     Take a screenshot each frame. Issue this command again to
                     stop taking screenshots. Note that you should disable
                     frame-dropping when using this mode - or you might
                     receive duplicate images in cases when a frame was
                     dropped. This flag can be combined with the other flags,
                     e.g. vviiddeeoo++eeaacchh--ffrraammee.

              Older mpv versions required passing ssiinnggllee and eeaacchh--ffrraammee as
              second argument (and did not have flags). This syntax is still
              understood, but deprecated and might be removed in the future.

              If you combine this command with another one using ;;, you can
              use the aassyynncc flag to make encoding/writing the image file
              asynchronous. For normal standalone commands, this is always
              asynchronous, and the flag has no effect. (This behavior changed
              with mpv 0.29.0.)

              On success, returns a mmppvv__nnooddee with a ffiilleennaammee field set to the
              saved screenshot location.

       ssccrreeeennsshhoott--ttoo--ffiillee <<ffiilleennaammee>> [[<<ffllaaggss>>]]
              Take a screenshot and save it to a given file. The format of the
              file will be guessed by the extension (and ----ssccrreeeennsshhoott--ffoorrmmaatt
              is ignored - the behavior when the extension is missing or
              unknown is arbitrary).

              The second argument is like the first argument to ssccrreeeennsshhoott and
              supports ssuubbttiittlleess, vviiddeeoo, wwiinnddooww.

              If the file already exists, it's overwritten.

              Like all input command parameters, the filename is subject to
              property expansion as described in Property Expansion.

       ssccrreeeennsshhoott--rraaww [[<<ffllaaggss>> [[<<ffoorrmmaatt>>]]]]
              Return a screenshot in memory. This can be used only through the
              client API. The MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP returned by this command has
              the ww, hh, ssttrriiddee fields set to obvious contents.

              The ffoorrmmaatt field is set to the format of the screenshot image
              data.  This can be controlled by the ffoorrmmaatt argument. The format
              can be one of the following:

              bbggrr00 ((ddeeffaauulltt))
                     This format is organized as BB88GG88RR88XX88 (where BB is the
                     LSB).  The contents of the padding XX are undefined.

              bbggrraa   This format is organized as BB88GG88RR88AA88 (where BB is the
                     LSB).

              rrggbbaa   This format is organized as RR88GG88BB88AA88 (where RR is the
                     LSB).

              rrggbbaa6644 This format is organized as RR1166GG1166BB1166AA1166 (where RR is the
                     LSB).  Each component occupies 2 bytes per pixel.  When
                     this format is used, the image data will be high bit
                     depth, and ----ssccrreeeennsshhoott--hhiigghh--bbiitt--ddeepptthh is ignored.

              The ddaattaa field is of type MPV_FORMAT_BYTE_ARRAY with the actual
              image data. The image is freed as soon as the result mpv_node is
              freed. As usual with client API semantics, you are not allowed
              to write to the image data.

              The ssttrriiddee is the number of bytes from a pixel at ((xx00,, yy00)) to
              the pixel at ((xx00,, yy00 ++ 11)). This can be larger than ww ** bbpppp if
              the image was cropped, or if there is padding. This number can
              be negative as well.  You access a pixel with bbyyttee__iinnddeexx == yy **
              ssttrriiddee ++ xx ** bbpppp.  Here, bbpppp is the number of bytes per pixel,
              which is 8 for rrggbbaa6644 format and 4 for other formats.

              The ffllaaggss argument is like the first argument to ssccrreeeennsshhoott and
              supports ssuubbttiittlleess, vviiddeeoo, wwiinnddooww.

   FFiilltteerr CCoommmmaannddss

       aaff <<ooppeerraattiioonn>> <<vvaalluuee>>
              Change audio filter chain. See vvff command.

       vvff <<ooppeerraattiioonn>> <<vvaalluuee>>
              Change video filter chain.

              The semantics are exactly the same as with option parsing (see
              VIDEO FILTERS). As such the text below is a redundant and
              incomplete summary.

              The first argument decides what happens:

              <<sseett>>  Overwrite the previous filter chain with the new one.

              <<aadddd>>  Append the new filter chain to the previous one.

              <<ttooggggllee>>
                     Check if the given filter (with the exact parameters) is
                     already in the video chain. If it is, remove the filter.
                     If it isn't, add the filter.  (If several filters are
                     passed to the command, this is done for each filter.)

                     A special variant is combining this with labels, and
                     using @@nnaammee without filter name and parameters as filter
                     entry. This toggles the enable/disable flag.

              <<rreemmoovvee>>
                     Like ttooggggllee, but always remove the given filter from the
                     chain.

              <<ccllrr>>  Remove all filters. Note that like the other
                     sub-commands, this does not control automatically
                     inserted filters.

              The argument is always needed. E.g. in case of ccllrr use vvff ccllrr
              """".

              You can assign labels to filter by prefixing them with @@nnaammee::
              (where nnaammee is a user-chosen arbitrary identifier). Labels can
              be used to refer to filters by name in all of the filter chain
              modification commands.  For aadddd, using an already used label
              will replace the existing filter.

              The vvff command shows the list of requested filters on the OSD
              after changing the filter chain. This is roughly equivalent to
              sshhooww--tteexxtt $${{vvff}}. Note that auto-inserted filters for format
              conversion are not shown on the list, only what was requested by
              the user.

              Normally, the commands will check whether the video chain is
              recreated successfully, and will undo the operation on failure.
              If the command is run before video is configured (can happen if
              the command is run immediately after opening a file and before a
              video frame is decoded), this check can't be run. Then it can
              happen that creating the video chain fails.

                 Example for input.conf

                 • aa vvff sseett vvfflliipp turn the video upside-down on the aa key

                 • bb vvff sseett """" remove all video filters on bb

                 • cc vvff ttooggggllee ggrraaddffuunn toggle debanding on cc

                 Example how to toggle disabled filters at runtime

                 • Add something like vvff--aadddd==@@ddeebbaanndd::!!ggrraaddffuunn to mmppvv..ccoonnff.
                   The @@ddeebbaanndd:: is the label, an arbitrary, user-given name
                   for this filter entry. The !! before the filter name
                   disables the filter by default. Everything after this is
                   the normal filter name and possibly filter parameters, like
                   in the normal ----vvff syntax.

                 • Add aa vvff ttooggggllee @@ddeebbaanndd to iinnppuutt..ccoonnff. This toggles the
                   "disabled" flag for the filter with the label ddeebbaanndd when
                   the aa key is hit.

       vvff--ccoommmmaanndd <<llaabbeell>> <<ccoommmmaanndd>> <<aarrgguummeenntt>> [[<<ttaarrggeett>>]]
              Send a command to the filter. Note that currently, this only
              works with the llaavvffii filter. Refer to the libavfilter
              documentation for the list of supported commands for each
              filter.

              <<llaabbeell>> is a mpv filter label, use aallll to send it to all filters
              at once.

              <<ccoommmmaanndd>> and <<aarrgguummeenntt>> are filter-specific strings.

              <<ttaarrggeett>> is a filter or filter instance name and defaults to
              aallll.  Note that the target is an additional specifier for
              filters that support them, such as complex llaavvffii filter chains.

       aaff--ccoommmmaanndd <<llaabbeell>> <<ccoommmmaanndd>> <<aarrgguummeenntt>> [[<<ttaarrggeett>>]]
              Same as vvff--ccoommmmaanndd, but for audio filters.

   MMiisscceellllaanneeoouuss CCoommmmaannddss

       iiggnnoorree Use this to "block" keys that should be unbound, and do nothing.
              Useful for disabling default bindings, without disabling all
              bindings with ----iinnppuutt--ddeeffaauulltt--bbiinnddiinnggss==nnoo.

       ddrroopp--bbuuffffeerrss
              Drop audio/video/demuxer buffers, and restart from fresh. Might
              help with unseekable streams that are going out of sync.  This
              command might be changed or removed in the future.

       dduummpp--ccaacchhee <<ssttaarrtt>> <<eenndd>> <<ffiilleennaammee>>
              Dump the current cache to the given filename. The <<ffiilleennaammee>>
              file is overwritten if it already exists. <<ssttaarrtt>> and <<eenndd>> give
              the time range of what to dump. If no data is cached at the
              given time range, nothing may be dumped (creating a file with no
              packets).

              Dumping a larger part of the cache will freeze the player. No
              effort was made to fix this, as this feature was meant mostly
              for creating small excerpts.

              See ----ssttrreeaamm--rreeccoorrdd for various caveats that mostly apply to
              this command too, as both use the same underlying code for
              writing the output file.

              If <<ffiilleennaammee>> is an empty string, an ongoing dduummpp--ccaacchhee is
              stopped.

              If <<eenndd>> is nnoo, then continuous dumping is enabled. Then, after
              dumping the existing parts of the cache, anything read from
              network is appended to the cache as well. This behaves similar
              to ----ssttrreeaamm--rreeccoorrdd (although it does not conflict with that
              option, and they can be both active at the same time).

              If the <<eenndd>> time is after the cache, the command will _not_
              wait and write newly received data to it.

              The end of the resulting file may be slightly damaged or
              incomplete at the end. (Not enough effort was made to ensure
              that the end lines up properly.)

              Note that this command will finish only once dumping ends. That
              means it works similar to the ssccrreeeennsshhoott command, just that it
              can block much longer. If continuous dumping is used, the
              command will not finish until playback is stopped, an error
              happens, another dduummpp--ccaacchhee command is run, or an API like
              mmpp..aabboorrtt__aassyynncc__ccoommmmaanndd was called to explicitly stop the
              command. See Synchronous vs. Asynchronous.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This was mostly created for network streams. For local files,
                 there may be much better methods to create excerpts and such.
                 There are tons of much more user-friendly Lua scripts, that
                 will re-encode parts of a file by spawning a separate
                 instance of ffffmmppeegg. With network streams, this is not that
                 easily possible, as the stream would have to be downloaded
                 again. Even if ----ssttrreeaamm--rreeccoorrdd is used to record the stream
                 to the local filesystem, there may be problems, because the
                 recorded file is still written to.

              This command is experimental, and all details about it may
              change in the future.

       aabb--lloooopp
              Cycle through A-B loop states. The first command will set the AA
              point (the aabb--lloooopp--aa property); the second the BB point, and the
              third will clear both points.

       aabb--lloooopp--dduummpp--ccaacchhee <<ffiilleennaammee>>
              Essentially calls dduummpp--ccaacchhee with the current AB-loop points as
              arguments. Like dduummpp--ccaacchhee, this will overwrite the file at
              <<ffiilleennaammee>>. Likewise, if the B point is set to nnoo, it will enter
              continuous dumping after the existing cache was dumped.

              The author reserves the right to remove this command if enough
              motivation is found to move this functionality to a trivial Lua
              script.

       aabb--lloooopp--aalliiggnn--ccaacchhee
              Re-adjust the A/B loop points to the start and end within the
              cache the aabb--lloooopp--dduummpp--ccaacchhee command will (probably) dump.
              Basically, it aligns the times on keyframes. The guess might be
              off especially at the end (due to granularity issues due to
              remuxing). If the cache shrinks in the meantime, the points set
              by the command will not be the effective parameters either.

              This command has an even more uncertain future than
              aabb--lloooopp--dduummpp--ccaacchhee and might disappear without replacement if
              the author decides it's useless.

       bbeeggiinn--vvoo--ddrraaggggiinngg
              Begin window dragging if supported by the current VO. This
              command should only be called while a mouse button is being
              pressed, otherwise it will be ignored. The exact effect of this
              command depends on the VO implementation of window dragging. For
              example, on Windows and macOS only the left mouse button can
              begin window dragging, while X11 and Wayland allow other mouse
              buttons.

       ccoonntteexxtt--mmeennuu
              Show context menu on the video window. See Context Menu section
              for details.

       Undocumented commands: aaoo--rreellooaadd (experimental/internal).

   LLiisstt ooff eevveennttss
       This is a partial list of events. This section describes what
       mmppvv__eevveenntt__ttoo__nnooddee(()) returns, and which is what scripting APIs and the
       JSON IPC sees. Note that the C API has separate C-level declarations
       with mmppvv__eevveenntt, which may be slightly different.

       Note that events are asynchronous: the player core continues running
       while events are delivered to scripts and other clients. In some cases,
       you can use hooks to enforce synchronous execution.

       All events can have the following fields:

       eevveenntt  Name as the event (as returned by mmppvv__eevveenntt__nnaammee(())).

       iidd     The rreeppllyy__uusseerrddaattaa field (opaque user value). If rreeppllyy__uusseerrddaattaa
              is 0, the field is not added.

       eerrrroorr  Set to an error string (as returned by mmppvv__eerrrroorr__ssttrriinngg(())). This
              field is missing if no error happened, or the event type does
              not report error.  Most events leave this unset.

       This list uses the event name field value, and the C API symbol in
       brackets:

       ssttaarrtt--ffiillee ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__SSTTAARRTT__FFIILLEE))
              Happens right before a new file is loaded. When you receive
              this, the player is loading the file (or possibly already done
              with it).

              This has the following fields:

              ppllaayylliisstt__eennttrryy__iidd
                     Playlist entry ID of the file being loaded now.

       eenndd--ffiillee ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__EENNDD__FFIILLEE))
              Happens after a file was unloaded. Typically, the player will
              load the next file right away, or quit if this was the last
              file.

              The event has the following fields:

              rreeaassoonn Has one of these values:

                     eeooff    The file has ended. This can (but doesn't have to)
                            include incomplete files or broken network
                            connections under circumstances.

                     ssttoopp   Playback was ended by a command.

                     qquuiitt   Playback was ended by sending the quit command.

                     eerrrroorr  An error happened. In this case, an eerrrroorr field is
                            present with the error string.

                     rreeddiirreecctt
                            Happens with playlists and similar. Details see
                            MMPPVV__EENNDD__FFIILLEE__RREEAASSOONN__RREEDDIIRREECCTT in the C API.

                     uunnkknnoowwnn
                            Unknown. Normally doesn't happen, unless the Lua
                            API is out of sync with the C API. (Likewise, it
                            could happen that your script gets reason strings
                            that did not exist yet at the time your script was
                            written.)

              ppllaayylliisstt__eennttrryy__iidd
                     Playlist entry ID of the file that was being played or
                     attempted to be played. This has the same value as the
                     ppllaayylliisstt__eennttrryy__iidd field in the corresponding ssttaarrtt--ffiillee
                     event.

              ffiillee__eerrrroorr
                     Set to mpv error string describing the approximate reason
                     why playback failed. Unset if no error known. (In Lua
                     scripting, this value was set on the eerrrroorr field
                     directly. This is deprecated since mpv 0.33.0.  In the
                     future, this eerrrroorr field will be unset for this specific
                     event.)

              ppllaayylliisstt__iinnsseerrtt__iidd
                     If loading ended, because the playlist entry to be played
                     was for example a playlist, and the current playlist
                     entry is replaced with a number of other entries. This
                     may happen at least with MPV_END_FILE_REASON_REDIRECT
                     (other event types may use this for similar but different
                     purposes in the future). In this case, playlist_insert_id
                     will be set to the playlist entry ID of the first
                     inserted entry, and playlist_insert_num_entries to the
                     total number of inserted playlist entries. Note this in
                     this specific case, the ID of the last inserted entry is
                     playlist_insert_id+num-1.  Beware that depending on
                     circumstances, you may observe the new playlist entries
                     before seeing the event (e.g. reading the "playlist"
                     property or getting a property change notification before
                     receiving the event).  If this is 0 in the C API, this
                     field isn't added.

              ppllaayylliisstt__iinnsseerrtt__nnuumm__eennttrriieess
                     See playlist_insert_id. Only present if
                     playlist_insert_id is present.

       ffiillee--llooaaddeedd ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__FFIILLEE__LLOOAADDEEDD))
              Happens after a file was loaded and begins playback.

       sseeeekk ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__SSEEEEKK))
              Happens on seeking. (This might include cases when the player
              seeks internally, even without user interaction. This includes
              e.g. segment changes when playing ordered chapters Matroska
              files.)

       ppllaayybbaacckk--rreessttaarrtt ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__PPLLAAYYBBAACCKK__RREESSTTAARRTT))
              Start of playback after seek or after file was loaded.

       sshhuuttddoowwnn ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__SSHHUUTTDDOOWWNN))
              Sent when the player quits, and the script should terminate.
              Normally handled automatically. See Details on the script
              initialization and lifecycle.

       lloogg--mmeessssaaggee ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__LLOOGG__MMEESSSSAAGGEE))
              Receives messages enabled with mmppvv__rreeqquueesstt__lloogg__mmeessssaaggeess(()) (Lua:
              mmpp..eennaabbllee__mmeessssaaggeess).

              This contains, in addition to the default event fields, the
              following fields:

              pprreeffiixx The module prefix, identifies the sender of the message.
                     This is what the terminal player puts in front of the
                     message text when using the ----vv option, and is also what
                     is used for ----mmssgg--lleevveell.

              lleevveell  The log level as string. See mmssgg..lloogg for possible log
                     level names.  Note that later versions of mpv might add
                     new levels or remove (undocumented) existing ones.

              tteexxtt   The log message. The text will end with a newline
                     character. Sometimes it can contain multiple lines.

              Keep in mind that these messages are meant to be hints for
              humans. You should not parse them, and prefix/level/text of
              messages might change any time.

       hhooookk   The event has the following fields:

              hhooookk__iidd
                     ID to pass to mmppvv__hhooookk__ccoonnttiinnuuee(()). The Lua scripting
                     wrapper provides a better API around this with
                     mmpp..aadddd__hhooookk(()).

       ggeett--pprrooppeerrttyy--rreeppllyy ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__GGEETT__PPRROOPPEERRTTYY__RREEPPLLYY))
              See C API.

       sseett--pprrooppeerrttyy--rreeppllyy ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__SSEETT__PPRROOPPEERRTTYY__RREEPPLLYY))
              See C API.

       ccoommmmaanndd--rreeppllyy ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__CCOOMMMMAANNDD__RREEPPLLYY))
              This is one of the commands for which the ``eerrrroorr field is
              meaningful.

              JSON IPC and Lua and possibly other backends treat this
              specially and may not pass the actual event to the user. See C
              API.

              The event has the following fields:

              rreessuulltt The result (on success) of any mmppvv__nnooddee type, if any.

       cclliieenntt--mmeessssaaggee ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__CCLLIIEENNTT__MMEESSSSAAGGEE))
              Lua and possibly other backends treat this specially and may not
              pass the actual event to the user.

              The event has the following fields:

              aarrggss   Array of strings with the message data.

       vviiddeeoo--rreeccoonnffiigg ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__VVIIDDEEOO__RREECCOONNFFIIGG))
              Happens on video output or filter reconfig.

       aauuddiioo--rreeccoonnffiigg ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__AAUUDDIIOO__RREECCOONNFFIIGG))
              Happens on audio output or filter reconfig.

       pprrooppeerrttyy--cchhaannggee ((MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__PPRROOPPEERRTTYY__CCHHAANNGGEE))
              Happens when a property that is being observed changes value.

              The event has the following fields:

              nnaammee   The name of the property.

              ddaattaa   The new value of the property.

       The following events also happen, but are deprecated: iiddllee, ttiicckk Use
       mmppvv__oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy(()) (Lua: mmpp..oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy(())) instead.

   HHooookkss
       Hooks are synchronous events between player core and a script or
       similar. This applies to client API (including the Lua scripting
       interface). Normally, events are supposed to be asynchronous, and the
       hook API provides an awkward and obscure way to handle events that
       require stricter coordination. There are no API stability guarantees
       made. Not following the protocol exactly can make the player freeze
       randomly. Basically, nobody should use this API.

       The C API is described in the header files. The Lua API is described in
       the Lua section.

       Before a hook is actually invoked on an API clients, it will attempt to
       return new values for all observed properties that were changed before
       the hook. This may make it easier for an application to set defined
       "barriers" between property change notifications by registering hooks.
       (That means these hooks will have an effect, even if you do nothing and
       make them continue immediately.)

       The following hooks are currently defined:

       oonn__llooaadd
              Called when a file is to be opened, before anything is actually
              done.  For example, you could read and write the
              ssttrreeaamm--ooppeenn--ffiilleennaammee property to redirect an URL to something
              else (consider support for streaming sites which rarely give the
              user a direct media URL), or you could set per-file options with
              by setting the property ffiillee--llooccaall--ooppttiioonnss//<<ooppttiioonn nnaammee>>. The
              player will wait until all hooks are run.

              Ordered after ssttaarrtt--ffiillee and before ppllaayybbaacckk--rreessttaarrtt.

       oonn__llooaadd__ffaaiill
              Called after after a file has been opened, but failed to. This
              can be used to provide a fallback in case native demuxers failed
              to recognize the file, instead of always running before the
              native demuxers like oonn__llooaadd. Demux will only be retried if
              ssttrreeaamm--ooppeenn--ffiilleennaammee was changed. If it fails again, this hook
              is _not_ called again, and loading definitely fails.

              Ordered after oonn__llooaadd, and before ppllaayybbaacckk--rreessttaarrtt and eenndd--ffiillee.

       oonn__pprreellooaaddeedd
              Called after a file has been opened, and before tracks are
              selected and decoders are created. This has some usefulness if
              an API users wants to select tracks manually, based on the set
              of available tracks. It's also useful to initialize
              ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx in a specific way by API, without having to
              "probe" the available streams at first.

              Note that this does not yet apply default track selection. Which
              operations exactly can be done and not be done, and what
              information is available and what is not yet available yet, is
              all subject to change.

              Ordered after oonn__llooaadd__ffaaiill etc. and before ppllaayybbaacckk--rreessttaarrtt.

       oonn__uunnllooaadd
              Run before closing a file, and before actually uninitializing
              everything. It's not possible to resume playback in this state.

              Ordered before eenndd--ffiillee. Will also happen in the error case
              (then after oonn__llooaadd__ffaaiill).

       oonn__bbeeffoorree__ssttaarrtt__ffiillee
              Run before a ssttaarrtt--ffiillee event is sent. (If any client changes
              the current playlist entry, or sends a quit command to the
              player, the corresponding event will not actually happen after
              the hook returns.)  Useful to drain property changes before a
              new file is loaded.

       oonn__aafftteerr__eenndd__ffiillee
              Run after an eenndd--ffiillee event. Useful to drain property changes
              after a file has finished.

   IInnppuutt CCoommmmaanndd PPrreeffiixxeess
       These prefixes are placed between key name and the actual command.
       Multiple prefixes can be specified. They are separated by whitespace.

       oossdd--aauuttoo
              Use the default behavior for this command. This is the default
              for iinnppuutt..ccoonnff commands. Some libmpv/scripting/IPC APIs do not
              use this as default, but use nnoo--oossdd instead.

       nnoo--oossdd Do not use any OSD for this command.

       oossdd--bbaarr
              If possible, show a bar with this command. Seek commands will
              show the progress bar, property changing commands may show the
              newly set value.

       oossdd--mmssgg
              If possible, show an OSD message with this command. Seek command
              show the current playback time, property changing commands show
              the newly set value as text.

       oossdd--mmssgg--bbaarr
              Combine osd-bar and osd-msg.

       rraaww    Do not expand properties in string arguments. (Like
              ""$${{pprrooppeerrttyy--nnaammee}}"".) This is the default for some
              libmpv/scripting/IPC APIs.

       eexxppaanndd--pprrooppeerrttiieess
              All string arguments are expanded as described in Property
              Expansion.  This is the default for iinnppuutt..ccoonnff commands.

       rreeppeeaattaabbllee
              For some commands, keeping a key pressed doesn't run the command
              repeatedly.  This prefix forces enabling key repeat in any case.
              For a list of commands: the first command determines the
              repeatability of the whole list (up to and including version
              0.33 - a list was always repeatable).

       nnoonnrreeppeeaattaabbllee
              For some commands, keeping a key pressed runs the command
              repeatedly.  This prefix forces disabling key repeat in any
              case.

       nnoonnssccaallaabbllee
              When some commands (e.g. aadddd) are bound to scalable keys
              associated to a high-precision input device like a touchpad
              (e.g. WWHHEEEELL__UUPP), the value specified in the command is scaled to
              smaller steps based on the high resolution input data if
              available.  This prefix forces disabling this behavior, so the
              value is always changed in the discrete unit specified in the
              key binding.

       aassyynncc  Allow asynchronous execution (if possible). Note that only a few
              commands will support this (usually this is explicitly
              documented). Some commands are asynchronous by default (or
              rather, their effects might manifest after completion of the
              command). The semantics of this flag might change in the future.
              Set it only if you don't rely on the effects of this command
              being fully realized when it returns. See Synchronous vs.
              Asynchronous.

       ssyynncc   Allow synchronous execution (if possible). Normally, all
              commands are synchronous by default, but some are asynchronous
              by default for compatibility with older behavior.

       All of the osd prefixes are still overridden by the global ----oossdd--lleevveell
       settings.

   SSyynncchhrroonnoouuss vvss.. AAssyynncchhrroonnoouuss
       The aassyynncc and ssyynncc prefix matter only for how the issuer of the command
       waits on the completion of the command. Normally it does not affect how
       the command behaves by itself. There are the following cases:

       • Normal input.conf commands are always run asynchronously. Slow
         running commands are queued up or run in parallel.

       • "Multi" input.conf commands (1 key binding, concatenated with ;;) will
         be executed in order, except for commands that are async (either
         prefixed with aassyynncc, or async by default for some commands). The
         async commands are run in a detached manner, possibly in parallel to
         the remaining sync commands in the list.

       • Normal Lua and libmpv commands (e.g. mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd(())) are run in a
         blocking manner, unless the aassyynncc prefix is used, or the command is
         async by default. This means in the sync case the caller will block,
         even if the core continues playback. Async mode runs the command in a
         detached manner.

       • Async libmpv command API (e.g. mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd__aassyynncc(())) never blocks the
         caller, and always notify their completion with a message. The ssyynncc
         and aassyynncc prefixes make no difference.

       • Lua also provides APIs for running async commands, which behave
         similar to the C counterparts.

       • In all cases, async mode can still run commands in a synchronous
         manner, even in detached mode. This can for example happen in cases
         when a command does not have an  asynchronous implementation. The
         async libmpv API still never blocks the caller in these cases.

       Before mpv 0.29.0, the aassyynncc prefix was only used by screenshot
       commands, and made them run the file saving code in a detached manner.
       This is the default now, and aassyynncc changes behavior only in the ways
       mentioned above.

       Currently the following commands have different waiting characteristics
       with sync vs. async: sub-add, audio-add, sub-reload, audio-reload,
       rescan-external-files, screenshot, screenshot-to-file, dump-cache,
       ab-loop-dump-cache.

   AAssyynncchhrroonnoouuss ccoommmmaanndd ddeettaaiillss
       On the API level, every asynchronous command is bound to the context
       which started it. For example, an asynchronous command started by
       mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd__aassyynncc is bound to the mmppvv__hhaannddllee passed to the function.
       Only this mmppvv__hhaannddllee receives the completion notification
       (MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__CCOOMMMMAANNDD__RREEPPLLYY), and only this handle can abort a still
       running command directly. If the mmppvv__hhaannddllee is destroyed, any still
       running async. commands started by it are terminated.

       The scripting APIs and JSON IPC give each script/connection its own
       implicit mmppvv__hhaannddllee.

       If the player is closed, the core may abort all pending async. commands
       on its own (like a forced mmppvv__aabboorrtt__aassyynncc__ccoommmmaanndd(()) call for each
       pending command on behalf of the API user). This happens at the same
       time MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__SSHHUUTTDDOOWWNN is sent, and there is no way to prevent this.

   IInnppuutt SSeeccttiioonnss
       Input sections group a set of bindings, and enable or disable them at
       once.  In iinnppuutt..ccoonnff, each key binding is assigned to an input section,
       rather than actually having explicit text sections.

       See also: eennaabbllee--sseeccttiioonn and ddiissaabbllee--sseeccttiioonn commands.

       Predefined bindings:

       ddeeffaauulltt
              Bindings without input section are implicitly assigned to this
              section. It is enabled by default during normal playback.

       eennccooddee Section which is active in encoding mode. It is enabled
              exclusively, so that bindings in the ddeeffaauulltt sections are
              ignored.

   PPrrooppeerrttiieess
       Properties are used to set mpv options during runtime, or to query
       arbitrary information. They can be manipulated with the sseett/aadddd/ccyyccllee
       commands, and retrieved with sshhooww--tteexxtt, or anything else that uses
       property expansion. (See Property Expansion.)

       If an option is referenced, the property will normally take/return
       exactly the same values as the option. In these cases, properties are
       merely a way to change an option at runtime.

       Note that many properties are unavailable at startup. See Details on
       the script initialization and lifecycle.

   PPrrooppeerrttyy lliisstt
       NNOOTTEE::
          Most options can be set at runtime via properties as well. Just
          remove the leading ---- from the option name. These are not documented
          below, see OPTIONS instead. Only properties which do not exist as
          option with the same name, or which have very different behavior
          from the options are documented below.

          Properties marked as (RW) are writeable, while those that aren't are
          read-only.

       aauuddiioo--ssppeeeedd--ccoorrrreeccttiioonn,, vviiddeeoo--ssppeeeedd--ccoorrrreeccttiioonn
              Factor multiplied with ssppeeeedd at which the player attempts to
              play the file. Usually it's exactly 1. (Display sync mode will
              make this useful.)

              OSD formatting will display it in the form of ++11..2233445566%%, with
              the number being ((rraaww -- 11)) ** 110000 for the given raw property
              value.

       ddiissppllaayy--ssyynncc--aaccttiivvee
              Whether ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc==ddiissppllaayy is actually active.

       ffiilleennaammee
              Currently played file, with path stripped. If this is an URL,
              try to undo percent encoding as well. (The result is not
              necessarily correct, but looks better for display purposes. Use
              the ppaatthh property to get an unmodified filename.)

              This has a sub-property:

              ffiilleennaammee//nnoo--eexxtt
                     Like the ffiilleennaammee property, but if the text contains a ..,
                     strip all text after the last ... Usually this removes the
                     file extension.

       ffiillee--ssiizzee
              Length in bytes of the source file/stream. (This is the same as
              $${{ssttrreeaamm--eenndd}}. For segmented/multi-part files, this will return
              the size of the main or manifest file, whatever it is.)

       eessttiimmaatteedd--ffrraammee--ccoouunntt
              Total number of frames in current file.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This is only an estimate. (It's computed from two unreliable
                 quantities: fps and stream length.)

       eessttiimmaatteedd--ffrraammee--nnuummbbeerr
              Number of current frame in current stream.

              NNOOTTEE::
                 This is only an estimate. (It's computed from two unreliable
                 quantities: fps and possibly rounded timestamps.)

       ppiidd    Process-id of mpv.

       ppaatthh   Full absolute path of the currently played file.

       ssttrreeaamm--ooppeenn--ffiilleennaammee
              The full path to the currently played media. This is different
              from ppaatthh only in special cases. In particular, if ----yyttddll==yyeess is
              used, and the URL is detected by yyoouuttuubbee--ddll, then the script
              will set this property to the actual media URL. This property
              should be set only during the oonn__llooaadd or oonn__llooaadd__ffaaiill hooks,
              otherwise it will have no effect (or may do something
              implementation defined in the future). The property is reset if
              playback of the current media ends.

       mmeeddiiaa--ttiittllee
              If the currently played file has a ttiittllee tag, use that.

              Otherwise, return the ffiilleennaammee property.

       ffiillee--ffoorrmmaatt
              Symbolic name of the file format. In some cases, this is a
              comma-separated list of format names, e.g. mp4 is
              mmoovv,,mmpp44,,mm44aa,,33ggpp,,33gg22,,mmjj22 (the list may grow in the future for any
              format).

       ccuurrrreenntt--ddeemmuuxxeerr
              Name of the current demuxer. (This is useless.)

              (Renamed from ddeemmuuxxeerr.)

       ssttrreeaamm--ppaatthh
              Filename (full path) of the stream layer filename. (This is
              probably useless and is almost never different from ppaatthh.)

       ssttrreeaamm--ppooss
              Raw byte position in source stream. Technically, this returns
              the position of the most recent packet passed to a decoder.

       ssttrreeaamm--eenndd
              Raw end position in bytes in source stream.

       dduurraattiioonn
              Duration of the current file in seconds. If the duration is
              unknown, the property is unavailable. Note that the file
              duration is not always exactly known, so this is an estimate.

              This replaces the lleennggtthh property, which was deprecated after
              the mpv 0.9 release. (The semantics are the same.)

              This has a sub-property:

              dduurraattiioonn//ffuullll
                     dduurraattiioonn with milliseconds.

       aavvssyynncc Last A/V synchronization difference. Unavailable if audio or
              video is disabled.

       ttoottaall--aavvssyynncc--cchhaannggee
              Total A-V sync correction done. Unavailable if audio or video is
              disabled.

       ddeeccooddeerr--ffrraammee--ddrroopp--ccoouunntt
              Video frames dropped by decoder, because video is too far behind
              audio (when using ----ffrraammeeddrroopp==ddeeccooddeerr). Sometimes, this may be
              incremented in other situations, e.g. when video packets are
              damaged, or the decoder doesn't follow the usual rules.
              Unavailable if video is disabled.

       ffrraammee--ddrroopp--ccoouunntt
              Frames dropped by VO (when using ----ffrraammeeddrroopp==vvoo).

       mmiissttiimmeedd--ffrraammee--ccoouunntt
              Number of video frames that were not timed correctly in
              display-sync mode for the sake of keeping A/V sync. This does
              not include external circumstances, such as video rendering
              being too slow or the graphics driver somehow skipping a vsync.
              It does not include rounding errors either (which can happen
              especially with bad source timestamps). For example, using the
              ddiissppllaayy--ddeessyynncc mode should never change this value from 0.

       vvssyynncc--rraattiioo
              For how many vsyncs a frame is displayed on average. This is
              available if display-sync is active only. For 30 FPS video on a
              60 Hz screen, this will be 2. This is the moving average of what
              actually has been scheduled, so 24 FPS on 60 Hz will never
              remain exactly on 2.5, but jitter depending on the last frame
              displayed.

       vvoo--ddeellaayyeedd--ffrraammee--ccoouunntt
              Estimated number of frames delayed due to external circumstances
              in display-sync mode. Note that in general, mpv has to guess
              that this is happening, and the guess can be inaccurate.

       ppeerrcceenntt--ppooss ((RRWW))
              Position in current file (0-100). The advantage over using this
              instead of calculating it out of other properties is that it
              properly falls back to estimating the playback position from the
              byte position, if the file duration is not known.

       ttiimmee--ppooss ((RRWW))
              Position in current file in seconds.

              This has a sub-property:

              ttiimmee--ppooss//ffuullll
                     ttiimmee--ppooss with milliseconds.

       ttiimmee--ssttaarrtt
              Deprecated. Always returns 0. Before mpv 0.14, this used to
              return the start time of the file (could affect e.g. transport
              streams). See ----rreebbaassee--ssttaarrtt--ttiimmee option.

       ttiimmee--rreemmaaiinniinngg
              Remaining length of the file in seconds. Note that the file
              duration is not always exactly known, so this is an estimate.

              This has a sub-property:

              ttiimmee--rreemmaaiinniinngg//ffuullll
                     ttiimmee--rreemmaaiinniinngg with milliseconds.

       aauuddiioo--ppttss
              Current audio playback position in current file in seconds.
              Unlike ttiimmee--ppooss, this updates more often than once per frame.
              This is mostly equivalent to ttiimmee--ppooss for audio-only files
              however it also takes into account the audio driver delay. This
              can lead to negative values in certain cases, so in general you
              probably want to simply use ttiimmee--ppooss.

              This has a sub-property:

              aauuddiioo--ppttss//ffuullll
                     aauuddiioo--ppttss with milliseconds.

       ppllaayyttiimmee--rreemmaaiinniinngg
              ttiimmee--rreemmaaiinniinngg scaled by the current ssppeeeedd.

              This has a sub-property:

              ppllaayyttiimmee--rreemmaaiinniinngg//ffuullll
                     ppllaayyttiimmee--rreemmaaiinniinngg with milliseconds.

       ppllaayybbaacckk--ttiimmee ((RRWW))
              Alias for ttiimmee--ppooss.

              Prior to mpv 0.39.0, ttiimmee--ppooss and ppllaayybbaacckk--ttiimmee could report
              different values in certain edge cases.

              This has a sub-property:

              ppllaayybbaacckk--ttiimmee//ffuullll
                     ppllaayybbaacckk--ttiimmee with milliseconds.

       rreemmaaiinniinngg--ffiillee--llooooppss
              How many more times the current file is going to be looped. This
              is initialized from the value of ----lloooopp--ffiillee. This counts the
              number of times it causes the player to seek to the beginning of
              the file, so it is 0 the last the time is played. -1 corresponds
              to infinity.

       rreemmaaiinniinngg--aabb--llooooppss
              How many more times the current A-B loop is going to be looped,
              if one is active. This is initialized from the value of
              ----aabb--lloooopp--ccoouunntt. This counts the number of times it causes the
              player to seek to ----aabb--lloooopp--aa, so it is 0 the last the time the
              loop is played. -1 corresponds to infinity.

       cchhaapptteerr ((RRWW))
              Current chapter number. The number of the first chapter is 0.  A
              value of -1 indicates that the current playback position is
              before the start of the first chapter,

              Setting this property results in an absolute seek to the start
              of the chapter. However, if the property is changed with aadddd or
              ccyyccllee command which results in a decrement in value, it may go
              to the start of the current chapter instead of the previous
              chapter.  See ----cchhaapptteerr--sseeeekk--tthhrreesshhoolldd for details.

       eeddiittiioonn ((RRWW))
              Current edition number. Setting this property to a different
              value will restart playback. The number of the first edition is
              0.

              For Matroska files, this is the edition. For DVD/Blu-ray, this
              is the title.

              Before mpv 0.31.0, this showed the actual edition selected at
              runtime, if you didn't set the option or property manually. With
              mpv 0.31.0 and later, this strictly returns the user-set option
              or property value, and the ccuurrrreenntt--eeddiittiioonn property was added to
              return the runtime selected edition (this matters with
              ----eeddiittiioonn==aauuttoo, the default).

       ccuurrrreenntt--eeddiittiioonn
              Currently selected edition. This property is unavailable if no
              file is loaded, or the file has no editions. (Matroska files
              make a difference between having no editions and a single
              edition, which will be reflected by the property, although in
              practice it does not matter.)

       cchhaapptteerrss
              Number of chapters.

       eeddiittiioonnss
              Number of editions.

       eeddiittiioonn--lliisstt
              List of editions, current entry marked.

              This has a number of sub-properties. Replace NN with the 0-based
              edition index.

              eeddiittiioonn--lliisstt//ccoouunntt
                     Number of editions. If there are no editions, this can be
                     0 or 1 (1 if there's a useless dummy edition).

              eeddiittiioonn--lliisstt//NN//iidd
                     Edition ID as integer. Currently, this is the same as the
                     edition index.

              eeddiittiioonn--lliisstt//NN//ddeeffaauulltt
                     Whether this is the default edition.

              eeddiittiioonn--lliisstt//NN//ttiittllee
                     Edition title as stored in the file. Not always
                     available.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (for each edition)
                         "id"                MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "title"             MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "default"           MPV_FORMAT_FLAG

       mmeettaaddaattaa
              Metadata key/value pairs.

              If the property is accessed with Lua's mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee,
              this returns a table with metadata keys mapping to metadata
              values. If it is accessed with the client API, this returns a
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE__MMAAPP, with tag keys mapping to tag values.

              For OSD, it returns a formatted list. Trying to retrieve this
              property as a raw string doesn't work.

              This has a number of sub-properties:

              mmeettaaddaattaa//bbyy--kkeeyy//<<kkeeyy>>
                     Value of metadata entry <<kkeeyy>>.

              mmeettaaddaattaa//lliisstt//ccoouunntt
                     Number of metadata entries.

              mmeettaaddaattaa//lliisstt//NN//kkeeyy
                     Key name of the Nth metadata entry. (The first entry is
                     00).

              mmeettaaddaattaa//lliisstt//NN//vvaalluuee
                     Value of the Nth metadata entry.

              mmeettaaddaattaa//<<kkeeyy>>
                     Old version of mmeettaaddaattaa//bbyy--kkeeyy//<<kkeeyy>>. Use is discouraged,
                     because the metadata key string could conflict with other
                     sub-properties.

              The layout of this property might be subject to change.
              Suggestions are welcome how exactly this property should work.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                     (key and string value for each metadata entry)

       ffiilltteerreedd--mmeettaaddaattaa
              Like mmeettaaddaattaa, but includes only fields listed in the
              ----ddiissppllaayy--ttaaggss option. This is the same set of tags that is
              printed to the terminal.

       cchhaapptteerr--mmeettaaddaattaa
              Metadata of current chapter. Works similar to mmeettaaddaattaa property.
              It also allows the same access methods (using sub-properties).

              Per-chapter metadata is very rare. Usually, only the chapter
              name (ttiittllee) is set.

              For accessing other information, like chapter start, see the
              cchhaapptteerr--lliisstt property.

       vvff--mmeettaaddaattaa//<<ffiilltteerr--llaabbeell>>
              Metadata added by video filters. Accessed by the filter label,
              which, if not explicitly specified using the @@ffiilltteerr--llaabbeell::
              syntax, will be <<ffiilltteerr--nnaammee>>..NNNN.

              Works similar to mmeettaaddaattaa property. It allows the same access
              methods (using sub-properties).

              An example of this kind of metadata are the cropping parameters
              added by ----vvff==llaavvffii==ccrrooppddeetteecctt.

       aaff--mmeettaaddaattaa//<<ffiilltteerr--llaabbeell>>
              Equivalent to vvff--mmeettaaddaattaa//<<ffiilltteerr--llaabbeell>>, but for audio filters.

       ddeeiinntteerrllaaccee--aaccttiivvee
              Returns yyeess/true if mpv's deinterlacing filter is active. Note
              that it will not detect any manually inserted deinterlacing
              filters done via ----vvff.

       iiddllee--aaccttiivvee
              Returns yyeess/true if no file is loaded, but the player is staying
              around because of the ----iiddllee option.

              (Renamed from iiddllee.)

       ccoorree--iiddllee
              Whether the playback core is paused. This can differ from ppaauussee
              in special situations, such as when the player pauses itself due
              to low network cache.

              This also returns yyeess/true if playback is restarting or if
              nothing is playing at all. In other words, it's only nnoo/false if
              there's actually video playing. (Behavior since mpv 0.7.0.)

       ccaacchhee--ssppeeeedd
              Current I/O read speed between the cache and the lower layer
              (like network).  This gives the number bytes per seconds over a
              1 second window (using the type MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__IINNTT6644 for the client
              API).

              This is the same as ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--ssttaattee//rraaww--iinnppuutt--rraattee.

       ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--dduurraattiioonn
              Approximate duration of video buffered in the demuxer, in
              seconds. The guess is very unreliable, and often the property
              will not be available at all, even if data is buffered.

       ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--ttiimmee
              Approximate time of video buffered in the demuxer, in seconds.
              Same as ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--dduurraattiioonn but returns the last timestamp of
              buffered data in demuxer.

       ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--iiddllee
              Whether the demuxer is idle, which means that the demuxer cache
              is filled to the requested amount, and is currently not reading
              more data.

       ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--ssttaattee
              Each entry in sseeeekkaabbllee--rraannggeess represents a region in the demuxer
              cache that can be seeked to, with a ssttaarrtt and eenndd fields
              containing the respective timestamps. If there are multiple
              demuxers active, this only returns information about the "main"
              demuxer, but might be changed in future to return unified
              information about all demuxers. The ranges are in arbitrary
              order. Often, ranges will overlap for a bit, before being
              joined.  In broken corner cases, ranges may overlap all over the
              place.

              The end of a seek range is usually smaller than the value
              returned by the ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--ttiimmee property, because that
              property returns the guessed buffering amount, while the seek
              ranges represent the buffered data that can actually be used for
              cached seeking.

              bbooff--ccaacchheedd indicates whether the seek range with the lowest
              timestamp points to the beginning of the stream (BOF). This
              implies you cannot seek before this position at all. eeooff--ccaacchheedd
              indicates whether the seek range with the highest timestamp
              points to the end of the stream (EOF). If both bbooff--ccaacchheedd and
              eeooff--ccaacchheedd are true, and there's only 1 cache range, the entire
              stream is cached.

              ffww--bbyytteess is the number of bytes of packets buffered in the range
              starting from the current decoding position. This is a rough
              estimate (may not account correctly for various overhead), and
              stops at the demuxer position (it ignores seek ranges after it).

              ffiillee--ccaacchhee--bbyytteess is the number of bytes stored in the file
              cache. This includes all overhead, and possibly unused data
              (like pruned data). This member is missing if the file cache
              wasn't enabled with ----ccaacchhee--oonn--ddiisskk==yyeess.

              ccaacchhee--eenndd is ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--ttiimmee. Missing if unavailable.

              rreeaaddeerr--ppttss is the approximate timestamp of the start of the
              buffered range. Missing if unavailable.

              ccaacchhee--dduurraattiioonn is ddeemmuuxxeerr--ccaacchhee--dduurraattiioonn. Missing if
              unavailable.

              rraaww--iinnppuutt--rraattee is the estimated input rate of the network layer
              (or any other byte-oriented input layer) in bytes per second.
              May be inaccurate or missing.

              ttss--ppeerr--ssttrreeaamm is an array containing an entry for each stream
              type: video, audio, and subtitle. For each stream type, the
              details for the demuxer cache for that stream type are available
              as ccaacchhee--dduurraattiioonn, rreeaaddeerr--ppttss and ccaacchhee--eenndd.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                     "seekable-ranges"   MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                         MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                             "start"             MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                             "end"               MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "bof-cached"        MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                     "eof-cached"        MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                     "fw-bytes"          MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "file-cache-bytes"  MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "cache-end"         MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "reader-pts"        MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "cache-duration"    MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "raw-input-rate"    MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "ts-per-stream"     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                         MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                               "type"            MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                               "cache-duration"  MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                               "reader-pts"      MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                               "cache-end"       MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE

              Other fields (might be changed or removed in the future):

              eeooff    Whether the reader thread has hit the end of the file.

              uunnddeerrrruunn
                     Whether the reader thread could not satisfy a decoder's
                     request for a new packet.

              iiddllee   Whether the thread is currently not reading.

              ttoottaall--bbyytteess
                     Sum of packet bytes (plus some overhead estimation) of
                     the entire packet queue, including cached seekable
                     ranges.

       ddeemmuuxxeerr--vviiaa--nneettwwoorrkk
              Whether the stream demuxed via the main demuxer is most likely
              played via network. What constitutes "network" is not always
              clear, might be used for other types of untrusted streams, could
              be wrong in certain cases, and its definition might be changing.
              Also, external files (like separate audio files or streams) do
              not influence the value of this property (currently).

       ddeemmuuxxeerr--ssttaarrtt--ttiimmee
              The start time reported by the demuxer in fractional seconds.

       ppaauusseedd--ffoorr--ccaacchhee
              Whether playback is paused because of waiting for the cache.

       ccaacchhee--bbuuffffeerriinngg--ssttaattee
              The percentage (0-100) of the cache fill status until the player
              will unpause (related to ppaauusseedd--ffoorr--ccaacchhee).

       eeooff--rreeaacchheedd
              Whether the end of playback was reached. Note that this is
              usually interesting only if ----kkeeeepp--ooppeenn is enabled, since
              otherwise the player will immediately play the next file (or
              exit or enter idle mode), and in these cases the eeooff--rreeaacchheedd
              property will logically be cleared immediately after it's set.

       sseeeekkiinngg
              Whether the player is currently seeking, or otherwise trying to
              restart playback. (It's possible that it returns yyeess/true while
              a file is loaded. This is because the same underlying code is
              used for seeking and resyncing.)

       mmiixxeerr--aaccttiivvee
              Whether the audio mixer is active.

              This option is relatively useless. Before mpv 0.18.1, it could
              be used to infer behavior of the vvoolluummee property.

       aaoo--vvoolluummee ((RRWW))
              System volume. This property is available only if mpv audio
              output is currently active, and only if the underlying
              implementation supports volume control. What this option does,
              or how the value is interpreted depends on the API. For example,
              on ALSA this usually changes system-wide audio volume on a
              linear curve, while with PulseAudio this controls
              per-application volume on a cubic curve.

       aaoo--mmuuttee ((RRWW))
              Similar to aaoo--vvoolluummee, but controls the mute state. May be
              unimplemented even if aaoo--vvoolluummee works.

       aauuddiioo--ppaarraammss
              Audio format as output by the audio decoder.  This has a number
              of sub-properties:

              aauuddiioo--ppaarraammss//ffoorrmmaatt
                     The sample format as string. This uses the same names as
                     used in other places of mpv.

              aauuddiioo--ppaarraammss//ssaammpplleerraattee
                     Samplerate.

              aauuddiioo--ppaarraammss//cchhaannnneellss
                     The channel layout as a string. This is similar to what
                     the ----aauuddiioo--cchhaannnneellss accepts.

              aauuddiioo--ppaarraammss//hhrr--cchhaannnneellss
                     As cchhaannnneellss, but instead of the possibly cryptic actual
                     layout sent to the audio device, return a hopefully more
                     human readable form.  (Usually only
                     aauuddiioo--oouutt--ppaarraammss//hhrr--cchhaannnneellss makes sense.)

              aauuddiioo--ppaarraammss//cchhaannnneell--ccoouunntt
                     Number of audio channels. This is redundant to the
                     cchhaannnneellss field described above.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                     "format"            MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "samplerate"        MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "channels"          MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "channel-count"     MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "hr-channels"       MPV_FORMAT_STRING

       aauuddiioo--oouutt--ppaarraammss
              Same as aauuddiioo--ppaarraammss, but the format of the data written to the
              audio API.

       ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx
              Redirects to vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx. This parameter (as well
              as similar ones) can be overridden with the ffoorrmmaatt video filter.

       ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx--iinnppuutt--rraannggee
              See ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx.

       ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx--pprriimmaarriieess
              See ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx.

       hhwwddeecc ((RRWW))
              Reflects the ----hhwwddeecc option.

              Writing to it may change the currently used hardware decoder, if
              possible.  (Internally, the player may reinitialize the decoder,
              and will perform a seek to refresh the video properly.) You can
              watch the other hwdec properties to see whether this was
              successful.

              Unlike in mpv 0.9.x and before, this does not return the
              currently active hardware decoder. Since mpv 0.18.0,
              hhwwddeecc--ccuurrrreenntt is available for this purpose.

       hhwwddeecc--ccuurrrreenntt
              The current hardware decoding in use. If decoding is active,
              return one of the values used by the hhwwddeecc option/property.
              nnoo/false indicates software decoding. If no decoder is loaded,
              the property is unavailable.

       hhwwddeecc--iinntteerroopp
              This returns the currently loaded hardware decoding/output
              interop driver.  This is known only once the VO has opened (and
              possibly later). With some VOs (like ggppuu), this might be never
              known in advance, but only when the decoder attempted to create
              the hw decoder successfully. (Using ----ggppuu--hhwwddeecc--iinntteerroopp can load
              it eagerly.) If there are multiple drivers loaded, they will be
              separated by ,,.

              If no VO is active or no interop driver is known, this property
              is unavailable.

              This does not necessarily use the same values as hhwwddeecc. There
              can be multiple interop drivers for the same hardware decoder,
              depending on platform and VO.

       wwiiddtthh,, hheeiigghhtt
              Video size. This uses the size of the video as decoded, or if no
              video frame has been decoded yet, the (possibly incorrect)
              container indicated size.

       vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss
              Video parameters, as output by the decoder (with overrides like
              aspect etc. applied). This has a number of sub-properties:

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ppiixxeellffoorrmmaatt
                     The pixel format as string. This uses the same names as
                     used in other places of mpv.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//hhww--ppiixxeellffoorrmmaatt
                     The underlying pixel format as string. This is relevant
                     for some cases of hardware decoding and unavailable
                     otherwise.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//aavveerraaggee--bbpppp
                     Average bits-per-pixel as integer. Subsampled planar
                     formats use a different resolution, which is the reason
                     this value can sometimes be odd or confusing. Can be
                     unavailable with some formats.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ww,, vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//hh
                     Video size as integers, with no aspect correction
                     applied.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ddww,, vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ddhh
                     Video size as integers, scaled for correct aspect ratio.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ccrroopp--xx,, vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ccrroopp--yy
                     Crop offset of the source video frame.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ccrroopp--ww,, vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ccrroopp--hh
                     Video size after cropping.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//aassppeecctt
                     Display aspect ratio as double.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//aassppeecctt--nnaammee
                     Display aspect ratio name as string. The name corresponds
                     to motion picture film format that introduced given
                     aspect ratio in film.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ppaarr
                     Pixel aspect ratio.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ssaarr
                     Storage aspect ratio.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ssaarr--nnaammee
                     Storage aspect ratio name as string.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ccoolloorrmmaattrriixx
                     The colormatrix in use as string. (Exact values subject
                     to change.)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ccoolloorrlleevveellss
                     The colorlevels as string. (Exact values subject to
                     change.)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//pprriimmaarriieess
                     The primaries in use as string. (Exact values subject to
                     change.)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ggaammmmaa
                     The gamma function in use as string. (Exact values
                     subject to change.)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//ssiigg--ppeeaakk ((ddeepprreeccaatteedd))
                     The video file's tagged signal peak as float.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//lliigghhtt
                     The light type in use as a string. (Exact values subject
                     to change.)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//cchhrroommaa--llooccaattiioonn
                     Chroma location as string. (Exact values subject to
                     change.)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//rroottaattee
                     Intended display rotation in degrees (clockwise).

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//sstteerreeoo--iinn
                     Source file stereo 3D mode. (See the ffoorrmmaatt video
                     filter's sstteerreeoo--iinn option.)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//aallpphhaa
                     Alpha type. If the format has no alpha channel, this will
                     be unavailable (but in future releases, it could change
                     to nnoo). If alpha is present, this is set to ssttrraaiigghhtt or
                     pprreemmuull.

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//mmiinn--lluummaa
                     Minimum luminance, as reported by HDR10 metadata (in
                     cd/m²)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//mmaaxx--lluummaa
                     Maximum luminance, as reported by HDR10 metadata (in
                     cd/m²)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//mmaaxx--ccllll
                     Maximum content light level, as reported by HDR10
                     metadata (in cd/m²)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//mmaaxx--ffaallll
                     Maximum frame average light level, as reported by HDR10
                     metadata (in cd/m²)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//sscceennee--mmaaxx--rr
                     MaxRGB of a scene for R component, as reported by HDR10+
                     metadata (in cd/m²)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//sscceennee--mmaaxx--gg
                     MaxRGB of a scene for G component, as reported by HDR10+
                     metadata (in cd/m²)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//sscceennee--mmaaxx--bb
                     MaxRGB of a scene for B component, as reported by HDR10+
                     metadata (in cd/m²)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//mmaaxx--ppqq--yy
                     Maximum PQ luminance of a frame, as reported by peak
                     detection (in PQ, 0-1)

              vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss//aavvgg--ppqq--yy
                     Average PQ luminance of a frame, as reported by peak
                     detection (in PQ, 0-1)

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                     "pixelformat"       MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "hw-pixelformat"    MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "w"                 MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "h"                 MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "dw"                MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "dh"                MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "aspect"            MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "par"               MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "colormatrix"       MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "colorlevels"       MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "primaries"         MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "gamma"             MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "sig-peak"          MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "light"             MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "chroma-location"   MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "rotate"            MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "stereo-in"         MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "average-bpp"       MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                     "alpha"             MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                     "min-luma"          MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "max-luma"          MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "max-cll"           MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "max-fall"          MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "scene-max-r"       MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "scene-max-g"       MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "scene-max-b"       MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "max-pq-y"          MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                     "avg-pq-y"          MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE

       ddwwiiddtthh,, ddhheeiigghhtt
              Video display size. This is the video size after filters and
              aspect scaling have been applied. The actual video window size
              can still be different from this, e.g. if the user resized the
              video window manually.

              These have the same values as vviiddeeoo--oouutt--ppaarraammss//ddww and
              vviiddeeoo--oouutt--ppaarraammss//ddhh.

       vviiddeeoo--ddeecc--ppaarraammss
              Exactly like vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss, but no overrides applied.

       vviiddeeoo--oouutt--ppaarraammss
              Same as vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss, but after video filters have been applied.
              If there are no video filters in use, this will contain the same
              values as vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss. Note that this is still not necessarily
              what the video window uses, since the user can change the window
              size, and all real VOs do their own scaling independently from
              the filter chain.

              Has the same sub-properties as vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss.

       vviiddeeoo--ttaarrggeett--ppaarraammss
              Same as vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss, but with the target properties that VO
              outputs to.

              Has the same sub-properties as vviiddeeoo--ppaarraammss.

       vviiddeeoo--ffrraammee--iinnffoo
              Approximate information of the current frame. Note that if any
              of these are used on OSD, the information might be off by a few
              frames due to OSD redrawing and frame display being somewhat
              disconnected, and you might have to pause and force a redraw.

              This has a number of sub-properties:

              vviiddeeoo--ffrraammee--iinnffoo//ppiiccttuurree--ttyyppee
                     The type of the picture. It can be "I" (intra), "P"
                     (predicted), "B" (bi-dir predicted) or unavailable.

              vviiddeeoo--ffrraammee--iinnffoo//iinntteerrllaacceedd
                     Whether the content of the frame is interlaced.

              vviiddeeoo--ffrraammee--iinnffoo//ttffff
                     If the content is interlaced, whether the top field is
                     displayed first.

              vviiddeeoo--ffrraammee--iinnffoo//rreeppeeaatt
                     Whether the frame must be delayed when decoding.

              vviiddeeoo--ffrraammee--iinnffoo//ggoopp--ttiimmeeccooddee
                     String with the GOP timecode encoded in the frame.

              vviiddeeoo--ffrraammee--iinnffoo//ssmmppttee--ttiimmeeccooddee
                     String with the SMPTE timecode encoded in the frame.

              vviiddeeoo--ffrraammee--iinnffoo//eessttiimmaatteedd--ssmmppttee--ttiimmeeccooddee
                     Estimated timecode based on the current playback position
                     and frame count.

       ccoonnttaaiinneerr--ffppss
              Container FPS. This can easily contain bogus values. For videos
              that use modern container formats or video codecs, this will
              often be incorrect.

              (Renamed from ffppss.)

       eessttiimmaatteedd--vvff--ffppss
              Estimated/measured FPS of the video filter chain output. (If no
              filters are used, this corresponds to decoder output.) This uses
              the average of the 10 past frame durations to calculate the FPS.
              It will be inaccurate if frame-dropping is involved (such as
              when framedrop is explicitly enabled, or after precise seeking).
              Files with imprecise timestamps (such as Matroska) might lead to
              unstable results.

       ccuurrrreenntt--wwiinnddooww--ssccaallee ((RRWW))
              The wwiinnddooww--ssccaallee value calculated from the current window size.
              This has the same value as wwiinnddooww--ssccaallee if the window size was
              not changed since setting the option, and the window size was
              not restricted in other ways. If the window is fullscreened,
              this will return the scale value calculated from the last
              non-fullscreen size of the window. The property is unavailable
              if no video is active.

              It is also possible to write to this property. This has the same
              behavior as writing wwiinnddooww--ssccaallee. Note that writing to
              ccuurrrreenntt--wwiinnddooww--ssccaallee will not affect the value of wwiinnddooww--ssccaallee.

       ffooccuusseedd
              Whether the window has focus. Might not be supported by all VOs.

       aammbbiieenntt--lliigghhtt
              Ambient lighting condition in lux. (macOS only)

       ddiissppllaayy--nnaammeess
              Names of the displays that the mpv window covers. On X11, these
              are the xrandr names (LVDS1, HDMI1, DP1, VGA1, etc.). On
              Windows, these are the GDI names (\.DISPLAY1, \.DISPLAY2, etc.)
              and the first display in the list will be the one that Windows
              considers associated with the window (as determined by the
              MonitorFromWindow API.) On macOS these are the Display Product
              Names as used in the System Information with a serial number in
              parentheses and only one display name is returned since a window
              can only be on one screen. On Wayland, these are the wl_output
              names if protocol version >= 4 is used (LVDS-1, HDMI-A-1, X11-1,
              etc.), or the wl_output model reported by the geometry event if
              protocol version < 4 is used.

       ddiissppllaayy--ffppss
              The refresh rate of the current display. Currently, this is the
              lowest FPS of any display covered by the video, as retrieved by
              the underlying system APIs (e.g. xrandr on X11). It is not the
              measured FPS. It's not necessarily available on all platforms.
              Note that any of the listed facts may change any time without a
              warning.

       eessttiimmaatteedd--ddiissppllaayy--ffppss
              The actual rate at which display refreshes seem to occur,
              measured by system time. Only available if display-sync mode (as
              selected by ----vviiddeeoo--ssyynncc) is active.

       vvssyynncc--jjiitttteerr
              Estimated deviation factor of the vsync duration.

       ddiissppllaayy--wwiiddtthh,, ddiissppllaayy--hheeiigghhtt
              The current display's horizontal and vertical resolution in
              pixels. Whether or not these values update as the mpv window
              changes displays depends on the windowing backend. It may not be
              available on all platforms.

       ddiissppllaayy--hhiiddppii--ssccaallee
              The HiDPI scale factor as reported by the windowing backend. If
              no VO is active, or if the VO does not report a value, this
              property is unavailable.  It may be saner to report an absolute
              DPI, however, this is the way HiDPI support is implemented on
              most OS APIs. See also ----hhiiddppii--wwiinnddooww--ssccaallee.

       oossdd--wwiiddtthh,, oossdd--hheeiigghhtt
              Last known OSD width (can be 0). This is needed if you want to
              use the oovveerrllaayy--aadddd command. It gives you the actual OSD/window
              size (not including decorations drawn by the OS window manager).

              Alias to oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//ww and oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//hh.

       oossdd--ppaarr
              Last known OSD display pixel aspect (can be 0).

              Alias to oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//oossdd--ppaarr.

       oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss
              Last known OSD dimensions.

              Has the following sub-properties (which can be read as
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE or Lua table with mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee):

              oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//ww
                     Size of the VO window in OSD render units (usually
                     pixels, but may be scaled pixels with VOs like xxvv).

              oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//hh
                     Size of the VO window in OSD render units,

              oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//ppaarr
                     Pixel aspect ratio of the OSD (usually 1).

              oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//aassppeecctt
                     Display aspect ratio of the VO window. (Computing from
                     the properties above.)

              oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//mmtt,, oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//mmbb,, oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//mmll,,
              oossdd--ddiimmeennssiioonnss//mmrr
                     OSD to video margins (top, bottom, left, right). This
                     describes the area into which the video is rendered.

              Any of these properties may be unavailable or set to dummy
              values if the VO window is not created or visible.

       tteerrmm--ssiizzee
              The current terminal size.

              This has two sub-properties.

              tteerrmm--ssiizzee//ww
                     width of the terminal in cells

              tteerrmm--ssiizzee//hh
                     height of the terminal in cells

              This property is not observable. Reacting to size changes
              requires polling.

       wwiinnddooww--iidd
              Read-only - mpv's window id. May not always be available, i.e
              due to window not being opened yet or not being supported by the
              VO.

       mmoouussee--ppooss
              Read-only - last known mouse position, normalized to OSD
              dimensions.

              Has the following sub-properties (which can be read as
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE or Lua table with mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee):

              mmoouussee--ppooss//xx,, mmoouussee--ppooss//yy
                     Last known coordinates of the mouse pointer.

              mmoouussee--ppooss//hhoovveerr
                     Boolean - whether the mouse pointer hovers the video
                     window. The coordinates should be ignored when this value
                     is false, because the video backends update them only
                     when the pointer hovers the window.

       ttoouucchh--ppooss
              Read-only - last known touch point positions, normalized to OSD
              dimensions.

              This has a number of sub-properties. Replace NN with the 0-based
              touch point index. Whenever a new finger touches the screen, a
              new touch point is added to the list of touch points with the
              smallest unused NN available.

              ttoouucchh--ppooss//ccoouunntt
                     Number of active touch points.

              ttoouucchh--ppooss//NN//xx,, ttoouucchh--ppooss//NN//yy
                     Position of the Nth touch point.

              ttoouucchh--ppooss//NN//iidd
                     Unique identifier of the touch point. This can be used to
                     identify individual touch points when their indexes
                     change.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (for each touch point)
                         "x"        MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "y"        MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "id"       MPV_FORMAT_INT64

       ssuubb--aassss--eexxttrraaddaattaa
              The current ASS subtitle track's extradata. There is no
              formatting done.  The extradata is returned as a string as-is.
              This property is not available for non-ASS subtitle tracks.

       ssuubb--tteexxtt
              The current subtitle text regardless of sub visibility.
              Formatting is stripped. If the subtitle is not text-based (i.e.
              DVD/BD subtitles), an empty string is returned.

              This has sub-properties for different formats:

              ssuubb--tteexxtt//aassss
                     Like ssuubb--tteexxtt, but return the text in ASS format. Text
                     subtitles in other formats are converted. For native ASS
                     subtitles, events that do not contain any text (but
                     vector drawings etc.) are not filtered out. If multiple
                     events match with the current playback time, they are
                     concatenated with line breaks. Contains only the "Text"
                     part of the events.

                     This property is not enough to render ASS subtitles
                     correctly, because ASS header and per-event metadata are
                     not returned. Use //aassss--ffuullll for that.

              ssuubb--tteexxtt//aassss--ffuullll
                     Like ssuubb--tteexxtt--aassss, but return the full event with all
                     fields, formatted as lines in a .ass text file. Use with
                     ssuubb--aassss--eexxttrraaddaattaa for style information.

       ssuubb--tteexxtt--aassss ((ddeepprreeccaatteedd))
              Deprecated alias for ssuubb--tteexxtt//aassss.

       sseeccoonnddaarryy--ssuubb--tteexxtt
              Same as ssuubb--tteexxtt (with the same sub-properties), but for the
              secondary subtitles.

       ssuubb--ssttaarrtt
              The current subtitle start time (in seconds). If there's
              multiple current subtitles, returns the first start time. If no
              current subtitle is present null is returned instead.

              This has a sub-property:

              ssuubb--ssttaarrtt//ffuullll
                     ssuubb--ssttaarrtt with milliseconds.

       sseeccoonnddaarryy--ssuubb--ssttaarrtt
              Same as ssuubb--ssttaarrtt, but for the secondary subtitles.

       ssuubb--eenndd
              The current subtitle end time (in seconds). If there's multiple
              current subtitles, return the last end time. If no current
              subtitle is present, or if it's present but has unknown or
              incorrect duration, null is returned instead.

              This has a sub-property:

              ssuubb--eenndd//ffuullll
                     ssuubb--eenndd with milliseconds.

       sseeccoonnddaarryy--ssuubb--eenndd
              Same as ssuubb--eenndd, but for the secondary subtitles.

       ppllaayylliisstt--ppooss ((RRWW))
              Current position on playlist. The first entry is on position 0.
              Writing to this property may start playback at the new position.

              In some cases, this is not necessarily the currently playing
              file. See explanation of ccuurrrreenntt and ppllaayyiinngg flags in ppllaayylliisstt.

              If there the playlist is empty, or if it's non-empty, but no
              entry is "current", this property returns -1. Likewise, writing
              -1 will put the player into idle mode (or exit playback if idle
              mode is not enabled). If an out of range index is written to the
              property, this behaves as if writing -1.  (Before mpv 0.33.0,
              instead of returning -1, this property was unavailable if no
              playlist entry was current.)

              Writing the current value back to the property will have no
              effect.  Use ppllaayylliisstt--ppllaayy--iinnddeexx to restart the playback of the
              current entry if desired.

       ppllaayylliisstt--ppooss--11 ((RRWW))
              Same as ppllaayylliisstt--ppooss, but 1-based.

       ppllaayylliisstt--ccuurrrreenntt--ppooss ((RRWW))
              Index of the "current" item on playlist. This usually, but not
              necessarily, the currently playing item (see
              ppllaayylliisstt--ppllaayyiinngg--ppooss). Depending on the exact internal state of
              the player, it may refer to the playlist item to play next, or
              the playlist item used to determine what to play next.

              For reading, this is exactly the same as ppllaayylliisstt--ppooss.

              For writing, this _o_n_l_y sets the position of the "current" item,
              without stopping playback of the current file (or starting
              playback, if this is done in idle mode). Use -1 to remove the
              current flag.

              This property is only vaguely useful. If set during playback, it
              will typically cause the playlist entry _a_f_t_e_r it to be played
              next. Another possibly odd observable state is that if
              ppllaayylliisstt--nneexxtt is run during playback, this property is set to
              the playlist entry to play next (unlike the previous case).
              There is an internal flag that decides whether the current
              playlist entry or the next one should be played, and this flag
              is currently inaccessible for API users. (Whether this behavior
              will kept is possibly subject to change.)

       ppllaayylliisstt--ppllaayyiinngg--ppooss
              Index of the "playing" item on playlist. A playlist item is
              "playing" if it's being loaded, actually playing, or being
              unloaded. This property is set during the MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__SSTTAARRTT__FFIILLEE
              (ssttaarrtt--ffiillee) and the MMPPVV__EEVVEENNTT__SSTTAARRTT__EENNDD (eenndd--ffiillee) events.
              Outside of that, it returns -1. If the playlist entry was
              somehow removed during playback, but playback hasn't stopped
              yet, or is in progress of being stopped, it also returns -1.
              (This can happen at least during state transitions.)

              In the "playing" state, this is usually the same as
              ppllaayylliisstt--ppooss, except during state changes, or if
              ppllaayylliisstt--ccuurrrreenntt--ppooss was written explicitly.

       ppllaayylliisstt--ccoouunntt
              Number of total playlist entries.

       ppllaayylliisstt--ppaatthh
              The original path of the playlist for the current entry before
              mpv expanded the entries. Unavailable if the file was not
              originally associated with a playlist in some way.

       ppllaayylliisstt
              Playlist, current entry marked. Currently, the raw property
              value is useless.

              This has a number of sub-properties. Replace NN with the 0-based
              playlist entry index.

              ppllaayylliisstt//ccoouunntt
                     Number of playlist entries (same as ppllaayylliisstt--ccoouunntt).

              ppllaayylliisstt//NN//ffiilleennaammee
                     Filename of the Nth entry.

              ppllaayylliisstt//NN//ppllaayyiinngg
                     yyeess/true if the ppllaayylliisstt--ppllaayyiinngg--ppooss property points to
                     this entry, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise.

              ppllaayylliisstt//NN//ccuurrrreenntt
                     yyeess/true if the ppllaayylliisstt--ccuurrrreenntt--ppooss property points to
                     this entry, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise.

              ppllaayylliisstt//NN//ttiittllee
                     Name of the Nth entry. Available if the playlist file
                     contains such fields and mpv's parser supports it for the
                     given playlist format, or if the playlist entry has been
                     opened before and a media-title other than filename has
                     been acquired.

              ppllaayylliisstt//NN//iidd
                     Unique ID for this entry. This is an automatically
                     assigned integer ID that is unique for the entire life
                     time of the current mpv core instance. Other commands,
                     events, etc. use this as ppllaayylliisstt__eennttrryy__iidd fields.

              ppllaayylliisstt//NN//ppllaayylliisstt--ppaatthh
                     The original path of the playlist for this entry before
                     mpv expanded it. Unavailable if the file was not
                     originally associated with a playlist in some way.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (for each playlist entry)
                         "filename"  MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "current"   MPV_FORMAT_FLAG (might be missing; since mpv 0.7.0)
                         "playing"   MPV_FORMAT_FLAG (same)
                         "title"     MPV_FORMAT_STRING (optional)
                         "id"        MPV_FORMAT_INT64

       ttrraacckk--lliisstt
              List of audio/video/sub tracks, current entry marked.

              This has a number of sub-properties. Replace NN with the 0-based
              track index.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//ccoouunntt
                     Total number of tracks.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//vviiddeeoo
                     The list of video tracks. This is only usable for
                     printing and its value can't be retrieved.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//aauuddiioo
                     The list of audio tracks. This is only usable for
                     printing and its value can't be retrieved.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//ssuubb
                     The list of sub tracks. This is only usable for printing
                     and its value can't be retrieved.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//iidd
                     The ID as it's used for ----ssiidd/----aaiidd/----vviidd. This is unique
                     within tracks of the same type (sub/audio/video), but
                     otherwise not.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ttyyppee
                     String describing the media type. One of aauuddiioo, vviiddeeoo,
                     ssuubb.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ssrrcc--iidd
                     Track ID as used in the source file. Not always
                     available. (It is missing if the format has no native ID,
                     if the track is a pseudo-track that does not exist in
                     this way in the actual file, or if the format is handled
                     by libavformat, and the format was not whitelisted as
                     having track IDs.)

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ttiittllee
                     Track title as it is stored in the file. Not always
                     available.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//llaanngg
                     Track language as identified by the file. Not always
                     available.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//iimmaaggee
                     yyeess/true if this is a video track that consists of a
                     single picture, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise. The
                     heuristic used to determine if a stream is an image
                     doesn't attempt to detect images in codecs normally used
                     for videos. Otherwise, it is reliable.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//aallbbuummaarrtt
                     yyeess/true if this is an image embedded in an audio file or
                     external cover art, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeeffaauulltt
                     yyeess/true if the track has the default flag set in the
                     file, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ffoorrcceedd
                     yyeess/true if the track has the forced flag set in the
                     file, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeeppeennddeenntt
                     yyeess/true if the track has the dependent flag set in the
                     file, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//vviissuuaall--iimmppaaiirreedd
                     yyeess/true if the track has the visual impaired flag set in
                     the file, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//hheeaarriinngg--iimmppaaiirreedd
                     yyeess/true if the track has the hearing impaired flag set
                     in the file, nnoo/false or unavailable otherwise.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//hhllss--bbiittrraattee
                     The bitrate of the HLS stream, if available.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//pprrooggrraamm--iidd
                     The program ID of the HLS stream, if available.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ccooddeecc
                     The codec name used by this track, for example hh226644.
                     Unavailable in some rare cases.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ccooddeecc--ddeesscc
                     The codec descriptive name used by this track.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ccooddeecc--pprrooffiillee
                     The codec profile used by this track. Available only if
                     the track has been already decoded.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//eexxtteerrnnaall
                     yyeess/true if the track is an external file, nnoo/false or
                     unavailable otherwise. This is set for separate subtitle
                     files.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//eexxtteerrnnaall--ffiilleennaammee
                     The filename if the track is from an external file,
                     unavailable otherwise.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//sseelleecctteedd
                     yyeess/true if the track is currently decoded, nnoo/false or
                     unavailable otherwise.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//mmaaiinn--sseelleeccttiioonn
                     It indicates the selection order of tracks for the same
                     type.  If a track is not selected, or is selected by the
                     ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx, it is not available. For subtitle
                     tracks, 00 represents the ssiidd, and 11 represents the
                     sseeccoonnddaarryy--ssiidd.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ffff--iinnddeexx
                     The stream index as usually used by the FFmpeg utilities.
                     Note that this can be potentially wrong if a demuxer
                     other than libavformat (----ddeemmuuxxeerr==llaavvff) is used. For mkv
                     files, the index will usually match even if the default
                     (builtin) demuxer is used, but there is no hard
                     guarantee.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeeccooddeerr
                     If this track is being decoded, the short decoder name,

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeeccooddeerr--ddeesscc
                     If this track is being decoded, the human-readable
                     decoder name,

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--ww,, ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--hh
                     Video size hint as indicated by the container. (Not
                     always accurate.)

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--ccrroopp--xx,, ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--ccrroopp--yy
                     Crop offset of the source video frame.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--ccrroopp--ww,, ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--ccrroopp--hh
                     Video size after cropping.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--cchhaannnneell--ccoouunntt
                     Number of audio channels as indicated by the container.
                     (Not always accurate - in particular, the track could be
                     decoded as a different number of channels.)

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--cchhaannnneellss
                     Channel layout as indicated by the container. (Not always
                     accurate.)

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--ssaammpplleerraattee
                     Audio sample rate as indicated by the container. (Not
                     always accurate.)

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--ffppss
                     Video FPS as indicated by the container. (Not always
                     accurate.)

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--bbiittrraattee
                     Audio average bitrate, in bits per second. (Not always
                     accurate.)

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--rroottaattiioonn
                     Video clockwise rotation metadata, in degrees.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--ppaarr
                     Pixel aspect ratio.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ffoorrmmaatt--nnaammee
                     Short name for format from ffmpeg. If the track is audio,
                     this will be the name of the sample format. If the track
                     is video, this will be the name of the pixel format.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//aauuddiioo--cchhaannnneellss ((ddeepprreeccaatteedd))
                     Deprecated alias for ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddeemmuuxx--cchhaannnneell--ccoouunntt.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//rreeppllaayyggaaiinn--ttrraacckk--ppeeaakk,,
              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//rreeppllaayyggaaiinn--ttrraacckk--ggaaiinn
                     Per-track replaygain values. Only available for audio
                     tracks with corresponding information stored in the
                     source file.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//rreeppllaayyggaaiinn--aallbbuumm--ppeeaakk,,
              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//rreeppllaayyggaaiinn--aallbbuumm--ggaaiinn
                     Per-album replaygain values. If the file has per-track
                     but no per-album information, the per-album values will
                     be copied from the per-track values currently. It's
                     possible that future mpv versions will make these
                     properties unavailable instead in this case.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddoollbbyy--vviissiioonn--pprrooffiillee,,
              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//ddoollbbyy--vviissiioonn--lleevveell
                     Dolby Vision profile and level. May not be available if
                     the container does not provide this information.

              ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//mmeettaaddaattaa,,
                     Works like the mmeettaaddaattaa property, but it accesses
                     metadata that is set per track/stream instead of global
                     values for the entire file.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (for each track)
                         "id"                MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "type"              MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "src-id"            MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "title"             MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "lang"              MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "image"             MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "albumart"          MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "default"           MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "forced"            MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "dependent"         MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "visual-impaired"   MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "hearing-impaired"  MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "hls-bitrate"       MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "program-id"        MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "selected"          MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "main-selection"    MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "external"          MPV_FORMAT_FLAG
                         "external-filename" MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "codec"             MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "codec-desc"        MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "codec-profile"     MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "ff-index"          MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "decoder"           MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "decoder-desc"      MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "demux-w"           MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-h"           MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-crop-x"      MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-crop-y"      MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-crop-w"      MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-crop-h"      MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-channel-count" MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-channels"    MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "demux-samplerate"  MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-fps"         MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                         "demux-bitrate"     MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-rotation"    MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "demux-par"         MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                         "format-name"       MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "audio-channels"    MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "replaygain-track-peak" MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                         "replaygain-track-gain" MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                         "replaygain-album-peak" MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                         "replaygain-album-gain" MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE
                         "dolby-vision-profile" MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "dolby-vision-level" MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "metadata"           MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                             (key and string value for each metadata entry)

       ccuurrrreenntt--ttrraacckkss//......
              This gives access to currently selected tracks. It redirects to
              the correct entry in ttrraacckk--lliisstt.

              The following sub-entries are defined: vviiddeeoo, aauuddiioo, ssuubb, ssuubb22

              For example, ccuurrrreenntt--ttrraacckkss//aauuddiioo//llaanngg returns the current audio
              track's language field (the same value as ttrraacckk--lliisstt//NN//llaanngg).

              If tracks of the requested type are selected via
              ----llaavvffii--ccoommpplleexx, the first one is returned.

       cchhaapptteerr--lliisstt ((RRWW))
              List of chapters, current entry marked.

              This has a number of sub-properties. Replace NN with the 0-based
              chapter index.

              cchhaapptteerr--lliisstt//ccoouunntt
                     Number of chapters.

              cchhaapptteerr--lliisstt//NN//ttiittllee
                     Chapter title as stored in the file. Not always
                     available.

              cchhaapptteerr--lliisstt//NN//ttiimmee
                     Chapter start time in seconds as float.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (for each chapter)
                         "title" MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "time"  MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE

       aaff,, vvff ((RRWW))
              See ----vvff/----aaff and the vvff/aaff command.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (for each filter entry)
                         "name"      MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "label"     MPV_FORMAT_STRING [optional]
                         "enabled"   MPV_FORMAT_FLAG [optional]
                         "params"    MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP [optional]
                             "key"   MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                             "value" MPV_FORMAT_STRING

              It's also possible to write the property using this format.

       sseeeekkaabbllee
              Whether it's generally possible to seek in the current file.

       ppaarrttiiaallllyy--sseeeekkaabbllee
              Whether the current file is considered seekable, but only
              because the cache is active. This means small relative seeks may
              be fine, but larger seeks may fail anyway. Whether a seek will
              succeed or not is generally not known in advance.

              If this property returns yyeess/true, so will sseeeekkaabbllee.

       ppllaayybbaacckk--aabboorrtt
              Whether playback is stopped or is to be stopped. (Useful in
              obscure situations like during oonn__llooaadd hook processing, when the
              user can stop playback, but the script has to explicitly end
              processing.)

       ccuurrssoorr--aauuttoohhiiddee ((RRWW))
              See ----ccuurrssoorr--aauuttoohhiiddee. Setting this to a new value will always
              update the cursor, and reset the internal timer.

       tteerrmm--cclliipp--cccc
              Inserts the symbol to force line truncation to the current
              terminal width.  This can be used for sshhooww--tteexxtt and other OSD
              messages. It must be the first character in the line. It takes
              effect until the end of the line.

       oossdd--ssyymm--cccc
              Inserts the current OSD symbol as opaque OSD control code (cc).
              This makes sense only with the sshhooww--tteexxtt command or options
              which set OSD messages.  The control code is implementation
              specific and is useless for anything else.

       oossdd--aassss--cccc
              $${{oossdd--aassss--cccc//00}} disables escaping ASS sequences of text in OSD,
              $${{oossdd--aassss--cccc//11}} enables it again. By default, ASS sequences are
              escaped to avoid accidental formatting, and this property can
              disable this behavior. Note that the properties return an opaque
              OSD control code, which only makes sense for the sshhooww--tteexxtt
              command or options which set OSD messages.

                 Example

                 • ----oossdd--mmssgg33==''TThhiiss iiss $${{oossdd--aassss--cccc//00}}{{\\\\bb11}}bboolldd tteexxtt''

                 • sshhooww--tteexxtt ""TThhiiss iiss $${{oossdd--aassss--cccc//00}}{{\\\\bb11}}bboolldd tteexxtt""

              Any ASS override tags as understood by libass can be used.

              Note that you need to escape the \\ character, because the string
              is processed for C escape sequences before passing it to the OSD
              code. See Flat command syntax for details.

              A list of tags can be found here:
               <https://aegisub.org/docs/latest/ass_tags/>

       vvoo--ccoonnffiigguurreedd
              Whether the VO is configured right now. Usually this corresponds
              to whether the video window is visible. If the ----ffoorrccee--wwiinnddooww
              option is used, this usually always returns yyeess/true.

       vvoo--ppaasssseess
              Contains introspection about the VO's active render passes and
              their execution times. Not implemented by all VOs.

              This is further subdivided into two frame types, vvoo--ppaasssseess//ffrreesshh
              for fresh frames (which have to be uploaded, scaled, etc.) and
              vvoo--ppaasssseess//rreeddrraaww for redrawn frames (which only have to be
              re-painted).  The number of passes for any given subtype can
              change from frame to frame, and should not be relied upon.

              Each frame type has a number of further sub-properties. Replace
              TTYYPPEE with the frame type, NN with the 0-based pass index, and MM
              with the 0-based sample index.

              vvoo--ppaasssseess//TTYYPPEE//ccoouunntt
                     Number of passes.

              vvoo--ppaasssseess//TTYYPPEE//NN//ddeesscc
                     Human-friendy description of the pass.

              vvoo--ppaasssseess//TTYYPPEE//NN//llaasstt
                     Last measured execution time, in nanoseconds.

              vvoo--ppaasssseess//TTYYPPEE//NN//aavvgg
                     Average execution time of this pass, in nanoseconds. The
                     exact timeframe varies, but it should generally be a
                     handful of seconds.

              vvoo--ppaasssseess//TTYYPPEE//NN//ppeeaakk
                     The peak execution time (highest value) within this
                     averaging range, in nanoseconds.

              vvoo--ppaasssseess//TTYYPPEE//NN//ccoouunntt
                     The number of samples for this pass.

              vvoo--ppaasssseess//TTYYPPEE//NN//ssaammpplleess//MM
                     The raw execution time of a specific sample for this
                     pass, in nanoseconds.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                 "TYPE" MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP
                         "desc"    MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "last"    MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "avg"     MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "peak"    MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "count"   MPV_FORMAT_INT64
                         "samples" MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                              MP_FORMAT_INT64

              Note that directly accessing this structure via subkeys is not
              supported, the only access is through aforementioned
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE.

       ppeerrff--iinnffoo
              Further performance data. Querying this property triggers
              internal collection of some data, and may slow down the player.
              Each query will reset some internal state. Property change
              notification doesn't and won't work.  All of this may change in
              the future, so don't use this. The builtin ssttaattss script is
              supposed to be the only user; since it's bundled and built with
              the source code, it can use knowledge of mpv internal to render
              the information properly. See ssttaattss script description for some
              details.

       vviiddeeoo--bbiittrraattee,, aauuddiioo--bbiittrraattee,, ssuubb--bbiittrraattee
              Bitrate values calculated on the packet level. This works by
              dividing the bit size of all packets between two keyframes by
              their presentation timestamp distance. (This uses the timestamps
              are stored in the file, so e.g. playback speed does not
              influence the returned values.) In particular, the video bitrate
              will update only per keyframe, and show the "past" bitrate. To
              make the property more UI friendly, updates to these properties
              are throttled in a certain way.

              The unit is bits per second. OSD formatting turns these values
              in kilobits (or megabits, if appropriate), which can be
              prevented by using the raw property value, e.g. with
              $${{==vviiddeeoo--bbiittrraattee}}.

              Note that the accuracy of these properties is influenced by a
              few factors.  If the underlying demuxer rewrites the packets on
              demuxing (done for some file formats), the bitrate might be
              slightly off. If timestamps are bad or jittery (like in
              Matroska), even constant bitrate streams might show fluctuating
              bitrate.

              How exactly these values are calculated might change in the
              future.

              In earlier versions of mpv, these properties returned a static
              (but bad) guess using a completely different method.

       aauuddiioo--ddeevviiccee--lliisstt
              The list of discovered audio devices. This is mostly for use
              with the client API, and reflects what ----aauuddiioo--ddeevviiccee==hheellpp with
              the command line player returns.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (for each device entry)
                         "name"          MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "description"   MPV_FORMAT_STRING

              The nnaammee is what is to be passed to the ----aauuddiioo--ddeevviiccee option
              (and often a rather cryptic audio API-specific ID), while
              ddeessccrriippttiioonn is human readable free form text. The description is
              set to the device name (minus mpv-specific <<ddrriivveerr>>// prefix) if
              no description is available or the description would have been
              an empty string.

              The special entry with the name set to aauuttoo selects the default
              audio output driver and the default device.

              The property can be watched with the property observation
              mechanism in the client API and in Lua scripts. (Technically,
              change notification is enabled the first time this property is
              read.)

       aauuddiioo--ddeevviiccee ((RRWW))
              Set the audio device. This directly reads/writes the
              ----aauuddiioo--ddeevviiccee option, but on write accesses, the audio output
              will be scheduled for reloading.

              Writing this property while no audio output is active will not
              automatically enable audio. (This is also true in the case when
              audio was disabled due to reinitialization failure after a
              previous write access to aauuddiioo--ddeevviiccee.)

              This property also doesn't tell you which audio device is
              actually in use.

              How these details are handled may change in the future.

       ccuurrrreenntt--vvoo
              Current video output driver (name as used with ----vvoo).

       ccuurrrreenntt--ggppuu--ccoonntteexxtt
              Current GPU context of video output driver (name as used with
              ----ggppuu--ccoonntteexxtt).  Valid for ----vvoo==ggppuu and ----vvoo==ggppuu--nneexxtt.

       ccuurrrreenntt--aaoo
              Current audio output driver (name as used with ----aaoo).

       uusseerr--ddaattaa ((RRWW))
              This is a recursive key/value map of arbitrary nodes shared
              between clients for general use (i.e. scripts, IPC clients, host
              applications, etc).  The player itself does not use any data in
              it (although some builtin scripts may).  The property is not
              preserved across player restarts.

              Sub-paths can be accessed directly; e.g.
              uusseerr--ddaattaa//mmyy--ssccrriipptt//ssttaattee//aa can be read, written, or observed.

              The top-level object itself cannot be written directly; write to
              sub-paths instead.

              Converting this property or its sub-properties to strings will
              give a JSON representation. If converting a leaf-level object
              (i.e. not a map or array) and not using raw mode, the underlying
              content will be given (e.g. strings will be printed directly,
              rather than quoted and JSON-escaped).

              The following sub-paths are reserved for internal uses or have
              special semantics: uusseerr--ddaattaa//oosscc, uusseerr--ddaattaa//mmppvv. Unless noted
              otherwise, the semantics of any properties under these sub-paths
              can change at any time and may not be relied upon, and writing
              to these properties may prevent builtin scripts from working
              properly.

              Currently, the following properties have defined special
              semantics:

              uusseerr--ddaattaa//oosscc//mmaarrggiinnss
                     This property is written by an OSC implementation to
                     indicate the margins that it occupies. Its sub-properties
                     ll, rr, tt, and bb should all be set to the left, right, top,
                     and bottom margins respectively.  Values are between 0.0
                     and 1.0, normalized to window width/height.

              uusseerr--ddaattaa//mmppvv//yyttddll
                     Data shared by the builtin ytdl hook script.

                     uusseerr--ddaattaa//mmppvv//yyttddll//ppaatthh
                            Path to the ytdl executable, if found, or an empty
                            string otherwise.  The property is not set until
                            the script attempts to find the ytdl executable,
                            i.e. until an URL is being loaded by the script.

                     uusseerr--ddaattaa//mmppvv//yyttddll//jjssoonn--ssuubbpprroocceessss--rreessuulltt
                            Result of executing ytdl to retrieve the JSON data
                            of the URL being loaded. The format is the same as
                            ssuubbpprroocceessss's result, capturing stdout and stderr.

              uusseerr--ddaattaa//mmppvv//ccoonnssoollee//ooppeenn
                     Whether the console is open.

       mmeennuu--ddaattaa ((RRWW))
              This property stores the raw menu definition. See Context Menu
              section for details.

              ttyyppee   Menu item type. Can be: sseeppaarraattoorr, ssuubbmmeennuu, or empty.

              ttiittllee  Menu item title. Required if type is not sseeppaarraattoorr.

              ccmmdd    Command to execute when the menu item is clicked.

              sshhoorrttccuutt
                     Menu item shortcut key which appears to the right of the
                     menu item.  A shortcut key does not have to be
                     functional; it's just a visual hint.

              ssttaattee  Menu item state. Can be: cchheecckkeedd, ddiissaabblleedd, hhiiddddeenn, or
                     empty.

              ssuubbmmeennuu
                     Submenu items, which is required if type is ssuubbmmeennuu.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (menu item)
                         "type"           MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "title"          MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "cmd"            MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "shortcut"       MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "state"          MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY[MPV_FORMAT_STRING]
                         "submenu"        MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY[menu item]

              Writing to this property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE or with Lua mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee will trigger
              an immediate update of the menu if mpv video output is currently
              active. You may observe the ccuurrrreenntt--vvoo property to check if this
              is the case.

       wwoorrkkiinngg--ddiirreeccttoorryy
              The working directory of the mpv process. Can be useful for JSON
              IPC users, because the command line player usually works with
              relative paths.

       ccuurrrreenntt--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ddiirr
              The directory in which watch later config files are stored. This
              returns ----wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ddiirr, or the default directory if
              ----wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ddiirr has not been modified, with tilde placeholders
              expanded.

       pprroottooccooll--lliisstt
              List of protocol prefixes potentially recognized by the player.
              They are returned without trailing :://// suffix (which is still
              always required).  In some cases, the protocol will not actually
              be supported (consider hhttttppss if ffmpeg is not compiled with TLS
              support).

       ddeeccooddeerr--lliisstt
              List of decoders supported. This lists decoders which can be
              passed to ----vvdd and ----aadd.

              ccooddeecc  Canonical codec name, which identifies the format the
                     decoder can handle.

              ddrriivveerr The name of the decoder itself. Often, this is the same
                     as ccooddeecc.  Sometimes it can be different. It is used to
                     distinguish multiple decoders for the same codec.

              ddeessccrriippttiioonn
                     Human readable description of the decoder and codec.

              When querying the property with the client API using
              MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE, or with Lua mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee, this will
              return a mpv_node with the following contents:

                 MPV_FORMAT_NODE_ARRAY
                     MPV_FORMAT_NODE_MAP (for each decoder entry)
                         "codec"         MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "driver"        MPV_FORMAT_STRING
                         "description"   MPV_FORMAT_STRING

       eennccooddeerr--lliisstt
              List of libavcodec encoders. This has the same format as
              ddeeccooddeerr--lliisstt.  The encoder names (ddrriivveerr entries) can be passed
              to ----oovvcc and ----ooaacc (without the llaavvcc:: prefix required by ----vvdd
              and ----aadd).

       ddeemmuuxxeerr--llaavvff--lliisstt
              List of available libavformat demuxers' names. This can be used
              to check for support for a specific format or use with
              ----ddeemmuuxxeerr--llaavvff--ffoorrmmaatt.

       iinnppuutt--kkeeyy--lliisstt
              List of Key names, same as output by ----iinnppuutt--kkeeyylliisstt.

       mmppvv--vveerrssiioonn
              The mpv version/copyright string. Depending on how the binary
              was built, it might contain either a release version, or just a
              git hash.

       mmppvv--ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
              The configuration arguments that were passed to the build
              system. If the meson version used to compile mpv is older than
              1.1.0, then a hardcoded string of a few, arbitrary options is
              displayed instead.

       ffffmmppeegg--vveerrssiioonn
              The contents of the aavv__vveerrssiioonn__iinnffoo(()) API call. This is a string
              which identifies the build in some way, either through a release
              version number, or a git hash. This property is unavailable if
              mpv is linked against older FFmpeg versions.

       lliibbaassss--vveerrssiioonn
              The value of aassss__lliibbrraarryy__vveerrssiioonn(()). This is an integer, encoded
              in a somewhat weird form (apparently "hex BCD"), indicating the
              release version of the libass library linked to mpv.

       ppllaattffoorrmm
              Returns a string describing what target platform mpv was built
              for. The value of this is dependent on what the underlying build
              system detects. Some of the most common values are: wwiinnddoowwss,
              ddaarrwwiinn (macos or ios), lliinnuuxx, aannddrrooiidd, and ffrreeeebbssdd. Note that
              this is not a complete listing.

       ooppttiioonnss//<<nnaammee>> ((RRWW))
              The value of option ----<<nnaammee>>. Most options can be changed at
              runtime by writing to this property. Note that many options
              require reloading the file for changes to take effect. If there
              is an equivalent property, prefer setting the property instead.

              There shouldn't be any reason to access ooppttiioonnss//<<nnaammee>> instead
              of <<nnaammee>>, except in situations in which the properties have
              different behavior or conflicting semantics.

       ffiillee--llooccaall--ooppttiioonnss//<<nnaammee>> ((RRWW))
              Similar to ooppttiioonnss//<<nnaammee>>, but when setting an option through
              this property, the option is reset to its old value once the
              current file has stopped playing. Trying to write an option
              while no file is playing (or is being loaded) results in an
              error.

              (Note that if an option is marked as file-local, even ooppttiioonnss//
              will access the local value, and the oolldd value, which will be
              restored on end of playback, cannot be read or written until end
              of playback.)

       ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>
              Additional per-option information.

              This has a number of sub-properties. Replace <<nnaammee>> with the
              name of a top-level option. No guarantee of stability is given
              to any of these sub-properties - they may change radically in
              the future.

              ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//nnaammee
                     The name of the option.

              ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//ttyyppee
                     The name of the option type, like SSttrriinngg or IInntteeggeerr. For
                     many complex types, this isn't very accurate.

              ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//sseett--ffrroomm--ccoommmmaannddlliinnee
                     Whether the option was set from the mpv command line.
                     What this is set to if the option is e.g. changed at
                     runtime is left undefined (meaning it could change in the
                     future).

              ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//sseett--llooccaallllyy
                     Whether the option was set per-file. This is the case
                     with automatically loaded profiles, file-dir configs, and
                     other cases. It means the option value will be restored
                     to the value before playback start when playback ends.

              ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//eexxppeeccttss--ffiillee
                     Whether the option takes file paths as arguments.

              ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//ddeeffaauulltt--vvaalluuee
                     The default value of the option. May not always be
                     available.

              ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//mmiinn,, ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//mmaaxx
                     Integer minimum and maximum values allowed for the
                     option. Only available if the options are numeric, and
                     the minimum/maximum has been set internally. It's also
                     possible that only one of these is set.

              ooppttiioonn--iinnffoo//<<nnaammee>>//cchhooiicceess
                     If the option is a choice option, the possible choices.
                     Choices that are integers may or may not be included
                     (they can be implied by mmiinn and mmaaxx). Note that options
                     which behave like choice options, but are not actual
                     choice options internally, may not have this info
                     available.

       pprrooppeerrttyy--lliisstt
              The list of top-level properties.

       pprrooffiillee--lliisstt
              The list of profiles and their contents. This is highly
              implementation-specific, and may change any time. Currently, it
              returns an array of options for each profile. Each option has a
              name and a value, with the value currently always being a
              string. Note that the options array is not a map, as order
              matters and duplicate entries are possible. Recursive profiles
              are not expanded, and show up as special pprrooffiillee options.

              The pprrooffiillee--rreessttoorree field is currently missing if it holds the
              default value (either because it was not set, or set explicitly
              to ddeeffaauulltt), but in the future it might hold the value ddeeffaauulltt.

       ccoommmmaanndd--lliisstt
              The list of input commands. This returns an array of maps, where
              each map node represents a command. This map currently only has
              a single entry: nnaammee for the name of the command. (This property
              is supposed to be a replacement for ----iinnppuutt--ccmmddlliisstt. The option
              dumps some more information, but it's a valid feature request to
              extend this property if needed.)

       iinnppuutt--bbiinnddiinnggss
              The list of current input key bindings. This returns an array of
              maps, where each map node represents a binding for a single
              key/command. This map has the following entries:

              kkeeyy    The key name. This is normalized and may look slightly
                     different from how it was specified in the source (e.g.
                     in input.conf).

              ccmmdd    The command mapped to the key. (Currently, this is
                     exactly the same string as specified in the source, other
                     than stripping whitespace and comments. It's possible
                     that it will be normalized in the future.)

              iiss__wweeaakk
                     If set to true, any existing and active user bindings
                     will take priority.

              oowwnneerr  If this entry exists, the name of the script (or similar)
                     which added this binding.

              sseeccttiioonn
                     Name of the section this binding is part of. This is a
                     rarely used mechanism. This entry may be removed or
                     change meaning in the future.

              pprriioorriittyy
                     A number. Bindings with a higher value are preferred over
                     bindings with a lower value. If the value is negative,
                     this binding is inactive and will not be triggered by
                     input. Note that mpv does not use this value internally,
                     and matching of bindings may work slightly differently in
                     some cases. In addition, this value is dynamic and can
                     change around at runtime.

              ccoommmmeenntt
                     If available, the comment following the command on the
                     same line. (For example, the input.conf entry ff ccyyccllee bbllaa
                     ## ttooggggllee bbllaa would result in an entry with ccoommmmeenntt ==
                     ""ttooggggllee bbllaa"",, ccmmdd == ""ccyyccllee bbllaa"".)

              This property is read-only, and change notification is not
              supported.

       cclliippbbooaarrdd
              The clipboard contents, only works when native clipboard
              (----cclliippbbooaarrdd--eennaabbllee) is supported on the platform.  Depending on
              the platform, some sub-properties, writing to properties, or
              change notifications are not currently functional.

              This has a number of sub-properties:

              cclliippbbooaarrdd//tteexxtt ((RRWW))
                     The text content in the clipboard (Windows, Wayland and
                     macOS only).  Writing to this property sets the text
                     clipboard content (Windows only).

              cclliippbbooaarrdd//tteexxtt--pprriimmaarryy
                     The text content in the primary selection (Wayland only).

              NNOOTTEE::
                 On Wayland with the vvoo clipboard backend, the clipboard
                 content is only updated when the compositor sends a selection
                 data offer (typically when VO window is focused). The wwaayyllaanndd
                 backend typically does not have this limitation.  See
                 ccuurrrreenntt--cclliippbbooaarrdd--bbaacckkeenndd property for more details.

       ccuurrrreenntt--cclliippbbooaarrdd--bbaacckkeenndd
              A string containing the currently active clipboard backend.  See
              ----cclliippbbooaarrdd--bbaacckkeennddss option for the list of available backends.

       cclloocckk  The current local time in hour:minutes format.

   IInnccoonnssiisstteenncciieess bbeettwweeeenn ooppttiioonnss aanndd pprrooppeerrttiieess
       You can access (almost) all options as properties, though there are
       some caveats with some properties (due to historical reasons):

       vviidd,, aaiidd,, ssiidd
              While playback is active, these return the actually active
              tracks. For example, if you set aaiidd==55, and the currently played
              file contains no audio track with ID 5, the aaiidd property will
              return nnoo.

              Before mpv 0.31.0, you could set existing tracks at runtime
              only.

       ddiissppllaayy--ffppss
              This inconsistent behavior is deprecated. Post-deprecation, the
              reported value and the option value are cleanly separated
              (oovveerrrriiddee--ddiissppllaayy--ffppss for the option value).

       vvff,, aaff If you set the properties during playback, and the filter chain
              fails to reinitialize, the option will be set, but the runtime
              filter chain does not change. On the other hand, the next video
              to be played will fail, because the initial filter chain cannot
              be created.

              This behavior changed in mpv 0.31.0. Before this, the new value
              was rejected _i_f_f a video (for vvff) or an audio (for aaff) track was
              active. If playback was not active, the behavior was the same as
              the current one.

       ppllaayylliisstt
              The property is read-only and returns the current internal
              playlist. The option is for loading playlist during command line
              parsing. For client API uses, you should use the llooaaddlliisstt
              command instead.

       pprrooffiillee,, iinncclluuddee
              These are write-only, and will perform actions as they are
              written to, exactly as if they were used on the mpv CLI
              commandline. Their only use is when using libmpv before
              mmppvv__iinniittiiaalliizzee(()), which in turn is probably only useful in
              encoding mode. Normal libmpv users should use other mechanisms,
              such as the aappppllyy--pprrooffiillee command, and the mmppvv__llooaadd__ccoonnffiigg__ffiillee
              API function. Avoid these properties.

   PPrrooppeerrttyy EExxppaannssiioonn
       All string arguments to input commands as well as certain options (like
       ----tteerrmm--ppllaayyiinngg--mmssgg) are subject to property expansion. Note that
       property expansion does not work in places where e.g. numeric
       parameters are expected.  (For example, the aadddd command does not do
       property expansion. The sseett command is an exception and not a general
       rule.)

          Example for input.conf

          ii sshhooww--tteexxtt ""FFiilleennaammee:: $${{ffiilleennaammee}}""
                 shows the filename of the current file when pressing the ii
                 key

       Whether property expansion is enabled by default depends on which API
       is used (see Flat command syntax, Commands specified as arrays and
       Named arguments), but it can always be enabled with the
       eexxppaanndd--pprrooppeerrttiieess prefix or disabled with the rraaww prefix, as described
       in Input Command Prefixes.

       The following expansions are supported:

       $${{NNAAMMEE}}
              Expands to the value of the property NNAAMMEE. If retrieving the
              property fails, expand to an error string. (Use $${{NNAAMMEE::}} with a
              trailing :: to expand to an empty string instead.)  If NNAAMMEE is
              prefixed with ==, expand to the raw value of the property (see
              section below).

       $${{NNAAMMEE::SSTTRR}}
              Expands to the value of the property NNAAMMEE, or SSTTRR if the
              property cannot be retrieved. SSTTRR is expanded recursively.

       $${{??NNAAMMEE::SSTTRR}}
              Expands to SSTTRR (recursively) if the property NNAAMMEE is available.

       $${{!!NNAAMMEE::SSTTRR}}
              Expands to SSTTRR (recursively) if the property NNAAMMEE cannot be
              retrieved.

       $${{??NNAAMMEE====VVAALLUUEE::SSTTRR}}
              Expands to SSTTRR (recursively) if the property NNAAMMEE expands to a
              string equal to VVAALLUUEE. You can prefix NNAAMMEE with == in order to
              compare the raw value of a property (see section below). If the
              property is unavailable, or other errors happen when retrieving
              it, the value is never considered equal.  Note that VVAALLUUEE can't
              contain any of the characters :: or }}.  Also, it is possible that
              escaping with "" or %% might be added in the future, should the
              need arise.

       $${{!!NNAAMMEE====VVAALLUUEE::SSTTRR}}
              Same as with the ?? variant, but SSTTRR is expanded if the value is
              not equal. (Using the same semantics as with ??.)

       $$$$     Expands to $$.

       $$}}     Expands to }}. (To produce this character inside recursive
              expansion.)

       $$>>     Disable property expansion and special handling of $$ for the
              rest of the string.

       In places where property expansion is allowed, C-style escapes are
       often accepted as well. Example:

          • \\nn becomes a newline character

          • \\\\ expands to \\

   RRaaww aanndd FFoorrmmaatttteedd PPrrooppeerrttiieess
       Normally, properties are formatted as human-readable text, meant to be
       displayed on OSD or on the terminal. It is possible to retrieve an
       unformatted (raw) value from a property by prefixing its name with ==.
       These raw values can be parsed by other programs and follow the same
       conventions as the options associated with the properties.
       Additionally, there is a >> prefix to format human-readable text, with
       fixed precision for floating-point values. This is useful for printing
       values where a constant width is important.

          Examples

          • $${{ttiimmee--ppooss}} expands to 0000::1144::2233 (if playback position is at 14
            minutes 23 seconds)

          • $${{==ttiimmee--ppooss}} expands to 886633..44 (same time, plus 400 milliseconds -
            milliseconds are normally not shown in the formatted case)

          • $${{aavvssyynncc}} expands to ++00..000033

          • $${{>>aavvssyynncc}} expands to ++00..00003300

          • $${{==aavvssyynncc}} expands to 00..000033002288

       Sometimes, the difference in amount of information carried by raw and
       formatted property values can be rather big. In some cases, raw values
       have more information, like higher precision than seconds with
       ttiimmee--ppooss. Sometimes it is the other way around, e.g. aaiidd shows track
       title and language in the formatted case, but only the track number if
       it is raw.

OONN SSCCRREEEENN CCOONNTTRROOLLLLEERR
       The On Screen Controller (short: OSC) is a minimal GUI integrated with
       mpv to offer basic mouse-controllability. It is intended to make
       interaction easier for new users and to enable precise and direct
       seeking.

       The OSC is enabled by default if mpv was compiled with Lua support. It
       can be disabled entirely using the ----oosscc==nnoo option.

   UUssiinngg tthhee OOSSCC
       By default, the OSC will show up whenever the mouse is moved inside the
       player window and will hide if the mouse is not moved outside the OSC
       for 0.5 seconds or if the mouse leaves the window.

   TThhee IInntteerrffaaccee

          +------+---------+---------+-----------------------------------------------+
          | menu | pl prev | pl next | title                                   cache |
          +------+------+------+---------+-----------+------+-------+-----+-----+----+
