       ssuubb__ttrraacckk__mmbbttnn__rriigghhtt__ccoommmmaanndd==ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg sseelleecctt//sseelleecctt--ssiidd;;
       ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo oosscc oosscc--hhiiddee

       ssuubb__ttrraacckk__wwhheeeell__ddoowwnn__ccoommmmaanndd==ccyyccllee ssuubb

       ssuubb__ttrraacckk__wwhheeeell__uupp__ccoommmmaanndd==ccyyccllee ssuubb ddoowwnn

       vvoolluummee__mmbbttnn__lleefftt__ccoommmmaanndd==nnoo--oossdd ccyyccllee mmuuttee

       vvoolluummee__mmbbttnn__mmiidd__ccoommmmaanndd==

       vvoolluummee__mmbbttnn__rriigghhtt__ccoommmmaanndd==ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg sseelleecctt//sseelleecctt--aauuddiioo--ddeevviiccee;;
       ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo oosscc oosscc--hhiiddee

       vvoolluummee__wwhheeeell__ddoowwnn__ccoommmmaanndd==aadddd vvoolluummee --55

       vvoolluummee__wwhheeeell__uupp__ccoommmmaanndd==aadddd vvoolluummee 55

       ffuullllssccrreeeenn__mmbbttnn__lleefftt__ccoommmmaanndd==""ccyyccllee ffuullllssccrreeeenn""

       ffuullllssccrreeeenn__mmbbttnn__mmiidd__ccoommmmaanndd==

       ffuullllssccrreeeenn__mmbbttnn__rriigghhtt__ccoommmmaanndd==""ccyyccllee wwiinnddooww--mmaaxxiimmiizzeedd""

   CCuussttoomm BBuuttttoonnss
       Additional script-opts are available to define custom buttons in
       bboottttoommbbaarr and ttooppbbaarr layouts.

          Example to add loop and shuffle buttons

                 custom_button_1_content=🔁
                 custom_button_1_mbtn_left_command=cycle-values loop-file inf
                 no custom_button_1_mbtn_right_command=cycle-values
                 loop-playlist inf no

                 custom_button_2_content=🔀
                 custom_button_2_mbtn_left_command=playlist-shuffle

                 custom_button_3_content=⏱
                 custom_button_3_mbtn_left_command=add speed 1
                 custom_button_3_mbtn_right_command=set speed 1
                 custom_button_3_wheel_up_command=add speed 0.25
                 custom_button_3_wheel_down_command=add speed -0.25

   SSccrriipptt CCoommmmaannddss
       The OSC script listens to certain script commands. These commands can
       bound in iinnppuutt..ccoonnff, or sent by other scripts.

       oosscc--vviissiibbiilliittyy
              Controls visibility mode nneevveerr / aauuttoo (on mouse move) / aallwwaayyss
              and also ccyyccllee to cycle between the modes. If a second argument
              is passed (any value), then the output on the OSD will be
              silenced.

       oosscc--sshhooww
              Triggers the OSC to show up, just as if user moved mouse.

       oosscc--hhiiddee
              Hide the OSC when vviissiibbiilliittyy is aauuttoo.

       Example

       You could put this into iinnppuutt..ccoonnff to hide the OSC with the aa key and
       to set auto mode (the default) with bb:

          a script-message osc-visibility never
          b script-message osc-visibility auto

       oosscc--iiddlleessccrreeeenn
              Controls the visibility of the mpv logo on idle. Valid arguments
              are yyeess, nnoo, and ccyyccllee to toggle between yes and no. If a second
              argument is passed (any value), then the output on the OSD will
              be silenced.

SSTTAATTSS
       This builtin script displays information and statistics for the
       currently played file. It is enabled by default if mpv was compiled
       with Lua support.  It can be disabled entirely using the
       ----llooaadd--ssttaattss--oovveerrllaayy==nnoo option.

   UUssaaggee
       The following key bindings are active by default unless something else
       is already bound to them:

                           ┌──┬───────────────────────────┐
                           │i │ Show stats for a fixed    │
                           │  │ duration                  │
                           ├──┼───────────────────────────┤
                           │I │ Toggle stats (shown until │
                           │  │ toggled again)            │
                           ├──┼───────────────────────────┤
                           │? │ Toggle displaying the key │
                           │  │ bindings                  │
                           └──┴───────────────────────────┘
       While the stats are visible on screen the following key bindings are
       active, regardless of existing bindings. They allow you to switch
       between _p_a_g_e_s of stats:

                            ┌──┬─────────────────────────┐
                            │1 │ Show usual stats        │
                            ├──┼─────────────────────────┤
                            │2 │ Show frame timings      │
                            │  │ (scroll)                │
                            ├──┼─────────────────────────┤
                            │3 │ Input cache stats       │
                            ├──┼─────────────────────────┤
                            │4 │ Active key bindings     │
                            │  │ (scroll)                │
                            ├──┼─────────────────────────┤
                            │5 │ Selected Tracks Info    │
                            │  │ (scroll)                │
                            ├──┼─────────────────────────┤
                            │0 │ Internal stuff (scroll) │
                            └──┴─────────────────────────┘
       If stats were displayed by toggling, these key bindings are also
       active:

                               ┌────┬─────────────────┐
                               │ESC │ Close the stats │
                               └────┴─────────────────┘
       On pages which support scroll, these key bindings are also active:

                            ┌─────┬──────────────────────┐
                            │UP   │ Scroll one line up   │
                            ├─────┼──────────────────────┤
                            │DOWN │ Scroll one line down │
                            └─────┴──────────────────────┘
       On page 4, these key bindings are also active:

                              ┌──┬─────────────────────┐
                              │/ │ Search key bindings │
                              └──┴─────────────────────┘
   CCoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
       This script can be customized through a config file
       ssccrriipptt--ooppttss//ssttaattss..ccoonnff placed in mpv's user directory and through the
       ----ssccrriipptt--ooppttss command-line option. The configuration syntax is
       described in mp.options functions.

   CCoonnffiigguurraabbllee OOppttiioonnss

       kkeeyy__ppaaggee__11
              Default: 1

       kkeeyy__ppaaggee__22
              Default: 2

       kkeeyy__ppaaggee__33
              Default: 3

       kkeeyy__ppaaggee__44
              Default: 4

       kkeeyy__ppaaggee__55
              Default: 5

       kkeeyy__ppaaggee__00
              Default: 0

       kkeeyy__eexxiitt
              Default: ESC

              Key bindings for page switching while stats are displayed.

       kkeeyy__ssccrroollll__uupp
              Default: UP

       kkeeyy__ssccrroollll__ddoowwnn
              Default: DOWN

       kkeeyy__ssccrroollll__sseeaarrcchh
              Default: /

       ssccrroollll__lliinneess
              Default: 1

              Scroll key bindings and number of lines to scroll on pages which
              support it.

       dduurraattiioonn
              Default: 4

              How long the stats are shown in seconds (oneshot).

       rreeddrraaww__ddeellaayy
              Default: 1

              How long it takes to refresh the displayed stats in seconds
              (toggling).

       ppeerrssiisstteenntt__oovveerrllaayy
              Default: no

              When _n_o, other scripts printing text to the screen can overwrite
              the displayed stats. When _y_e_s, displayed stats are persistently
              shown for the respective duration. This can result in
              overlapping text when multiple scripts decide to print text at
              the same time.

       ffiillee__ttaagg__mmaaxx__lleennggtthh
              Default: 128

              Only show file tags shorter than this length, in bytes.

       ffiillee__ttaagg__mmaaxx__ccoouunntt
              Default: 16

              Only show the first specified amount of file tags.

       tteerrmm__cclliipp
              Default: yes

              Whether to clip lines to the terminal width.

       pplloott__ppeerrffddaattaa
              Default: yes

              Show graphs for performance data (page 2).

       pplloott__vvssyynncc__rraattiioo
              Default: yes

       pplloott__vvssyynncc__jjiitttteerr
              Default: yes

              Show graphs for vsync and jitter values (page 1). Only when
              toggled.

       pplloott__ttoonneemmaappppiinngg__lluutt
              Default: no

              Enable tone-mapping LUT visualization automatically. Only when
              toggled.

       fflluusshh__ggrraapphh__ddaattaa
              Default: yes

              Clear data buffers used for drawing graphs when toggling.

       ffoonntt   Default: same as oossdd--ffoonntt

              Font name. Should support as many font weights as possible for
              optimal visual experience.

       ffoonntt__mmoonnoo
              Default: monospace

              Font name for parts where monospaced characters are necessary to
              align text. Currently, monospaced digits are sufficient.

       ffoonntt__ssiizzee
              Default: 20

              Font size used to render text.

       ffoonntt__ccoolloorr
              Default: same as oossdd--ccoolloorr

              Color of the text.

       bboorrddeerr__ssiizzee
              Default: 1.65

              Size of border drawn around the font.

       bboorrddeerr__ccoolloorr
              Default: same as oossdd--bboorrddeerr--ccoolloorr

              Color of the text border.

       sshhaaddooww__xx__ooffffsseett
              Default: same as ----oossdd--sshhaaddooww--ooffffsseett

              The horizontal distance from the text to position the shadow at.

       sshhaaddooww__yy__ooffffsseett
              Default: same as ----oossdd--sshhaaddooww--ooffffsseett

              The vertical distance from the text to position the shadow at.

       sshhaaddooww__ccoolloorr
              Default: same as oossdd--sshhaaddooww--ccoolloorr

              Color of the text shadow.

       aallpphhaa  Default: 11

              Transparency of text when ffoonntt__ccoolloorr is specified, of text
              borders when bboorrddeerr__ccoolloorr is specified, and of text shadows when
              sshhaaddooww__ccoolloorr is specified.

       pplloott__bbgg__bboorrddeerr__ccoolloorr
              Default: 0000FF

              Border color used for drawing graphs.

       pplloott__bbgg__bboorrddeerr__wwiiddtthh
              Default: 1.25

              Border width used for drawing graphs.

       pplloott__bbgg__ccoolloorr
              Default: 262626

              Background color used for drawing graphs.

       pplloott__ccoolloorr
              Default: FFFFFF

              Color used for drawing graphs.

       vviiddssccaallee
              Default: auto

              Scale the text and graphs with the video.  nnoo tries to keep the
              sizes constant.  aauuttoo scales the text and graphs with the OSD,
              which is scaled with the window or kept at a constant size,
              depending on the ----oossdd--ssccaallee--bbyy--wwiinnddooww option.

       Note: colors are given as hexadecimal values and use ASS tag order:
       BBGGRR (blue green red).

   DDiiffffeerreenntt kkeeyy bbiinnddiinnggss
       Additional keys can be configured in iinnppuutt..ccoonnff to display the stats:

          e script-binding stats/display-stats
          E script-binding stats/display-stats-toggle

       And to display a certain page directly:

          i script-binding stats/display-page-1
          h script-binding stats/display-page-4-toggle

   AAccttiivvee kkeeyy bbiinnddiinnggss ppaaggee
       Lists the active key bindings and the commands they're bound to,
       excluding the interactive keys of the stats script itself. See also
       ----iinnppuutt--tteesstt for more detailed view of each binding.

       The keys are grouped automatically using a simple analysis of the
       command string, and one should not expect documentation-level grouping
       accuracy, however, it should still be reasonably useful.

       Using ----iiddllee ----ssccrriipptt--oopptt==ssttaattss--bbiinnddlliisstt==yyeess will print the list to the
       terminal and quit immediately. Long lines are clipped to the terminal
       width unless this is disabled with ----ssccrriipptt--oopptt==ssttaattss--tteerrmm__cclliipp==nnoo.
       Escape sequences can be disabled by adding -- before yyeess, i.e.
       ----ssccrriipptt--oopptt==ssttaattss--bbiinnddlliisstt==--yyeess.

       Like with ----iinnppuutt--tteesstt, the list includes bindings from iinnppuutt..ccoonnff and
       from user scripts. Use ----nnoo--ccoonnffiigg to list only built-in bindings.

   IInntteerrnnaall ssttuuffff ppaaggee
       Most entries shown on this page have rather vague meaning. Likely none
       of this is useful for you. Don't attempt to use it. Forget its
       existence.

       Selecting this for the first time will start collecting some internal
       performance data. That means performance will be slightly lower than
       normal for the rest of the time the player is running (even if the
       stats page is closed).  Note that the stats page itself uses a lot of
       CPU and even GPU resources, and may have a heavy impact on performance.

       The displayed information is accumulated over the redraw delay (shown
       as ppoollll--ttiimmee field).

       This adds entries for each Lua script. If there are too many scripts
       running, parts of the list will simply be out of the screen, but it can
       be scrolled.

       If the underlying platform does not support pthread per thread times,
       the displayed times will be 0 or something random (I suspect that at
       time of this writing, only Linux provides the correct via pthread APIs
       for per thread times).

       Most entries are added lazily and only during data collection, which is
       why entries may pop up randomly after some time. It's also why the
       memory usage entries for scripts that have been inactive since the
       start of data collection are missing.

       Memory usage is approximate and does not reflect internal
       fragmentation.

       JS scripts memory reporting is disabled by default because collecting
       the data at the JS side has an overhead and will increase memory usage.
       It can be enabled by setting the ----jjss--mmeemmoorryy--rreeppoorrtt option before
       starting mpv.

       If entries have //ttiimmee and //ccppuu variants, the former gives the real time
       (monotonic clock), while the latter the thread CPU time (only if the
       corresponding pthread API works and is supported).

CCOONNSSOOLLEE
       This script provides the ability to process the user's textual input to
       other scripts through the mmpp..iinnppuutt API. It can be displayed on both the
       video window and the terminal. It can be disabled entirely using the
       ----llooaadd--ccoonnssoollee==nnoo option.

       Console can either process free-form text or select from a predefined
       list of items.

   FFrreeee--ffoorrmm tteexxtt mmooddee kkeeyybbiinnddiinnggss

       EESSCC aanndd CCttrrll++[[
              Hide the console.

       EENNTTEERR,, CCttrrll++jj aanndd CCttrrll++mm
              Select the first completion if one wasn't already manually
              selected, and run the typed command.

       SShhiifftt++EENNTTEERR
              Type a literal newline character.

       LLEEFFTT aanndd CCttrrll++bb
              Move the cursor to the previous character.

       RRIIGGHHTT aanndd CCttrrll++ff
              Move the cursor to the next character.

       CCttrrll++LLEEFFTT aanndd AAlltt++bb
              Move the cursor to the beginning of the current word, or if
              between words, to the beginning of the previous word.

       CCttrrll++RRIIGGHHTT aanndd AAlltt++ff
              Move the cursor to the end of the current word, or if between
              words, to the end of the next word.

       HHOOMMEE aanndd CCttrrll++aa
              Move the cursor to the start of the current line.

       EENNDD aanndd CCttrrll++ee
              Move the cursor to the end of the current line.

       BBAACCKKSSPPAACCEE aanndd CCttrrll++hh
              Delete the previous character.

       CCttrrll++dd Hide the console if the current line is empty, otherwise delete
              the next character.

       CCttrrll++BBAACCKKSSPPAACCEE aanndd CCttrrll++ww
              Delete text from the cursor to the beginning of the current
              word, or if between words, to the beginning of the previous
              word.

       CCttrrll++DDEELL aanndd AAlltt++dd
              Delete text from the cursor to the end of the current word, or
              if between words, to the end of the next word.

       CCttrrll++uu Delete text from the cursor to the beginning of the current
              line.

       CCttrrll++kk Delete text from the cursor to the end of the current line.

       CCttrrll++cc Clear the current line.

       UUPP aanndd CCttrrll++pp
              Move back in the command history.

       DDOOWWNN aanndd CCttrrll++nn
              Move forward in the command history.

       PPGGUUPP   Go to the first command in the history.

       PPGGDDNN   Stop navigating the command history.

       CCttrrll++rr Search the command history. See SELECT for the key bindings in
              this mode.

       IINNSSEERRTT Toggle insert mode.

       CCttrrll++vv Paste text (uses the clipboard on X11 and Wayland).

       SShhiifftt++IINNSSEERRTT
              Paste text (uses the primary selection on X11 and Wayland).

       TTAABB aanndd CCttrrll++ii
              Cycle through completions.

       SShhiifftt++TTAABB
              Cycle through the completions backwards.

       CCttrrll++ll Clear all log messages from the console.

       MMBBTTNN__MMIIDD
              Paste text (uses the primary selection on X11 and Wayland).

       WWHHEEEELL__UUPP
              Move back in the command history.

       WWHHEEEELL__DDOOWWNN
              Move forward in the command history.

   KKnnoowwnn iissssuueess

       • Non-ASCII keyboard input has restrictions

       • The cursor keys move between Unicode code-points, not grapheme
         clusters

   CCoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
       This script can be customized through a config file
       ssccrriipptt--ooppttss//ccoonnssoollee..ccoonnff placed in mpv's user directory and through the
       ----ssccrriipptt--ooppttss command-line option. The configuration syntax is
       described in mp.options functions.

   CCoonnffiigguurraabbllee OOppttiioonnss

       ffoonntt   The font name.

              When necessary to align completions in a grid, a monospace font
              depending on the platform is the default. When there are no
              completions, ----oossdd--ffoonntt is the default.

       ffoonntt__ssiizzee
              Default: 24

              The font size. This will be multiplied by ddiissppllaayy--hhiiddppii--ssccaallee
              when the console is not scaled with the window.

       bboorrddeerr__ssiizzee
              Default: 1.65

              The font border size.

       bbaacckkggrroouunndd__aallpphhaa
              Default: 80

              The transparency of the menu's background. Ranges from 0
              (opaque) to 255 (fully transparent).

       ppaaddddiinngg
              Default: 10

              The padding of the menu.

       mmeennuu__oouuttlliinnee__ssiizzee
              Default: 0

              The size of the menu's border.

       mmeennuu__oouuttlliinnee__ccoolloorr
              Default: #FFFFFF

              The color of the menu's border.

       ccoorrnneerr__rraaddiiuuss
              Default: 8

              The radius of the menu's corners.

       mmaarrggiinn__xx
              Default: same as ----oossdd--mmaarrggiinn--xx

              The margin from the left of the window.

       mmaarrggiinn__yy
              Default: same as ----oossdd--mmaarrggiinn--yy

              The margin from the bottom of the window.

       ssccaallee__wwiitthh__wwiinnddooww
              Default: aauuttoo

              Whether to scale the console with the window height. Can be yyeess,
              nnoo, or aauuttoo, which follows the value of ----oossdd--ssccaallee--bbyy--wwiinnddooww.

       sseelleecctteedd__ccoolloorr
              Default: ##222222222222

              The color of the selected item.

       sseelleecctteedd__bbaacckk__ccoolloorr
              Default: ##FFFFFFFFFFFF

              The background color of the selected item.

       mmaattcchh__ccoolloorr
              Default: ##00008888FFFF

              The color of characters that match the searched string.

       ccaassee__sseennssiittiivvee
              Default: no on Windows, yes on other platforms.

              Whether autocompletion is case sensitive. Only works with ASCII
              characters.

       hhiissttoorryy__ddeedduupp
              Default: true

              Remove duplicate entries in history as to only keep the latest
              one.

       ffoonntt__hhww__rraattiioo
              Default: auto

              The ratio of font height to font width.  Adjusts grid width of
              completions.  Values in the range 1.8..2.5 make sense for common
              monospace fonts.

CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
       This script allows running and completing input commands in the console
       interactively, and also adds mpv's log to the console's log.

   KKeeyybbiinnddiinnggss

       ``      Open the console to enter commands.

   CCoommmmaannddss
       ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg ccoommmmaannddss//ooppeenn
          Open the console to enter commands.

       ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo ccoommmmaannddss ttyyppee <<tteexxtt>> [[<<ccuurrssoorr__ppooss>>]]
              Show the console and pre-fill it with the provided text,
              optionally specifying the initial cursor position as a positive
              integer starting from 1. The console is automatically closed
              after running the command.

                 Examples for input.conf

                 %% ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo ccoommmmaannddss ttyyppee ""sseeeekk aabbssoolluuttee--ppeerrcceenntt"" 66
                        Enter a percent position to seek to.

                 CCttrrll++oo ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo ccoonnssoollee ttyyppee ""llooaaddffiillee ''''"" 1111
                        Enter a file or URL to play, with autocompletion of
                        paths in the filesystem.

   CCoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
       This script can be customized through a config file
       ssccrriipptt--ooppttss//ccoommmmaannddss..ccoonnff placed in mpv's user directory and through
       the ----ssccrriipptt--ooppttss command-line option. The configuration syntax is
       described in mp.options functions.

   CCoonnffiigguurraabbllee OOppttiioonnss

       ppeerrssiisstt__hhiissttoorryy
              Default: no

              Whether to save the command history to a file and load it.

       hhiissttoorryy__ppaatthh
              Default: ~~~~ssttaattee//ccoommmmaanndd__hhiissttoorryy..ttxxtt

              The file path for ppeerrssiisstt__hhiissttoorryy (see PATHS).

SSEELLEECCTT
       console can present a list of items to browse and select from with the
       mmpp..iinnppuutt..sseelleecctt API. sseelleecctt..lluuaa is a builtin client of this API
       providing script bindings that gather and format the data to be
       selected in the console and do operations on the selected item. It can
       be disabled using the ----llooaadd--sseelleecctt==nnoo option.

   KKeeyy bbiinnddiinnggss
       When using mmpp..iinnppuutt..sseelleecctt, typing printable characters does a fuzzy
       search of the presented items, and key bindings listed in CONSOLE are
       extended with the following:

       EENNTTEERR,, CCttrrll++jj aanndd CCttrrll++mm
              Confirm the selection of the highlighted item.

       UUPP aanndd CCttrrll++pp
              Select the item above, or the last one when the first item is
              selected.

       DDOOWWNN aanndd CCttrrll++nn
              Select the item below, or the first one when the last item is
              selected.

       PPGGUUPP aanndd CCttrrll++bb
              Scroll up one page.

       PPGGDDNN aanndd CCttrrll++ff
              Scroll down one page.

       MMBBTTNN__LLEEFFTT
              Confirm the selection of the highlighted item, or close the
              console if clicking outside of the menu rectangle.

       WWHHEEEELL__UUPP
              Scroll up.

       WWHHEEEELL__DDOOWWNN
              Scroll down.

   SSccrriipptt bbiinnddiinnggss
       By default select.lua's script bindings are bound to key sequences
       starting with gg listed in Keyboard Control. The names of the script
       bindings listed below can be used to bind them to different keys.

          Example to rebind playlist selection in input.conf

                 Ctrl+p script-binding select/select-playlist

       Available script bindings are:

       sseelleecctt--ppllaayylliisstt
              Select a playlist entry. ----oossdd--ppllaayylliisstt--eennttrryy determines how
              playlist entries are formatted.

       sseelleecctt--ssiidd
              Select a subtitle track, or disable the current one.

       sseelleecctt--sseeccoonnddaarryy--ssiidd
              Select a secondary subtitle track, or disable the current one.

       sseelleecctt--aaiidd
              Select an audio track, or disable the current one.

       sseelleecctt--vviidd
              Select a video track, or disable the current one.

       sseelleecctt--ttrraacckk
              Select a track of any type, or disable a selected track.

       sseelleecctt--cchhaapptteerr
              Select a chapter.

       sseelleecctt--eeddiittiioonn
              Select an MKV edition or DVD/Blu-ray title.

       sseelleecctt--ssuubbttiittllee--lliinnee
              Select a subtitle line to seek to. This doesn't work with image
              subtitles.

              This currently requires ffffmmppeegg in PPAATTHH, or in the same folder as
              mpv on Windows.

       sseelleecctt--aauuddiioo--ddeevviiccee
              Select an audio device.

       sseelleecctt--wwaattcchh--hhiissttoorryy
              Select a file from the watch history. Requires
              ----ssaavvee--wwaattcchh--hhiissttoorryy.

              If you don't already use ----aauuttooccrreeaattee--ppllaayylliisstt, it is
              recommended to enable it with this script binding to populate
              the playlist with the other files in the entry's directory.

                 Example for input.conf to play files adjacent to the history
                 entry

                        g-h script-binding select/select-watch-history; no-osd
                        set autocreate-playlist filter

       sseelleecctt--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr
              Select a file from watch later config files (see RESUMING
              PLAYBACK) to resume playing. Requires
              ----wwrriittee--ffiilleennaammee--iinn--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ccoonnffiigg. This doesn't work with
              ----iiggnnoorree--ppaatthh--iinn--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ccoonnffiigg.

              If you don't already use ----aauuttooccrreeaattee--ppllaayylliisstt, it is
              recommended to enable it with this script binding to populate
              the playlist with the other files in the entry's directory.

                 Example for input.conf to play files adjacent to the watch
                 later entry

                        g-w script-binding select/select-watch-later; no-osd
                        set autocreate-playlist filter

       sseelleecctt--bbiinnddiinngg
              List the defined input bindings. You can also select one to run
              the associated command.

       sshhooww--pprrooppeerrttiieess
              List the names and values of all properties. You can also select
              one to print its value on the OSD, which is useful for long
              values that get clipped.

       mmeennuu   Show a menu with miscellaneous entries.

   CCoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
       This script can be customized through a config file
       ssccrriipptt--ooppttss//sseelleecctt..ccoonnff placed in mpv's user directory and through the
       ----ssccrriipptt--ooppttss command-line option. The configuration syntax is
       described in mp.options functions.

   CCoonnffiigguurraabbllee ooppttiioonnss

       hhiissttoorryy__ddaattee__ffoorrmmaatt
              Default: %Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S

              The format of dates of history entries. This is passed to Lua's
              ooss..ddaattee, which uses the same formats as ssttrrffttiimmee((33)).

       hhiiddee__hhiissttoorryy__dduupplliiccaatteess
              Default: yes

              Whether to show only the last of history entries with the same
              path.

PPOOSSIITTIIOONNIINNGG
       This script provides script bindings to pan videos and images. It can
       be disabled using the ----llooaadd--ppoossiittiioonniinngg==nnoo option.

   SSccrriipptt bbiinnddiinnggss

       ppaann--xx <<aammoouunntt>>
              Adjust ----vviiddeeoo--aalliiggnn--xx relatively to the OSD width, rather than
              relatively to the video width like the option. This is useful to
              pan large images consistently.

              aammoouunntt is a number such that an amount of 1 scrolls as much as
              the OSD width.

       ppaann--yy <<aammoouunntt>>
              Adjust ----vviiddeeoo--aalliiggnn--yy relatively to the OSD height, rather than
              relatively to the video height like the option.

              aammoouunntt is a number such that an amount of 1 scrolls as much as
              the OSD height.

       ddrraagg--ttoo--ppaann
              Pan the video while holding a mouse button, keeping the clicked
              part of the video under the cursor.

       aalliiggnn--ttoo--ccuurrssoorr
              Pan through the whole video while holding a mouse button, or
              after clicking once if ttooggggllee__aalliiggnn__ttoo__ccuurrssoorr is yyeess.

       ccuurrssoorr--cceennttrriicc--zzoooomm <<aammoouunntt>>
              Increase ----vviiddeeoo--zzoooomm by aammoouunntt while keeping the part of the
              video hovered by the cursor under it, or the average position of
              touch points if known.

   CCoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
       This script can be customized through a config file
       ssccrriipptt--ooppttss//ppoossiittiioonniinngg..ccoonnff placed in mpv's user directory and through
       the ----ssccrriipptt--ooppttss command-line option. The configuration syntax is
       described in mp.options functions.

   CCoonnffiigguurraabbllee OOppttiioonnss

       ttooggggllee__aalliiggnn__ttoo__ccuurrssoorr
              Default: no

              Whether aalliiggnn--ttoo--ccuurrssoorr requires holding down a mouse button to
              pan. If nnoo, dragging pans. If yyeess, clicking the first time makes
              pan follow the cursor, and clicking a second time disables this.

       ssuupppprreessss__oossdd
              Default: no

              Whether to not print the new value of ----vviiddeeoo--zzoooomm when using
              ccuurrssoorr--cceennttrriicc--zzoooomm.

LLUUAA SSCCRRIIPPTTIINNGG
       mpv can load Lua scripts. (See Script location.)

       mpv provides the built-in module mmpp, which contains functions to send
       commands to the mpv core and to retrieve information about playback
       state, user settings, file information, and so on.

       These scripts can be used to control mpv in a similar way to slave
       mode.  Technically, the Lua code uses the client API internally.

   EExxaammppllee
       A script which leaves fullscreen mode when the player is paused:

          function on_pause_change(name, value)
              if value == true then
                  mp.set_property("fullscreen", "no")
              end
          end
          mp.observe_property("pause", "bool", on_pause_change)

   SSccrriipptt llooccaattiioonn
       Scripts can be passed to the ----ssccrriipptt option, and are automatically
       loaded from the ssccrriippttss subdirectory of the mpv configuration directory
       (usually ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//ssccrriippttss//).

       A script can be a single file. The file extension is used to select the
       scripting backend to use for it. For Lua, it is ..lluuaa. If the extension
       is not recognized, an error is printed. (If an error happens, the
       extension is either mistyped, or the backend was not compiled into your
       mpv binary.)

       mpv internally loads the script's name by stripping the ..lluuaa extension
       and replacing all nonalphanumeric characters with __. E.g., mmyy--ttoooollss..lluuaa
       becomes mmyy__ttoooollss. If there are several scripts with the same name, it
       is made unique by appending a number. This is the name returned by
       mmpp..ggeett__ssccrriipptt__nnaammee(()).

       Entries with ..ddiissaabbllee extension are always ignored.

       If a script is a directory (either if a directory is passed to
       ----ssccrriipptt, or any sub-directories in the script directory, such as for
       example ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//ssccrriippttss//ssoommeetthhiinngg//), then the directory
       represents a single script. The player will try to load a file named
       mmaaiinn..xx, where xx is replaced with the file extension. For example, if
       mmaaiinn..lluuaa exists, it is loaded with the Lua scripting backend.

       You must not put any other files or directories that start with mmaaiinn..
       into the script's top level directory. If the script directory contains
       for example both mmaaiinn..lluuaa and mmaaiinn..jjss, only one of them will be loaded
       (and which one depends on mpv internals that may change any time).
       Likewise, if there is for example mmaaiinn..ffoooo, your script will break as
       soon as mpv adds a backend that uses the ..ffoooo file extension.

       mpv also appends the top level directory of the script to the start of
       Lua's package path so you can import scripts from there too. Be aware
       that this will shadow Lua libraries that use the same package path.
       (Single file scripts do not include mpv specific directories in the Lua
       package path. This was silently changed in mpv 0.32.0.)

       Using a script directory is the recommended way to package a script
       that consists of multiple source files, or requires other files (you
       can use mmpp..ggeett__ssccrriipptt__ddiirreeccttoorryy(()) to get the location and e.g. load
       data files).

       Making a script a git repository, basically a repository which contains
       a mmaaiinn..lluuaa file in the root directory, makes scripts easily updateable
       (without the dangers of auto-updates). Another suggestion is to use git
       submodules to share common files or libraries.

   DDeettaaiillss oonn tthhee ssccrriipptt iinniittiiaalliizzaattiioonn aanndd lliiffeeccyyccllee
       Your script will be loaded by the player at program start from the
       ssccrriippttss configuration subdirectory, or from a path specified with the
       ----ssccrriipptt option. Some scripts are loaded internally (like ----oosscc). Each
       script runs in its own thread. Your script is first run "as is", and
       once that is done, the event loop is entered. This event loop will
       dispatch events received by mpv and call your own event handlers which
       you have registered with mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__eevveenntt, or timers added with
       mmpp..aadddd__ttiimmeeoouutt or similar. Note that since the script starts execution
       concurrently with player initialization, some properties may not be
       populated with meaningful values until the relevant subsystems have
       initialized. Rather than retrieving these properties at the top of
       scripts, you should use mmpp..oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy or read them within event
       handlers.

       When the player quits, all scripts will be asked to terminate. This
       happens via a sshhuuttddoowwnn event, which by default will make the event loop
       return. If your script got into an endless loop, mpv will probably
       behave fine during playback, but it won't terminate when quitting,
       because it's waiting on your script.

       Internally, the C code will call the Lua function mmpp__eevveenntt__lloooopp after
       loading a Lua script. This function is normally defined by the default
       prelude loaded before your script (see ppllaayyeerr//lluuaa//ddeeffaauullttss..lluuaa in the
       mpv sources).  The event loop will wait for events and dispatch events
       registered with mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__eevveenntt. It will also handle timers added
       with mmpp..aadddd__ttiimmeeoouutt and similar (by waiting with a timeout).

       Since mpv 0.6.0, the player will wait until the script is fully loaded
       before continuing normal operation. The player considers a script as
       fully loaded as soon as it starts waiting for mpv events (or it exits).
       In practice this means the player will more or less hang until the
       script returns from the main chunk (and mmpp__eevveenntt__lloooopp is called), or
       the script calls mmpp__eevveenntt__lloooopp or mmpp..ddiissppaattcchh__eevveennttss directly. This is
       done to make it possible for a script to fully setup event handlers
       etc. before playback actually starts. In older mpv versions, this
       happened asynchronously. With mpv 0.29.0, this changes slightly, and it
       merely waits for scripts to be loaded in this manner before starting
       playback as part of the player initialization phase. Scripts run though
       initialization in parallel. This might change again.

   mmpp ffuunnccttiioonnss
       The mmpp module is preloaded, although it can be loaded manually with
       rreeqquuiirree ''mmpp''. It provides the core client API.

       mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd((ssttrriinngg))
              Run the given command. This is similar to the commands used in
              input.conf.  See List of Input Commands.

              By default, this will show something on the OSD (depending on
              the command), as if it was used in iinnppuutt..ccoonnff. See Input Command
              Prefixes how to influence OSD usage per command.

              Returns ttrruuee on success, or nniill,, eerrrroorr on error.

       mmpp..ccoommmmaannddvv((aarrgg11,, aarrgg22,, ......))
              Similar to mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd, but pass each command argument as
              separate parameter. This has the advantage that you don't have
              to care about quoting and escaping in some cases.

              Example:

                 mp.command("loadfile " .. filename .. " append")
                 mp.commandv("loadfile", filename, "append")

              These two commands are equivalent, except that the first version
              breaks if the filename contains spaces or certain special
              characters.

              Note that properties are _n_o_t expanded.  You can use either
              mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd, the eexxppaanndd--pprrooppeerrttiieess prefix, or the mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy
              family of functions.

              Unlike mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd, this will not use OSD by default either
              (except for some OSD-specific commands).

       mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee((ttaabbllee [[,,ddeeff]]))
              Similar to mmpp..ccoommmmaannddvv, but pass the argument list as table.
              This has the advantage that in at least some cases, arguments
              can be passed as native types. It also allows you to use named
              argument.

              If the table is an array, each array item is like an argument in
              mmpp..ccoommmmaannddvv(()) (but can be a native type instead of a string).

              If the table contains string keys, it's interpreted as command
              with named arguments. This requires at least an entry with the
              key nnaammee to be present, which must be a string, and contains the
              command name. The special entry __ffllaaggss is optional, and if
              present, must be an array of Input Command Prefixes to apply.
              All other entries are interpreted as arguments.

              Returns a result table on success (usually empty), or ddeeff,, eerrrroorr
              on error. ddeeff is the second parameter provided to the function,
              and is nil if it's missing.

       mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee__aassyynncc((ttaabbllee [[,,ffnn]]))
              Like mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee(()), but the command is ran asynchronously
              (as far as possible), and upon completion, fn is called. fn has
              three arguments: ffnn((ssuucccceessss,, rreessuulltt,, eerrrroorr)):

                    ssuucccceessss
                           Always a Boolean and is true if the command was
                           successful, otherwise false.

                 rreessuulltt The result value (can be nil) in case of success, nil
                        otherwise (as returned by mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee(())).

                 eerrrroorr  The error string in case of an error, nil otherwise.

              Returns a table with undefined contents, which can be used as
              argument for mmpp..aabboorrtt__aassyynncc__ccoommmmaanndd.

              If starting the command failed for some reason, nniill,, eerrrroorr is
              returned, and ffnn is called indicating failure, using the same
              error value.

              ffnn is always called asynchronously, even if the command failed
              to start.

       mmpp..aabboorrtt__aassyynncc__ccoommmmaanndd((tt))
              Abort a mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee__aassyynncc call. The argument is the return
              value of that command (which starts asynchronous execution of
              the command).  Whether this works and how long it takes depends
              on the command and the situation. The abort call itself is
              asynchronous. Does not return anything.

       mmpp..ddeell__pprrooppeerrttyy((nnaammee))
              Delete the given property. See mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy and Properties
              for more information about properties. Most properties cannot be
              deleted.

              Returns true on success, or nniill,, eerrrroorr on error.

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]]))
              Return the value of the given property as string. These are the
              same properties as used in input.conf. See Properties for a list
              of properties. The returned string is formatted similar to
              $${{==nnaammee}} (see Property Expansion).

              Returns the string on success, or ddeeff,, eerrrroorr on error. ddeeff is
              the second parameter provided to the function, and is nil if
              it's missing.

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__oossdd((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]]))
              Similar to mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy, but return the property value
              formatted for OSD. This is the same string as printed with
              $${{nnaammee}} when used in input.conf.

              Returns the string on success, or ddeeff,, eerrrroorr on error. ddeeff is
              the second parameter provided to the function, and is an empty
              string if it's missing. Unlike ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy(()), assigning the
              return value to a variable will always result in a string.

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__bbooooll((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]]))
              Similar to mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy, but return the property value as
              Boolean.

              Returns a Boolean on success, or ddeeff,, eerrrroorr on error.

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnuummbbeerr((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]]))
              Similar to mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy, but return the property value as
              number.

              Note that while Lua does not distinguish between integers and
              floats, mpv internals do. This function simply request a double
              float from mpv, and mpv will usually convert integer property
              values to float.

              Returns a number on success, or ddeeff,, eerrrroorr on error.

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]]))
              Similar to mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy, but return the property value using
              the best Lua type for the property. Most time, this will return
              a string, Boolean, or number. Some properties (for example
              cchhaapptteerr--lliisstt) are returned as tables.

              Returns a value on success, or ddeeff,, eerrrroorr on error. Note that
              nniill might be a possible, valid value too in some corner cases.

       mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy((nnaammee,, vvaalluuee))
              Set the given property to the given string value. See
              mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy and Properties for more information about
              properties.

              Returns true on success, or nniill,, eerrrroorr on error.

       mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__bbooooll((nnaammee,, vvaalluuee))
              Similar to mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy, but set the given property to the
              given Boolean value.

       mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnuummbbeerr((nnaammee,, vvaalluuee))
              Similar to mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy, but set the given property to the
              given numeric value.

              Note that while Lua does not distinguish between integers and
              floats, mpv internals do. This function will test whether the
              number can be represented as integer, and if so, it will pass an
              integer value to mpv, otherwise a double float.

       mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee((nnaammee,, vvaalluuee))
              Similar to mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy, but set the given property using its
              native type.

              Since there are several data types which cannot represented
              natively in Lua, this might not always work as expected. For
              example, while the Lua wrapper can do some guesswork to decide
              whether a Lua table is an array or a map, this would fail with
              empty tables. Also, there are not many properties for which it
              makes sense to use this, instead of sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy,
              sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__bbooooll, sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnuummbbeerr.  For these reasons, this
              function should probably be avoided for now, except for
              properties that use tables natively.

       mmpp..ggeett__ttiimmee(())
              Return the current mpv internal time in seconds as a number.
              This is basically the system time, with an arbitrary offset.

       mmpp..aadddd__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg((kkeeyy,, nnaammee||ffnn [[,,ffnn [[,,ffllaaggss]]]]))
              Register callback to be run on a key binding. The binding will
              be mapped to the given kkeeyy, which is a string describing the
              physical key. This uses the same key names as in input.conf, and
              also allows combinations (e.g. ccttrrll++aa). If the key is empty or
              nniill, no physical key is registered, but the user still can
              create own bindings (see below).

              After calling this function, key presses will cause the function
              ffnn to be called (unless the user remapped the key with another
              binding).  However, if the key binding is canceled , the
              function will not be called, unless ccoommpplleexx flag is set to ttrruuee,
              where the function will be called with the ccaanncceelleedd entry set to
              ttrruuee.

              For example, a canceled key binding can happen in the following
              situations:

              • If key A is pressed while key B is being held down, key B is
                logically released ("canceled" by key A), which stops the
                current autorepeat action key B has.

              • If key A is pressed while a mouse button is being held down,
                the mouse button is logically released, but the mouse button's
                action will not be called, unless ccoommpplleexx flag is set to ttrruuee.

              The nnaammee argument should be a short symbolic string. It allows
              the user to remap the key binding via input.conf using the
              ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee command, and the name of the key binding (see
              below for an example). The name should be unique across other
              bindings in the same script - if not, the previous binding with
              the same name will be overwritten. You can omit the name, in
              which case a random name is generated internally. (Omitting
              works as follows: either pass nniill for nnaammee, or pass the ffnn
              argument in place of the name. The latter is not recommended and
              is handled for compatibility only.)

              The ffllaaggss argument is used for optional parameters. This is a
              table, which can have the following entries:

                 rreeppeeaattaabbllee
                        If set to ttrruuee, enables key repeat for this specific
                        binding.  This option only makes sense when ccoommpplleexx is
                        not set to ttrruuee.

                 ssccaallaabbllee
                        If set to ttrruuee, enables key scaling for this specific
                        binding.  This option only makes sense when ccoommpplleexx is
                        set to ttrruuee.  Note that this has no effect if the key
                        binding is invoked by ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg command, where
                        the scalability of the command takes precedence.

                 ccoommpplleexx
                        If set to ttrruuee, then ffnn is called on key down, repeat
                        and up events, with the first argument being a table.
                        This table has the following entries (and may contain
                        undocumented ones):

                           eevveenntt  Set to one of the strings ddoowwnn, rreeppeeaatt, uupp
                                  or pprreessss (the latter if key up/down/repeat
                                  can't be tracked), which indicates the key's
                                  logical state.

                           iiss__mmoouussee
                                  Boolean: Whether the event was caused by a
                                  mouse button.

                           ccaanncceelleedd
                                  Boolean: Whether the event was canceled.
                                  Not all types of cancellations set this
                                  flag.

                           kkeeyy__nnaammee
                                  The name of they key that triggered this, or
                                  nniill if invoked artificially. If the key name
                                  is unknown, it's an empty string.

                           kkeeyy__tteexxtt
                                  Text if triggered by a text key, otherwise
                                  nniill. See description of ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg
                                  command for details (this field is
                                  equivalent to the 5th argument).

                           ssccaallee  The scale of the key, such as the ones
                                  produced by WWHHEEEELL__** keys. The scale is 1 if
                                  the key is nonscalable.

                           aarrgg    User-provided string in the aarrgg argument in
                                  the ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg command if the key
                                  binding is invoked by that command.

              Internally, key bindings are dispatched via the
              ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo or ssccrriipptt--bbiinnddiinngg input commands and
              mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__ssccrriipptt__mmeessssaaggee.

              Trying to map multiple commands to a key will essentially prefer
              a random binding, while the other bindings are not called. It is
              guaranteed that user defined bindings in the central input.conf
              are preferred over bindings added with this function (but see
              mmpp..aadddd__ffoorrcceedd__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg).

              Example:

                 function something_handler()
                     print("the key was pressed")
                 end
                 mp.add_key_binding("x", "something", something_handler)

              This will print the message tthhee kkeeyy wwaass pprreesssseedd when xx was
              pressed.

              The user can remap these key bindings. Then the user has to put
              the following into their input.conf to remap the command to the
              yy key:

                 y script-binding something

              This will print the message when the key yy is pressed. (xx will
              still work, unless the user remaps it.)

              You can also explicitly send a message to a named script only.
              Assume the above script was using the filename ffoooossccrriipptt..lluuaa:

                 y script-binding fooscript/something

       mmpp..aadddd__ffoorrcceedd__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg((......))
              This works almost the same as mmpp..aadddd__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg, but registers
              the key binding in a way that will overwrite the user's custom
              bindings in their input.conf. (mmpp..aadddd__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg overwrites
              default key bindings only, but not those by the user's
              input.conf.)

       mmpp..rreemmoovvee__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg((nnaammee))
              Remove a key binding added with mmpp..aadddd__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg or
              mmpp..aadddd__ffoorrcceedd__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg. Use the same name as you used when
              adding the bindings. It's not possible to remove bindings for
              which you omitted the name.

       mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__eevveenntt((nnaammee,, ffnn))
              Call a specific function when an event happens. The event name
              is a string, and the function fn is a Lua function value.

              Some events have associated data. This is put into a Lua table
              and passed as argument to fn. The Lua table by default contains
              a nnaammee field, which is a string containing the event name. If
              the event has an error associated, the eerrrroorr field is set to a
              string describing the error, on success it's not set.

              If multiple functions are registered for the same event, they
              are run in registration order, which the first registered
              function running before all the other ones.

              Returns true if such an event exists, false otherwise.

              See Events and List of events for details.

       mmpp..uunnrreeggiisstteerr__eevveenntt((ffnn))
              Undo mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__eevveenntt((......,, ffnn)). This removes all event handlers
              that are equal to the ffnn parameter. This uses normal Lua ====
              comparison, so be careful when dealing with closures.

       mmpp..oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy((nnaammee,, ttyyppee,, ffnn))
              Watch a property for changes. If the property nnaammee is changed,
              then the function ffnn((nnaammee)) will be called. ttyyppee can be nniill, or
              be set to one of nnoonnee, nnaattiivvee, bbooooll, ssttrriinngg, or nnuummbbeerr.  nnoonnee is
              the same as nniill. For all other values, the new value of the
              property will be passed as second argument to ffnn, using
              mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__<<ttyyppee>> to retrieve it. This means if ttyyppee is for
              example ssttrriinngg, ffnn is roughly called as in ffnn((nnaammee,,
              mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__ssttrriinngg((nnaammee)))).

              If possible, change events are coalesced. If a property is
              changed a bunch of times in a row, only the last change triggers
              the change function. (The exact behavior depends on timing and
              other things.)

              If a property is unavailable, or on error, the value argument to
              ffnn is nniill. (The oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy(()) call always succeeds, even if
              a property does not exist.)

              In some cases the function is not called even if the property
              changes.  This depends on the property, and it's a valid feature
              request to ask for better update handling of a specific
              property.

              If the ttyyppee is nnoonnee or nniill, the change function ffnn will be
              called sporadically even if the property doesn't actually
              change. You should therefore avoid using these types.

              You always get an initial change notification. This is meant to
              initialize the user's state to the current value of the
              property.

       mmpp..uunnoobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy((ffnn))
              Undo mmpp..oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy((......,, ffnn)). This removes all property
              handlers that are equal to the ffnn parameter. This uses normal
              Lua ==== comparison, so be careful when dealing with closures.

       mmpp..aadddd__ttiimmeeoouutt((sseeccoonnddss,, ffnn [[,, ddiissaabblleedd]]))
              Call the given function fn when the given number of seconds has
              elapsed.  Note that the number of seconds can be fractional. For
              now, the timer's resolution may be as low as 50 ms, although
              this will be improved in the future.

              If the ddiissaabblleedd argument is set to ttrruuee or a truthy value, the
              timer will wait to be manually started with a call to its
              rreessuummee(()) method.

              This is a one-shot timer: it will be removed when it's fired.

              Returns a timer object. See mmpp..aadddd__ppeerriiooddiicc__ttiimmeerr for details.

       mmpp..aadddd__ppeerriiooddiicc__ttiimmeerr((sseeccoonnddss,, ffnn [[,, ddiissaabblleedd]]))
              Call the given function periodically. This is like
              mmpp..aadddd__ttiimmeeoouutt, but the timer is re-added after the function fn
              is run.

              RReettuurrnnss aa ttiimmeerr oobbjjeecctt.. TThhee ttiimmeerr oobbjjeecctt pprroovviiddeess tthhee ffoolllloowwiinngg
              mmeetthhooddss::

                     ssttoopp(()) Disable the timer. Does nothing if the timer is
                            already disabled.  This will remember the current
                            elapsed time when stopping, so that rreessuummee(())
                            essentially unpauses the timer.

                     kkiillll(()) Disable the timer. Resets the elapsed time.
                            rreessuummee(()) will restart the timer.

                     rreessuummee(())
                            Restart the timer. If the timer was disabled with
                            ssttoopp(()), this will resume at the time it was
                            stopped. If the timer was disabled with kkiillll(()), or
                            if it's a previously fired one-shot timer (added
                            with aadddd__ttiimmeeoouutt(())), this starts the timer from
                            the beginning, using the initially configured
                            timeout.

                     iiss__eennaabblleedd(())
                            Whether the timer is currently enabled or was
                            previously disabled (e.g. by ssttoopp(()) or kkiillll(())).

                     ttiimmeeoouutt ((RRWW))
                            This field contains the current timeout period.
                            This value is not updated as time progresses. It's
                            only used to calculate when the timer should fire
                            next when the timer expires.

                            If you write this, you can call tt::kkiillll(()) ;;
                            tt::rreessuummee(()) to reset the current timeout to the new
                            one. (tt::ssttoopp(()) won't use the new timeout.)

                     oonneesshhoott ((RRWW))
                            Whether the timer is periodic (ffaallssee) or fires
                            just once (ttrruuee). This value is used when the
                            timer expires (but before the timer callback
                            function fn is run).

              Note that these are methods, and you have to call them using ::
              instead of .. (Refer to
              <https://www.lua.org/manual/5.2/manual.html#3.4.9>  .)

              Example:

                 seconds = 0
                 timer = mp.add_periodic_timer(1, function()
                     print("called every second")
                     -- stop it after 10 seconds
                     seconds = seconds + 1
                     if seconds >= 10 then
                         timer:kill()
                     end
                 end)

       mmpp..ggeett__oopptt((kkeeyy))
              Return a setting from the ----ssccrriipptt--ooppttss option. It's up to the
              user and the script how this mechanism is used. Currently, all
              scripts can access this equally, so you should be careful about
              collisions.

       mmpp..ggeett__ssccrriipptt__nnaammee(())
              Return the name of the current script. The name is usually made
              of the filename of the script, with directory and file extension
              removed. If there are several scripts which would have the same
              name, it's made unique by appending a number. Any
              nonalphanumeric characters are replaced with __.

                 Example

                        The script //ppaatthh//ttoo//ffoooo--ssccrriipptt..lluuaa becomes ffoooo__ssccrriipptt.

       mmpp..ggeett__ssccrriipptt__ddiirreeccttoorryy(())
              Return the directory if this is a script packaged as directory
              (see Script location for a description). Return nothing if this
              is a single file script.

       mmpp..oossdd__mmeessssaaggee((tteexxtt [[,,dduurraattiioonn]]))
              Show an OSD message on the screen. dduurraattiioonn is in seconds, and
              is optional (uses ----oossdd--dduurraattiioonn by default).

   AAddvvaanncceedd mmpp ffuunnccttiioonnss
       These also live in the mmpp module, but are documented separately as they
       are useful only in special situations.

       mmpp..ggeett__wwaakkeeuupp__ppiippee(())
              Calls mmppvv__ggeett__wwaakkeeuupp__ppiippee(()) and returns the read end of the
              wakeup pipe. This is deprecated, but still works. (See cclliieenntt..hh
              for details.)

       mmpp..ggeett__nneexxtt__ttiimmeeoouutt(())
              Return the relative time in seconds when the next timer
              (mmpp..aadddd__ttiimmeeoouutt and similar) expires. If there is no timer,
              return nniill.

       mmpp..ddiissppaattcchh__eevveennttss(([[aallllooww__wwaaiitt]]))
              This can be used to run custom event loops. If you want to have
              direct control what the Lua script does (instead of being called
              by the default event loop), you can set the global variable
              mmpp__eevveenntt__lloooopp to your own function running the event loop. From
              your event loop, you should call mmpp..ddiissppaattcchh__eevveennttss(()) to dequeue
              and dispatch mpv events.

              If the aallllooww__wwaaiitt parameter is set to ttrruuee, the function will
              block until the next event is received or the next timer
              expires. Otherwise (and this is the default behavior), it
              returns as soon as the event loop is emptied. It's strongly
              recommended to use mmpp..ggeett__nneexxtt__ttiimmeeoouutt(()) and
              mmpp..ggeett__wwaakkeeuupp__ppiippee(()) if you're interested in properly working
              notification of new events and working timers.

       mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__iiddllee((ffnn))
              Register an event loop idle handler. Idle handlers are called
              before the script goes to sleep after handling all new events.
              This can be used for example to delay processing of property
              change events: if you're observing multiple properties at once,
              you might not want to act on each property change, but only when
              all change notifications have been received.

       mmpp..uunnrreeggiisstteerr__iiddllee((ffnn))
              Undo mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__iiddllee((ffnn)). This removes all idle handlers that
              are equal to the ffnn parameter. This uses normal Lua ====
              comparison, so be careful when dealing with closures.

       mmpp..eennaabbllee__mmeessssaaggeess((lleevveell))
              Set the minimum log level of which mpv message output to
              receive. These messages are normally printed to the terminal. By
              calling this function, you can set the minimum log level of
              messages which should be received with the lloogg--mmeessssaaggee event.
              See the description of this event for details.  The level is a
              string, see mmssgg..lloogg for allowed log levels.

       mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__ssccrriipptt__mmeessssaaggee((nnaammee,, ffnn))
              This is a helper to dispatch ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee or ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo
              invocations to Lua functions. ffnn is called if ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee or
              ssccrriipptt--mmeessssaaggee--ttoo (with this script as destination) is run with
              nnaammee as first parameter. The other parameters are passed to ffnn.
              If a message with the given name is already registered, it's
              overwritten.

              Used by mmpp..aadddd__kkeeyy__bbiinnddiinngg, so be careful about name collisions.

       mmpp..uunnrreeggiisstteerr__ssccrriipptt__mmeessssaaggee((nnaammee))
              Undo a previous registration with mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__ssccrriipptt__mmeessssaaggee.
              Does nothing if the nnaammee wasn't registered.

       mmpp..ccrreeaattee__oossdd__oovveerrllaayy((ffoorrmmaatt))
              Create an OSD overlay. This is a very thin wrapper around the
              oossdd--oovveerrllaayy command. The function returns a table, which mostly
              contains fields that will be passed to oossdd--oovveerrllaayy. The ffoorrmmaatt
              parameter is used to initialize the ffoorrmmaatt field. The ddaattaa field
              contains the text to be used as overlay. For details, see the
              oossdd--oovveerrllaayy command.

              In addition, it provides the following methods:

              uuppddaattee(())
                     Commit the OSD overlay to the screen, or in other words,
                     run the oossdd--oovveerrllaayy command with the current fields of
                     the overlay table.  Returns the result of the oossdd--oovveerrllaayy
                     command itself.

              rreemmoovvee(())
                     Remove the overlay from the screen. A uuppddaattee(()) call will
                     add it again.

              Example:

                 ov = mp.create_osd_overlay("ass-events")
                 ov.data = "{\\an5}{\\b1}hello world!"
                 ov:update()

              The advantage of using this wrapper (as opposed to running
              oossdd--oovveerrllaayy directly) is that the iidd field is allocated
              automatically.

       mmpp..ggeett__oossdd__ssiizzee(())
              Returns a tuple of oossdd__wwiiddtthh,, oossdd__hheeiigghhtt,, oossdd__ppaarr. The first two
              give the size of the OSD in pixels (for video outputs like
              ----vvoo==xxvv, this may be "scaled" pixels). The third is the display
              pixel aspect ratio.

              May return invalid/nonsense values if OSD is not initialized
              yet.

       eexxiitt(()) ((gglloobbaall))
              Make the script exit at the end of the current event loop
              iteration. This does not terminate mpv itself or other scripts.

              This can be polyfilled to support mpv versions older than 0.40
              with:

                 if not _G.exit then
                     function exit()
                         mp.keep_running = false
                     end
                 end

   mmpp..mmssgg ffuunnccttiioonnss
       This module allows outputting messages to the terminal, and can be
       loaded with rreeqquuiirree ''mmpp..mmssgg''.

       mmssgg..lloogg((lleevveell,, ......))
              The level parameter is the message priority. It's a string and
              one of ffaattaall, eerrrroorr, wwaarrnn, iinnffoo, vv, ddeebbuugg, ttrraaccee. The user's
              settings will determine which of these messages will be visible.
              Normally, all messages are visible, except vv, ddeebbuugg and ttrraaccee.

              The parameters after that are all converted to strings. Spaces
              are inserted to separate multiple parameters.

              You don't need to add newlines.

       mmssgg..ffaattaall((......)),, mmssgg..eerrrroorr((......)),, mmssgg..wwaarrnn((......)),, mmssgg..iinnffoo((......)),,
       mmssgg..vveerrbboossee((......)),, mmssgg..ddeebbuugg((......)),, mmssgg..ttrraaccee((......))
              All of these are shortcuts and equivalent to the corresponding
              mmssgg..lloogg((lleevveell,, ......)) call.

   mmpp..ooppttiioonnss ffuunnccttiioonnss
       mpv comes with a built-in module to manage options from config-files
       and the command-line. All you have to do is to supply a table with
       default options to the read_options function. The function will
       overwrite the default values with values found in the config-file and
       the command-line (in that order).

       ooppttiioonnss..rreeaadd__ooppttiioonnss((ttaabbllee [[,, iiddeennttiiffiieerr [[,, oonn__uuppddaattee]]]]))
              A ttaabbllee with key-value pairs. The type of the default values is
              important for converting the values read from the config file or
              command-line back. Do not use nniill as a default value!

              The iiddeennttiiffiieerr is used to identify the config-file and the
              command-line options. These needs to unique to avoid collisions
              with other scripts.  Defaults to mmpp..ggeett__ssccrriipptt__nnaammee(()) if the
              parameter is nniill or missing.

              The oonn__uuppddaattee parameter enables run-time updates of all matching
              option values via the ssccrriipptt--ooppttss option/property. If any of the
              matching options changes, the values in the ttaabbllee (which was
              originally passed to the function) are changed, and
              oonn__uuppddaattee((lliisstt)) is called. lliisstt is a table where each updated
              option has a lliisstt[[ooppttiioonn__nnaammee]] == ttrruuee entry.  There is no
              initial oonn__uuppddaattee(()) call. This never re-reads the config file.
              ssccrriipptt--ooppttss is always applied on the original config file,
              ignoring previous ssccrriipptt--ooppttss values (for example, if an option
              is removed from ssccrriipptt--ooppttss at runtime, the option will have the
              value in the config file). ttaabbllee entries are only written for
              option values whose values effectively change (this is important
              if the script changes ttaabbllee entries independently).

       Example implementation:

          local options = {
              optionA = "defaultvalueA",
              optionB = -0.5,
              optionC = true,
          }

          require "mp.options".read_options(options, "myscript")
          print(options.optionA)

       The config file will be stored in ssccrriipptt--ooppttss//iiddeennttiiffiieerr..ccoonnff in mpv's
       user folder. Comment lines can be started with # and stray spaces are
       not removed.  Boolean values will be represented with yes/no.

       Example config:

          # comment
          optionA=Hello World
          optionB=9999
          optionC=no

       Command-line options are read from the ----ssccrriipptt--ooppttss parameter. To
       avoid collisions, all keys have to be prefixed with iiddeennttiiffiieerr--.

       Example command-line:

          --script-opts=myscript-optionA=TEST,myscript-optionB=0,myscript-optionC=yes

   mmpp..uuttiillss ffuunnccttiioonnss
       This built-in module provides generic helper functions for Lua, and
       have strictly speaking nothing to do with mpv or video/audio playback.
       They are provided for convenience. Most compensate for Lua's scarce
       standard library.

       Be warned that any of these functions might disappear any time. They
       are not strictly part of the guaranteed API.

       uuttiillss..ggeettccwwdd(())
              Returns the directory that mpv was launched from. On error, nniill,,
              eerrrroorr is returned.

       uuttiillss..rreeaaddddiirr((ppaatthh [[,, ffiilltteerr]]))
              Enumerate all entries at the given path on the filesystem, and
              return them as array. Each entry is a directory entry (without
              the path).  The list is unsorted (in whatever order the
              operating system returns it).

              If the ffiilltteerr argument is given, it must be one of the following
              strings:

                 ffiilleess  List regular files only. This excludes directories,
                        special files (like UNIX device files or FIFOs), and
                        dead symlinks. It includes UNIX symlinks to regular
                        files.

                 ddiirrss   List directories only, or symlinks to directories. ..
                        and .... are not included.

                 nnoorrmmaall Include the results of both ffiilleess and ddiirrss. (This is
                        the default.)

                 aallll    List all entries, even device files, dead symlinks,
                        FIFOs, and the .. and .... entries.

              On error, nniill,, eerrrroorr is returned.

       uuttiillss..ffiillee__iinnffoo((ppaatthh))
              Stats the given path for information and returns a table with
              the following entries:

                 mmooddee   protection bits (on Windows, always 755 (octal) for
                        directories and 644 (octal) for files)

                 ssiizzee   size in bytes

                 aattiimmee  time of last access

                 mmttiimmee  time of last modification

                 ccttiimmee  time of last metadata change

                 iiss__ffiillee
                        Whether ppaatthh is a regular file (boolean)

                 iiss__ddiirr Whether ppaatthh is a directory (boolean)

              mmooddee and ssiizzee are integers.  Timestamps (aattiimmee, mmttiimmee and ccttiimmee)
              are integer seconds since the Unix epoch (Unix time).  The
              booleans iiss__ffiillee and iiss__ddiirr are provided as a convenience; they
              can be and are derived from mmooddee.

              On error (e.g. path does not exist), nniill,, eerrrroorr is returned.

       uuttiillss..sspplliitt__ppaatthh((ppaatthh))
              Split a path into directory component and filename component,
              and return them. The first return value is always the directory.
              The second return value is the trailing part of the path, the
              directory entry.

       uuttiillss..jjooiinn__ppaatthh((pp11,, pp22))
              Return the concatenation of the 2 paths. Tries to be clever. For
              example, if pp22 is an absolute path, pp22 is returned without
              change.

       uuttiillss..ssuubbpprroocceessss((tt))
              Runs an external process and waits until it exits. Returns
              process status and the captured output. This is a legacy wrapper
              around calling the ssuubbpprroocceessss command with mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee. It
              does the following things:

              • copy the table tt

              • rename ccaanncceellllaabbllee field to ppllaayybbaacckk__oonnllyy

              • rename mmaaxx__ssiizzee to ccaappttuurree__ssiizzee

              • set ccaappttuurree__ssttddoouutt field to ttrruuee if unset

              • set nnaammee field to ssuubbpprroocceessss

              • call mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee((ccooppiieedd__tt))

              • if the command failed, create a dummy result table

              • copy eerrrroorr__ssttrriinngg to eerrrroorr field if the string is non-empty

              • return the result table

              It is recommended to use mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee or
              mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee__aassyynncc directly, instead of calling this legacy
              wrapper. It is for compatibility only.

              See the ssuubbpprroocceessss documentation for semantics and further
              parameters.

       uuttiillss..ssuubbpprroocceessss__ddeettaacchheedd((tt))
              Runs an external process and detaches it from mpv's control.

              The parameter tt is a table. The function reads the following
              entries:

                 aarrggss   Array of strings of the same semantics as the aarrggss
                        used in the ssuubbpprroocceessss function.

              The function returns nniill.

              This is a legacy wrapper around calling the rruunn command with
              mmpp..ccoommmmaannddvv and other functions.

       uuttiillss..ggeettppiidd(())
              Returns the process ID of the running mpv process. This can be
              used to identify the calling mpv when launching (detached)
              subprocesses.

       uuttiillss..ggeett__eennvv__lliisstt(())
              Returns the C environment as a list of strings. (Do not confuse
              this with the Lua "environment", which is an unrelated concept.)

       uuttiillss..ppaarrssee__jjssoonn((ssttrr [[,, ttrraaiill]]))
              Parses the given string argument as JSON, and returns it as a
              Lua table. On error, returns nniill,, eerrrroorr. (Currently, eerrrroorr is
              just a string reading eerrrroorr, because there is no fine-grained
              error reporting of any kind.)

              The returned value uses similar conventions as
              mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee(()) to distinguish empty objects and
              arrays.

              If the ttrraaiill parameter is ttrruuee (or any value equal to ttrruuee),
              then trailing non-whitespace text is tolerated by the function,
              and the trailing text is returned as 3rd return value. (The 3rd
              return value is always there, but with ttrraaiill set, no error is
              raised.)

       uuttiillss..ffoorrmmaatt__jjssoonn((vv))
              Format the given Lua table (or value) as a JSON string and
              return it. On error, returns nniill,, eerrrroorr. (Errors usually only
              happen on value types incompatible with JSON.)

              The argument value uses similar conventions as
              mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee(()) to distinguish empty objects and
              arrays.

       uuttiillss..ttoo__ssttrriinngg((vv))
              Turn the given value into a string. Formats tables and their
              contents. This doesn't do anything special; it is only needed
              because Lua is terrible.

   mmpp..iinnppuutt ffuunnccttiioonnss
       This module lets scripts get textual input from the user using the
       console REPL.

       iinnppuutt..ggeett((ttaabbllee))
              Show the console to let the user enter text.

              The following entries of ttaabbllee are read:

              pprroommpptt The string to be displayed before the input field.

              ssuubbmmiitt A callback invoked when the user presses Enter. The first
                     argument is the text in the console.

              kkeeeepp__ooppeenn
                     Whether to keep the console open on submit. Defaults to
                     ffaallssee.

              ooppeenneedd A callback invoked when the console is shown. This can be
                     used to present a list of options with iinnppuutt..sseett__lloogg(()).

              eeddiitteedd A callback invoked when the text changes. The first
                     argument is the text in the console.

              ccoommpplleettee
                     A callback invoked when the user edits the text or moves
                     the cursor. The first argument is the text before the
                     cursor. The callback should return a table of the string
                     candidate completion values and the 1-based cursor
                     position from which the completion starts. console will
                     show the completions that fuzzily match the text between
                     this position and the cursor and allow selecting them.

                     The third and optional return value is a string that will
                     be appended to the input line without displaying it in
                     the completions.

              aauuttoosseelleecctt__ccoommpplleettiioonn
                     Whether to automatically select the first completion on
                     submit if one wasn't already manually selected. Defaults
                     to ffaallssee.

              cclloosseedd A callback invoked when the console is hidden, either
                     because iinnppuutt..tteerrmmiinnaattee(()) was invoked from the other
                     callbacks, or because the user closed it with a key
                     binding. The first argument is the text in the console,
                     and the second argument is the cursor position.

              ddeeffaauulltt__tteexxtt
                     A string to pre-fill the input field with.

              ccuurrssoorr__ppoossiittiioonn
                     The initial cursor position, starting from 1.

              hhiissttoorryy__ppaatthh
                     If specified, the path to save and load the history of
                     the entered lines.

              iidd     An identifier that determines which input history and log
                     buffer to use among the ones stored for iinnppuutt..ggeett(())
                     calls. Defaults to the calling script name with pprroommpptt
                     appended.

       iinnppuutt..tteerrmmiinnaattee(())
              Close the console.

       iinnppuutt..lloogg((mmeessssaaggee,, ssttyyllee,, tteerrmmiinnaall__ssttyyllee))
              Add a line to the log buffer. ssttyyllee can contain additional ASS
              tags to apply to mmeessssaaggee, and tteerrmmiinnaall__ssttyyllee can contain escape
              sequences that are used when the console is displayed in the
              terminal.

       iinnppuutt..lloogg__eerrrroorr((mmeessssaaggee))
              Helper to add a line to the log buffer with the same color as
              the one used for commands that error. Useful when the user
              submits invalid input.

       iinnppuutt..sseett__lloogg((lloogg))
              Replace the entire log buffer.

              lloogg is a table of strings, or tables with tteexxtt, ssttyyllee and
              tteerrmmiinnaall__ssttyyllee keys.

              Example:

                 input.set_log({
                     "regular text",
                     {
                         text = "error text",
                         style = "{\\c&H7a77f2&}",
                         terminal_style = "\027[31m",
                     }
                 })

       iinnppuutt..sseelleecctt((ttaabbllee))
              Specify a list of items that are presented to the user for
              selection.

              The following entries of ttaabbllee are read:

              pprroommpptt The string to be displayed before the input field.

              iitteemmss  The table of the entries to choose from.

              ddeeffaauulltt__iitteemm
                     The 1-based integer index of the preselected item.

              ssuubbmmiitt The callback invoked when the user presses Enter. The
                     first argument is the 1-based index of the selected item.

              kkeeeepp__ooppeenn
                     Whether to keep the console open on submit. Defaults to
                     ffaallssee.

              Example:

                    input.select({
                        items = {
                            "First playlist entry",
                            "Second playlist entry",
                        },
                        submit = function (id)
                            mp.commandv("playlist-play-index", id - 1)
                        end,
                    })

   EEvveennttss
       Events are notifications from player core to scripts. You can register
       an event handler with mmpp..rreeggiisstteerr__eevveenntt.

       Note that all scripts (and other parts of the player) receive events
       equally, and there's no such thing as blocking other scripts from
       receiving events.

       Example:

          function my_fn(event)
              print("start of playback!")
          end

          mp.register_event("file-loaded", my_fn)

       For the existing event types, see List of events.

   EExxttrraass
       This documents experimental features, or features that are "too
       special" to guarantee a stable interface.

       mmpp..aadddd__hhooookk((ttyyppee,, pprriioorriittyy,, ffnn))
              Add a hook callback for ttyyppee (a string identifying a certain
              kind of hook). These hooks allow the player to call script
              functions and wait for their result (normally, the Lua scripting
              interface is asynchronous from the point of view of the player
              core). pprriioorriittyy is an arbitrary integer that allows ordering
              among hooks of the same kind. Using the value 50 is recommended
              as neutral default value.

              ffnn((hhooookk)) is the function that will be called during execution of
              the hook. The parameter passed to it (hhooookk) is a Lua object that
              can control further aspects about the currently invoked hook. It
              provides the following methods:

                 ddeeffeerr(())
                        Returning from the hook function should not
                        automatically continue the hook. Instead, the API user
                        wants to call hhooookk::ccoonntt(()) on its own at a later point
                        in time (before or after the function has returned).

                 ccoonntt(()) Continue the hook. Doesn't need to be called unless
                        ddeeffeerr(()) was called.

              See Hooks for currently existing hooks and what they do - only
              the hook list is interesting; handling hook execution is done by
              the Lua script function automatically.

JJAAVVAASSCCRRIIPPTT
       JavaScript support in mpv is near identical to its Lua support. Use
       this section as reference on differences and availability of APIs, but
       otherwise you should refer to the Lua documentation for API details and
       general scripting in mpv.

   EExxaammppllee
       JavaScript code which leaves fullscreen mode when the player is paused:

          function on_pause_change(name, value) {
              if (value == true)
                  mp.set_property("fullscreen", "no");
          }
          mp.observe_property("pause", "bool", on_pause_change);

   SSiimmiillaarriittiieess wwiitthh LLuuaa
       mpv tries to load a script file as JavaScript if it has a ..jjss
       extension, but otherwise, the documented Lua options, script
       directories, loading, etc apply to JavaScript files too.

       Script initialization and lifecycle is the same as with Lua, and most
       of the Lua functions in the modules mmpp, mmpp..uuttiillss, mmpp..mmssgg, mmpp..ooppttiioonnss
       and mmpp..iinnppuutt are available to JavaScript with identical APIs -
       including running commands, getting/setting properties, registering
       events/key-bindings/hooks, etc.

   DDiiffffeerreenncceess ffrroomm LLuuaa
       No need to load modules. mmpp, mmpp..uuttiillss,  mmpp..mmssgg, mmpp..ooppttiioonnss and mmpp..iinnppuutt
       are preloaded, and you can use e.g. vvaarr ccwwdd == mmpp..uuttiillss..ggeettccwwdd(());;
       without prior setup.

       Errors are slightly different. Where the Lua APIs return nniill for error,
       the JavaScript ones return uunnddeeffiinneedd. Where Lua returns ssoommeetthhiinngg,,
       eerrrroorr JavaScript returns only ssoommeetthhiinngg - and makes eerrrroorr available via
       mmpp..llaasstt__eerrrroorr(()). Note that only some of the functions have this
       additional eerrrroorr value - typically the same ones which have it in Lua.

       Standard APIs are preferred. For instance sseettTTiimmeeoouutt and JJSSOONN..ssttrriinnggiiffyy
       are available, but mmpp..aadddd__ttiimmeeoouutt and mmpp..uuttiillss..ffoorrmmaatt__jjssoonn are not.

       No standard library. This means that interaction with anything outside
       of mpv is limited to the available APIs, typically via mmpp..uuttiillss.
       However, some file functions were added, and CommonJS rreeqquuiirree is
       available too - where the loaded modules have the same privileges as
       normal scripts.

   LLaanngguuaaggee ffeeaattuurreess -- EECCMMAASSccrriipptt 55
       The scripting backend which mpv currently uses is MuJS - a compatible
       minimal ES5 interpreter. As such, SSttrriinngg..ssuubbssttrriinngg is implemented for
       instance, while the common but non-standard SSttrriinngg..ssuubbssttrr is not.
       Please consult the MuJS pages on language features and platform support
       -  <https://mujs.com>  .

   UUnnssuuppppoorrtteedd LLuuaa AAPPIIss aanndd tthheeiirr JJSS aalltteerrnnaattiivveess
       mmpp..aadddd__ttiimmeeoouutt((sseeccoonnddss,, ffnn))  JS: iidd == sseettTTiimmeeoouutt((ffnn,, mmss))

       mmpp..aadddd__ppeerriiooddiicc__ttiimmeerr((sseeccoonnddss,, ffnn))  JS: iidd == sseettIInntteerrvvaall((ffnn,, mmss))

       uuttiillss..ppaarrssee__jjssoonn((ssttrr [[,, ttrraaiill]]))  JS: JJSSOONN..ppaarrssee((ssttrr))

       uuttiillss..ffoorrmmaatt__jjssoonn((vv))  JS: JJSSOONN..ssttrriinnggiiffyy((vv))

       uuttiillss..ttoo__ssttrriinngg((vv))  see dduummpp below.

       mmpp..ggeett__nneexxtt__ttiimmeeoouutt(()) see event loop below.

       mmpp..ddiissppaattcchh__eevveennttss(([[aallllooww__wwaaiitt]])) see event loop below.

   SSccrriippttiinngg AAPPIIss -- iiddeennttiiccaall ttoo LLuuaa
       (LE) - Last-Error, indicates that mmpp..llaasstt__eerrrroorr(()) can be used after the
       call to test for success (empty string) or failure (non empty reason
       string).  Where the Lua APIs use nniill to indicate error, JS APIs use
       uunnddeeffiinneedd.

       mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd((ssttrriinngg)) (LE)

       mmpp..ccoommmmaannddvv((aarrgg11,, aarrgg22,, ......)) (LE)

       mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee((ttaabbllee [[,,ddeeff]])) (LE)

       iidd == mmpp..ccoommmmaanndd__nnaattiivvee__aassyynncc((ttaabbllee [[,,ffnn]])) (LE) Notes: iidd is true-thy on
       success, eerrrroorr is empty string on success.

       mmpp..aabboorrtt__aassyynncc__ccoommmmaanndd((iidd))

       mmpp..ddeell__pprrooppeerrttyy((nnaammee)) (LE)

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]])) (LE)

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__oossdd((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]])) (LE)

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__bbooooll((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]])) (LE)

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnuummbbeerr((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]])) (LE)

       mmpp..ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnaattiivvee((nnaammee [[,,ddeeff]])) (LE)

       mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy((nnaammee,, vvaalluuee)) (LE)

       mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__bbooooll((nnaammee,, vvaalluuee)) (LE)

       mmpp..sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__nnuummbbeerr((nnaammee,, vvaalluuee)) (LE)

