   TTiimmeerrss ((gglloobbaall))
       The standard HTML/node.js timers are available:

       iidd == sseettTTiimmeeoouutt((ffnn [[,,dduurraattiioonn [[,,aarrgg11 [[,,aarrgg22......]]]]]]))

       iidd == sseettTTiimmeeoouutt((ccooddee__ssttrriinngg [[,,dduurraattiioonn]]))

       cclleeaarrTTiimmeeoouutt((iidd))

       iidd == sseettIInntteerrvvaall((ffnn [[,,dduurraattiioonn [[,,aarrgg11 [[,,aarrgg22......]]]]]]))

       iidd == sseettIInntteerrvvaall((ccooddee__ssttrriinngg [[,,dduurraattiioonn]]))

       cclleeaarrIInntteerrvvaall((iidd))

       sseettTTiimmeeoouutt and sseettIInntteerrvvaall return id, and later call ffnn (or execute
       ccooddee__ssttrriinngg) after dduurraattiioonn ms. Interval also repeat every dduurraattiioonn.

       dduurraattiioonn has a minimum and default value of 0, ccooddee__ssttrriinngg is a plain
       string which is evaluated as JS code, and [[,,aarrgg11 [[,,aarrgg22....]]]] are used as
       arguments (if provided) when calling back ffnn.

       The cclleeaarr......((iidd)) functions cancel timer iidd, and are irreversible.

       Note: timers always call back asynchronously, e.g. sseettTTiimmeeoouutt((ffnn)) will
       never call ffnn before returning. ffnn will be called either at the end of
       this event loop iteration or at a later event loop iteration. This is
       true also for intervals - which also never call back twice at the same
       event loop iteration.

       Additionally, timers are processed after the event queue is empty, so
       it's valid to use sseettTTiimmeeoouutt((ffnn)) as a one-time idle observer.

   CCoommmmoonnJJSS mmoodduulleess aanndd rreeqquuiirree((iidd))
       CommonJS Modules are a standard system where scripts can export common
       functions for use by other scripts. Specifically, a module is a script
       which adds properties (functions, etc) to its pre-existing eexxppoorrttss
       object, which another script can access with rreeqquuiirree((mmoodduullee--iidd)). This
       runs the module and returns its eexxppoorrttss object. Further calls to
       rreeqquuiirree for the same module will return its cached eexxppoorrttss object
       without running the module again.

       Modules and rreeqquuiirree are supported, standard compliant, and generally
       similar to node.js. However, most node.js modules won't run due to
       missing modules such as ffss, pprroocceessss, etc, but some node.js modules with
       minimal dependencies do work. In general, this is for mpv modules and
       not a node.js replacement.

       A ..jjss file extension is always added to iidd, e.g. rreeqquuiirree((""..//ffoooo"")) will
       load the file ..//ffoooo..jjss and return its eexxppoorrttss object.

       An id which starts with ..// or ....// is relative to the script or module
       which rreeqquuiirree it. Otherwise it's considered a top-level id (CommonJS
       term).

       Top-level id is evaluated as absolute filesystem path if possible, e.g.
       //xx//yy or ~~//xx. Otherwise it's considered a global module id and searched
       according to mmpp..mmoodduullee__ppaatthhss in normal array order, e.g. rreeqquuiirree((""xx""))
       tries to load xx..jjss at one of the array paths, and id ffoooo//xx tries to
       load xx..jjss inside dir ffoooo at one of the paths.

       The mmpp..mmoodduullee__ppaatthhss array is empty by default except for scripts which
       are loaded as a directory where it contains one item -
       <<ddiirreeccttoorryy>>//mmoodduulleess// .  The array may be updated from a script (or
       using custom init - see below) which will affect future calls to
       rreeqquuiirree for global module id's which are not already loaded/cached.

       No gglloobbaall variable, but a module's tthhiiss at its top lexical scope is the
       global object - also in strict mode. If you have a module which needs
       gglloobbaall as the global object, you could do tthhiiss..gglloobbaall == tthhiiss;; before
       rreeqquuiirree.

       Functions and variables declared at a module don't pollute the global
       object.

   CCuussttoomm iinniittiiaalliizzaattiioonn
       After mpv initializes the JavaScript environment for a script but
       before it loads the script - it tries to run the file iinniitt..jjss at the
       root of the mpv configuration directory. Code at this file can update
       the environment further for all scripts. E.g. if it contains
       mmpp..mmoodduullee__ppaatthhss..ppuusshh((""//ffoooo"")) then rreeqquuiirree at all scripts will search
       global module id's also at //ffoooo (do NOT do mmpp..mmoodduullee__ppaatthhss == [[""//ffoooo""]];;
       because this will remove existing paths - like <<ssccrriipptt--ddiirr>>//mmoodduulleess for
       scripts which load from a directory).

       The custom-init file is ignored if mpv is invoked with ----nnoo--ccoonnffiigg.

       Before mpv 0.34, the file name was ..iinniitt..jjss (with dot) at the same dir.

   TThhee eevveenntt lloooopp
       The event loop poll/dispatch mpv events as long as the queue is not
       empty, then processes the timers, then waits for the next event, and
       repeats this forever.

       You could put this code at your script to replace the built-in event
       loop, and also print every event which mpv sends to your script:

          function mp_event_loop() {
              var wait = 0;
              do {
                  var e = mp.wait_event(wait);
                  dump(e);  // there could be a lot of prints...
                  if (e.event != "none") {
                      mp.dispatch_event(e);
                      wait = 0;
                  } else {
                      wait = mp.process_timers() / 1000;
                      if (wait != 0) {
                          mp.notify_idle_observers();
                          wait = mp.peek_timers_wait() / 1000;
                      }
                  }
              } while (mp.keep_running);
          }

       mmpp__eevveenntt__lloooopp is a name which mpv tries to call after the script loads.
       The internal implementation is similar to this (without dduummpp though..).

       ee == mmpp..wwaaiitt__eevveenntt((wwaaiitt)) returns when the next mpv event arrives, or
       after wwaaiitt seconds if positive and no mpv events arrived. wwaaiitt value of
       0 returns immediately (with ee..eevveenntt ==== ""nnoonnee"" if the queue is empty).

       mmpp..ddiissppaattcchh__eevveenntt((ee)) calls back the handlers registered for ee..eevveenntt, if
       there are such (event handlers, property observers, script messages,
       etc).

       mmpp..pprroocceessss__ttiimmeerrss(()) calls back the already-added, non-canceled due
       timers, and returns the duration in ms till the next due timer
       (possibly 0), or -1 if there are no pending timers. Must not be called
       recursively.

       mmpp..nnoottiiffyy__iiddllee__oobbsseerrvveerrss(()) calls back the idle observers, which we do
       when we're about to sleep (wait != 0), but the observers may add timers
       or take non-negligible duration to complete, so we re-calculate wwaaiitt
       afterwards.

       mmpp..ppeeeekk__ttiimmeerrss__wwaaiitt(()) returns the same values as mmpp..pprroocceessss__ttiimmeerrss(())
       but without doing anything. Invalid result if called from a timer
       callback.

       Note: eexxiitt(()) is also registered for the sshhuuttddoowwnn event, and its
       implementation is a simple mmpp..kkeeeepp__rruunnnniinngg == ffaallssee.

JJSSOONN IIPPCC
       mpv can be controlled by external programs using the JSON-based IPC
       protocol.  It can be enabled by specifying the path to a unix socket or
       a named pipe using the option ----iinnppuutt--iippcc--sseerrvveerr, or the file
       descriptor number of a unix socket or a named pipe using
       ----iinnppuutt--iippcc--cclliieenntt.  Clients can connect to this socket and send
       commands to the player or receive events from it.

       WWAARRNNIINNGG::
          This is not intended to be a secure network protocol. It is
          explicitly insecure: there is no authentication, no encryption, and
          the commands themselves are insecure too. For example, the rruunn
          command is exposed, which can run arbitrary system commands. The
          use-case is controlling the player locally. This is not different
          from the MPlayer slave protocol.

   SSooccaatt eexxaammppllee
       You can use the ssooccaatt tool to send commands (and receive replies) from
       the shell. Assuming mpv was started with:

          mpv file.mkv --input-ipc-server=/tmp/mpvsocket

       Then you can control it using socat:

          > echo '{ "command": ["get_property", "playback-time"] }' | socat - /tmp/mpvsocket
          {"data":190.482000,"error":"success"}

       In this case, socat copies data between stdin/stdout and the mpv socket
       connection.

       See the ----iiddllee option how to make mpv start without exiting immediately
       or playing a file.

       It's also possible to send input.conf style text-only commands:

          > echo 'show-text ${playback-time}' | socat - /tmp/mpvsocket

       But you won't get a reply over the socket. (This particular command
       shows the playback time on the player's OSD.)

   CCoommmmaanndd PPrroommpptt eexxaammppllee
       Unfortunately, it's not as easy to test the IPC protocol on Windows,
       since Windows ports of socat (in Cygwin and MSYS2) don't understand
       named pipes. In the absence of a simple tool to send and receive from
       bidirectional pipes, the eecchhoo command can be used to send commands, but
       not receive replies from the command prompt.

       Assuming mpv was started with:

          mpv file.mkv --input-ipc-server=\\.\pipe\mpvsocket

       You can send commands from a command prompt:

          echo show-text ${playback-time} >\\.\pipe\mpvsocket

       To be able to simultaneously read and write from the IPC pipe, like on
       Linux, it's necessary to write an external program that uses overlapped
       file I/O (or some wrapper like .NET's NamedPipeClientStream.)

       You can open the pipe in PuTTY as "serial" device. This is not very
       comfortable, but gives a way to test interactively without having to
       write code.

   PPrroottooccooll
       The protocol uses UTF-8-only JSON as defined by RFC-8259. Unlike
       standard JSON, "u" escape sequences are not allowed to construct
       surrogate pairs. To avoid getting conflicts, encode all text characters
       including and above codepoint U+0020 as UTF-8. mpv might output broken
       UTF-8 in corner cases (see "UTF-8" section below).

       Clients can execute commands on the player by sending JSON messages of
       the following form:

          { "command": ["command_name", "param1", "param2", ...] }

       where ccoommmmaanndd__nnaammee is the name of the command to be executed, followed
       by a list of parameters. Parameters must be formatted as native JSON
       values (integers, strings, booleans, ...). Every message mmuusstt be
       terminated with \\nn. Additionally, \\nn must not appear anywhere inside
       the message. In practice this means that messages should be minified
       before being sent to mpv.

       mpv will then send back a reply indicating whether the command was run
       correctly, and an additional field holding the command-specific return
       data (it can also be null).

          { "error": "success", "data": null }

       mpv will also send events to clients with JSON messages of the
       following form:

          { "event": "event_name" }

       where eevveenntt__nnaammee is the name of the event. Additional event-specific
       fields can also be present. See List of events for a list of all
       supported events.

       Because events can occur at any time, it may be difficult at times to
       determine which response goes with which command. Commands may
       optionally include a rreeqquueesstt__iidd which, if provided in the command
       request, will be copied verbatim into the response. mpv does not
       interpret the rreeqquueesstt__iidd in any way; it is solely for the use of the
       requester. The only requirement is that the rreeqquueesstt__iidd field must be an
       integer (a number without fractional parts in the range --22^^6633....22^^6633--11).
       Using other types is deprecated and will currently show a warning. In
       the future, this will raise an error.

       For example, this request:

          { "command": ["get_property", "time-pos"], "request_id": 100 }

       Would generate this response:

          { "error": "success", "data": 1.468135, "request_id": 100 }

       If you don't specify a rreeqquueesstt__iidd, command replies will set it to 0.

       All commands, replies, and events are separated from each other with a
       line break character (\\nn).

       If the first character (after skipping whitespace) is not {{, the
       command will be interpreted as non-JSON text command, as they are used
       in input.conf (or mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd__ssttrriinngg(()) in the client API).
       Additionally, lines starting with ## and empty lines are ignored.

       Currently, embedded 0 bytes terminate the current line, but you should
       not rely on this.

   DDaattaa ffllooww
       Currently, the mpv-side IPC implementation does not service the socket
       while a command is executed and the reply is written. It is for example
       not possible that other events, that happened during the execution of
       the command, are written to the socket before the reply is written.

       This might change in the future. The only guarantee is that replies to
       IPC messages are sent in sequence.

       Also, since socket I/O is inherently asynchronous, it is possible that
       you read unrelated event messages from the socket, before you read the
       reply to the previous command you sent. In this case, these events were
       queued by the mpv side before it read and started processing your
       command message.

       If the mpv-side IPC implementation switches away from blocking writes
       and blocking command execution, it may attempt to send events at any
       time.

       You can also use asynchronous commands, which can return in any order,
       and which do not block IPC protocol interaction at all while the
       command is executed in the background.

   AAssyynncchhrroonnoouuss ccoommmmaannddss
       Command can be run asynchronously. This behaves exactly as with normal
       command execution, except that execution is not blocking. Other
       commands can be sent while it's executing, and command completion can
       be arbitrarily reordered.

       The aassyynncc field controls this. If present, it must be a boolean. If
       missing, ffaallssee is assumed.

       For example, this initiates an asynchronous command:

          { "command": ["screenshot"], "request_id": 123, "async": true }

       And this is the completion:

          {"request_id":123,"error":"success","data":null}

       By design, you will not get a confirmation that the command was
       started. If a command is long running, sending the message will not
       lead to any reply until much later when the command finishes.

       Some commands execute synchronously, but these will behave like
       asynchronous commands that finished execution immediately.

       Cancellation of asynchronous commands is available in the libmpv API,
       but has not yet been implemented in the IPC protocol.

   CCoommmmaannddss wwiitthh nnaammeedd aarrgguummeennttss
       If the ccoommmmaanndd field is a JSON object, named arguments are expected.
       This is described in the C API mmppvv__ccoommmmaanndd__nnooddee(()) documentation (the
       MMPPVV__FFOORRMMAATT__NNOODDEE__MMAAPP case). In some cases, this may make commands more
       readable, while some obscure commands basically require using named
       arguments.

       Currently, only "proper" commands (as listed by List of Input Commands)
       support named arguments.

   CCoommmmaannddss
       In addition to the commands described in List of Input Commands, a few
       extra commands can also be used as part of the protocol:

       cclliieenntt__nnaammee
              Return the name of the client as string. This is the string
              iippcc--NN with N being an integer number.

       ggeett__ttiimmee__uuss
              Return the current mpv internal time in microseconds as a
              number. This is basically the system time, with an arbitrary
              offset.

       ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy
              Return the value of the given property. The value will be sent
              in the data field of the replay message.

              Example:

                 { "command": ["get_property", "volume"] }
                 { "data": 50.0, "error": "success" }

       ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy__ssttrriinngg
              Like ggeett__pprrooppeerrttyy, but the resulting data will always be a
              string.

              Example:

                 { "command": ["get_property_string", "volume"] }
                 { "data": "50.000000", "error": "success" }

       sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy
              Set the given property to the given value. See Properties for
              more information about properties.

              Example:

                 { "command": ["set_property", "pause", true] }
                 { "error": "success" }

       sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy__ssttrriinngg
              Alias for sseett__pprrooppeerrttyy. Both commands accept native values and
              strings.

       oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy
              Watch a property for changes. If the given property is changed,
              then an event of type pprrooppeerrttyy--cchhaannggee will be generated

              Example:

                 { "command": ["observe_property", 1, "volume"] }
                 { "error": "success" }
                 { "event": "property-change", "id": 1, "data": 52.0, "name": "volume" }

              WWAARRNNIINNGG::
                 If the connection is closed, the IPC client is destroyed
                 internally, and the observed properties are unregistered.
                 This happens for example when sending commands to a socket
                 with separate ssooccaatt invocations.  This can make it seem like
                 property observation does not work. You must keep the IPC
                 connection open to make it work.

       oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy__ssttrriinngg
              Like oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy, but the resulting data will always be a
              string.

              Example:

                 { "command": ["observe_property_string", 1, "volume"] }
                 { "error": "success" }
                 { "event": "property-change", "id": 1, "data": "52.000000", "name": "volume" }

       uunnoobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy
              Undo oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy or oobbsseerrvvee__pprrooppeerrttyy__ssttrriinngg. This requires
              the numeric id passed to the observed command as argument.

              Example:

                 { "command": ["unobserve_property", 1] }
                 { "error": "success" }

       rreeqquueesstt__lloogg__mmeessssaaggeess
              Enable output of mpv log messages. They will be received as
              events. The parameter to this command is the log-level (see
              mmppvv__rreeqquueesstt__lloogg__mmeessssaaggeess C API function).

              Log message output is meant for humans only (mostly for
              debugging).  Attempting to retrieve information by parsing these
              messages will just lead to breakages with future mpv releases.
              Instead, make a feature request, and ask for a proper event that
              returns the information you need.

       eennaabbllee__eevveenntt,, ddiissaabbllee__eevveenntt
              Enables or disables the named event. Mirrors the
              mmppvv__rreeqquueesstt__eevveenntt C API function. If the string aallll is used
              instead of an event name, all events are enabled or disabled.

              By default, most events are enabled, and there is not much use
              for this command.

       ggeett__vveerrssiioonn
              Returns the client API version the C API of the remote mpv
              instance provides.

              See also: DDOOCCSS//cclliieenntt--aappii--cchhaannggeess..rrsstt.

   UUTTFF--88
       Normally, all strings are in UTF-8. Sometimes it can happen that
       strings are in some broken encoding (often happens with file tags and
       such, and filenames on many Unixes are not required to be in UTF-8
       either). This means that mpv sometimes sends invalid JSON. If that is a
       problem for the client application's parser, it should filter the raw
       data for invalid UTF-8 sequences and perform the desired replacement,
       before feeding the data to its JSON parser.

       mpv will not attempt to construct invalid UTF-8 with broken "u" escape
       sequences. This includes surrogate pairs.

   JJSSOONN eexxtteennssiioonnss
       The following non-standard extensions are supported:

          • a list or object item can have a trailing ","

          • object syntax accepts "=" in addition of ":"

          • object keys can be unquoted, if they start with a character in
            "A-Za-z_" and contain only characters in "A-Za-z0-9_"

          • byte escapes with "xAB" are allowed (with AB being a 2 digit hex
            number)

       Example:

          { objkey = "value\x0A" }

       Is equivalent to:

          { "objkey": "value\n" }

   AAlltteerrnnaattiivvee wwaayyss ooff ssttaarrttiinngg cclliieennttss
       You can create an anonymous IPC connection without having to set
       ----iinnppuutt--iippcc--sseerrvveerr. This is achieved through a mpv pseudo scripting
       backend that starts processes.

       You can put ..rruunn file extension in the mpv scripts directory in its
       config directory (see the FILES section for details), or load them
       through other means (see Script location). These scripts are simply
       executed with the OS native mechanism (as if you ran them in the
       shell). They must have a proper shebang and have the executable bit
       set.

       When executed, a socket (the IPC connection) is passed to them through
       file descriptor inheritance. The file descriptor is indicated as the
       special command line argument ----mmppvv--iippcc--ffdd==NN, where NN is the numeric
       file descriptor.

       The rest is the same as with a normal ----iinnppuutt--iippcc--sseerrvveerr IPC
       connection. mpv does not attempt to observe or other interact with the
       started script process.

       This does not work in Windows yet.

CCHHAANNGGEELLOOGG
       There is no real changelog, but you can look at the following things:

       • The release changelog, which should contain most user-visible
         changes, including new features and bug fixes:

          <https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/releases>

       • The git log, which is the "real" changelog

       • The file
         <https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/blob/master/DOCS/interface-changes.rst>
         documents changes to the command and user interface, such as options
         and properties.

       • C API changes are listed in
          <https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/blob/master/DOCS/client-api-changes.rst>

       • The file mmppllaayyeerr--cchhaannggeess..rrsstt in the DDOOCCSS sub directory on the git
         repository, which used to be in place of this section. It documents
         some changes that happened since mplayer2 forked off MPlayer. (Not
         updated anymore.)

EEMMBBEEDDDDIINNGG IINNTTOO OOTTHHEERR PPRROOGGRRAAMMSS ((LLIIBBMMPPVV))
       mpv can be embedded into other programs as video/audio playback
       backend. The recommended way to do so is using libmpv. See
       iinncclluuddee//mmppvv//cclliieenntt..hh in the mpv source code repository. This provides a
       C API. Bindings for other languages might be available (see wiki).

       Since libmpv merely allows access to underlying mechanisms that can
       control mpv, further documentation is spread over a few places:

       •
          <https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/blob/master/include/mpv/client.h>

       •
          <https://mpv.io/manual/master/#options>

       •
          <https://mpv.io/manual/master/#list-of-input-commands>

       •
          <https://mpv.io/manual/master/#properties>

       •
          <https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv-examples/tree/master/libmpv>

CC PPLLUUGGIINNSS
       You can write C plugins for mpv. These use the libmpv API, although
       they do not use the libmpv library itself.

       They are enabled by default if compiler supports linking with the
       --rrddyynnaammiicc flag on Linux/BSD platforms. On Windows the are always
       enabled.

   CC pplluuggiinnss llooccaattiioonn
       C plugins are put into the mpv scripts directory in its config
       directory (see the FILES section for details). They must have a ..ssoo or
       ..ddllll file extension. They can also be explicitly loaded with the
       ----ssccrriipptt option.

   AAPPII
       A C plugin must export the following function:

          int mpv_open_cplugin(mpv_handle *handle)

       The plugin function will be called on loading time. This function does
       not return as long as your plugin is loaded (it runs in its own
       thread). The hhaannddllee will be deallocated as soon as the plugin function
       returns.

       The return value is interpreted as error status. A value of 00 is
       interpreted as success, while --11 signals an error. In the latter case,
       the player prints an uninformative error message that loading failed.

       Return values other than 00 and --11 are reserved, and trigger undefined
       behavior.

       Within the plugin function, you can call libmpv API functions. The
       hhaannddllee is created by mmppvv__ccrreeaattee__cclliieenntt(()) (or actually an internal
       equivalent), and belongs to you. You can call mmppvv__wwaaiitt__eevveenntt(()) to wait
       for things happening, and so on.

       Note that the player might block until your plugin calls
       mmppvv__wwaaiitt__eevveenntt(()) for the first time. This gives you a chance to install
       initial hooks etc.  before playback begins.

       The details are quite similar to Lua scripts.

   LLiinnkkaaggee ttoo lliibbmmppvv
       The current implementation requires that your plugins are nnoott linked
       against libmpv. What your plugins use are not symbols from a libmpv
       binary, but symbols from the mpv host binary.

       On Windows to make symbols from the host binary available, you have to
       define MPV_CPLUGIN_DYNAMIC_SYM when compiling cplugin. This will load
       symbols dynamically, before calling mmppvv__ooppeenn__ccpplluuggiinn(()).

   EExxaammpplleess
       See:

       •
          <https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv-examples/tree/master/cplugins>

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT VVAARRIIAABBLLEESS
       There are a number of environment variables that can be used to control
       the behavior of mpv.

       HHOOMMEE,, XXDDGG__CCOONNFFIIGG__HHOOMMEE
              Used to determine mpv config directory. If XXDDGG__CCOONNFFIIGG__HHOOMMEE is
              not set, $$HHOOMMEE//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv is used.

              $$HHOOMMEE//..mmppvv is always added to the list of config search paths
              with a lower priority.

       MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE
              Directory where mpv looks for user settings. Overrides HHOOMMEE, and
              mpv will try to load the config file as $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE//mmppvv..ccoonnff.

       MMPPVV__VVEERRBBOOSSEE ((sseeee aallssoo --vv aanndd ----mmssgg--lleevveell))
              Set the initial verbosity level across all message modules
              (default: 0).  This is an integer, and the resulting verbosity
              corresponds to the number of ----vv options passed to the command
              line.

       MMPPVV__LLEEAAKK__RREEPPOORRTT
              If set to 11, enable internal talloc leak reporting. If set to
              another value, disable leak reporting.

       LLAADDSSPPAA__PPAATTHH
              Specifies the search path for LADSPA plugins. If it is unset,
              fully qualified path names must be used.

       DDIISSPPLLAAYY
              Standard X11 display name to use.

       FFFFmmppeegg::
              This library accesses various environment variables. However,
              they are not centrally documented, and documenting them is not
              our job. Therefore, this list is incomplete.

              Notable environment variables:

              hhttttpp__pprrooxxyy
                     URL to proxy for hhttttpp:://// and hhttttppss:://// URLs.

              nnoo__pprrooxxyy
                     List of domain patterns for which no proxy should be
                     used.  List entries are separated by ,,. Patterns can
                     include **.

       lliibbddvvddccssss::

              DDVVDDCCSSSS__CCAACCHHEE
                     Specify a directory in which to store title key values.
                     This will speed up descrambling of DVDs which are in the
                     cache. The DDVVDDCCSSSS__CCAACCHHEE directory is created if it does
                     not exist, and a subdirectory is created named after the
                     DVD's title or manufacturing date. If DDVVDDCCSSSS__CCAACCHHEE is not
                     set or is empty, libdvdcss will use the default value
                     which is $${{HHOOMMEE}}//..ddvvddccssss// under Unix and the roaming
                     application data directory (%%AAPPPPDDAATTAA%%) under Windows. The
                     special value "off" disables caching.

              DDVVDDCCSSSS__MMEETTHHOODD
                     Sets the authentication and decryption method that
                     libdvdcss will use to read scrambled discs. Can be one of
                     ttiittllee, kkeeyy or ddiisscc.

                     kkeeyy    is the default method. libdvdcss will use a set of
                            calculated player keys to try to get the disc key.
                            This can fail if the drive does not recognize any
                            of the player keys.

                     ddiisscc   is a fallback method when key has failed. Instead
                            of using player keys, libdvdcss will crack the
                            disc key using a brute force algorithm. This
                            process is CPU intensive and requires 64 MB of
                            memory to store temporary data.

                     ttiittllee  is the fallback when all other methods have
                            failed. It does not rely on a key exchange with
                            the DVD drive, but rather uses a crypto attack to
                            guess the title key. On rare cases this may fail
                            because there is not enough encrypted data on the
                            disc to perform a statistical attack, but on the
                            other hand it is the only way to decrypt a DVD
                            stored on a hard disc, or a DVD with the wrong
                            region on an RPC2 drive.

              DDVVDDCCSSSS__RRAAWW__DDEEVVIICCEE
                     Specify the raw device to use. Exact usage will depend on
                     your operating system, the Linux utility to set up raw
                     devices is raw(8) for instance. Please note that on most
                     operating systems, using a raw device requires highly
                     aligned buffers: Linux requires a 2048 bytes alignment
                     (which is the size of a DVD sector).

              DDVVDDCCSSSS__VVEERRBBOOSSEE
                     Sets the libdvdcss verbosity level.

                     00      Outputs no messages at all.

                     11      Outputs error messages to stderr.

                     22      Outputs error messages and debug messages to
                            stderr.

              DDVVDDRREEAADD__NNOOKKEEYYSS
                     Skip retrieving all keys on startup. Currently disabled.

              HHOOMMEE   FIXME: Document this.

EEXXIITT CCOODDEESS
       Normally mmppvv returns 0 as exit code after finishing playback
       successfully.  If errors happen, the following exit codes can be
       returned:

          11      Error initializing mpv. This is also returned if unknown
                 options are passed to mpv.

          22      The file passed to mpv couldn't be played. This is somewhat
                 fuzzy: currently, playback of a file is considered to be
                 successful if initialization was mostly successful, even if
                 playback fails immediately after initialization.

          33      There were some files that could be played, and some files
                 which couldn't (using the definition of success from above).

          44      Quit due to a signal, Ctrl+c in a VO window (by default), or
                 from the default quit key bindings in encoding mode.

       Note that quitting the player manually will always lead to exit code 0,
       overriding the exit code that would be returned normally. Also, the
       qquuiitt input command can take an exit code: in this case, that exit code
       is returned.

OOPPTTIICCAALL DDRRIIVVEESS
       Depending on the OS, mpv will choose a different disc device by
       default.  This applies for all optical disc playback (CDDA, DVD, and
       BD).

                              ┌────────┬───────────────┐
                              │OS      │ Default Drive │
                              ├────────┼───────────────┤
                              │Linux   │ /dev/sr0      │
                              ├────────┼───────────────┤
                              │Windows │ D:            │
                              ├────────┼───────────────┤
                              │macOS   │ /dev/disk1    │
                              ├────────┼───────────────┤
                              │FreeBSD │ /dev/cd0      │
                              ├────────┼───────────────┤
                              │OpenBSD │ /dev/rcd0c    │
                              └────────┴───────────────┘
FFIILLEESS
       Note that this section assumes Linux/BSD. On other platforms the paths
       may be different.  For Windows-specifics, see FILES ON WINDOWS section.

       All configuration files should be encoded in UTF-8.

       //uussrr//llooccaall//eettcc//mmppvv//mmppvv..ccoonnff
              mpv system-wide settings (depends on ----pprreeffiixx passed to
              configure - mpv in default configuration will use
              //uussrr//llooccaall//eettcc//mmppvv// as config directory, while most Linux
              distributions will set it to //eettcc//mmppvv//).

       ~~//..ccaacchhee//mmppvv
              The standard cache directory. Certain options within mpv may
              cause it to write cache files to disk. This can be overridden by
              environment variables, in ascending order:

              11      If $$XXDDGG__CCAACCHHEE__HHOOMMEE is set, then the derived cache
                     directory will be $$XXDDGG__CCAACCHHEE__HHOOMMEE//mmppvv.

              22      If $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE is set, then the derived cache directory
                     will be $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE.

              If the directory does not exist, mpv will try to create it
              automatically.

       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv
              The standard configuration directory. This can be overridden by
              environment variables, in ascending order:

              11      If $$XXDDGG__CCOONNFFIIGG__HHOOMMEE is set, then the derived
                     configuration directory will be $$XXDDGG__CCOONNFFIIGG__HHOOMMEE//mmppvv.

              22      If $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE is set, then the derived configuration
                     directory will be $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE.

              If this directory, nor the original configuration directory (see
              below) do not exist, mpv tries to create this directory
              automatically.

       ~~//..mmppvv//
              The original (pre 0.5.0) configuration directory. It will
              continue to be read if present. If this directory is present and
              the standard configuration directory is not present, then cache
              files and watch later config files will also be written to this
              directory.

              If both this directory and the standard configuration directory
              are present, configuration will be read from both with the
              standard configuration directory content taking precedence.
              However, you should fully migrate to the standard directory and
              a warning will be shown in this situation.

       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//mmppvv..ccoonnff
              mpv user settings (see CONFIGURATION FILES section)

       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//iinnppuutt..ccoonnff
              key bindings (see INPUT.CONF section)

       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//ffoonnttss..ccoonnff
              Fontconfig fonts.conf that is customized for mpv. You should
              include system fonts.conf in this file or mpv would not know
              about fonts that you already have in the system.

              Only available when libass is built with fontconfig.

       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//ssuubbffoonntt..ttttff
              fallback subtitle font

       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//ffoonnttss//
              Default location for ----ssuubb--ffoonnttss--ddiirr (see Subtitles) and
              ----oossdd--ffoonnttss--ddiirr (see OSD).

       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//ssccrriippttss//
              All files in this directory are loaded as if they were passed to
              the ----ssccrriipptt option. They are loaded in alphabetical order.

              The ----llooaadd--ssccrriippttss==nnoo option disables loading these files.

              See Script location for details.

       ~~//..llooccaall//ssttaattee//mmppvv//wwaattcchh__llaatteerr//
              Contains temporary config files needed for resuming playback of
              files with the watch later feature. See for example the QQ key
              binding, or the qquuiitt--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr input command.

              This can be overridden by environment variables, in ascending
              order:

              11      If $$XXDDGG__SSTTAATTEE__HHOOMMEE is set, then the derived watch later
                     directory will be $$XXDDGG__SSTTAATTEE__HHOOMMEE//mmppvv//wwaattcchh__llaatteerr.

              22      If $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE is set, then the derived watch later
                     directory will be $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE//wwaattcchh__llaatteerr.

              Each file is a small config file which is loaded if the
              corresponding media file is loaded. It contains the playback
              position and some (not necessarily all) settings that were
              changed during playback. The filenames are hashed from the full
              paths of the media files. It's in general not possible to
              extract the media filename from this hash. However, you can set
              the ----wwrriittee--ffiilleennaammee--iinn--wwaattcchh--llaatteerr--ccoonnffiigg option, and the
              player will add the media filename to the contents of the resume
              config file.

       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//ssccrriipptt--ooppttss//oosscc..ccoonnff
              This is loaded by the OSC script. See the ON SCREEN CONTROLLER
              docs for details.

              Other files in this directory are specific to the corresponding
              scripts as well, and the mpv core doesn't touch them.

FFIILLEESS OONN WWIINNDDOOWWSS
       On win32 (if compiled with MinGW, but not Cygwin), the default config
       file locations are different. They are generally located under
       %%AAPPPPDDAATTAA%%//mmppvv//.  For example, the path to mpv.conf is
       %%AAPPPPDDAATTAA%%//mmppvv//mmppvv..ccoonnff, which maps to a system and user-specific path,
       for example
          CC::\\uusseerrss\\UUSSEERRNNAAMMEE\\AAppppDDaattaa\\RRooaammiinngg\\mmppvv\\mmppvv..ccoonnff

       You can find the exact path by running eecchhoo %%AAPPPPDDAATTAA%%\\mmppvv\\mmppvv..ccoonnff in
       cmd.exe.

       Other config files (such as iinnppuutt..ccoonnff) are in the same directory. See
       the FILES section above.

       The cache directory is located at %%LLOOCCAALLAAPPPPDDAATTAA%%//mmppvv//ccaacchhee.

       The watch_later directory is located at %%LLOOCCAALLAAPPPPDDAATTAA%%//mmppvv//wwaattcchh__llaatteerr.

       The environment variable $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE completely overrides these, like on
       UNIX.

       If a directory named ppoorrttaabbllee__ccoonnffiigg next to the mpv.exe exists, all
       config will be loaded from this directory only. Watch later config
       files and cache files are written to this directory as well. (This
       exists on Windows only and is redundant with $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE. However, since
       Windows is very scripting unfriendly, a wrapper script just setting
       $$MMPPVV__HHOOMMEE, like you could do it on other systems, won't work.
       ppoorrttaabbllee__ccoonnffiigg is provided for convenience to get around this
       restriction.)

       Config files located in the same directory as mmppvv..eexxee are loaded with
       lower priority. Some config files are loaded only once, which means
       that e.g. of 2 iinnppuutt..ccoonnff files located in two config directories, only
       the one from the directory with higher priority will be loaded.

       A third config directory with the lowest priority is the directory
       named mmppvv in the same directory as mmppvv..eexxee. This used to be the
       directory with the highest priority, but is now discouraged to use and
       might be removed in the future.

       Note that mpv likes to mix // and \\ path separators for simplicity.
       kernel32.dll accepts this, but cmd.exe does not.

FFIILLEESS OONN MMAACCOOSS
       On macOS the watch later directory is located at
       ~~//..ccoonnffiigg//mmppvv//wwaattcchh__llaatteerr// and the cache directory is set to
       ~~//LLiibbrraarryy//CCaacchheess//iioo..mmppvv//. These directories can't be overwritten by
       environment variables.  Everything else is the same as FILES.

CCOOPPYYRRIIGGHHTT
